{"run_id": "25278e8f-8de3-4ca9-abfb-df23094b1afc", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:51:50.556878+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20250071412A1", "independent_claim": "1. A method for enhancing media, the method comprising:\nidentifying whether a scene of a first preview frame captured by at least one primary camera was captured in low light based on at least one pre-defined parameter of the first preview frame;\nbased on identifying that the first preview frame was captured in low light, triggering at least one secondary camera to capture a second preview frame of the scene, wherein the second preview frame has an exposure time higher than an exposure time of the first preview frame, and wherein the second preview frame comprises one of a higher field of view (FOV) and a same FOV as compared to the first preview frame; and\ngenerating an output frame based on the first preview frame, the second preview frame, and at least one of the at least one pre-defined parameter", "specification": "The electronic device 102 on determining that the first preview frame has captured a low light scene, can trigger the secondary camera. In an embodiment herein, the secondary camera can be triggered to capture frames with a larger Field of View (FOV), as compared to the first preview frame. The larger FOV allows the secondary camera to capture more efficient images containing more scenes to capture the entire frame. In an embodiment herein, the secondary camera can be triggered to capture frames with a smaller Field of View (FOV), as compared to the first preview frame. In an embodiment herein, the secondary camera can be triggered to capture frames with the same FOV.\n[0062]\nThe secondary camera can be configured with a higher exposure time (as compared to the exposure time of the first preview frame) to obtain at least one secondary preview frame of the scene. The secondary preview frame can comprise more details. The electronic device 102 may generate an output frame from the captured first preview frame and secondary preview frame based on at least one pre-defined parameter. The electronic device 102 may provide the output frame by combining the first and second preview frames based on an artificial intelligent (AI) model to enhance the low light frame captured in a media.\n\n[0093]\nThe low-light analysis module 302 may analyze the low-light conditions of the captured first preview frame using the primary camera based on at least one pre-defined parameter. The pre-defined parameter(s) can be used to determine if the captured scene/media has captured a low light scene. The at least one pre-defined parameter of the captured scene may include but not limited to the primary camera's/sensor's luminance value, International Organization for Standardization (ISO), exposure gain and the like. The luminance value of the primary camera may refer to an exposure value that measures the luminous intensity per unit area of light travelling in a given direction. ISO may refer to camera settings which can be used to brighten or darken the scene while capturing the media."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20220053142A1", "independent_claim": ". A method, comprising:\nat an electronic device having a display device and one or more cameras:\nreceiving a request to display a camera user interface; and\nin response to receiving the request to display the camera user interface, displaying, via the display device, a camera user interface that includes:\ndisplaying, via the display device, a representation of a field-of-view of the one or more cameras;\nin accordance with a determination that low-light conditions have been met, wherein the low-light conditions include a condition that is met when ambient light in the field-of-view of the one or more cameras is below a respective threshold, displaying, concurrently with the representation of the field-of-view of the one or more cameras, a control for adjusting a capture duration for capturing media in response to a request to capture media; and\nin accordance with a determination that the low-light conditions have not been met, forgoing display of the control for adjusting the capture duration.", "specification": "[0618]\nAs illustrated in FIG. 18C, in response to input 1895A, device 600 updates low-light mode status indicator 602 c to indicate that low-light mode is active and flash mode status indicator 602 a to indicate that the flash operation is inactive. While low-light mode and the flash operation are both useful when capturing media in a darker environment, in the present embodiment, low-light mode is mutually exclusive with the flash operation. In addition, in response to input 1895A, device 600 displays adjustable low-light mode control 1804 for setting a capture duration for capturing media in the low-light mode. Indication 1818 on adjustable low-light mode control 1804 indicates that the low-light mode is set to a particular capture duration, where each tick mark on adjustable low-light mode control 1804 represents a different capture duration.\n[0619]\nNotably, live preview 630 is visually brighter in FIG. 18C than it was in FIG. 18B. This is because when low-light mode is active, device 600 operates one or more of its cameras using a lower frame rate (e.g., corresponding to longer exposure times). Using the standard frame rate (e.g., a higher frame rate) in a low light environment captures darker images (as shown in FIG. 18B) because exposure times for each frame are short. Thus, when device 600 is operating in low-light mode (as shown in 18C), device 600 lowers the frame rate from the standard frame rate.\n[0620]\nIn FIG. 18C, device 600 is being held substantially still and the subject in the FOV is likewise substantially still. In some embodiments, if the content in the FOV is moving above a threshold speed (e.g., due to movement of device 600 and/or movement of the subjects in the FOV), device 600 forgoes lowering the frame rate or lowers the frame rate to a lesser degree than if movement is not detected, as lower framerates can result in blurred images, when content is moving in the FOV. Thus, device 600 can be configured to balance the options between decreasing the frame rate due to low-light in the environment and increasing the frame rate due to detected movement in the environment.\n[0621]\nAs illustrated in FIG. 18D, in response to detecting input 1895B, device 600 has started capturing media using low-light mode. When initiating capture of the media, live preview 630 ceases to be displayed. In particular, live preview 630 darkens to black. Moreover, device 600 also replaces display of shutter affordance 610 with stop affordance 1806 and generates tactile response 1820A. Stop affordance 1806 indicates that low-light mode capture can be stopped by an input on stop affordance 1806. Further in response to detecting input 1895B, device 600 also initiates movement of indication 1818 towards a capture duration of zero (e.g., a countdown from 1 sec to zero). In some embodiments, adjustable low-light mode control 1804 also changes color (e.g., white to red) in response to detecting input 1895B."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20250071412A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A method for enhancing media, the method comprising:\nidentifying whether a scene of a first preview frame captured by at least one primary camera was captured in low light based on at least one pre-defined parameter of the first preview frame;\nbased on identifying that the first preview frame was captured in low light, triggering at least one secondary camera to capture a second preview frame of the scene, wherein the second preview frame has an exposure time higher than an exposure time of the first preview frame, and wherein the second preview frame comprises one of a higher field of view (FOV) and a same FOV as compared to the first preview frame; and\ngenerating an output frame based on the first preview frame, the second preview frame, and at least one of the at least one pre-defined parameter\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe electronic device 102 on determining that the first preview frame has captured a low light scene, can trigger the secondary camera. In an embodiment herein, the secondary camera can be triggered to capture frames with a larger Field of View (FOV), as compared to the first preview frame. The larger FOV allows the secondary camera to capture more efficient images containing more scenes to capture the entire frame. In an embodiment herein, the secondary camera can be triggered to capture frames with a smaller Field of View (FOV), as compared to the first preview frame. In an embodiment herein, the secondary camera can be triggered to capture frames with the same FOV.\n[0062]\nThe secondary camera can be configured with a higher exposure time (as compared to the exposure time of the first preview frame) to obtain at least one secondary preview frame of the scene. The secondary preview frame can comprise more details. The electronic device 102 may generate an output frame from the captured first preview frame and secondary preview frame based on at least one pre-defined parameter. The electronic device 102 may provide the output frame by combining the first and second preview frames based on an artificial intelligent (AI) model to enhance the low light frame captured in a media.\n\n[0093]\nThe low-light analysis module 302 may analyze the low-light conditions of the captured first preview frame using the primary camera based on at least one pre-defined parameter. The pre-defined parameter(s) can be used to determine if the captured scene/media has captured a low light scene. The at least one pre-defined parameter of the captured scene may include but not limited to the primary camera's/sensor's luminance value, International Organization for Standardization (ISO), exposure gain and the like. The luminance value of the primary camera may refer to an exposure value that measures the luminous intensity per unit area of light travelling in a given direction. ISO may refer to camera settings which can be used to brighten or darken the scene while capturing the media.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20220053142A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. A method, comprising:\nat an electronic device having a display device and one or more cameras:\nreceiving a request to display a camera user interface; and\nin response to receiving the request to display the camera user interface, displaying, via the display device, a camera user interface that includes:\ndisplaying, via the display device, a representation of a field-of-view of the one or more cameras;\nin accordance with a determination that low-light conditions have been met, wherein the low-light conditions include a condition that is met when ambient light in the field-of-view of the one or more cameras is below a respective threshold, displaying, concurrently with the representation of the field-of-view of the one or more cameras, a control for adjusting a capture duration for capturing media in response to a request to capture media; and\nin accordance with a determination that the low-light conditions have not been met, forgoing display of the control for adjusting the capture duration.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n[0618]\nAs illustrated in FIG. 18C, in response to input 1895A, device 600 updates low-light mode status indicator 602 c to indicate that low-light mode is active and flash mode status indicator 602 a to indicate that the flash operation is inactive. While low-light mode and the flash operation are both useful when capturing media in a darker environment, in the present embodiment, low-light mode is mutually exclusive with the flash operation. In addition, in response to input 1895A, device 600 displays adjustable low-light mode control 1804 for setting a capture duration for capturing media in the low-light mode. Indication 1818 on adjustable low-light mode control 1804 indicates that the low-light mode is set to a particular capture duration, where each tick mark on adjustable low-light mode control 1804 represents a different capture duration.\n[0619]\nNotably, live preview 630 is visually brighter in FIG. 18C than it was in FIG. 18B. This is because when low-light mode is active, device 600 operates one or more of its cameras using a lower frame rate (e.g., corresponding to longer exposure times). Using the standard frame rate (e.g., a higher frame rate) in a low light environment captures darker images (as shown in FIG. 18B) because exposure times for each frame are short. Thus, when device 600 is operating in low-light mode (as shown in 18C), device 600 lowers the frame rate from the standard frame rate.\n[0620]\nIn FIG. 18C, device 600 is being held substantially still and the subject in the FOV is likewise substantially still. In some embodiments, if the content in the FOV is moving above a threshold speed (e.g., due to movement of device 600 and/or movement of the subjects in the FOV), device 600 forgoes lowering the frame rate or lowers the frame rate to a lesser degree than if movement is not detected, as lower framerates can result in blurred images, when content is moving in the FOV. Thus, device 600 can be configured to balance the options between decreasing the frame rate due to low-light in the environment and increasing the frame rate due to detected movement in the environment.\n[0621]\nAs illustrated in FIG. 18D, in response to detecting input 1895B, device 600 has started capturing media using low-light mode. When initiating capture of the media, live preview 630 ceases to be displayed. In particular, live preview 630 darkens to black. Moreover, device 600 also replaces display of shutter affordance 610 with stop affordance 1806 and generates tactile response 1820A. Stop affordance 1806 indicates that low-light mode capture can be stopped by an input on stop affordance 1806. Further in response to detecting input 1895B, device 600 also initiates movement of indication 1818 towards a capture duration of zero (e.g., a countdown from 1 sec to zero). In some embodiments, adjustable low-light mode control 1804 also changes color (e.g., white to red) in response to detecting input 1895B."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"identifying whether a scene of a first preview frame captured by at least one primary camera was captured in low light based on at least one pre-defined parameter of the first preview frame\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s claim and specification determine low-light conditions only from ambient light levels (e.g., \\\"low-light conditions include a condition that is met when ambient light in the field-of-view of the one or more cameras is below a respective threshold\\\"). It does not disclose using a pre-defined parameter of the preview frame such as luminance, ISO, or exposure gain. Therefore the element is not disclosed (novelty N). While detecting low-light from ambient light is known, using frame\u2011specific parameters would be an obvious alternative to a skilled practitioner, so the inventive step is considered obvious (Y). Verdict is N because novelty is lacking.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"based on identifying that the first preview frame was captured in low light, triggering at least one secondary camera to capture a second preview frame of the scene, wherein the second preview frame has an exposure time higher than an exposure time of the first preview frame, and wherein the second preview frame comprises one of a higher field of view (FOV) and a same FOV as compared to the first preview frame\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discusses lowering the frame rate (longer exposure) in low\u2011light mode (e.g., \\\"device 600 operates one or more of its cameras using a lower frame rate (e.g., corresponding to longer exposure times)\\\") but never mentions triggering a secondary camera, nor selecting a different or same field\u2011of\u2011view for that secondary capture. Consequently the element is not disclosed (novelty N). The combination of a secondary camera with selectable exposure and FOV to improve low\u2011light imaging is not taught or suggested, making it a non\u2011obvious improvement (inventive_step N). Verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating an output frame based on the first preview frame, the second preview frame, and at least one of the at least one pre-defined parameter\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s disclosure stops at displaying UI controls and adjusting capture duration; it does not describe combining a first and second preview frame (or any pre\u2011defined parameter) to produce an enhanced output frame. Hence the element is not found in the prior art (novelty N). Creating a composite output frame from multiple captures for low\u2011light enhancement is not suggested, rendering the step non\u2011obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict is N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014detecting low\u2011light via frame\u2011specific parameters, invoking a secondary camera with higher exposure and selectable field\u2011of\u2011view, and synthesizing an enhanced output frame\u2014is not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent B. Each element is novel, and at least two of them are non\u2011obvious over the teachings of the target. Accordingly, the source patent retains substantive novelty and inventive step, supporting its validity.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2641, "tokens_output": 1962, "latency_ms": 6186}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "identifying whether a scene of a first preview frame captured by at least one primary camera was captured in low light based on at least one pre-defined parameter of the first preview frame", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s claim and specification determine low-light conditions only from ambient light levels (e.g., \"low-light conditions include a condition that is met when ambient light in the field-of-view of the one or more cameras is below a respective threshold\"). It does not disclose using a pre-defined parameter of the preview frame such as luminance, ISO, or exposure gain. Therefore the element is not disclosed (novelty N). While detecting low-light from ambient light is known, using frame\u2011specific parameters would be an obvious alternative to a skilled practitioner, so the inventive step is considered obvious (Y). Verdict is N because novelty is lacking."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "based on identifying that the first preview frame was captured in low light, triggering at least one secondary camera to capture a second preview frame of the scene, wherein the second preview frame has an exposure time higher than an exposure time of the first preview frame, and wherein the second preview frame comprises one of a higher field of view (FOV) and a same FOV as compared to the first preview frame", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent discusses lowering the frame rate (longer exposure) in low\u2011light mode (e.g., \"device 600 operates one or more of its cameras using a lower frame rate (e.g., corresponding to longer exposure times)\") but never mentions triggering a secondary camera, nor selecting a different or same field\u2011of\u2011view for that secondary capture. Consequently the element is not disclosed (novelty N). The combination of a secondary camera with selectable exposure and FOV to improve low\u2011light imaging is not taught or suggested, making it a non\u2011obvious improvement (inventive_step N). Verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "generating an output frame based on the first preview frame, the second preview frame, and at least one of the at least one pre-defined parameter", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s disclosure stops at displaying UI controls and adjusting capture duration; it does not describe combining a first and second preview frame (or any pre\u2011defined parameter) to produce an enhanced output frame. Hence the element is not found in the prior art (novelty N). Creating a composite output frame from multiple captures for low\u2011light enhancement is not suggested, rendering the step non\u2011obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict is N."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014detecting low\u2011light via frame\u2011specific parameters, invoking a secondary camera with higher exposure and selectable field\u2011of\u2011view, and synthesizing an enhanced output frame\u2014is not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent B. Each element is novel, and at least two of them are non\u2011obvious over the teachings of the target. Accordingly, the source patent retains substantive novelty and inventive step, supporting its validity."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "a6c76d48-82cf-4629-a298-1773424f96ad", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:52:26.606556+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20250209806A1", "independent_claim": "A method performed by one or more computers, the method comprising:\nreceiving a text prompt describing a scene;\nprocessing the text prompt, using a text encoder neural network, to generate a contextual embedding of the text prompt;\nprocessing the contextual embedding, using a sequence of generative neural networks, to generate a final output representation of a video depicting the scene,\nwherein the sequence of generative neural networks comprises:\nan initial generative neural network configured to:\nreceive the contextual embedding; and\nprocess the contextual embedding to generate, as output, an initial output representation of the video having an initial dimensionality; and\none or more subsequent generative neural networks each configured to:\nreceive a respective input comprising an input representation of the video generated as output by a preceding generative neural network in the sequence; and\nprocess the respective input to generate, as output, a respective output representation of the video having higher dimensionality than the input representation; and\nprocessing the final output representation of the video, using an output neural network, to generate the video depicting the scene.", "specification": "At a high-level, the video generation system 100 includes a text encoder neural network 110, a sequence of generative neural networks (GNNs) 121 and, in some implementations, a post-processor 130. The system 100 is configured to receive text prompts 102 as input and to generate final videos 108 as output.\n[0064]\nMore particularly, the system 100 can receive a text prompt (Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00001) 102 describing a scene. The text prompt 102 can be a text sequence that includes multiple tokens Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00001 1,2, . . . in a natural language Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00001=(Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00001 1, Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00001 2, . . . ). For example, as shown in FIG. 1A, the text prompt 102 can include: \u201cA bunch of autumn leaves falling on a calm lake to form the text \u2018Imagen Video\u2019. Smooth.\u201d In general, the text prompt 102 can describe any particular scene and the system 100, when appropriately trained (e.g., by a training engine), is capable of generating high resolution videos that faithfully depict the scene. The text prompt 102 can also include text modifiers such as \u201cSmooth\u201d, \u201cStudio lighting\u201d, \u201cPixel art\u201d, \u201cin the style of Van Gough\u201d, etc., that impart various styles, modifications and/or characteristics on final videos 108 generated by the system 100. The system 100 can generate various different types of videos such as photorealistic videos, cartoon videos, abstract visualizations, imaging videos of different modalities, among others. For example, the system 100 can generate medical videos, e.g., videos depicting a sequence of MRI, CT or ultrasound video frames.\n[0065]\nThe text encoder 110 is configured to process the text prompt 102 to generate a contextual embedding (u) of the text prompt 102. In some implementations, the text encoder 110 is a pre-trained natural language text encoder, e.g., a T5 text encoder such as T5-XXL, a CLIP text encoder, among others. The contextual embedding 104 can also be referred to as an encoded representation of the text prompt 102 that provides a computationally amenable representation for processing by the system 100. For example, the contextual embedding 104 can be a set, vector, or array of values (e.g., in UNICODE or Base64 encoding), alphanumeric values, symbols, or any convenient encoding.\n[0066]\nThe sequence of GNNs 121 includes multiple GNNs 120 that are each configured to receive a respective input (c). Each GNN 120 is configured to process their respective input to generate a respective output video ({circumflex over (x)}). In general, the sequence 121 includes an initial GNN that generates an initial output video (e.g., at low resolution) and one or more subsequent GNNs that progressively refine the resolution of the initial output video, i.e., the spatial resolution, the temporal resolution, or both. For example, each subsequent GNN can be a spatial super-resolution (SSR) model to increase spatial resolution, a temporal super-resolution (TSR) model to increase temporal resolution, or a joint spatial-temporal super-resolution (STSR) model to increase both spatial and temporal resolution. Accordingly, the respective input for the initial GNN includes the contextual embedding 104, while the respective input for each subsequent GNN includes the output video generated by a preceding GNN in the sequence 121. In some cases, the respective input to each subsequent GNN may include one or more output videos generated at lower depth in the sequence 121, as opposed to only the preceding GNN. Such cases are not described in detail but can be realized using the techniques outlined herein. In some implementations, the respective input for each subsequent GNN also includes the contextual embedding 104 which generally improves performance of the system 100, e.g., such that each subsequent GNN generates a respective output video that is strongly conditioned on the text prompt 102. The system 100 processes the contextual embedding 104 through the sequence 121 to generate an output video 106 at a high resolution, with few (if any) artifacts. The output video 106 is usually the respective output video of a final GNN in the sequence 121 but can, in principle, be provided by any GNN 120 in the sequence 121.\n[0067]\nIn some implementations, the output video 106 is further processed by the post-processor 130 to generate a final video (x) 108. For example, the post-processor 130 can perform transformations on the output video 106 such as video enhancement, motion blur, filtering, transitions, luminance, lens flare, warp stabilization, de-shaking, brightening, sharpening, contrast, among other video effects. Some or all of the transformations performed by the post-processor 130 may also be performed by the sequence 121 when the GNNs 120 are suitably trained (e.g., by a training engine). For example, the GNNs 120 can learn these transformations and associate them with respective text modifiers included in text prompts 102. In some implementations, the system 100 does not include the post-processor 130 and the output video 106 generated by the sequence 121 is the final video 108. Alternatively, system 100 can disable the post-processor 130 such that transformations performed on the output video 106 by the post-processor 130 are equivalent to the identity operation.\n[0068]\nIn some implementations, the post-processor 130 may perform analysis on the output video 106 such as video classification and/or video quality analysis. The post-processor 130 may include one or more neural networks such as a convolutional neural network (CNN), a recurrent neural network (RNN), or a video encoder to perform such analysis. For example, the post-processor 130 can determine if the output video 106 accurately depicts the scene described by the text prompt 102 by encoding the output video 106 into a video embedding and comparing it with the contextual embedding 104. In these cases, the post-processor 130 may include a video encoder that is paired with the text encoder 110, such as pre-trained text-video encoder pair (similar to a CLIP text-image encoder pair). This also presents a means of zero-shot (or semi-supervised) training of the sequence 121 by comparing video embeddings with contextual embeddings 104. In other words, the sequence 121 may be trained (e.g., by a training engine) to generate output videos 106 from text-based training sets (as opposed to only labelled text-video training sets) by generating output videos 106 that faithfully reconstruct contextual embeddings 104 once encoded into video embeddings. As another example, the post-processor 130 can determine if the output video 106 has high resolution, temporal consistency, few artifacts, etc., using a CNN and/or a RNN that quantify spatial and/or temporal coherence, as well as using traditional objective video quality models.\n[0069]\nThe final video 108 depicts the scene described by the text prompt 102 and is output by the system 100 with a final resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00002=Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y. For example, as shown in FIG. 1A, the final video 108 (represented by sample frames 109.1-5) depicts a bunch of autumn leaves falling on a calm lake to form the text \u2018Imagen Video\u2019. Accordingly, the final video 108 is accurately captioned by the corresponding text prompt 102 in FIG. 1A. FIGS. 7A-7F show other examples of videos that can be generated from text prompts by the video generation system 100.\n[0070]\nThe final resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00002 corresponds to the final temporal resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 multiplied by the final spatial resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y and is a measure of the information content of the video 108, i.e., the dimensionality of the video 108. As mentioned above, temporal resolution can correspond to a framerate, i.e., a number of independent frames Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 over a pre-defined period of time. In this case, the final video x=(x1, x2, . . . , xFigure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003) includes multiple frames, with more (independent) frames providing higher temporal resolution. Also mentioned above, spatial resolution can correspond to a per frame pixel resolution, i.e., a number of independent pixels per frame Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 xy=Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y over pre-defined lengths (or a pre-defined area). In this case, each frame\nFigure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00004\ncan include a respective Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y sized array of pixel values (e.g., corresponding to RGB color codes), with more (independent) pixels providing higher spatial resolution. Hence, the number of independent frames N multiplied by the number of independent pixels per frame Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y provides the final resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00002 and total dimension of the final video 108. In most cases, the final resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00002 of the final video 108 is equal to the resolution R of the output video 106, but these may differ in some implementations (e.g., if the post-processor 130 resizes the output video 106)."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US12462348B2", "independent_claim": "1. A method comprising:\nobtaining a noise input and guidance information indicating a target image control;\ngenerating, using an image generation model, a noise prediction based on the noise input, wherein the noise prediction comprises a prediction of noise to remove from the noise input;\ngenerating noise modulation parameters based on the guidance information using a conditioning network, wherein the noise modulation parameters represent the target image control;\ncombining the noise prediction and the noise modulation parameters to obtain a modified noise prediction, wherein the modified noise prediction comprises a modified prediction of noise to remove from the noise input based on the target image control; and\ngenerating, using the image generation model, a synthetic image based on the modified noise prediction, wherein the synthetic image depicts a scene based on the target image control.", "specification": "According to some embodiments, diffusion model 230 generates an intermediate noise prediction for the image based on the noise image. Diffusion model 230 combines the intermediate noise prediction and the noise modulation parameters to obtain a modified intermediate noise prediction. Diffusion model 230 generates the image based on the modified intermediate noise prediction, where the image depicts a scene based on the guidance information. In some examples, diffusion model 230 performs element-wise multiplication of the intermediate noise prediction and a first portion of the noise modulation parameters. Diffusion model 230 performs element-wise addition of the intermediate noise prediction and a second portion of the noise modulation parameters.\nIn some examples, diffusion model 230 iteratively generates a set of intermediate noise predictions corresponding to a set of diffusion steps, respectively. In some examples, diffusion model 230 generates an intermediate image prediction, where the noise modulation parameters are generated based on the intermediate image prediction. In some examples, diffusion model 230 generates a subsequent image prediction based on the intermediate image prediction and the modified intermediate noise prediction, where the image is generated based on the subsequent image prediction.\nAccording to some embodiments, diffusion model 230 obtains a noise image. Diffusion model 230 generates an intermediate noise prediction based on the noise image. Diffusion model 230 combines the intermediate noise prediction and the noise modulation parameters to obtain a modified intermediate noise prediction. Diffusion model 230 generates a predicted image based on the modified intermediate noise prediction, where the training is based on the predicted image. In some examples, diffusion model 230 generates a final output prediction at an intermediate diffusion step. In some examples, diffusion model 230 is pre-trained prior to training conditioning network 235.\nAccording to some embodiments, diffusion model 230 is trained to generate images by iteratively generating intermediate noise predictions. In some embodiments, diffusion model 230 includes a U-Net architecture, where the noise modulation parameters are applied to an output of the U-Net architecture of the diffusion model. In some embodiments, the diffusion model includes text-guided diffusion model, and the diffusion model takes a text prompt as an input. In some embodiments, diffusion model 230 includes a denoising diffusion implicit model (DDIM). Diffusion model 230 is an example of, or includes aspects of, the corresponding element described with reference to FIGS. 3 and 5 .\nAccording to some embodiments, DDIM are a class of diffusion models. In some cases, DDIM refers to a denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM) that is generalized via a class of non-Markovian diffusion processes. For example, the DDIM can be trained with the same objective function as DDPM. In some cases, DDIM includes forward processing that becomes deterministic in the image generation step. The implicit probabilistic model includes a sample generation process from latent variables with a fixed procedure that results in superior sample generation quality compared to DDPM."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20250209806A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method performed by one or more computers, the method comprising:\nreceiving a text prompt describing a scene;\nprocessing the text prompt, using a text encoder neural network, to generate a contextual embedding of the text prompt;\nprocessing the contextual embedding, using a sequence of generative neural networks, to generate a final output representation of a video depicting the scene,\nwherein the sequence of generative neural networks comprises:\nan initial generative neural network configured to:\nreceive the contextual embedding; and\nprocess the contextual embedding to generate, as output, an initial output representation of the video having an initial dimensionality; and\none or more subsequent generative neural networks each configured to:\nreceive a respective input comprising an input representation of the video generated as output by a preceding generative neural network in the sequence; and\nprocess the respective input to generate, as output, a respective output representation of the video having higher dimensionality than the input representation; and\nprocessing the final output representation of the video, using an output neural network, to generate the video depicting the scene.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nAt a high-level, the video generation system 100 includes a text encoder neural network 110, a sequence of generative neural networks (GNNs) 121 and, in some implementations, a post-processor 130. The system 100 is configured to receive text prompts 102 as input and to generate final videos 108 as output.\n[0064]\nMore particularly, the system 100 can receive a text prompt (Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00001) 102 describing a scene. The text prompt 102 can be a text sequence that includes multiple tokens Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00001 1,2, . 1A, the text prompt 102 can include: \u201cA bunch of autumn leaves falling on a calm lake to form the text \u2018Imagen Video\u2019. Smooth.\u201d In general, the text prompt 102 can describe any particular scene and the system 100, when appropriately trained (e.g., by a training engine), is capable of generating high resolution videos that faithfully depict the scene. The text prompt 102 can also include text modifiers such as \u201cSmooth\u201d, \u201cStudio lighting\u201d, \u201cPixel art\u201d, \u201cin the style of Van Gough\u201d, etc., that impart various styles, modifications and/or characteristics on final videos 108 generated by the system 100. The system 100 can generate various different types of videos such as photorealistic videos, cartoon videos, abstract visualizations, imaging videos of different modalities, among others. For example, the system 100 can generate medical videos, e.g., videos depicting a sequence of MRI, CT or ultrasound video frames.\n[0065]\nThe text encoder 110 is configured to process the text prompt 102 to generate a contextual embedding (u) of the text prompt 102. In some implementations, the text encoder 110 is a pre-trained natural language text encoder, e.g., a T5 text encoder such as T5-XXL, a CLIP text encoder, among others. The contextual embedding 104 can also be referred to as an encoded representation of the text prompt 102 that provides a computationally amenable representation for processing by the system 100. For example, the contextual embedding 104 can be a set, vector, or array of values (e.g., in UNICODE or Base64 encoding), alphanumeric values, symbols, or any convenient encoding.\n[0066]\nThe sequence of GNNs 121 includes multiple GNNs 120 that are each configured to receive a respective input (c). Each GNN 120 is configured to process their respective input to generate a respective output video ({circumflex over (x)}). In general, the sequence 121 includes an initial GNN that generates an initial output video (e.g., at low resolution) and one or more subsequent GNNs that progressively refine the resolution of the initial output video, i.e., the spatial resolution, the temporal resolution, or both. For example, each subsequent GNN can be a spatial super-resolution (SSR) model to increase spatial resolution, a temporal super-resolution (TSR) model to increase temporal resolution, or a joint spatial-temporal super-resolution (STSR) model to increase both spatial and temporal resolution. Accordingly, the respective input for the initial GNN includes the contextual embedding 104, while the respective input for each subsequent GNN includes the output video generated by a preceding GNN in the sequence 121. In some cases, the respective input to each subsequent GNN may include one or more output videos generated at lower depth in the sequence 121, as opposed to only the preceding GNN. In some implementations, the respective input for each subsequent GNN also includes the contextual embedding 104 which generally improves performance of the system 100, e.g., such that each subsequent GNN generates a respective output video that is strongly conditioned on the text prompt 102. The system 100 processes the contextual embedding 104 through the sequence 121 to generate an output video 106 at a high resolution, with few (if any) artifacts. The output video 106 is usually the respective output video of a final GNN in the sequence 121 but can, in principle, be provided by any GNN 120 in the sequence 121.\n[0067]\nIn some implementations, the output video 106 is further processed by the post-processor 130 to generate a final video (x) 108. For example, the post-processor 130 can perform transformations on the output video 106 such as video enhancement, motion blur, filtering, transitions, luminance, lens flare, warp stabilization, de-shaking, brightening, sharpening, contrast, among other video effects. Some or all of the transformations performed by the post-processor 130 may also be performed by the sequence 121 when the GNNs 120 are suitably trained (e.g., by a training engine). For example, the GNNs 120 can learn these transformations and associate them with respective text modifiers included in text prompts 102. In some implementations, the system 100 does not include the post-processor 130 and the output video 106 generated by the sequence 121 is the final video 108. Alternatively, system 100 can disable the post-processor 130 such that transformations performed on the output video 106 by the post-processor 130 are equivalent to the identity operation.\n[0068]\nIn some implementations, the post-processor 130 may perform analysis on the output video 106 such as video classification and/or video quality analysis. The post-processor 130 may include one or more neural networks such as a convolutional neural network (CNN), a recurrent neural network (RNN), or a video encoder to perform such analysis. For example, the post-processor 130 can determine if the output video 106 accurately depicts the scene described by the text prompt 102 by encoding the output video 106 into a video embedding and comparing it with the contextual embedding 104. In these cases, the post-processor 130 may include a video encoder that is paired with the text encoder 110, such as pre-trained text-video encoder pair (similar to a CLIP text-image encoder pair). This also presents a means of zero-shot (or semi-supervised) training of the sequence 121 by comparing video embeddings with contextual embeddings 104. In other words, the sequence 121 may be trained (e.g., by a training engine) to generate output videos 106 from text-based training sets (as opposed to only labelled text-video training sets) by generating output videos 106 that faithfully reconstruct contextual embeddings 104 once encoded into video embeddings. As another example, the post-processor 130 can determine if the output video 106 has high resolution, temporal consistency, few artifacts, etc., using a CNN and/or a RNN that quantify spatial and/or temporal coherence, as well as using traditional objective video quality models.\n[0069]\nThe final video 108 depicts the scene described by the text prompt 102 and is output by the system 100 with a final resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00002=Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y. 1A, the final video 108 (represented by sample frames 109.1-5) depicts a bunch of autumn leaves falling on a calm lake to form the text \u2018Imagen Video\u2019. Accordingly, the final video 108 is accurately captioned by the corresponding text prompt 102 in FIG. 7A-7F show other examples of videos that can be generated from text prompts by the video generation system 100.\n[0070]\nThe final resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00002 corresponds to the final temporal resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 multiplied by the final spatial resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y and is a measure of the information content of the video 108, i.e., the dimensionality of the video 108. In this case, the final video x=(x1, x2, . , xFigure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003) includes multiple frames, with more (independent) frames providing higher temporal resolution. In this case, each frame\nFigure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00004\ncan include a respective Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y sized array of pixel values (e.g., corresponding to RGB color codes), with more (independent) pixels providing higher spatial resolution. Hence, the number of independent frames N multiplied by the number of independent pixels per frame Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 x\u00d7Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00003 y provides the final resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00002 and total dimension of the final video 108. In most cases, the final resolution Figure US20250209806A1-20250626-P00002 of the final video 108 is equal to the resolution R of the output video 106, but these may differ in some implementations (e.g., if the post-processor 130 resizes the output video 106).\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US12462348B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A method comprising:\nobtaining a noise input and guidance information indicating a target image control;\ngenerating, using an image generation model, a noise prediction based on the noise input, wherein the noise prediction comprises a prediction of noise to remove from the noise input;\ngenerating noise modulation parameters based on the guidance information using a conditioning network, wherein the noise modulation parameters represent the target image control;\ncombining the noise prediction and the noise modulation parameters to obtain a modified noise prediction, wherein the modified noise prediction comprises a modified prediction of noise to remove from the noise input based on the target image control; and\ngenerating, using the image generation model, a synthetic image based on the modified noise prediction, wherein the synthetic image depicts a scene based on the target image control.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nAccording to some embodiments, diffusion model 230 generates an intermediate noise prediction for the image based on the noise image. Diffusion model 230 combines the intermediate noise prediction and the noise modulation parameters to obtain a modified intermediate noise prediction. Diffusion model 230 generates the image based on the modified intermediate noise prediction, where the image depicts a scene based on the guidance information. In some examples, diffusion model 230 performs element-wise multiplication of the intermediate noise prediction and a first portion of the noise modulation parameters. Diffusion model 230 performs element-wise addition of the intermediate noise prediction and a second portion of the noise modulation parameters.\nIn some examples, diffusion model 230 iteratively generates a set of intermediate noise predictions corresponding to a set of diffusion steps, respectively. In some examples, diffusion model 230 generates an intermediate image prediction, where the noise modulation parameters are generated based on the intermediate image prediction. In some examples, diffusion model 230 generates a subsequent image prediction based on the intermediate image prediction and the modified intermediate noise prediction, where the image is generated based on the subsequent image prediction.\nAccording to some embodiments, diffusion model 230 obtains a noise image. Diffusion model 230 generates an intermediate noise prediction based on the noise image. Diffusion model 230 combines the intermediate noise prediction and the noise modulation parameters to obtain a modified intermediate noise prediction. Diffusion model 230 generates a predicted image based on the modified intermediate noise prediction, where the training is based on the predicted image. In some examples, diffusion model 230 generates a final output prediction at an intermediate diffusion step. In some examples, diffusion model 230 is pre-trained prior to training conditioning network 235.\nAccording to some embodiments, diffusion model 230 is trained to generate images by iteratively generating intermediate noise predictions. In some embodiments, diffusion model 230 includes a U-Net architecture, where the noise modulation parameters are applied to an output of the U-Net architecture of the diffusion model. In some embodiments, the diffusion model includes text-guided diffusion model, and the diffusion model takes a text prompt as an input. In some embodiments, diffusion model 230 includes a denoising diffusion implicit model (DDIM). Diffusion model 230 is an example of, or includes aspects of, the corresponding element described with reference to FIGS. 3 and 5 .\nAccording to some embodiments, DDIM are a class of diffusion models. In some cases, DDIM refers to a denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM) that is generalized via a class of non-Markovian diffusion processes. For example, the DDIM can be trained with the same objective function as DDPM. In some cases, DDIM includes forward processing that becomes deterministic in the image generation step. The implicit probabilistic model includes a sample generation process from latent variables with a fixed procedure that results in superior sample generation quality compared to DDPM."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "", "model": "", "tokens_input": 0, "tokens_output": 0, "latency_ms": 0}, "parsed_output": null, "status": "api_error", "error": "Error code: 413 - {'error': {'message': 'Request too large for model `openai/gpt-oss-120b` in organization `org_01kabyas5zfmqvk2yfzv78z8ya` service tier `on_demand` on tokens per minute (TPM): Limit 8000, Requested 8265, please reduce your message size and try again. Need more tokens? Upgrade to Dev Tier today at https://console.groq.com/settings/billing', 'type': 'tokens', 'code': 'rate_limit_exceeded'}}", "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "601df5ee-9c14-4f71-91e8-8fe13bc1c697", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:53:08.535592+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20250391415A1", "independent_claim": "An audio encoder, comprising:\none or more processors configured to:\nreceive an input multi-channel audio signal comprising a primary input audio channel and L non-primary input audio channels;\ndetermine a set of L input gains, where L is a positive integer greater than one;\nfor each of the L non-primary input audio channels and L input gains, form a respective scaled non-primary input audio channel from the respective non-primary input audio channel scaled according to the input gain;\nform a primary output audio channel from the sum of the primary input audio channel and the scaled non-primary input audio channels;\ndetermine a set of L prediction gains:\nfor each of the L prediction gains, form a prediction channel from the primary output audio channel scaled according to the prediction gain;\nform L non-primary output audio channels from the difference of the respective non-primary input audio channel and the respective prediction signal;\nform an output multi-channel audio signal from the primary output audio channel and the L non-primary output audio channels;\nencode with an audio encoder, the output multi-channel audio signal; and\ntransmit or store the encoded output multi-channel audio signal.", "specification": "FIG. 1 is a block diagram of an arrangement 10 of a simple audio encoder and simple audio decoder, intended to form a multi-channel audio signal 17 (7S) that is a facsimile of multi-channel audio signal 13 (Z). Multi-channel audio signal 13 is processed by simple audio encoder 14 to produce encoded representation 15, which may be stored 20 and/or transmitted 21 to simple audio decoder 16 which produces multi-channel audio signal 17. Preferably, the data size of encoded representation 15 is minimized whilst minimizing the difference between multi-channel audio signal 13 and multi-channel audio signal 17. Furthermore, the difference between multi-channel audio signal 13 and multi-channel audio signal 17 may be measured according to similarity as perceived by a human listener. The measure of human-perceived similarity between audio signal 13 and audio signal 17 is based on a reference playback method (that is, the assumed default means by which the audio channels of multi-channel audio signals 13, 17 are presented as an auditory experience to the listener).\n[0032]\nThe efficiency of simple audio encoder 14 and decoder 16 may be defined in terms of the data rate (measured in bits per second) of the encoded representation 15 required to provide multi-channel audio signal 17 that will be judged by a listener to match multi-channel audio signal 13 with a particular perceived quality level. Simple audio encoder 14 and decoder 16 may achieve greater efficiency (that is, a lower data rate) when the multi-channel audio signal 13 is known to possess particular attributes. In particular, greater efficiency may be achieved when it is known that multi-channel audio signal 13 possesses the following attributes (DD1 and DD2):\nDD1: One or more channels of the multi-channel audio signal are generally more dominant than others, where a more dominant audio channel is one that will contain substantial elements of most (or all) of the sonic elements in the scene. That is, a dominant audio signal, when presented as a single audio channel to a listener, will contain most (or all) of the sonic elements of the multi-channel signal, when the multi-channel audio signal is presented to a listener through a reference playback method.\nDD2: Each of the audio channels of the multi-channel audio signal is largely uncorrelated to each of the other audio channels\n[0035]\nGiven the knowledge that multi-channel audio signal 13 possesses attributes DD1 and DD2, simple audio encoder 14 may achieve improved efficiency using several techniques including, but not limited to: allocating fewer bits to the encoding of less dominant channels or choosing to discard less dominant channels entirely. Simple audio encoder 14 may provide data to simple audio decoder 16 to assist in the regeneration of channels that were discarded by simple encoder audio encoder 14. Preferably, a multi-channel audio signal that does not possess attributes DD1 and DD2 may be processed by an encoder pre-mixer to form, e.g., to calculate, to determine, to construct or to generate, a multi-channel audio signal that does possess attributes DD1 and DD2, as described further in reference to FIG. 2 . A corresponding decoder post-mixer may be applied to the simple decoder output to form an output multi-channel audio signal, such that the decoder post-mixer performs an approximate inverse operation relative to the operation of the encoder pre-mixer."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US7391870B2", "independent_claim": ". Apparatus for generating a multi-channel output signal having K output channels, the multi-channel output signal corresponding to a multi-channel input signal having C input channels, using E transmission channels, the E transmission channels representing a result of a downmix operation having C input channels as an input, and using parametric information related to the input channels, wherein E is \u22672, C is >E, and K is >1 and \u2266C, and wherein the downmix operation is effective to introduce a first input channel in a first transmission channel and in a second transmission channel, and to additionally introduce a second input channel in the first transmission channel, comprising:\na cancellation channel calculator for calculating a cancellation channel using information related to the first input channel included in the first transmission channel, the second transmission channel or the parametric information;\na combiner for combining the cancellation channel and the first transmission channel or a processed version thereof to obtain a second base channel, in which an influence of the first input channel is reduced compared to the influence of the first input channel on the first transmission channel; and\na channel reconstructor for reconstructing a second output channel corresponding to the second input channel using the second base channel and parametric information related to the second input channel, and for reconstructing a first output channel corresponding to the first input channel using a first base channel being different from the second base channel in that the influence of the first channel is higher compared to the second base channel, and parametric information related to the first input channel.", "specification": "Instead, the invention is a simple concept that does not have these disadvantages and aims at reducing the influence of the center channel signal component in the side channels.\nAs has been discussed in connection with FIGS. 7 a-7 d, the base channels for the five reconstructed output channels of 5-to-2 BCC are:\n{tilde over (s)} 1(k)={tilde over (y)} 1(k)={tilde over (x)} 1(k)+{tilde over (x)} 3(k)/+\u221a{square root over (2)}+{tilde over (x)} 4(k)\n{tilde over (s)} 2(k)={tilde over (y)} 2(k)={tilde over (x)} 2(k)+{tilde over (x)} 3(k)/+\u221a{square root over (2)}+{tilde over (x)} 5(k)\n{tilde over (s)} 3(k)={tilde over (y)} 1(k)+{tilde over (y)} 2(k)={tilde over (x)}1(k) +{tilde over (x)} 2(k)+\u221a{square root over (2)}{tilde over (x)} 3(k)+{tilde over (x)}4(k)+{tilde over (x)}\n{tilde over (s)} 4(k)={tilde over (s)} 1(k)\n{tilde over (x)} 5(k)={tilde over (s)} 2(k)\nIt is to be noted that the original center channel signal component x3 appears 3 dB amplified in the center base channel subband s3 (factor 1/\u221a2) and 3 dB attenuated in the remaining (side channel) base channel subbands.\nIn order to further attenuate the influence of the center channel signal component in the side base channel subbands according to this invention, the following general idea is applied as illustrated in FIG. 2.\nAn estimate of the final decoded center channel signal is computed by preferably scaling it to the desired target level as described by the corresponding level information such as an ICLD value in BCC environments. Preferably, this decoded center signal is calculated in the spectral domain in order to save computation, i.e. no synthesis filterbank processing is applied.\nAdditionally, this center decoded signal or center reconstructed signal, which corresponds to the cancellation channel, can be weighted and then combined to both the base channel signals of the other output channels. This combining is preferably a subtraction. Nevertheless, when the weighting factors have a different sign, then an addition also results in the reduction of the influence of the center channel in the base channel used for reconstructing the left or the right output channel. This processing results in forming a modified base channel for reconstruction of left and left surround or for reconstruction of right or right surround. Preferably a weighting factor of \u22123 dB is preferred, but also any other value is possible.\nInstead of the original transmission base channel signals as used in FIG. 7 b, modified base channel signals are used for the computation of the decoded output channel of the other output channels, i.e. the channels other than the center channel.\nIn the following, a block diagram of the inventive concept will be discussed by reference to FIG. 2. FIG. 2 shows an apparatus for generating a multi-channel output signal having K output channels, the multi-channel output signal corresponding to a multi-channel input signal having C input channels, using E transmission channels, the E transmission channels representing a result of a downmix operation having the C input channels as an input, and using parametric side information on the input channels, wherein C is \u22672, C is >E, and K is >1 and \u2266C."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20250391415A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nAn audio encoder, comprising:\none or more processors configured to:\nreceive an input multi-channel audio signal comprising a primary input audio channel and L non-primary input audio channels;\ndetermine a set of L input gains, where L is a positive integer greater than one;\nfor each of the L non-primary input audio channels and L input gains, form a respective scaled non-primary input audio channel from the respective non-primary input audio channel scaled according to the input gain;\nform a primary output audio channel from the sum of the primary input audio channel and the scaled non-primary input audio channels;\ndetermine a set of L prediction gains:\nfor each of the L prediction gains, form a prediction channel from the primary output audio channel scaled according to the prediction gain;\nform L non-primary output audio channels from the difference of the respective non-primary input audio channel and the respective prediction signal;\nform an output multi-channel audio signal from the primary output audio channel and the L non-primary output audio channels;\nencode with an audio encoder, the output multi-channel audio signal; and\ntransmit or store the encoded output multi-channel audio signal.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n1 is a block diagram of an arrangement 10 of a simple audio encoder and simple audio decoder, intended to form a multi-channel audio signal 17 (7S) that is a facsimile of multi-channel audio signal 13 (Z). Multi-channel audio signal 13 is processed by simple audio encoder 14 to produce encoded representation 15, which may be stored 20 and/or transmitted 21 to simple audio decoder 16 which produces multi-channel audio signal 17. Preferably, the data size of encoded representation 15 is minimized whilst minimizing the difference between multi-channel audio signal 13 and multi-channel audio signal 17. Furthermore, the difference between multi-channel audio signal 13 and multi-channel audio signal 17 may be measured according to similarity as perceived by a human listener. The measure of human-perceived similarity between audio signal 13 and audio signal 17 is based on a reference playback method (that is, the assumed default means by which the audio channels of multi-channel audio signals 13, 17 are presented as an auditory experience to the listener).\n[0032]\nThe efficiency of simple audio encoder 14 and decoder 16 may be defined in terms of the data rate (measured in bits per second) of the encoded representation 15 required to provide multi-channel audio signal 17 that will be judged by a listener to match multi-channel audio signal 13 with a particular perceived quality level. Simple audio encoder 14 and decoder 16 may achieve greater efficiency (that is, a lower data rate) when the multi-channel audio signal 13 is known to possess particular attributes. In particular, greater efficiency may be achieved when it is known that multi-channel audio signal 13 possesses the following attributes (DD1 and DD2):\nDD1: One or more channels of the multi-channel audio signal are generally more dominant than others, where a more dominant audio channel is one that will contain substantial elements of most (or all) of the sonic elements in the scene. That is, a dominant audio signal, when presented as a single audio channel to a listener, will contain most (or all) of the sonic elements of the multi-channel signal, when the multi-channel audio signal is presented to a listener through a reference playback method.\nDD2: Each of the audio channels of the multi-channel audio signal is largely uncorrelated to each of the other audio channels\n[0035]\nGiven the knowledge that multi-channel audio signal 13 possesses attributes DD1 and DD2, simple audio encoder 14 may achieve improved efficiency using several techniques including, but not limited to: allocating fewer bits to the encoding of less dominant channels or choosing to discard less dominant channels entirely. Simple audio encoder 14 may provide data to simple audio decoder 16 to assist in the regeneration of channels that were discarded by simple encoder audio encoder 14. Preferably, a multi-channel audio signal that does not possess attributes DD1 and DD2 may be processed by an encoder pre-mixer to form, e.g., to calculate, to determine, to construct or to generate, a multi-channel audio signal that does possess attributes DD1 and DD2, as described further in reference to FIG. A corresponding decoder post-mixer may be applied to the simple decoder output to form an output multi-channel audio signal, such that the decoder post-mixer performs an approximate inverse operation relative to the operation of the encoder pre-mixer.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US7391870B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. Apparatus for generating a multi-channel output signal having K output channels, the multi-channel output signal corresponding to a multi-channel input signal having C input channels, using E transmission channels, the E transmission channels representing a result of a downmix operation having C input channels as an input, and using parametric information related to the input channels, wherein E is \u22672, C is >E, and K is >1 and \u2266C, and wherein the downmix operation is effective to introduce a first input channel in a first transmission channel and in a second transmission channel, and to additionally introduce a second input channel in the first transmission channel, comprising:\na cancellation channel calculator for calculating a cancellation channel using information related to the first input channel included in the first transmission channel, the second transmission channel or the parametric information;\na combiner for combining the cancellation channel and the first transmission channel or a processed version thereof to obtain a second base channel, in which an influence of the first input channel is reduced compared to the influence of the first input channel on the first transmission channel; and\na channel reconstructor for reconstructing a second output channel corresponding to the second input channel using the second base channel and parametric information related to the second input channel, and for reconstructing a first output channel corresponding to the first input channel using a first base channel being different from the second base channel in that the influence of the first channel is higher compared to the second base channel, and parametric information related to the first input channel.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nInstead, the invention is a simple concept that does not have these disadvantages and aims at reducing the influence of the center channel signal component in the side channels.\nAs has been discussed in connection with FIGS. 7 a-7 d, the base channels for the five reconstructed output channels of 5-to-2 BCC are:\n{tilde over (s)} 1(k)={tilde over (y)} 1(k)={tilde over (x)} 1(k)+{tilde over (x)} 3(k)/+\u221a{square root over (2)}+{tilde over (x)} 4(k)\n{tilde over (s)} 2(k)={tilde over (y)} 2(k)={tilde over (x)} 2(k)+{tilde over (x)} 3(k)/+\u221a{square root over (2)}+{tilde over (x)} 5(k)\n{tilde over (s)} 3(k)={tilde over (y)} 1(k)+{tilde over (y)} 2(k)={tilde over (x)}1(k) +{tilde over (x)} 2(k)+\u221a{square root over (2)}{tilde over (x)} 3(k)+{tilde over (x)}4(k)+{tilde over (x)}\n{tilde over (s)} 4(k)={tilde over (s)} 1(k)\n{tilde over (x)} 5(k)={tilde over (s)} 2(k)\nIt is to be noted that the original center channel signal component x3 appears 3 dB amplified in the center base channel subband s3 (factor 1/\u221a2) and 3 dB attenuated in the remaining (side channel) base channel subbands.\nIn order to further attenuate the influence of the center channel signal component in the side base channel subbands according to this invention, the following general idea is applied as illustrated in FIG. 2.\nAn estimate of the final decoded center channel signal is computed by preferably scaling it to the desired target level as described by the corresponding level information such as an ICLD value in BCC environments. Preferably, this decoded center signal is calculated in the spectral domain in order to save computation, i.e. no synthesis filterbank processing is applied.\nAdditionally, this center decoded signal or center reconstructed signal, which corresponds to the cancellation channel, can be weighted and then combined to both the base channel signals of the other output channels. This combining is preferably a subtraction. Nevertheless, when the weighting factors have a different sign, then an addition also results in the reduction of the influence of the center channel in the base channel used for reconstructing the left or the right output channel. This processing results in forming a modified base channel for reconstruction of left and left surround or for reconstruction of right or right surround. Preferably a weighting factor of \u22123 dB is preferred, but also any other value is possible.\nInstead of the original transmission base channel signals as used in FIG. 7 b, modified base channel signals are used for the computation of the decoded output channel of the other output channels, i.e. the channels other than the center channel.\nIn the following, a block diagram of the inventive concept will be discussed by reference to FIG. 2 shows an apparatus for generating a multi-channel output signal having K output channels, the multi-channel output signal corresponding to a multi-channel input signal having C input channels, using E transmission channels, the E transmission channels representing a result of a downmix operation having the C input channels as an input, and using parametric side information on the input channels, wherein C is \u22672, C is >E, and K is >1 and \u2266C."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receive an input multi-channel audio signal comprising a primary input audio channel and L non-primary input audio channels\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"multi-channel input signal having C input channels\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s independent claim explicitly recites a \\\"multi-channel input signal having C input channels\\\", which discloses receiving a multi\u2011channel audio signal. Since this feature is directly disclosed, it lacks novelty (Y). Given that merely receiving a multi\u2011channel signal is routine, the approach would be obvious to one skilled in the art, so the inventive\u2011step assessment is also Y, leading to a verdict of Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"determine a set of L input gains, where L is a positive integer greater than one\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not contain any language that discloses determining a set of input gains; there is no verbatim phrase about \\\"input gains\\\". Therefore the element is novel (N). Because the concept of calculating gain values for channels is not taught, it is not obvious, so the inventive\u2011step assessment is also N, resulting in a verdict of N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"for each of the L non-primary input audio channels and L input gains, form a respective scaled non-primary input audio channel from the respective non-primary input audio channel scaled according to the input gain\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose scaling individual non\u2011primary channels by per\u2011channel input gains; no verbatim passage matches this teaching. Hence the element is novel (N) and, lacking any teaching, is non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"form a primary output audio channel from the sum of the primary input audio channel and the scaled non-primary input audio channels\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent describes cancellation and combination operations but does not disclose forming a primary channel by summing the primary input with scaled non\u2011primary channels. No verbatim text matches, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"determine a set of L prediction gains: for each of the L prediction gains, form a prediction channel from the primary output audio channel scaled according to the prediction gain\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent contains no reference to \\\"prediction gains\\\" or to forming a prediction channel by scaling the primary output. Absence of such disclosure means the element is novel (N) and not obvious (N). Verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"form L non-primary output audio channels from the difference of the respective non-primary input audio channel and the respective prediction signal\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"While the specification mentions subtraction (\\\"this combining is preferably a subtraction\\\"), it does not expressly disclose forming non\u2011primary output channels as the difference between a non\u2011primary input channel and a prediction signal. No verbatim match exists, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"form an output multi-channel audio signal from the primary output audio channel and the L non-primary output audio channels\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"multi-channel output signal having K output channels\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The independent claim of the target patent recites a \\\"multi-channel output signal having K output channels\\\", which directly covers forming an output multi\u2011channel signal from constituent channels. This disclosure removes novelty (Y). As generating a multi\u2011channel output from processed channels is a routine function of the described apparatus, the element is also obvious (Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"encode with an audio encoder, the output multi-channel audio signal\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art focuses on channel reconstruction and does not mention encoding the resulting multi\u2011channel signal. No verbatim language about \\\"encode\\\" is present, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"transmit or store the encoded output multi-channel audio signal\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose transmitting or storing an encoded multi\u2011channel audio signal. Absence of such teaching makes the element novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014determining per\u2011channel input gains, applying per\u2011channel scaling, generating prediction channels with prediction gains, and deriving non\u2011primary outputs by subtracting prediction signals\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent. Only the generic notion of handling a multi\u2011channel input and producing a multi\u2011channel output is taught, which is routine. Because the essential inventive features are novel and non\u2011obvious, the source patent retains substantive validity despite overlap on basic multi\u2011channel handling.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3225, "tokens_output": 2671, "latency_ms": 6041}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receive an input multi-channel audio signal comprising a primary input audio channel and L non-primary input audio channels", "corresponding_text": "multi-channel input signal having C input channels", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s independent claim explicitly recites a \"multi-channel input signal having C input channels\", which discloses receiving a multi\u2011channel audio signal. Since this feature is directly disclosed, it lacks novelty (Y). Given that merely receiving a multi\u2011channel signal is routine, the approach would be obvious to one skilled in the art, so the inventive\u2011step assessment is also Y, leading to a verdict of Y."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "determine a set of L input gains, where L is a positive integer greater than one", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not contain any language that discloses determining a set of input gains; there is no verbatim phrase about \"input gains\". Therefore the element is novel (N). Because the concept of calculating gain values for channels is not taught, it is not obvious, so the inventive\u2011step assessment is also N, resulting in a verdict of N."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "for each of the L non-primary input audio channels and L input gains, form a respective scaled non-primary input audio channel from the respective non-primary input audio channel scaled according to the input gain", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose scaling individual non\u2011primary channels by per\u2011channel input gains; no verbatim passage matches this teaching. Hence the element is novel (N) and, lacking any teaching, is non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "form a primary output audio channel from the sum of the primary input audio channel and the scaled non-primary input audio channels", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent describes cancellation and combination operations but does not disclose forming a primary channel by summing the primary input with scaled non\u2011primary channels. No verbatim text matches, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "determine a set of L prediction gains: for each of the L prediction gains, form a prediction channel from the primary output audio channel scaled according to the prediction gain", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent contains no reference to \"prediction gains\" or to forming a prediction channel by scaling the primary output. Absence of such disclosure means the element is novel (N) and not obvious (N). Verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "form L non-primary output audio channels from the difference of the respective non-primary input audio channel and the respective prediction signal", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "While the specification mentions subtraction (\"this combining is preferably a subtraction\"), it does not expressly disclose forming non\u2011primary output channels as the difference between a non\u2011primary input channel and a prediction signal. No verbatim match exists, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "form an output multi-channel audio signal from the primary output audio channel and the L non-primary output audio channels", "corresponding_text": "multi-channel output signal having K output channels", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The independent claim of the target patent recites a \"multi-channel output signal having K output channels\", which directly covers forming an output multi\u2011channel signal from constituent channels. This disclosure removes novelty (Y). As generating a multi\u2011channel output from processed channels is a routine function of the described apparatus, the element is also obvious (Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "encode with an audio encoder, the output multi-channel audio signal", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art focuses on channel reconstruction and does not mention encoding the resulting multi\u2011channel signal. No verbatim language about \"encode\" is present, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 9, "element_text": "transmit or store the encoded output multi-channel audio signal", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose transmitting or storing an encoded multi\u2011channel audio signal. Absence of such teaching makes the element novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014determining per\u2011channel input gains, applying per\u2011channel scaling, generating prediction channels with prediction gains, and deriving non\u2011primary outputs by subtracting prediction signals\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent. Only the generic notion of handling a multi\u2011channel input and producing a multi\u2011channel output is taught, which is routine. Because the essential inventive features are novel and non\u2011obvious, the source patent retains substantive validity despite overlap on basic multi\u2011channel handling."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "b247f372-1a0c-4218-b619-b9e0f6b84bd4", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:53:50.585888+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20250024222A1", "independent_claim": ". A method, comprising:\nreceiving audio reproduction data comprising one or more audio objects and metadata associated with each of the one or more audio objects;\nreceiving reproduction environment data comprising an indication of a number of reproduction speakers in the reproduction environment and an indication of the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment; and\nrendering the audio objects into one or more speaker feed signals by applying an amplitude panning process to each audio object, wherein the amplitude panning process is based, at least in part, on the metadata associated with each audio object, a location of each of one or more virtual speakers, and the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment, and wherein each speaker feed signal corresponds to at least one of the reproduction speakers within the reproduction environment;\nwherein the metadata associated with each audio object includes audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position of the audio object within the reproduction environment and zone constraint metadata indicating whether rendering the audio object involves imposing speaker zone constraints.", "specification": "IG. 1 shows an example of a reproduction environment having a Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration. Dolby Surround 5.1 was developed in the 1990s, but this configuration is still widely deployed in cinema sound system environments. A projector 105 may be configured to project video images, e.g. for a movie, on the screen 150. Audio reproduction data may be synchronized with the video images and processed by the sound processor 110. The power amplifiers 115 may provide speaker feed signals to speakers of the reproduction environment 100.\n[0066]\nThe Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration includes left surround array 120, right surround array 125, each of which is gang-driven by a single channel. The Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration also includes separate channels for the left screen channel 130, the center screen channel 135 and the right screen channel 140. A separate channel for the subwoofer 145 is provided for low-frequency effects (LFE).\n[0067]\nIn 2010, Dolby provided enhancements to digital cinema sound by introducing Dolby Surround 7.1. FIG. 2 shows an example of a reproduction environment having a Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration. A digital projector 205 may be configured to receive digital video data and to project video images on the screen 150. Audio reproduction data may be processed by the sound processor 210. The power amplifiers 215 may provide speaker feed signals to speakers of the reproduction environment 200.\n[0068]\nThe Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration includes the left side surround array 220 and the right side surround array 225, each of which may be driven by a single channel. Like Dolby Surround 5.1, the Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration includes separate channels for the left screen channel 230, the center screen channel 235, the right screen channel 240 and the subwoofer 245. However, Dolby Surround 7.1 increases the number of surround channels by splitting the left and right surround channels of Dolby Surround 5.1 into four zones: in addition to the left side surround array 220 and the right side surround array 225, separate channels are included for the left rear surround speakers 224 and the right rear surround speakers 226. Increasing the number of surround zones within the reproduction environment 200 can significantly improve the localization of sound.\n[0069]\nIn an effort to create a more immersive environment, some reproduction environments may be configured with increased numbers of speakers, driven by increased numbers of channels. Moreover, some reproduction environments may include speakers deployed at various elevations, some of which may be above a seating area of the reproduction environment."}, "target_patent": {"label": "WO2011054876A1", "independent_claim": "Apparatus (500) for calculating driving coefficients (512) for loudspeakers of a loudspeaker arrangement for an audio signal associated with a virtual source, the apparatus comprising: a multi-channel Tenderer (510) configured to calculate driving coefficients (512) for loudspeakers (550) of the loudspeaker arrangement based on a first calculation rule, if a position (502) of the virtual source is located outside a loudspeaker transition zone (530), and configured to calculate driving coefficients (512) for loudspeakers (550) of the loudspeaker arrangement based on a second calculation rule, if a position (502) of the virtual source is located within the loudspeaker transition zone (530), wherein a border of the loudspeaker transition zone (530) comprises a minimal distance to a loudspeaker (550) of the loudspeaker arrangement depending on a distance between the loudspeaker (550) and a loudspeaker (550) adjacent to this loudspeaker (550), wherein the loudspeaker arrangement comprises at least two pairs of adjacent loudspeakers (550) with different distances between the loudspeakers (550) of the respective pair of loudspeakers (550).", "specification": "he following embodiments describe concepts for calculating drive coefficients for loudspeakers or for generating drive signals for loudspeakers based on driving coefficients. These driving coefficients may also be called filter coefficients. A driving coefficient or a filter coefficient of the loudspeaker may be a scaling parameter or a delay parameter of an audio signal or an audio object to be reproduced by the loudspeaker arrangement. For example, for a virtual source, a scaling parameter is calculated as a driving filter coefficient and a delay parameter is calculated as a second driving coefficient for a loudspeaker of the loudspeaker arrangement. The scaling parameter may also be called amplitude parameter.\nAn audio object may represent an audio source as for example a car, a train, a raindrop or a speaking person, wherein the virtual source position of an audio object may be for example an absolute position or a relative position in relation to the loudspeaker arrangement (e.g. a coordinate origin may be predefined). An audio object may be assumed to be a point source emitting spherical waves located at the virtual source position. For audio objects located far away from the loudspeaker arrangement, the spherical wave may be approximated by a plane wave.\nIn the following embodiments a multi-channel renderer is used for calculating driving coefficients or for generating or providing drive signals for loudspeakers. For this, a known multi-channel renderer may be adapted according to the aspects of the invention described below. The multi-channel renderer may be, for example, a wave field synthesis renderer or a surround sound renderer. Some of the following examples are explained in terms of a wave field synthesis renderer, but using other multi-channel Tenderers for other applications may also be possible.\nAs an example for a multi-channel renderer a wave field synthesis renderer (also called wave field synthesis module) is shown in Fig. 2. A wave field synthesis module comprising several inputs 202, 204, 206 and 208 as well as several outputs 210, 212, 214 and 216 is the center of a wave field synthesis environment. Different audio signals for virtual sources are supplied to the wave field synthesis module via inputs 202 to 204. Thus, input 202 receives, for example, an audio signal of the virtual source 1 as well as associated position information of the virtual source. In a cinema setting, for example, the audio signal 1 would be, for example, the speech of an actor moving from a left side of the screen to a right side of the screen and possibly additionally away from the audience or towards the audience. Then, the audio signal 1 would be the actual speech of the actor, while the position information as function of time represents the current position of the first actor in the scene at a certain time. In contrary, the audio signal n would be the speech, for example of a further actor which moves in the same way or in a different way than the first actor. The current position of the other actor to which the audio signal n is associated, is provided to the wave field synthesis module by position information synchronized with the audio signal n. In practice, different virtual sources exist, depending on the scene describing their attributes, wherein the audio signal of every virtual source is supplied as individual audio track to the wave field synthesis module 120."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20250024222A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. A method, comprising:\nreceiving audio reproduction data comprising one or more audio objects and metadata associated with each of the one or more audio objects;\nreceiving reproduction environment data comprising an indication of a number of reproduction speakers in the reproduction environment and an indication of the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment; and\nrendering the audio objects into one or more speaker feed signals by applying an amplitude panning process to each audio object, wherein the amplitude panning process is based, at least in part, on the metadata associated with each audio object, a location of each of one or more virtual speakers, and the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment, and wherein each speaker feed signal corresponds to at least one of the reproduction speakers within the reproduction environment;\nwherein the metadata associated with each audio object includes audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position of the audio object within the reproduction environment and zone constraint metadata indicating whether rendering the audio object involves imposing speaker zone constraints.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nIG. 1 shows an example of a reproduction environment having a Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration. Dolby Surround 5.1 was developed in the 1990s, but this configuration is still widely deployed in cinema sound system environments. A projector 105 may be configured to project video images, e.g. for a movie, on the screen 150. Audio reproduction data may be synchronized with the video images and processed by the sound processor 110. The power amplifiers 115 may provide speaker feed signals to speakers of the reproduction environment 100.\n[0066]\nThe Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration includes left surround array 120, right surround array 125, each of which is gang-driven by a single channel. The Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration also includes separate channels for the left screen channel 130, the center screen channel 135 and the right screen channel 140. A separate channel for the subwoofer 145 is provided for low-frequency effects (LFE).\n[0067]\nIn 2010, Dolby provided enhancements to digital cinema sound by introducing Dolby Surround 7.1. FIG. 2 shows an example of a reproduction environment having a Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration. A digital projector 205 may be configured to receive digital video data and to project video images on the screen 150. Audio reproduction data may be processed by the sound processor 210. The power amplifiers 215 may provide speaker feed signals to speakers of the reproduction environment 200.\n[0068]\nThe Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration includes the left side surround array 220 and the right side surround array 225, each of which may be driven by a single channel. Like Dolby Surround 5.1, the Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration includes separate channels for the left screen channel 230, the center screen channel 235, the right screen channel 240 and the subwoofer 245. However, Dolby Surround 7.1 increases the number of surround channels by splitting the left and right surround channels of Dolby Surround 5.1 into four zones: in addition to the left side surround array 220 and the right side surround array 225, separate channels are included for the left rear surround speakers 224 and the right rear surround speakers 226. Increasing the number of surround zones within the reproduction environment 200 can significantly improve the localization of sound.\n[0069]\nIn an effort to create a more immersive environment, some reproduction environments may be configured with increased numbers of speakers, driven by increased numbers of channels. Moreover, some reproduction environments may include speakers deployed at various elevations, some of which may be above a seating area of the reproduction environment.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** WO2011054876A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nApparatus (500) for calculating driving coefficients (512) for loudspeakers of a loudspeaker arrangement for an audio signal associated with a virtual source, the apparatus comprising: a multi-channel Tenderer (510) configured to calculate driving coefficients (512) for loudspeakers (550) of the loudspeaker arrangement based on a first calculation rule, if a position (502) of the virtual source is located outside a loudspeaker transition zone (530), and configured to calculate driving coefficients (512) for loudspeakers (550) of the loudspeaker arrangement based on a second calculation rule, if a position (502) of the virtual source is located within the loudspeaker transition zone (530), wherein a border of the loudspeaker transition zone (530) comprises a minimal distance to a loudspeaker (550) of the loudspeaker arrangement depending on a distance between the loudspeaker (550) and a loudspeaker (550) adjacent to this loudspeaker (550), wherein the loudspeaker arrangement comprises at least two pairs of adjacent loudspeakers (550) with different distances between the loudspeakers (550) of the respective pair of loudspeakers (550).\n\n**Specification Support:**\nhe following embodiments describe concepts for calculating drive coefficients for loudspeakers or for generating drive signals for loudspeakers based on driving coefficients. These driving coefficients may also be called filter coefficients. A driving coefficient or a filter coefficient of the loudspeaker may be a scaling parameter or a delay parameter of an audio signal or an audio object to be reproduced by the loudspeaker arrangement. For example, for a virtual source, a scaling parameter is calculated as a driving filter coefficient and a delay parameter is calculated as a second driving coefficient for a loudspeaker of the loudspeaker arrangement. The scaling parameter may also be called amplitude parameter.\nAn audio object may represent an audio source as for example a car, a train, a raindrop or a speaking person, wherein the virtual source position of an audio object may be for example an absolute position or a relative position in relation to the loudspeaker arrangement (e.g. An audio object may be assumed to be a point source emitting spherical waves located at the virtual source position. For audio objects located far away from the loudspeaker arrangement, the spherical wave may be approximated by a plane wave.\nIn the following embodiments a multi-channel renderer is used for calculating driving coefficients or for generating or providing drive signals for loudspeakers. For this, a known multi-channel renderer may be adapted according to the aspects of the invention described below. The multi-channel renderer may be, for example, a wave field synthesis renderer or a surround sound renderer. Some of the following examples are explained in terms of a wave field synthesis renderer, but using other multi-channel Tenderers for other applications may also be possible.\nAs an example for a multi-channel renderer a wave field synthesis renderer (also called wave field synthesis module) is shown in Fig. Different audio signals for virtual sources are supplied to the wave field synthesis module via inputs 202 to 204. Thus, input 202 receives, for example, an audio signal of the virtual source 1 as well as associated position information of the virtual source. In a cinema setting, for example, the audio signal 1 would be, for example, the speech of an actor moving from a left side of the screen to a right side of the screen and possibly additionally away from the audience or towards the audience. Then, the audio signal 1 would be the actual speech of the actor, while the position information as function of time represents the current position of the first actor in the scene at a certain time. In contrary, the audio signal n would be the speech, for example of a further actor which moves in the same way or in a different way than the first actor. The current position of the other actor to which the audio signal n is associated, is provided to the wave field synthesis module by position information synchronized with the audio signal n. In practice, different virtual sources exist, depending on the scene describing their attributes, wherein the audio signal of every virtual source is supplied as individual audio track to the wave field synthesis module 120."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving audio reproduction data comprising one or more audio objects and metadata associated with each of the one or more audio objects\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"different audio signals for virtual sources are supplied to the wave field synthesis module via inputs 202 to 204. Thus, input 202 receives, for example, an audio signal of the virtual source 1 as well as associated position information of the virtual source.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly discloses that audio signals (representing audio objects) and associated position information (metadata) are supplied to a renderer. Therefore the element is not novel (Y) and, given the routine nature of receiving such data, it is also obvious (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving reproduction environment data comprising an indication of a number of reproduction speakers in the reproduction environment and an indication of the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose any step of receiving explicit reproduction\u2011environment data that specifies the number and locations of speakers. Consequently the element is novel (N) and not rendered obvious by the prior art (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"rendering the audio objects into one or more speaker feed signals by applying an amplitude panning process to each audio object, wherein the amplitude panning process is based, at least in part, on the metadata associated with each audio object, a location of each of one or more virtual speakers, and the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art describes calculating driving coefficients (scaling/amplitude parameters) based on virtual\u2011source position and transition\u2011zone rules, but it does not disclose an amplitude\u2011panning process that combines object metadata, virtual\u2011speaker locations, and actual speaker locations as claimed. Hence the element is novel (N) and not obvious (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"each speaker feed signal corresponds to at least one of the reproduction speakers within the reproduction environment\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"driving coefficient or a filter coefficient of the loudspeaker may be a scaling parameter or a delay parameter of an audio signal or an audio object to be reproduced by the loudspeaker arrangement.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches that a scaling (amplitude) or delay parameter is calculated for each loudspeaker so that the audio object is reproduced by that loudspeaker, which directly maps to the claim element that each feed signal corresponds to a reproduction speaker. This is disclosed (Y) and is a routine mapping in loudspeaker rendering (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"metadata associated with each audio object includes audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position of the audio object within the reproduction environment and zone constraint metadata indicating whether rendering the audio object involves imposing speaker zone constraints\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"if a position (502) of the virtual source is located within the loudspeaker transition zone (530) ... the virtual source position of an audio object may be for example an absolute position or a relative position in relation to the loudspeaker arrangement\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art defines a \\\"loudspeaker transition zone\\\" and conditions based on the virtual\u2011source position being inside that zone, which serves as a zone\u2011constraint concept. It also provides that the virtual source position (metadata) can be absolute or relative. Thus the element is disclosed (Y) and, given the straightforward use of zone constraints in existing rendering methods, it is obvious (Y).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core inventive contribution of the source patent lies in the combination of (i) receiving explicit reproduction\u2011environment data, (ii) applying an amplitude\u2011panning process that jointly uses object metadata, virtual\u2011speaker locations, and actual speaker locations, and (iii) rendering based on zone\u2011constraint metadata. Elements 2 and 3, which embody these advances, are not disclosed in the prior art and are therefore novel and non\u2011obvious. While other elements (1, 4, 5) are found in the target patent and are obvious, the presence of the undisclosed, technically significant elements means the source patent retains a substantive inventive step. Consequently, the source patent is likely to be considered valid over the cited prior art.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2799, "tokens_output": 2872, "latency_ms": 6530}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving audio reproduction data comprising one or more audio objects and metadata associated with each of the one or more audio objects", "corresponding_text": "different audio signals for virtual sources are supplied to the wave field synthesis module via inputs 202 to 204. Thus, input 202 receives, for example, an audio signal of the virtual source 1 as well as associated position information of the virtual source.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly discloses that audio signals (representing audio objects) and associated position information (metadata) are supplied to a renderer. Therefore the element is not novel (Y) and, given the routine nature of receiving such data, it is also obvious (Y)."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receiving reproduction environment data comprising an indication of a number of reproduction speakers in the reproduction environment and an indication of the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose any step of receiving explicit reproduction\u2011environment data that specifies the number and locations of speakers. Consequently the element is novel (N) and not rendered obvious by the prior art (N)."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "rendering the audio objects into one or more speaker feed signals by applying an amplitude panning process to each audio object, wherein the amplitude panning process is based, at least in part, on the metadata associated with each audio object, a location of each of one or more virtual speakers, and the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art describes calculating driving coefficients (scaling/amplitude parameters) based on virtual\u2011source position and transition\u2011zone rules, but it does not disclose an amplitude\u2011panning process that combines object metadata, virtual\u2011speaker locations, and actual speaker locations as claimed. Hence the element is novel (N) and not obvious (N)."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "each speaker feed signal corresponds to at least one of the reproduction speakers within the reproduction environment", "corresponding_text": "driving coefficient or a filter coefficient of the loudspeaker may be a scaling parameter or a delay parameter of an audio signal or an audio object to be reproduced by the loudspeaker arrangement.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent teaches that a scaling (amplitude) or delay parameter is calculated for each loudspeaker so that the audio object is reproduced by that loudspeaker, which directly maps to the claim element that each feed signal corresponds to a reproduction speaker. This is disclosed (Y) and is a routine mapping in loudspeaker rendering (Y)."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "metadata associated with each audio object includes audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position of the audio object within the reproduction environment and zone constraint metadata indicating whether rendering the audio object involves imposing speaker zone constraints", "corresponding_text": "if a position (502) of the virtual source is located within the loudspeaker transition zone (530) ... the virtual source position of an audio object may be for example an absolute position or a relative position in relation to the loudspeaker arrangement", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The prior art defines a \"loudspeaker transition zone\" and conditions based on the virtual\u2011source position being inside that zone, which serves as a zone\u2011constraint concept. It also provides that the virtual source position (metadata) can be absolute or relative. Thus the element is disclosed (Y) and, given the straightforward use of zone constraints in existing rendering methods, it is obvious (Y)."}], "overall_opinion": "The core inventive contribution of the source patent lies in the combination of (i) receiving explicit reproduction\u2011environment data, (ii) applying an amplitude\u2011panning process that jointly uses object metadata, virtual\u2011speaker locations, and actual speaker locations, and (iii) rendering based on zone\u2011constraint metadata. Elements 2 and 3, which embody these advances, are not disclosed in the prior art and are therefore novel and non\u2011obvious. While other elements (1, 4, 5) are found in the target patent and are obvious, the presence of the undisclosed, technically significant elements means the source patent retains a substantive inventive step. Consequently, the source patent is likely to be considered valid over the cited prior art."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "ada673e2-f15c-4fc5-8803-8c9bbb63a875", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:54:49.697032+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20250024222A1", "independent_claim": ". A method, comprising:\nreceiving audio reproduction data comprising one or more audio objects and metadata associated with each of the one or more audio objects;\nreceiving reproduction environment data comprising an indication of a number of reproduction speakers in the reproduction environment and an indication of the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment; and\nrendering the audio objects into one or more speaker feed signals by applying an amplitude panning process to each audio object, wherein the amplitude panning process is based, at least in part, on the metadata associated with each audio object, a location of each of one or more virtual speakers, and the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment, and wherein each speaker feed signal corresponds to at least one of the reproduction speakers within the reproduction environment;\nwherein the metadata associated with each audio object includes audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position of the audio object within the reproduction environment and zone constraint metadata indicating whether rendering the audio object involves imposing speaker zone constraints.", "specification": "IG. 1 shows an example of a reproduction environment having a Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration. Dolby Surround 5.1 was developed in the 1990s, but this configuration is still widely deployed in cinema sound system environments. A projector 105 may be configured to project video images, e.g. for a movie, on the screen 150. Audio reproduction data may be synchronized with the video images and processed by the sound processor 110. The power amplifiers 115 may provide speaker feed signals to speakers of the reproduction environment 100.\n[0066]\nThe Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration includes left surround array 120, right surround array 125, each of which is gang-driven by a single channel. The Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration also includes separate channels for the left screen channel 130, the center screen channel 135 and the right screen channel 140. A separate channel for the subwoofer 145 is provided for low-frequency effects (LFE).\n[0067]\nIn 2010, Dolby provided enhancements to digital cinema sound by introducing Dolby Surround 7.1. FIG. 2 shows an example of a reproduction environment having a Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration. A digital projector 205 may be configured to receive digital video data and to project video images on the screen 150. Audio reproduction data may be processed by the sound processor 210. The power amplifiers 215 may provide speaker feed signals to speakers of the reproduction environment 200.\n[0068]\nThe Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration includes the left side surround array 220 and the right side surround array 225, each of which may be driven by a single channel. Like Dolby Surround 5.1, the Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration includes separate channels for the left screen channel 230, the center screen channel 235, the right screen channel 240 and the subwoofer 245. However, Dolby Surround 7.1 increases the number of surround channels by splitting the left and right surround channels of Dolby Surround 5.1 into four zones: in addition to the left side surround array 220 and the right side surround array 225, separate channels are included for the left rear surround speakers 224 and the right rear surround speakers 226. Increasing the number of surround zones within the reproduction environment 200 can significantly improve the localization of sound.\n[0069]\nIn an effort to create a more immersive environment, some reproduction environments may be configured with increased numbers of speakers, driven by increased numbers of channels. Moreover, some reproduction environments may include speakers deployed at various elevations, some of which may be above a seating area of the reproduction environment."}, "target_patent": {"label": "KR101842411B1", "independent_claim": " A system for adapting transmission of an object-oriented audio stream, the system comprising:\na resource monitor configured to receive resource information from a remote computer system regarding available computing resources of the remote computer system;\nan object-oriented encoder comprising one or more processors configured to select a plurality of audio objects for transmission to the remote computer system, wherein the object-oriented encoder selects relatively more audio objects for transmission when the resource information indicates that the remote computer system has relatively more available computing resources and selects relatively fewer audio objects for transmission when the resource information indicates that the remote computer system has relatively fewer available computing resources;\nthe plurality of audio objects selected for transmission comprising:\nchannel objects, each channel object comprising a channel of audio, and\ndynamic objects, each dynamic object comprising metadata, the metadata for each of the dynamic objects comprising object attributes; and\na streaming module configured to provide the channel objects and the dynamic objects to the remote computer system, thereby facilitating backwards compatibility with the remote computer system if the remote computer system is a fixed channel system and enabling the remote computer system to achieve enhanced rendering if the remote computer system is capable of also rendering the dynamic objects.", "specification": "FIGS. 1A and 1B illustrate embodiments of object-oriented audio systems 100A, 100B. The object-oriented audio systems 100A, 100B can be implemented in computer hardware and/or software. Advantageously, in certain embodiments, the object-oriented audio systems 100A, 100B can enable content creators to create audio objects, stream such objects, and render the objects without being bound to the fixed channel model.\nReferring specifically to FIG. 1A, the object-oriented audio system 100A includes an audio object creation system 110A, a streaming module 122A implemented in a content server 120A, and a renderer 142A implemented in a user system 140. The audio object creation system 110A can provide functionality for users to create and modify audio objects. The streaming module 122A, shown installed on a content server 120A, can be used to stream audio objects to a user system 140 over a network 130. The network 130 can include a LAN, a WAN, the Internet, or combinations of the same. The renderer 142A on the user system 140 can render the audio objects for output to one or more loudspeakers.\nIn the depicted embodiment, the audio object creation system 110A includes an object creation module 114 and an object-oriented encoder 112A. The object creation module 114 can provide functionality for creating objects, for example, by associating audio data with attributes of the audio data. Any type of audio can be used to generate an audio object. Some examples of audio that can be generated into objects and streamed can include audio associated with movies, television, movie trailers, music, music videos, other online videos, video games, and the like.\nInitially, audio data can be recorded or otherwise obtained. The object creation module 114 can provide a user interface that enables a user to access, edit, or otherwise manipulate the audio data. The audio data can represent a sound source or a collection of sound sources. Some examples of sound sources include dialog, background music, and sounds generated by any item (such as a car, an airplane, or any prop). More generally, a sound source can be any audio clip.\nSound sources can have one or more attributes that the object creation module 114 can associate with the audio data to create an object. Examples of attributes include a location of the sound source, a velocity of a sound source, directivity of a sound source, and the like. Some attributes may be obtained directly from the audio data, such as a time attribute reflecting a time when the audio data was recorded. Other attributes can be supplied by a user to the object creation module 114, such as the type of sound source that generated the audio (e.g., a car versus an actor). Still other attributes can be automatically imported by the object creation module 114 from other devices. As an example, the location of a sound source can be retrieved from a Global Positioning System (GPS) device or the like and imported into the object creation module 114. Additional examples of attributes and techniques for identifying attributes are described in greater detail below. The object creation module 114 can store the audio objects in an object data repository 116, which can include a database or other data storage.\nThe object-oriented encoder 112A can encode one or more audio objects into an audio stream suitable for transmission over a network. In one embodiment, the object-oriented encoder 112A encodes the audio objects as uncompressed PCM (pulse code modulated) audio together with associated attribute metadata. In another embodiment, the object-oriented encoder 112A also applies compression to the objects when creating the stream.\nAdvantageously, in certain embodiments, the audio stream generated by the object-oriented encoder can include at least one object represented by a metadata header and an audio payload. The audio stream can be composed of frames, which can each include object metadata headers and audio payloads. Some objects may include metadata only and no audio payload. Other objects may include an audio payload but little or no metadata. Examples of such objects are described in detail below.\nThe audio object creation system 110A can supply the encoded audio objects to the content server 120A over a network (not shown). The content server 120A can host the encoded audio objects for later transmission. The content server 120A can include one or more machines, such as physical computing devices. The content server 120A can be accessible to user systems over the network 130. For instance, the content server 120A can be a web server, an edge node in a content delivery network (CDN), or the like."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20250024222A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. A method, comprising:\nreceiving audio reproduction data comprising one or more audio objects and metadata associated with each of the one or more audio objects;\nreceiving reproduction environment data comprising an indication of a number of reproduction speakers in the reproduction environment and an indication of the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment; and\nrendering the audio objects into one or more speaker feed signals by applying an amplitude panning process to each audio object, wherein the amplitude panning process is based, at least in part, on the metadata associated with each audio object, a location of each of one or more virtual speakers, and the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment, and wherein each speaker feed signal corresponds to at least one of the reproduction speakers within the reproduction environment;\nwherein the metadata associated with each audio object includes audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position of the audio object within the reproduction environment and zone constraint metadata indicating whether rendering the audio object involves imposing speaker zone constraints.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nIG. 1 shows an example of a reproduction environment having a Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration. Dolby Surround 5.1 was developed in the 1990s, but this configuration is still widely deployed in cinema sound system environments. A projector 105 may be configured to project video images, e.g. for a movie, on the screen 150. Audio reproduction data may be synchronized with the video images and processed by the sound processor 110. The power amplifiers 115 may provide speaker feed signals to speakers of the reproduction environment 100.\n[0066]\nThe Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration includes left surround array 120, right surround array 125, each of which is gang-driven by a single channel. The Dolby Surround 5.1 configuration also includes separate channels for the left screen channel 130, the center screen channel 135 and the right screen channel 140. A separate channel for the subwoofer 145 is provided for low-frequency effects (LFE).\n[0067]\nIn 2010, Dolby provided enhancements to digital cinema sound by introducing Dolby Surround 7.1. FIG. 2 shows an example of a reproduction environment having a Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration. A digital projector 205 may be configured to receive digital video data and to project video images on the screen 150. Audio reproduction data may be processed by the sound processor 210. The power amplifiers 215 may provide speaker feed signals to speakers of the reproduction environment 200.\n[0068]\nThe Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration includes the left side surround array 220 and the right side surround array 225, each of which may be driven by a single channel. Like Dolby Surround 5.1, the Dolby Surround 7.1 configuration includes separate channels for the left screen channel 230, the center screen channel 235, the right screen channel 240 and the subwoofer 245. However, Dolby Surround 7.1 increases the number of surround channels by splitting the left and right surround channels of Dolby Surround 5.1 into four zones: in addition to the left side surround array 220 and the right side surround array 225, separate channels are included for the left rear surround speakers 224 and the right rear surround speakers 226. Increasing the number of surround zones within the reproduction environment 200 can significantly improve the localization of sound.\n[0069]\nIn an effort to create a more immersive environment, some reproduction environments may be configured with increased numbers of speakers, driven by increased numbers of channels. Moreover, some reproduction environments may include speakers deployed at various elevations, some of which may be above a seating area of the reproduction environment.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** KR101842411B1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n A system for adapting transmission of an object-oriented audio stream, the system comprising:\na resource monitor configured to receive resource information from a remote computer system regarding available computing resources of the remote computer system;\nan object-oriented encoder comprising one or more processors configured to select a plurality of audio objects for transmission to the remote computer system, wherein the object-oriented encoder selects relatively more audio objects for transmission when the resource information indicates that the remote computer system has relatively more available computing resources and selects relatively fewer audio objects for transmission when the resource information indicates that the remote computer system has relatively fewer available computing resources;\nthe plurality of audio objects selected for transmission comprising:\nchannel objects, each channel object comprising a channel of audio, and\ndynamic objects, each dynamic object comprising metadata, the metadata for each of the dynamic objects comprising object attributes; and\na streaming module configured to provide the channel objects and the dynamic objects to the remote computer system, thereby facilitating backwards compatibility with the remote computer system if the remote computer system is a fixed channel system and enabling the remote computer system to achieve enhanced rendering if the remote computer system is capable of also rendering the dynamic objects.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n1A and 1B illustrate embodiments of object-oriented audio systems 100A, 100B. The object-oriented audio systems 100A, 100B can be implemented in computer hardware and/or software. Advantageously, in certain embodiments, the object-oriented audio systems 100A, 100B can enable content creators to create audio objects, stream such objects, and render the objects without being bound to the fixed channel model.\nReferring specifically to FIG. 1A, the object-oriented audio system 100A includes an audio object creation system 110A, a streaming module 122A implemented in a content server 120A, and a renderer 142A implemented in a user system 140. The audio object creation system 110A can provide functionality for users to create and modify audio objects. The streaming module 122A, shown installed on a content server 120A, can be used to stream audio objects to a user system 140 over a network 130. The renderer 142A on the user system 140 can render the audio objects for output to one or more loudspeakers.\nIn the depicted embodiment, the audio object creation system 110A includes an object creation module 114 and an object-oriented encoder 112A. The object creation module 114 can provide functionality for creating objects, for example, by associating audio data with attributes of the audio data. Any type of audio can be used to generate an audio object. Some examples of audio that can be generated into objects and streamed can include audio associated with movies, television, movie trailers, music, music videos, other online videos, video games, and the like.\nInitially, audio data can be recorded or otherwise obtained. The object creation module 114 can provide a user interface that enables a user to access, edit, or otherwise manipulate the audio data. The audio data can represent a sound source or a collection of sound sources. More generally, a sound source can be any audio clip.\nSound sources can have one or more attributes that the object creation module 114 can associate with the audio data to create an object. Examples of attributes include a location of the sound source, a velocity of a sound source, directivity of a sound source, and the like. Some attributes may be obtained directly from the audio data, such as a time attribute reflecting a time when the audio data was recorded. Other attributes can be supplied by a user to the object creation module 114, such as the type of sound source that generated the audio (e.g., a car versus an actor). Still other attributes can be automatically imported by the object creation module 114 from other devices. As an example, the location of a sound source can be retrieved from a Global Positioning System (GPS) device or the like and imported into the object creation module 114. Additional examples of attributes and techniques for identifying attributes are described in greater detail below. The object creation module 114 can store the audio objects in an object data repository 116, which can include a database or other data storage.\nThe object-oriented encoder 112A can encode one or more audio objects into an audio stream suitable for transmission over a network. In one embodiment, the object-oriented encoder 112A encodes the audio objects as uncompressed PCM (pulse code modulated) audio together with associated attribute metadata. In another embodiment, the object-oriented encoder 112A also applies compression to the objects when creating the stream.\nAdvantageously, in certain embodiments, the audio stream generated by the object-oriented encoder can include at least one object represented by a metadata header and an audio payload. The audio stream can be composed of frames, which can each include object metadata headers and audio payloads. Some objects may include metadata only and no audio payload. Other objects may include an audio payload but little or no metadata. Examples of such objects are described in detail below.\nThe audio object creation system 110A can supply the encoded audio objects to the content server 120A over a network (not shown). The content server 120A can host the encoded audio objects for later transmission. The content server 120A can include one or more machines, such as physical computing devices. The content server 120A can be accessible to user systems over the network 130."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving audio reproduction data comprising one or more audio objects and metadata associated with each of the one or more audio objects\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose any step of receiving audio reproduction data that includes audio objects together with metadata for each object. The closest disclosure relates to creating and storing audio objects, but there is no verbatim language matching this element. Therefore the element is novel (N) and, lacking any teaching, is non\u2011obvious (N), leading to a verdict of N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving reproduction environment data comprising an indication of a number of reproduction speakers in the reproduction environment and an indication of the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art describes audio objects, attributes, and streaming, but provides no disclosure of receiving data that describes the number or locations of reproduction speakers. No verbatim passage matches this element, so it is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N), resulting in a verdict of N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"rendering the audio objects into one or more speaker feed signals by applying an amplitude panning process to each audio object, wherein the amplitude panning process is based, at least in part, on the metadata associated with each audio object, a location of each of one or more virtual speakers, and the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment, and wherein each speaker feed signal corresponds to at least one of the reproduction speakers within the reproduction environment\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not teach rendering audio objects to speaker feeds, amplitude panning, virtual speakers, or using speaker location data for panning. Consequently the element is not disclosed, making it novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N); verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"wherein the metadata associated with each audio object includes audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position of the audio object within the reproduction environment and zone constraint metadata indicating whether rendering the audio object involves imposing speaker zone constraints\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"While the prior art mentions metadata attributes such as location of a sound source, it does not disclose \\\"audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position\\\" nor \\\"zone constraint metadata\\\". No verbatim language matches, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N), yielding a verdict of N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014metadata\u2011driven amplitude panning with virtual speaker positioning and zone\u2011constraint handling\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art KR101842411B1. All claim elements are novel and non\u2011obvious over the reference, indicating that the source patent likely retains validity.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3057, "tokens_output": 1779, "latency_ms": 23362}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving audio reproduction data comprising one or more audio objects and metadata associated with each of the one or more audio objects", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose any step of receiving audio reproduction data that includes audio objects together with metadata for each object. The closest disclosure relates to creating and storing audio objects, but there is no verbatim language matching this element. Therefore the element is novel (N) and, lacking any teaching, is non\u2011obvious (N), leading to a verdict of N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receiving reproduction environment data comprising an indication of a number of reproduction speakers in the reproduction environment and an indication of the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art describes audio objects, attributes, and streaming, but provides no disclosure of receiving data that describes the number or locations of reproduction speakers. No verbatim passage matches this element, so it is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N), resulting in a verdict of N."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "rendering the audio objects into one or more speaker feed signals by applying an amplitude panning process to each audio object, wherein the amplitude panning process is based, at least in part, on the metadata associated with each audio object, a location of each of one or more virtual speakers, and the location of each reproduction speaker within the reproduction environment, and wherein each speaker feed signal corresponds to at least one of the reproduction speakers within the reproduction environment", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not teach rendering audio objects to speaker feeds, amplitude panning, virtual speakers, or using speaker location data for panning. Consequently the element is not disclosed, making it novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N); verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "wherein the metadata associated with each audio object includes audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position of the audio object within the reproduction environment and zone constraint metadata indicating whether rendering the audio object involves imposing speaker zone constraints", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "While the prior art mentions metadata attributes such as location of a sound source, it does not disclose \"audio object coordinates indicating the intended reproduction position\" nor \"zone constraint metadata\". No verbatim language matches, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N), yielding a verdict of N."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014metadata\u2011driven amplitude panning with virtual speaker positioning and zone\u2011constraint handling\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art KR101842411B1. All claim elements are novel and non\u2011obvious over the reference, indicating that the source patent likely retains validity."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "a0df6f16-08b2-4e90-b7bd-0e402154bb48", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:55:32.578083+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20260064345A1", "independent_claim": "A method, comprising:\nwhile a user wearing a head-wearable device is performing a physical activity, obtaining sensor data sensed from the user via one or more sensors, the sensor data including at least biometric data and physical activity data,\nin response to an indication that the user is performing the physical activity, initiating an artificial intelligence (AI) agent,\ndetermining, based on the sensor data, whether one or more activity-based thresholds are satisfied,\nin accordance with a determination that an activity-based threshold is satisfied, generating, by the AI agent, a coaching message to assist the user in performance of the physical activity, and\ncausing presentation of the coaching message via the head-wearable device.", "specification": "he systems and methods disclosed herein provide different ways in which wearable devices can utilize artificial intelligence (AI) and/or an AI Agent (also referred to as a AI digital assistant or AI assistant). For example, in some embodiments, a head-wearable device can retrieve information and use the information with an AI agent to generate responses and/or recommendations that are displayed at the head-wearable device and/or another communicatively device. The systems and method disclosed here can be used collaborate with other users (including wearers of other wearable devices), and interact with third party applications using built-in AI models, in accordance with some embodiments. The systems and methods disclosed herein can utilize a user interactable AI agent to perform various tasks at the user's request, as well as utilize the AI agent to monitor situations and provide user-specific assistance.\n[0044]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize AI agent to work with wearable devices and other devices (e.g., laptop, tablet, watches, desktops, phones, and other internet connected devices) within an ecosystem to accomplish tasks across multiple devices (e.g., XR systems described below in reference to FIGS. 7A-7C-2 ). For example, an AI agent can be configured to control an aspect of one or more of the other devices based on a request from the user. In some embodiments, the AI agent can also be invoked on different devices based on a determination that the user is interacting with a device other than a wearable device.\n[0045]\nIn some embodiments, the systems and methods disclosed herein can use an AI agent to augment a user experience. In particular, the AI agent can receive sensor data and/or other information captured by a wearable device, and use the sensor data and/or other information to generate and provide recommended actions and/or context-based responses. For example, a head-wearable device worn by the user can capture information corresponding to a field of view of the user 105 and/or a location of the user to generate and provide recommended actions and/or context-based responses. The systems and methods disclosed herein generate and provide tailored information to a user based on location and/or data received from one or more wearable devices (e.g., sensor data and/or image data of a wrist-wearable device, a head-wearable device, etc.).\n[0046]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize an AI agent to collate recorded information (e.g., camera photos and videos) across multiple wearable devices to produce unique media (e.g., a single video which stitches the multiple head-wearable devices video feed into a single viewing experience). In some embodiments, positional data of each communicatively coupled device (e.g., wearable device, such as a head-wearable device) can be used to determine how the media is presented.\n[0047]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize an AI agent to work with third-party applications through the use of an API. In other words, the user can use an AI agent implemented at a wearable device to perform a task of applications by utilizing the API to communicate with the AI agent. In some embodiments, the AI agent can be configured to interact with applications and graphical user interfaces (GUIs) without the use of an API."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20260045084A1", "independent_claim": ". A head-wearable device, comprising:\none or more sensors; and\none or more processors configured to execute instructions for causing performance of:\nin response to initiation of an artificially intelligent assistant, capturing contextual data, the contextual data including one or more of image data and audio data;\ndetermining, based on the contextual data, a contextual cue;\nproviding a portion of the contextual data and the contextual cue to the artificially intelligent assistant;\ndetermining, by the artificially intelligent assistant, a user request based on the portion of the contextual data and the contextual cue;\nreceiving a response to the user request, wherein the response is generated using a machine-learning model; and\ncausing the head-wearable device to present the response.", "specification": "FIGS. 1A-1F illustrate a head-wearable device including an artificially intelligent assistant, in accordance with some embodiments. The AI assistant can be a conversational AI that is configured to understand, process, and respond to human language. The AI assistant can be customizable by the user to select languages, voices, personality, speech characteristics, etc. The AI assistant is included at a head-wearable device 110. The head-wearable device 110 can be part of any XR system described below in reference to FIGS. 16A-16C-2 . An example system can include the head-wearable device 110 (e.g., AR device 1628 or MR device 1632), a wrist-wearable device 120 (e.g., wrist-wearable device 1626), a handheld intermediary processing device (HIPD) 1642, a mobile device 1650, and/or any other device described below in reference to FIGS. 16A-16C-2 . A user 105 can wear and/or be carry one or more devices in an XR system. As shown and described below in reference to FIGS. 4 and 14 , one or more models of the AI assistant can be included on-device (e.g., on a head-wearable device 110, wrist-wearable device 120, mobile device 1650, HIPD 1642, and/or other portable devices) and/or off-device or remotely (e.g., on a server 1630, a computer 1640, the HIPD 1642, and/or other devices with additional computational resources and/or larger power supplies).\n[0052]\nOn-device modules (including AI or machine-learning models) are used for processing operations that are not computationally intensive and allow for fast processing, whereas off-device modules (including AI or machine-learning models) are used for processing computationally intensive operations and provide higher accuracy outputs. Because on-device modules have low power consumption, can perform tasks with low latency, and require a minimal amount of computational resources, one or more on-device modules are included on wearable devices to reduce overall processing times. In some embodiments, on-device modules disclosed herein have a size less than or equal to 20 MB and a peak memory usage of less than or equal to 200 MB. In some embodiments, on-device modules disclosed herein have a size 8 MB or less. In some embodiments, on-device modules disclosed herein have a size 5 MB or less.\n[0053]\nThe user 105 can receive one or more alerts (e.g., alerts 102 and 122), haptic feedback 126, and/or other notifications via a device of the XR system. The different devices of the XR system can present visual and/or audio representations to the user 105. For example, the head-wearable device 110, the wrist-wearable device 120, a mobile device (not shown) can include one or more speakers and/or displays for presenting visual and/or audio representations to the user 105. Additionally, the different devices of the XR system can capture audio data, image data, sensor data, and/or any other device data (generally referred to as \u201ccontextual data\u201d) to assist the user 105 in performing one or more operations. For example, the head-wearable device 110 can include an image sensor, a microphones, a GPS, bio-potential sensors, IMUs, eye-tracking sensors, thermometer, altimeters, and/or other sensors to capture data. Sensor data obtained by any sensors described herein can be used the XR system.\n[0054]\nThe user 105 can initiate the AI assistant via any one of the devices of the XR system. For example, the user 105 can initiate the AI assistant via one or more hand gestures, touch inputs (e.g., touch screen inputs, button inputs, touch inputs, etc. at a device), voice commands, and/or any other inputs detected by a device of the XR system. Alternatively, or in addition, in some embodiments, the user 105 can initiate the AI assistant via an application operating on the head-wearable device 110, the wrist-wearable device 120, and/or any other device of the XR system. For ease, one or more operations described below as described as benign performed by the AI assistant included in wearable device, such as head-wearable device 110.\n[0055]\nIn FIG. 1A, the user 105 wears the head-wearable device 110 and the wrist-wearable device 120. The user 105 is walking about and provides a request to an AI assistant\u2014\u201cHey Virtual Assistant, look for Shibuya Station.\u201d In some embodiments, the request initiates the AI assistant. More specifically, the request can include one or more query trigger cues (e.g., \u201cHey\u201d or \u201cHey Virtual Assistant\u201d) that, when detected by a device of the XR system, initiate the AI assistant. Alternatively, or in addition, in some embodiments, the user 105 can initiate the AI assistant using one or more devices of the XR system as described above. In some embodiments, the head-wearable device 110, the wrist-wearable device 120, and/or any other device of the XR system provide a notification that the AI system is active. For example, the head-wearable device 110, the wrist-wearable device 120 and/or any other device of the XR system can present via a display, a speaker, or other user-facing device a notification that the AI assistant is active."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20260064345A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method, comprising:\nwhile a user wearing a head-wearable device is performing a physical activity, obtaining sensor data sensed from the user via one or more sensors, the sensor data including at least biometric data and physical activity data,\nin response to an indication that the user is performing the physical activity, initiating an artificial intelligence (AI) agent,\ndetermining, based on the sensor data, whether one or more activity-based thresholds are satisfied,\nin accordance with a determination that an activity-based threshold is satisfied, generating, by the AI agent, a coaching message to assist the user in performance of the physical activity, and\ncausing presentation of the coaching message via the head-wearable device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nhe systems and methods disclosed herein provide different ways in which wearable devices can utilize artificial intelligence (AI) and/or an AI Agent (also referred to as a AI digital assistant or AI assistant). For example, in some embodiments, a head-wearable device can retrieve information and use the information with an AI agent to generate responses and/or recommendations that are displayed at the head-wearable device and/or another communicatively device. The systems and method disclosed here can be used collaborate with other users (including wearers of other wearable devices), and interact with third party applications using built-in AI models, in accordance with some embodiments. The systems and methods disclosed herein can utilize a user interactable AI agent to perform various tasks at the user's request, as well as utilize the AI agent to monitor situations and provide user-specific assistance.\n[0044]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize AI agent to work with wearable devices and other devices (e.g., laptop, tablet, watches, desktops, phones, and other internet connected devices) within an ecosystem to accomplish tasks across multiple devices (e.g., XR systems described below in reference to FIGS. 7A-7C-2 ). For example, an AI agent can be configured to control an aspect of one or more of the other devices based on a request from the user. In some embodiments, the AI agent can also be invoked on different devices based on a determination that the user is interacting with a device other than a wearable device.\n[0045]\nIn some embodiments, the systems and methods disclosed herein can use an AI agent to augment a user experience. In particular, the AI agent can receive sensor data and/or other information captured by a wearable device, and use the sensor data and/or other information to generate and provide recommended actions and/or context-based responses. For example, a head-wearable device worn by the user can capture information corresponding to a field of view of the user 105 and/or a location of the user to generate and provide recommended actions and/or context-based responses. The systems and methods disclosed herein generate and provide tailored information to a user based on location and/or data received from one or more wearable devices (e.g., sensor data and/or image data of a wrist-wearable device, a head-wearable device, etc.).\n[0046]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize an AI agent to collate recorded information (e.g., camera photos and videos) across multiple wearable devices to produce unique media (e.g., a single video which stitches the multiple head-wearable devices video feed into a single viewing experience). In some embodiments, positional data of each communicatively coupled device (e.g., wearable device, such as a head-wearable device) can be used to determine how the media is presented.\n[0047]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize an AI agent to work with third-party applications through the use of an API. In other words, the user can use an AI agent implemented at a wearable device to perform a task of applications by utilizing the API to communicate with the AI agent. In some embodiments, the AI agent can be configured to interact with applications and graphical user interfaces (GUIs) without the use of an API.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20260045084A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. A head-wearable device, comprising:\none or more sensors; and\none or more processors configured to execute instructions for causing performance of:\nin response to initiation of an artificially intelligent assistant, capturing contextual data, the contextual data including one or more of image data and audio data;\ndetermining, based on the contextual data, a contextual cue;\nproviding a portion of the contextual data and the contextual cue to the artificially intelligent assistant;\ndetermining, by the artificially intelligent assistant, a user request based on the portion of the contextual data and the contextual cue;\nreceiving a response to the user request, wherein the response is generated using a machine-learning model; and\ncausing the head-wearable device to present the response.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n1A-1F illustrate a head-wearable device including an artificially intelligent assistant, in accordance with some embodiments. The AI assistant can be a conversational AI that is configured to understand, process, and respond to human language. The AI assistant can be customizable by the user to select languages, voices, personality, speech characteristics, etc. The AI assistant is included at a head-wearable device 110. The head-wearable device 110 can be part of any XR system described below in reference to FIGS. An example system can include the head-wearable device 110 (e.g., AR device 1628 or MR device 1632), a wrist-wearable device 120 (e.g., wrist-wearable device 1626), a handheld intermediary processing device (HIPD) 1642, a mobile device 1650, and/or any other device described below in reference to FIGS. A user 105 can wear and/or be carry one or more devices in an XR system. 4 and 14 , one or more models of the AI assistant can be included on-device (e.g., on a head-wearable device 110, wrist-wearable device 120, mobile device 1650, HIPD 1642, and/or other portable devices) and/or off-device or remotely (e.g., on a server 1630, a computer 1640, the HIPD 1642, and/or other devices with additional computational resources and/or larger power supplies).\n[0052]\nOn-device modules (including AI or machine-learning models) are used for processing operations that are not computationally intensive and allow for fast processing, whereas off-device modules (including AI or machine-learning models) are used for processing computationally intensive operations and provide higher accuracy outputs. Because on-device modules have low power consumption, can perform tasks with low latency, and require a minimal amount of computational resources, one or more on-device modules are included on wearable devices to reduce overall processing times. In some embodiments, on-device modules disclosed herein have a size less than or equal to 20 MB and a peak memory usage of less than or equal to 200 MB. In some embodiments, on-device modules disclosed herein have a size 8 MB or less. In some embodiments, on-device modules disclosed herein have a size 5 MB or less.\n[0053]\nThe user 105 can receive one or more alerts (e.g., alerts 102 and 122), haptic feedback 126, and/or other notifications via a device of the XR system. The different devices of the XR system can present visual and/or audio representations to the user 105. For example, the head-wearable device 110, the wrist-wearable device 120, a mobile device (not shown) can include one or more speakers and/or displays for presenting visual and/or audio representations to the user 105. Additionally, the different devices of the XR system can capture audio data, image data, sensor data, and/or any other device data (generally referred to as \u201ccontextual data\u201d) to assist the user 105 in performing one or more operations. For example, the head-wearable device 110 can include an image sensor, a microphones, a GPS, bio-potential sensors, IMUs, eye-tracking sensors, thermometer, altimeters, and/or other sensors to capture data. Sensor data obtained by any sensors described herein can be used the XR system.\n[0054]\nThe user 105 can initiate the AI assistant via any one of the devices of the XR system. For example, the user 105 can initiate the AI assistant via one or more hand gestures, touch inputs (e.g., touch screen inputs, button inputs, touch inputs, etc. at a device), voice commands, and/or any other inputs detected by a device of the XR system. Alternatively, or in addition, in some embodiments, the user 105 can initiate the AI assistant via an application operating on the head-wearable device 110, the wrist-wearable device 120, and/or any other device of the XR system. For ease, one or more operations described below as described as benign performed by the AI assistant included in wearable device, such as head-wearable device 110.\n[0055]\nIn FIG. 1A, the user 105 wears the head-wearable device 110 and the wrist-wearable device 120. The user 105 is walking about and provides a request to an AI assistant\u2014\u201cHey Virtual Assistant, look for Shibuya Station.\u201d In some embodiments, the request initiates the AI assistant. More specifically, the request can include one or more query trigger cues (e.g., \u201cHey\u201d or \u201cHey Virtual Assistant\u201d) that, when detected by a device of the XR system, initiate the AI assistant. Alternatively, or in addition, in some embodiments, the user 105 can initiate the AI assistant using one or more devices of the XR system as described above. In some embodiments, the head-wearable device 110, the wrist-wearable device 120, and/or any other device of the XR system provide a notification that the AI system is active. For example, the head-wearable device 110, the wrist-wearable device 120 and/or any other device of the XR system can present via a display, a speaker, or other user-facing device a notification that the AI assistant is active."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"while a user wearing a head-wearable device is performing a physical activity, obtaining sensor data sensed from the user via one or more sensors, the sensor data including at least biometric data and physical activity data\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"The head-wearable device 110 can include an image sensor, a microphones, a GPS, bio-potential sensors, IMUs, eye-tracking sensors, thermometer, altimeters, and/or other sensors to capture data ... Sensor data obtained by any sensors described herein can be used the XR system\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly discloses a head\u2011wearable device equipped with multiple sensors (including bio\u2011potential sensors and IMUs) that capture data, and states that sensor data obtained by any sensors can be used by the XR system. This directly anticipates the source element of obtaining sensor data, including biometric and activity data, while the user wears the device. Hence the element lacks novelty (Y). Because the disclosure is identical, the step is also obvious (Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"in response to an indication that the user is performing the physical activity, initiating an artificial intelligence (AI) agent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent describes initiation of the AI assistant by user actions (voice, gestures, etc.) or by system triggers, but does not disclose initiating the AI agent specifically in response to a detected physical\u2011activity indication. Therefore the element is not disclosed (novelty N) and is not rendered obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, based on the sensor data, whether one or more activity-based thresholds are satisfied\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches determining a contextual cue from captured data, but it does not teach evaluating activity\u2011based thresholds on biometric or activity data. No such threshold logic is disclosed, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"in accordance with a determination that an activity-based threshold is satisfied, generating, by the AI agent, a coaching message to assist the user in performance of the physical activity\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent provides a generic response generated by a machine\u2011learning model, but it does not disclose a coaching message tailored to assist the user\u2019s physical\u2011activity performance based on threshold satisfaction. Thus the element is not disclosed (novelty N) and is not obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"causing presentation of the coaching message via the head-wearable device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"causing the head-wearable device to present the response\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target independent claim includes the step 'causing the head-wearable device to present the response,' which directly anticipates the source step of presenting a coaching message via the head\u2011wearable device. The element therefore lacks novelty (Y). Presenting a response on the device is a routine function disclosed, making the step obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014using sensor\u2011derived biometric/activity data to evaluate activity thresholds and generate AI\u2011driven coaching messages\u2014is not disclosed in the prior art. While the generic steps of capturing sensor data and presenting a response are anticipated (elements 1 and 5), the inventive concepts of activity\u2011based threshold detection and tailored coaching generation (elements 2\u20114) remain novel and non\u2011obvious. Consequently, the source patent retains substantive inventive merit despite overlap in routine data capture and display functions.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3281, "tokens_output": 2940, "latency_ms": 7064}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "while a user wearing a head-wearable device is performing a physical activity, obtaining sensor data sensed from the user via one or more sensors, the sensor data including at least biometric data and physical activity data", "corresponding_text": "The head-wearable device 110 can include an image sensor, a microphones, a GPS, bio-potential sensors, IMUs, eye-tracking sensors, thermometer, altimeters, and/or other sensors to capture data ... Sensor data obtained by any sensors described herein can be used the XR system", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly discloses a head\u2011wearable device equipped with multiple sensors (including bio\u2011potential sensors and IMUs) that capture data, and states that sensor data obtained by any sensors can be used by the XR system. This directly anticipates the source element of obtaining sensor data, including biometric and activity data, while the user wears the device. Hence the element lacks novelty (Y). Because the disclosure is identical, the step is also obvious (Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "in response to an indication that the user is performing the physical activity, initiating an artificial intelligence (AI) agent", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent describes initiation of the AI assistant by user actions (voice, gestures, etc.) or by system triggers, but does not disclose initiating the AI agent specifically in response to a detected physical\u2011activity indication. Therefore the element is not disclosed (novelty N) and is not rendered obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "determining, based on the sensor data, whether one or more activity-based thresholds are satisfied", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent teaches determining a contextual cue from captured data, but it does not teach evaluating activity\u2011based thresholds on biometric or activity data. No such threshold logic is disclosed, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "in accordance with a determination that an activity-based threshold is satisfied, generating, by the AI agent, a coaching message to assist the user in performance of the physical activity", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent provides a generic response generated by a machine\u2011learning model, but it does not disclose a coaching message tailored to assist the user\u2019s physical\u2011activity performance based on threshold satisfaction. Thus the element is not disclosed (novelty N) and is not obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "causing presentation of the coaching message via the head-wearable device", "corresponding_text": "causing the head-wearable device to present the response", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target independent claim includes the step 'causing the head-wearable device to present the response,' which directly anticipates the source step of presenting a coaching message via the head\u2011wearable device. The element therefore lacks novelty (Y). Presenting a response on the device is a routine function disclosed, making the step obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014using sensor\u2011derived biometric/activity data to evaluate activity thresholds and generate AI\u2011driven coaching messages\u2014is not disclosed in the prior art. While the generic steps of capturing sensor data and presenting a response are anticipated (elements 1 and 5), the inventive concepts of activity\u2011based threshold detection and tailored coaching generation (elements 2\u20114) remain novel and non\u2011obvious. Consequently, the source patent retains substantive inventive merit despite overlap in routine data capture and display functions."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "62303a37-1e75-42a8-9f70-97776253e4ee", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:56:14.769200+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20260064345A1", "independent_claim": "A method, comprising:\nwhile a user wearing a head-wearable device is performing a physical activity, obtaining sensor data sensed from the user via one or more sensors, the sensor data including at least biometric data and physical activity data,\nin response to an indication that the user is performing the physical activity, initiating an artificial intelligence (AI) agent,\ndetermining, based on the sensor data, whether one or more activity-based thresholds are satisfied,\nin accordance with a determination that an activity-based threshold is satisfied, generating, by the AI agent, a coaching message to assist the user in performance of the physical activity, and\ncausing presentation of the coaching message via the head-wearable device.", "specification": "he systems and methods disclosed herein provide different ways in which wearable devices can utilize artificial intelligence (AI) and/or an AI Agent (also referred to as a AI digital assistant or AI assistant). For example, in some embodiments, a head-wearable device can retrieve information and use the information with an AI agent to generate responses and/or recommendations that are displayed at the head-wearable device and/or another communicatively device. The systems and method disclosed here can be used collaborate with other users (including wearers of other wearable devices), and interact with third party applications using built-in AI models, in accordance with some embodiments. The systems and methods disclosed herein can utilize a user interactable AI agent to perform various tasks at the user's request, as well as utilize the AI agent to monitor situations and provide user-specific assistance.\n[0044]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize AI agent to work with wearable devices and other devices (e.g., laptop, tablet, watches, desktops, phones, and other internet connected devices) within an ecosystem to accomplish tasks across multiple devices (e.g., XR systems described below in reference to FIGS. 7A-7C-2 ). For example, an AI agent can be configured to control an aspect of one or more of the other devices based on a request from the user. In some embodiments, the AI agent can also be invoked on different devices based on a determination that the user is interacting with a device other than a wearable device.\n[0045]\nIn some embodiments, the systems and methods disclosed herein can use an AI agent to augment a user experience. In particular, the AI agent can receive sensor data and/or other information captured by a wearable device, and use the sensor data and/or other information to generate and provide recommended actions and/or context-based responses. For example, a head-wearable device worn by the user can capture information corresponding to a field of view of the user 105 and/or a location of the user to generate and provide recommended actions and/or context-based responses. The systems and methods disclosed herein generate and provide tailored information to a user based on location and/or data received from one or more wearable devices (e.g., sensor data and/or image data of a wrist-wearable device, a head-wearable device, etc.).\n[0046]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize an AI agent to collate recorded information (e.g., camera photos and videos) across multiple wearable devices to produce unique media (e.g., a single video which stitches the multiple head-wearable devices video feed into a single viewing experience). In some embodiments, positional data of each communicatively coupled device (e.g., wearable device, such as a head-wearable device) can be used to determine how the media is presented.\n[0047]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize an AI agent to work with third-party applications through the use of an API. In other words, the user can use an AI agent implemented at a wearable device to perform a task of applications by utilizing the API to communicate with the AI agent. In some embodiments, the AI agent can be configured to interact with applications and graphical user interfaces (GUIs) without the use of an API."}, "target_patent": {"label": "CN121708772A", "independent_claim": "A method for intelligent technical support of AI through VR glasses is characterized by comprising the following steps,\nPerforming primary inspection on the intelligent berth manager through the worn VR glasses, acquiring field visual information by utilizing the VR glasses, automatically identifying abnormality of the intelligent berth manager based on an AI model built in the VR glasses, and marking probability fault points in the field visual information by combining fault description of the abnormality of the intelligent berth manager;\nthe VR glasses are connected with the abnormal intelligent berth manager through Bluetooth to acquire abnormal operation data of the intelligent berth manager, synchronously execute test action instructions issued by a background AI intelligent technology system, and simultaneously, the VR glasses call overhaul flows matched with the abnormal operation data in a knowledge base to guide a class of personnel to complete primary diagnosis operation;\nWhen the personnel cannot finish the on-site independent fault solving of the diagnosis operation, the VR glasses are connected with the mobile phone through Bluetooth, an audio and video communication link of a remote end is established, the comprehensive diagnosis information is synchronously shared, the two kinds of personnel synchronously share the remote operation through the comprehensive diagnosis information, and the two kinds of personnel cooperatively finish the secondary diagnosis operation;\nAfter the diagnosis operation is finished, the AI intelligent technology system automatically inputs the abnormal operation flow into a background knowledge base to form an intelligent operation and maintenance closed loop so as to finish the AI intelligent technology support process.", "specification": "At this time, the VR glasses start a bluetooth pairing process, a bluetooth connection request containing an identity check token is made to the intelligent berth manager entity, and the intelligent berth manager responds to the request after verifying the validity of the identity check token, so that a secure communication channel is established, and it is ensured that the VR glasses are connected with the intelligent berth manager entity which is marked with abnormality at present.\nIn a second aspect, the embodiment of the invention provides an AI intelligent technical support system through VR glasses, which comprises an intelligent patrol and fault labeling module, a remote collaborative diagnosis module, a local diagnosis and flow guide module, a secondary diagnosis and flow guide module, a first-stage diagnosis operation and a second-stage diagnosis and operation optimization module, wherein the intelligent patrol and fault labeling module is used for carrying out preliminary patrol on an intelligent berth manager through the worn VR glasses, utilizing the VR glasses to acquire field visual information, automatically identifying abnormality of the intelligent berth manager based on an AI model built in the VR glasses, integrating abnormal fault description of the intelligent berth manager, marking probability fault points in the field visual information, the local diagnosis and flow guide module is used for enabling the VR glasses to be connected with the abnormality of the intelligent berth manager through Bluetooth to acquire abnormal operation data of the intelligent berth manager, synchronously executing test action instructions issued by a background AI intelligent technical system, simultaneously enabling the VR glasses to invoke an overhaul flow matched with the abnormal operation data in a knowledge base, guiding a class of staff to complete first-stage diagnosis operation, and a remote collaborative diagnosis module is used for synchronously sharing the comprehensive diagnosis information through Bluetooth connection with the remote-stage diagnosis and a remote-end audio-video communication link when the staff cannot complete the diagnosis operation independently, enabling the second-stage diagnosis operation independently to complete the fault, and the class staff synchronously shares the comprehensive diagnosis information through Bluetooth connection with the Bluetooth, automatically completes the second-stage diagnosis operation through the diagnosis operation and the abnormal diagnosis operation, and the second-stage diagnosis operation is completed through the intelligent diagnosis and the second-stage diagnosis operation optimization module after the intelligent diagnosis operation has completed the abnormal operation diagnosis operation, and the abnormal diagnosis operation by the diagnosis.\nIn a third aspect, an embodiment of the present invention provides a computer device, including a memory and a processor, where the memory stores a computer program, and the processor implements any step of the above-mentioned method for AI intelligent technical support through VR glasses when executing the computer program.\nIn a fourth aspect, embodiments of the present invention provide a computer-readable storage medium having a computer program stored thereon, wherein the computer program, when executed by a processor, implements any of the steps of the above-described method for AI-intelligent technical support via VR glasses.\nThe intelligent parking space management system has the advantages that an intelligent operation and maintenance system integrating VR glasses, an AI model, bluetooth Internet of things and remote cooperation is constructed, so that sensing, diagnosis, treatment and evolution of faults of an intelligent parking space manager are realized, on one hand, synchronous sharing and remote collaborative intervention of complex fault efficient treatment are realized by utilizing a multi-mode sensing module of the VR glasses and a built-in lightweight multi-mode fault inference engine, combining fault description text and visual information, marking probability fault points on site in real time, accuracy and efficiency of preliminary inspection are remarkably improved, on the other hand, running data are acquired through Bluetooth direct connection abnormal equipment, test instructions issued by a background AI are dynamically executed, a matched overhaul flow is called, a class of personnel is effectively guided to finish standardized first-stage diagnosis, when the problem can not be independently solved on site, an end-to-end encrypted remote audio and video communication link is automatically triggered, synchronous sharing and remote collaborative intervention of comprehensive diagnosis information are realized, finally, a complete operation context is automatically archived to a background knowledge base, the multi-mode fault knowledge base is continuously updated and the AI model is optimized, recognition and treatment capability of follow-up similar faults is enabled to be continuous, the intelligent parking space management system can not be greatly improved, the intelligent parking space management system has self-iterative diagnosis capability and self-adaptive to the intelligent operation system is improved, and the intelligent operation and the intelligent parking space management system has self-adaptive fault diagnosis capability is improved."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20260064345A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method, comprising:\nwhile a user wearing a head-wearable device is performing a physical activity, obtaining sensor data sensed from the user via one or more sensors, the sensor data including at least biometric data and physical activity data,\nin response to an indication that the user is performing the physical activity, initiating an artificial intelligence (AI) agent,\ndetermining, based on the sensor data, whether one or more activity-based thresholds are satisfied,\nin accordance with a determination that an activity-based threshold is satisfied, generating, by the AI agent, a coaching message to assist the user in performance of the physical activity, and\ncausing presentation of the coaching message via the head-wearable device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nhe systems and methods disclosed herein provide different ways in which wearable devices can utilize artificial intelligence (AI) and/or an AI Agent (also referred to as a AI digital assistant or AI assistant). For example, in some embodiments, a head-wearable device can retrieve information and use the information with an AI agent to generate responses and/or recommendations that are displayed at the head-wearable device and/or another communicatively device. The systems and method disclosed here can be used collaborate with other users (including wearers of other wearable devices), and interact with third party applications using built-in AI models, in accordance with some embodiments. The systems and methods disclosed herein can utilize a user interactable AI agent to perform various tasks at the user's request, as well as utilize the AI agent to monitor situations and provide user-specific assistance.\n[0044]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize AI agent to work with wearable devices and other devices (e.g., laptop, tablet, watches, desktops, phones, and other internet connected devices) within an ecosystem to accomplish tasks across multiple devices (e.g., XR systems described below in reference to FIGS. For example, an AI agent can be configured to control an aspect of one or more of the other devices based on a request from the user. In some embodiments, the AI agent can also be invoked on different devices based on a determination that the user is interacting with a device other than a wearable device.\n[0045]\nIn some embodiments, the systems and methods disclosed herein can use an AI agent to augment a user experience. In particular, the AI agent can receive sensor data and/or other information captured by a wearable device, and use the sensor data and/or other information to generate and provide recommended actions and/or context-based responses. For example, a head-wearable device worn by the user can capture information corresponding to a field of view of the user 105 and/or a location of the user to generate and provide recommended actions and/or context-based responses. The systems and methods disclosed herein generate and provide tailored information to a user based on location and/or data received from one or more wearable devices (e.g., sensor data and/or image data of a wrist-wearable device, a head-wearable device, etc.).\n[0046]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize an AI agent to collate recorded information (e.g., camera photos and videos) across multiple wearable devices to produce unique media (e.g., a single video which stitches the multiple head-wearable devices video feed into a single viewing experience). In some embodiments, positional data of each communicatively coupled device (e.g., wearable device, such as a head-wearable device) can be used to determine how the media is presented.\n[0047]\nThe systems and methods disclosed herein utilize an AI agent to work with third-party applications through the use of an API. In other words, the user can use an AI agent implemented at a wearable device to perform a task of applications by utilizing the API to communicate with the AI agent. In some embodiments, the AI agent can be configured to interact with applications and graphical user interfaces (GUIs) without the use of an API.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** CN121708772A\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for intelligent technical support of AI through VR glasses is characterized by comprising the following steps,\nPerforming primary inspection on the intelligent berth manager through the worn VR glasses, acquiring field visual information by utilizing the VR glasses, automatically identifying abnormality of the intelligent berth manager based on an AI model built in the VR glasses, and marking probability fault points in the field visual information by combining fault description of the abnormality of the intelligent berth manager;\nthe VR glasses are connected with the abnormal intelligent berth manager through Bluetooth to acquire abnormal operation data of the intelligent berth manager, synchronously execute test action instructions issued by a background AI intelligent technology system, and simultaneously, the VR glasses call overhaul flows matched with the abnormal operation data in a knowledge base to guide a class of personnel to complete primary diagnosis operation;\nWhen the personnel cannot finish the on-site independent fault solving of the diagnosis operation, the VR glasses are connected with the mobile phone through Bluetooth, an audio and video communication link of a remote end is established, the comprehensive diagnosis information is synchronously shared, the two kinds of personnel synchronously share the remote operation through the comprehensive diagnosis information, and the two kinds of personnel cooperatively finish the secondary diagnosis operation;\nAfter the diagnosis operation is finished, the AI intelligent technology system automatically inputs the abnormal operation flow into a background knowledge base to form an intelligent operation and maintenance closed loop so as to finish the AI intelligent technology support process.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nAt this time, the VR glasses start a bluetooth pairing process, a bluetooth connection request containing an identity check token is made to the intelligent berth manager entity, and the intelligent berth manager responds to the request after verifying the validity of the identity check token, so that a secure communication channel is established, and it is ensured that the VR glasses are connected with the intelligent berth manager entity which is marked with abnormality at present.\nIn a second aspect, the embodiment of the invention provides an AI intelligent technical support system through VR glasses, which comprises an intelligent patrol and fault labeling module, a remote collaborative diagnosis module, a local diagnosis and flow guide module, a secondary diagnosis and flow guide module, a first-stage diagnosis operation and a second-stage diagnosis and operation optimization module, wherein the intelligent patrol and fault labeling module is used for carrying out preliminary patrol on an intelligent berth manager through the worn VR glasses, utilizing the VR glasses to acquire field visual information, automatically identifying abnormality of the intelligent berth manager based on an AI model built in the VR glasses, integrating abnormal fault description of the intelligent berth manager, marking probability fault points in the field visual information, the local diagnosis and flow guide module is used for enabling the VR glasses to be connected with the abnormality of the intelligent berth manager through Bluetooth to acquire abnormal operation data of the intelligent berth manager, synchronously executing test action instructions issued by a background AI intelligent technical system, simultaneously enabling the VR glasses to invoke an overhaul flow matched with the abnormal operation data in a knowledge base, guiding a class of staff to complete first-stage diagnosis operation, and a remote collaborative diagnosis module is used for synchronously sharing the comprehensive diagnosis information through Bluetooth connection with the remote-stage diagnosis and a remote-end audio-video communication link when the staff cannot complete the diagnosis operation independently, enabling the second-stage diagnosis operation independently to complete the fault, and the class staff synchronously shares the comprehensive diagnosis information through Bluetooth connection with the Bluetooth, automatically completes the second-stage diagnosis operation through the diagnosis operation and the abnormal diagnosis operation, and the second-stage diagnosis operation is completed through the intelligent diagnosis and the second-stage diagnosis operation optimization module after the intelligent diagnosis operation has completed the abnormal operation diagnosis operation, and the abnormal diagnosis operation by the diagnosis.\nIn a third aspect, an embodiment of the present invention provides a computer device, including a memory and a processor, where the memory stores a computer program, and the processor implements any step of the above-mentioned method for AI intelligent technical support through VR glasses when executing the computer program.\nIn a fourth aspect, embodiments of the present invention provide a computer-readable storage medium having a computer program stored thereon, wherein the computer program, when executed by a processor, implements any of the steps of the above-described method for AI-intelligent technical support via VR glasses.\nThe intelligent parking space management system has the advantages that an intelligent operation and maintenance system integrating VR glasses, an AI model, bluetooth Internet of things and remote cooperation is constructed, so that sensing, diagnosis, treatment and evolution of faults of an intelligent parking space manager are realized, on one hand, synchronous sharing and remote collaborative intervention of complex fault efficient treatment are realized by utilizing a multi-mode sensing module of the VR glasses and a built-in lightweight multi-mode fault inference engine, combining fault description text and visual information, marking probability fault points on site in real time, accuracy and efficiency of preliminary inspection are remarkably improved, on the other hand, running data are acquired through Bluetooth direct connection abnormal equipment, test instructions issued by a background AI are dynamically executed, a matched overhaul flow is called, a class of personnel is effectively guided to finish standardized first-stage diagnosis, when the problem can not be independently solved on site, an end-to-end encrypted remote audio and video communication link is automatically triggered, synchronous sharing and remote collaborative intervention of comprehensive diagnosis information are realized, finally, a complete operation context is automatically archived to a background knowledge base, the multi-mode fault knowledge base is continuously updated and the AI model is optimized, recognition and treatment capability of follow-up similar faults is enabled to be continuous, the intelligent parking space management system can not be greatly improved, the intelligent parking space management system has self-iterative diagnosis capability and self-adaptive to the intelligent operation system is improved, and the intelligent operation and the intelligent parking space management system has self-adaptive fault diagnosis capability is improved."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"while a user wearing a head-wearable device is performing a physical activity, obtaining sensor data sensed from the user via one or more sensors, the sensor data including at least biometric data and physical activity data\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent describes VR glasses acquiring field visual information and abnormal operation data from an intelligent berth manager, but it does not disclose obtaining biometric or physical\u2011activity sensor data from a user wearing a head\u2011wearable device. Therefore the element is not found (novelty N) and, lacking any teaching, it is not obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"in response to an indication that the user is performing the physical activity, initiating an artificial intelligence (AI) agent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target disclosure triggers AI processing based on detection of equipment abnormality, not on a user\u2019s physical activity. No language matches the step of initiating an AI agent when the user is performing a physical activity. Hence the element is novel (N) and not obvious (N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, based on the sensor data, whether one or more activity-based thresholds are satisfied\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art determines equipment abnormality using an AI model, but it does not disclose evaluating activity\u2011based thresholds derived from biometric or activity sensor data. No corresponding disclosure, so novelty N and inventive step N. Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"in accordance with a determination that an activity-based threshold is satisfied, generating, by the AI agent, a coaching message to assist the user in performance of the physical activity\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent generates fault\u2011labeling or diagnostic instructions, not a coaching message aimed at assisting a user\u2019s physical activity. No matching disclosure, thus the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"causing presentation of the coaching message via the head-wearable device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"While the VR glasses present visual fault points, the claim element specifically requires presenting a coaching message generated for physical\u2011activity assistance. The prior art does not disclose such a message, so the element is not found (novelty N) and is not obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"All core technical elements of the source claim are neither disclosed nor rendered obvious by the target patent. The prior art focuses on equipment fault diagnosis via VR glasses, whereas the source claim addresses real\u2011time biometric sensing, activity\u2011based threshold evaluation, AI\u2011generated coaching, and presentation of that coaching on a head\u2011wearable device. Consequently, the source patent retains novelty and an inventive step for its principal contributions, supporting its validity.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3154, "tokens_output": 1657, "latency_ms": 6346}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "while a user wearing a head-wearable device is performing a physical activity, obtaining sensor data sensed from the user via one or more sensors, the sensor data including at least biometric data and physical activity data", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent describes VR glasses acquiring field visual information and abnormal operation data from an intelligent berth manager, but it does not disclose obtaining biometric or physical\u2011activity sensor data from a user wearing a head\u2011wearable device. Therefore the element is not found (novelty N) and, lacking any teaching, it is not obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "in response to an indication that the user is performing the physical activity, initiating an artificial intelligence (AI) agent", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target disclosure triggers AI processing based on detection of equipment abnormality, not on a user\u2019s physical activity. No language matches the step of initiating an AI agent when the user is performing a physical activity. Hence the element is novel (N) and not obvious (N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "determining, based on the sensor data, whether one or more activity-based thresholds are satisfied", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art determines equipment abnormality using an AI model, but it does not disclose evaluating activity\u2011based thresholds derived from biometric or activity sensor data. No corresponding disclosure, so novelty N and inventive step N. Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "in accordance with a determination that an activity-based threshold is satisfied, generating, by the AI agent, a coaching message to assist the user in performance of the physical activity", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent generates fault\u2011labeling or diagnostic instructions, not a coaching message aimed at assisting a user\u2019s physical activity. No matching disclosure, thus the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "causing presentation of the coaching message via the head-wearable device", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "While the VR glasses present visual fault points, the claim element specifically requires presenting a coaching message generated for physical\u2011activity assistance. The prior art does not disclose such a message, so the element is not found (novelty N) and is not obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N."}], "overall_opinion": "All core technical elements of the source claim are neither disclosed nor rendered obvious by the target patent. The prior art focuses on equipment fault diagnosis via VR glasses, whereas the source claim addresses real\u2011time biometric sensing, activity\u2011based threshold evaluation, AI\u2011generated coaching, and presentation of that coaching on a head\u2011wearable device. Consequently, the source patent retains novelty and an inventive step for its principal contributions, supporting its validity."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "3dd2fbfe-0fe4-4bdd-a8ac-caf54956c680", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:56:55.237425+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20250274411A1", "independent_claim": "1. A system for interacting with a language model, the system comprising:\none or more processors; and\none or more storage devices storing instructions that, when executed, configure the one or more processors to perform operations comprising:\nreceiving, through a user interface, a user message to a language model;\ngenerating an input for the language model based on the message, the input comprising a prompt for a reaction token associated with the user message;\nin response to the input, generating a response comprising the reaction token; and\nrendering the reaction token in the user interface as a model reaction to the user message.", "specification": "0047]\nSystem 100 can further include predictive output generation engine 140, output validation engine 150 (e.g., configured to apply validation data to machine learning model output), feedback engine 170 (e.g., configured to apply feedback from a user and/or machine to a model), and model refinement engine 160 (e.g., configured to update or re-configure a model). In some of the disclosed systems and methods, feedback engine 170 can receive input and/or transmit output (e.g., output from a trained, partially trained, or untrained model) to outcome metrics database 180. Outcome metrics database 180 can be configured to store output from one or more models and can also be configured to associate output with one or more models. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, outcome metrics database 180, or other device (e.g., model refinement engine 160 or feedback engine 170), can be configured to correlate output, detect trends in output data, and/or infer a change to input or model parameters to cause a particular model output or type of model output. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, model refinement engine 160 can receive output from predictive output generation engine 140 or output validation engine 150. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, model refinement engine 160 can transmit the received output to featurization engine 120 or ML modeling engine 130 in one or more iterative cycles.\n[0048]\nAny or each engine of system 100 can be a module (e.g., a program module), which can be a packaged functional hardware unit designed for use with other components or a part of a program that performs a particular function (e.g., of related functions). Any or each of these modules can be implemented using a computing device. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, the functionality of system 100 can be split across multiple computing devices to allow for distributed processing of the data, which can improve output speed and reduce computational load on individual devices. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, system 100 can use load-balancing to maintain a stable resource load (e.g., processing load, memory load, or bandwidth load) across multiple computing devices and to reduce the risk of a computing device or connection becoming overloaded. In these or other systems and methods, the different components can communicate over one or more I/O devices and/or network interfaces.\n[0049]\nSystem 100 can be related to different domains or fields of use. Descriptions of systems and methods related to specific domains, such as natural language processing or language modeling, is not intended to limit the disclosed systems and methods to those specific domains, and systems and methods consistent with the present disclosure can apply to any domain that utilizes predictive modeling based on available data.\n[0050]\nFIG. 2 depicts a flowchart illustrating an exemplary process 200 for interacting with a language model using model reactions, according to some of the disclosed systems and methods. The process shown in FIG. 2 or any of its constituent steps can be implemented using systems in FIG. 1 or 8 , or any component thereof. The steps illustrated in FIG. 2 are exemplary, and steps can be added, merged, divided, duplicated, repeated (e.g., as part of a machine learning process), modified, performed sequentially, performed in parallel, and/or deleted in some of the disclosed systems and methods.\n[0051]\nStep 201 of method 200 may include training and/or configuration of a language model with reaction token capabilities (e.g., the ability to interpret and respond with reaction tokens). The training of the language model may involve one or more sub-steps for the training of the model.\n[0052]\nFor example the training and/or configuration of the language model in step 201 may involve data collection and preparation. The data collection can include collecting conversations and texts that include both text and reaction tokens. This data can be collected from social media posts, forums or chat logs, among other sources. In step 201 the data may be vetted to ensure diversity of reaction tokens and their use in different contexts. In step 201 the data may also be processed to facilitate training. For example, the collected data may be filtered by removing elements in the training data that could be confusing or unhelpful during model training. For example, the data may be processed to remove URLs or special characters like \u2018#\u2019 or \u2018$.\u2019\n[0053]\nStep 201 may also involve choosing a base model for training of the language model with reaction token capabilities. For example, step 201 may involve starting with a pre-trained transformer model (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa, among others) that is modified to include reaction token capabilities. For example, the pre-trained model can be modified to include emoji tokens by updating the model vocabulary (e.g., the set of words, subwords, or tokens that the model recognizes and uses to process or generate text) and/or by finetuning the pre-trained model using collected data. Finetuning and/or training in step 201 may also involve performing a training loop with multiple iterations of prediction and true value for training and assessment. In each iteration, the model may calculate the loss (error) between its predictions and the true values, use backpropagation to compute gradients, and updates its parameters using an optimization algorithm like gradient descent to minimize the loss. This process can repeat for multiple epochs until the model's performance converges or meets desired criteria. Step 201 may also involve evaluation and testing. For example, in step 201 the accuracy of the reaction token may be evaluated by determining context appropriateness, and token-to-token patterns. Additionally step 201 may include deployment and optimization. Deployment may include selection of the interface implementation (e.g., via an API or within a graphical user interface). For example, in some embodiments the model may be configured to interpret user messages received through a user interface. Optimization may include pruning of the size model (to improve efficiencies and operations), quantization for faster inference, and caching of frequent reaction token responses. Optimization for the training of the model in step 201 may also involve implementing a response caching, configuring batch processing for multiple requests, and performing load balancing for high-traffic deployment. Further, training in step 201 may include optimization of a tokenization process for the language model with reaction token capabilities. For example, the tokenizer for the language model trained in step 201 may be trained to perform operations like text splitting, while preserving reaction tokens, converting the reaction tokens to special characters, or combination of characters. In some embodiments, the tokenizer may be split in two, one for handling regular text and one for handling reaction tokens for proper handling of reaction tokens. In some embodiments, the tokenizer may be configured to be attached to the user interface."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US12367342B1", "independent_claim": "1. A system for automated analysis of computerized conversational agent (CCA) conversational data, comprising:\na computer system comprising a memory, a processor, and a data store residing on a non-volatile data storage device;\na conversation indicator extractor comprising a first plurality of programming instructions stored in the memory which, when operating on the processor, causes the computer system to:\nreceive transcript data for analysis, the transcript data comprising text of one or more conversations between a computerized conversational agent (CCA) and a user, each conversation comprising a plurality of messages, each message being labeled as having been authored either by the CCA or by the user;\nanalyze the messages to extract one or more conversational indicators for the one or more conversations;\nassociate each conversational indicator with one or more messages of the plurality of messages from which the conversational indicator was extracted; and\nstore the conversational indicators and their message associations in the data store;\na fallback analyzer comprising a second plurality of programming instructions stored in the memory which, when operating on the processor, causes the computer system to:\nretrieve from the non-volatile data storage device messages associated with a fallback indicator;\nfor each retrieved message:\nextract fallback context from the conversation, the fallback context comprising:\nan agent message indicated as containing a fallback message;\na customer message preceding the fallback message; and\nan agent message that elicited the customer message preceding the fallback message;\nsend the fallback context to an artificial intelligence assistant (AI assistant) with instructions to the AI assistant to assign a fallback type for the fallback message as either intent-based or prompt-based; and\nstore the assigned fallback type in the data store;\na topic modeling engine comprising a third plurality of programming instructions stored in the memory which, when operating on the processor, causes the computer system to:\nsample data from the data store according to a sampling time window;\nsend the sample data to a natural language understanding (NLU) engine with instructions to generate a list of frequently-occurring topic labels in the form of a term-frequency inverse document frequency (TF-IDF) list;\nreceive the TF-IDF list from the NLU engine;\nreceive a list of labeled topics from an administrator, or from the data store, or both;\nsend the TD-IDF list and the list of labeled topics to a natural language generation (NLG) engine with instructions to assign natural language topic labels for the sample data, wherein the instructions comprise directives to:\nassign a topic label from the list of labeled topics if terms in the TF-IDF list fit a label from the list of labeled topics; and\nassign a topic label from the NLG engine's own judgment if terms in the TF-IDF list do not fit a label from the list of labeled topics; and\nstore the assigned topic labels in the data store;\na CCA score generator comprising a fourth plurality of programming instructions stored in the memory which, when operating on the processor, causes the computer system to:\ngenerate a numerical indicator of conversation quality for one or more of the one or more conversational indicators; and\ndisplay the numerical indicator to an administrator, the display comprising a link from the numerical indicator to one or more messages associated with the conversational indicator.", "specification": "Computerized conversational agents (also sometimes known as \u201cdialog agents\u201d) are a class of computer programs designed to have, or at least mimic, conversations with humans in order to obtain information and assist with various activities such as routing to a particular agent, troubleshooting a problem with a product or service, or to provide requested information. Depending on their configuration, CCAs can have conversations via typewritten text, handwritten text, speech, or even gestures such as sign language. The most common types of CCAs are chatCCAs (primarily typed-text-based, such as via a website) and interactive voice response (IVR) systems (primarily voice-based, such as over the telephone), although CCAs can be configured to have conversations using handwritten text and gestures. There are three primary CCA models: rule-based CCAs which use lists of pre-defined rules to process queries and responses, statistically-enhanced CCAs which are primarily rule-based but are enhanced with some predictive language capabilities, and artificial intelligence (AI) based CCAs which use trained neural networks (of which large language models (LLMs) are one type) to process language in a manner mimicking that of the human brain.\nComputerized conversational agents (CCAs) are designed to allow for natural language inputs and outputs, mimicking natural human conversations which, in theory, provide greater case of use and better outcomes. However, in actual use, CCAs often fail to achieve their goals, leading to a frustrating user experience. Human conversations are complex and subtle, and CCAs can often fail to understand the user's inquiry or fail to provide appropriate responses, leading to failure of the system to achieve the goal of helping the user.\nWhile there are automated systems for providing quantitative analysis of CCAs, quantitative analysis provides only statistical data about how often things are occurring with a CCA. For example, quantitative analysis can provide numerical data about how many conversations have been had, the length of those conversations, whether those conversations were routed to an agent, and similar data. However, none of these statistics provides information about how well a CCA is working (i.e., whether it is properly understanding the user, providing relevant information in response, and importantly, whether it is achieving the goals that its operators intend for it to achieve).\nOnly qualitative analysis can provide information about how well a CCA is performing, but existing automated conversational analysis systems cannot perform qualitative analysis. The only existing way to perform qualitative analysis of CCAs is through human review, wherein humans select a small sample of conversations of a given CCA, qualitatively evaluate the CCA's performance, and make manual corrections and changes the CCA's operation where the CCA is failing to perform as intended. Unfortunately, this manual sampling and correction by humans is inherently limited in scale. It cannot comprehensively analyze the performance of large-scale CCAs (e.g., those having thousands or millions of conversations or more) by analyzing all conversations in the CCA, or identify exceptional cases which are unlikely to come up in sampling, or troubleshoot complex errors in CCA operation that may result from links between many conversations or conversational aspects. Further, manual sampling and analysis cannot take into account external factors such as a business' objectives and key performance indicators (KPIs), so is unable to optimize a CCA for the purposes of a given business or to optimize a CCA for a particular context, such as certain industries or fields of operation.\nMore recent innovations in CCAs make manual sampling and human correction even more difficult and less reliable. Some conversational AI systems, for example, are black boxes. Their operation has been generated by training a machine learning algorithm on sample conversational inputs and outputs. However, it is not possible to know exactly how the machine learning algorithm has structured its internal rules, so unless a comprehensive qualitative analysis of the conversational Al's conversations is conducted, it may not be possible to re-train the machine learning algorithm to fix the problem. For example, Large Language Models (LLMs) are often used in conversational AI systems, and can generate so-called \u201challucinations,\u201d which are generation of content that is irrelevant, made-up, misleading, or inconsistent with the input data. The source of LLM hallucinations is extremely difficult to identify and correct using current manual methods.\nThe system and method described herein provide a solution to the problems described above by allowing for comprehensive, large-scale, automated, and qualitative conversational analysis of CCAs. The system and method use custom-built algorithms to extract conversation indicators (e.g. system message indicators, CCA error message indicators, fallback message indicators, escalation message indicators, profanity indicators, etc.) to determine what conversational qualities the conversation possesses, and to process the conversations through a topic modeling engine to determine what types of issues the CCA is encountering on a regular basis (e.g., over a 7-day window). The system and method further use a unique methodology to discriminate between fallback message types, providing additional information about CCA failures. Primary analysis is undertaken to gather basic data points, such as number of messages in each conversation, message lengths, and whether an error was encountered during a conversation. Conversation data is augmented with these additional basic data points in a proprietary database. Conversations are then analyzed against a set of features which are derived from industry best practices and conversational Al expertise. Features may include measurements such as identifying overly-long CCA messages, understanding how often the CCA repeats itself during a conversation, what the outcome of each conversation was, and what the topic of each conversation was, amongst others. The system and method may provide improvement recommendations based on scoring of the various conversational qualities and topics. In some embodiments, the system and method may automatically adjust a CCA to correct conversational quality problems by, for example, re-writing conversational rules for rule-based CCAs or by retraining a conversational AI CCA using labeled conversation training data containing corrections."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20250274411A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A system for interacting with a language model, the system comprising:\none or more processors; and\none or more storage devices storing instructions that, when executed, configure the one or more processors to perform operations comprising:\nreceiving, through a user interface, a user message to a language model;\ngenerating an input for the language model based on the message, the input comprising a prompt for a reaction token associated with the user message;\nin response to the input, generating a response comprising the reaction token; and\nrendering the reaction token in the user interface as a model reaction to the user message.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n0047]\nSystem 100 can further include predictive output generation engine 140, output validation engine 150 (e.g., configured to apply validation data to machine learning model output), feedback engine 170 (e.g., configured to apply feedback from a user and/or machine to a model), and model refinement engine 160 (e.g., configured to update or re-configure a model). In some of the disclosed systems and methods, feedback engine 170 can receive input and/or transmit output (e.g., output from a trained, partially trained, or untrained model) to outcome metrics database 180. Outcome metrics database 180 can be configured to store output from one or more models and can also be configured to associate output with one or more models. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, outcome metrics database 180, or other device (e.g., model refinement engine 160 or feedback engine 170), can be configured to correlate output, detect trends in output data, and/or infer a change to input or model parameters to cause a particular model output or type of model output. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, model refinement engine 160 can receive output from predictive output generation engine 140 or output validation engine 150. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, model refinement engine 160 can transmit the received output to featurization engine 120 or ML modeling engine 130 in one or more iterative cycles.\n[0048]\nAny or each engine of system 100 can be a module (e.g., a program module), which can be a packaged functional hardware unit designed for use with other components or a part of a program that performs a particular function (e.g., of related functions). In some of the disclosed systems and methods, the functionality of system 100 can be split across multiple computing devices to allow for distributed processing of the data, which can improve output speed and reduce computational load on individual devices. In some of the disclosed systems and methods, system 100 can use load-balancing to maintain a stable resource load (e.g., processing load, memory load, or bandwidth load) across multiple computing devices and to reduce the risk of a computing device or connection becoming overloaded. In these or other systems and methods, the different components can communicate over one or more I/O devices and/or network interfaces.\n[0049]\nSystem 100 can be related to different domains or fields of use. Descriptions of systems and methods related to specific domains, such as natural language processing or language modeling, is not intended to limit the disclosed systems and methods to those specific domains, and systems and methods consistent with the present disclosure can apply to any domain that utilizes predictive modeling based on available data.\n[0050]\nFIG. 2 depicts a flowchart illustrating an exemplary process 200 for interacting with a language model using model reactions, according to some of the disclosed systems and methods. 2 or any of its constituent steps can be implemented using systems in FIG. 2 are exemplary, and steps can be added, merged, divided, duplicated, repeated (e.g., as part of a machine learning process), modified, performed sequentially, performed in parallel, and/or deleted in some of the disclosed systems and methods.\n[0051]\nStep 201 of method 200 may include training and/or configuration of a language model with reaction token capabilities (e.g., the ability to interpret and respond with reaction tokens). The training of the language model may involve one or more sub-steps for the training of the model.\n[0052]\nFor example the training and/or configuration of the language model in step 201 may involve data collection and preparation. The data collection can include collecting conversations and texts that include both text and reaction tokens. In step 201 the data may be vetted to ensure diversity of reaction tokens and their use in different contexts. For example, the collected data may be filtered by removing elements in the training data that could be confusing or unhelpful during model training. For example, the data may be processed to remove URLs or special characters like \u2018#\u2019 or \u2018$.\u2019\n[0053]\nStep 201 may also involve choosing a base model for training of the language model with reaction token capabilities. For example, step 201 may involve starting with a pre-trained transformer model (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa, among others) that is modified to include reaction token capabilities. For example, the pre-trained model can be modified to include emoji tokens by updating the model vocabulary (e.g., the set of words, subwords, or tokens that the model recognizes and uses to process or generate text) and/or by finetuning the pre-trained model using collected data. Finetuning and/or training in step 201 may also involve performing a training loop with multiple iterations of prediction and true value for training and assessment. In each iteration, the model may calculate the loss (error) between its predictions and the true values, use backpropagation to compute gradients, and updates its parameters using an optimization algorithm like gradient descent to minimize the loss. This process can repeat for multiple epochs until the model's performance converges or meets desired criteria. For example, in step 201 the accuracy of the reaction token may be evaluated by determining context appropriateness, and token-to-token patterns. Deployment may include selection of the interface implementation (e.g., via an API or within a graphical user interface). For example, in some embodiments the model may be configured to interpret user messages received through a user interface. Optimization may include pruning of the size model (to improve efficiencies and operations), quantization for faster inference, and caching of frequent reaction token responses. Optimization for the training of the model in step 201 may also involve implementing a response caching, configuring batch processing for multiple requests, and performing load balancing for high-traffic deployment. Further, training in step 201 may include optimization of a tokenization process for the language model with reaction token capabilities. For example, the tokenizer for the language model trained in step 201 may be trained to perform operations like text splitting, while preserving reaction tokens, converting the reaction tokens to special characters, or combination of characters. In some embodiments, the tokenizer may be split in two, one for handling regular text and one for handling reaction tokens for proper handling of reaction tokens. In some embodiments, the tokenizer may be configured to be attached to the user interface.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US12367342B1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A system for automated analysis of computerized conversational agent (CCA) conversational data, comprising:\na computer system comprising a memory, a processor, and a data store residing on a non-volatile data storage device;\na conversation indicator extractor comprising a first plurality of programming instructions stored in the memory which, when operating on the processor, causes the computer system to:\nreceive transcript data for analysis, the transcript data comprising text of one or more conversations between a computerized conversational agent (CCA) and a user, each conversation comprising a plurality of messages, each message being labeled as having been authored either by the CCA or by the user;\nanalyze the messages to extract one or more conversational indicators for the one or more conversations;\nassociate each conversational indicator with one or more messages of the plurality of messages from which the conversational indicator was extracted; and\nstore the conversational indicators and their message associations in the data store;\na fallback analyzer comprising a second plurality of programming instructions stored in the memory which, when operating on the processor, causes the computer system to:\nretrieve from the non-volatile data storage device messages associated with a fallback indicator;\nfor each retrieved message:\nextract fallback context from the conversation, the fallback context comprising:\nan agent message indicated as containing a fallback message;\na customer message preceding the fallback message; and\nan agent message that elicited the customer message preceding the fallback message;\nsend the fallback context to an artificial intelligence assistant (AI assistant) with instructions to the AI assistant to assign a fallback type for the fallback message as either intent-based or prompt-based; and\nstore the assigned fallback type in the data store;\na topic modeling engine comprising a third plurality of programming instructions stored in the memory which, when operating on the processor, causes the computer system to:\nsample data from the data store according to a sampling time window;\nsend the sample data to a natural language understanding (NLU) engine with instructions to generate a list of frequently-occurring topic labels in the form of a term-frequency inverse document frequency (TF-IDF) list;\nreceive the TF-IDF list from the NLU engine;\nreceive a list of labeled topics from an administrator, or from the data store, or both;\nsend the TD-IDF list and the list of labeled topics to a natural language generation (NLG) engine with instructions to assign natural language topic labels for the sample data, wherein the instructions comprise directives to:\nassign a topic label from the list of labeled topics if terms in the TF-IDF list fit a label from the list of labeled topics; and\nassign a topic label from the NLG engine's own judgment if terms in the TF-IDF list do not fit a label from the list of labeled topics; and\nstore the assigned topic labels in the data store;\na CCA score generator comprising a fourth plurality of programming instructions stored in the memory which, when operating on the processor, causes the computer system to:\ngenerate a numerical indicator of conversation quality for one or more of the one or more conversational indicators; and\ndisplay the numerical indicator to an administrator, the display comprising a link from the numerical indicator to one or more messages associated with the conversational indicator.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nComputerized conversational agents (also sometimes known as \u201cdialog agents\u201d) are a class of computer programs designed to have, or at least mimic, conversations with humans in order to obtain information and assist with various activities such as routing to a particular agent, troubleshooting a problem with a product or service, or to provide requested information. Depending on their configuration, CCAs can have conversations via typewritten text, handwritten text, speech, or even gestures such as sign language. The most common types of CCAs are chatCCAs (primarily typed-text-based, such as via a website) and interactive voice response (IVR) systems (primarily voice-based, such as over the telephone), although CCAs can be configured to have conversations using handwritten text and gestures. There are three primary CCA models: rule-based CCAs which use lists of pre-defined rules to process queries and responses, statistically-enhanced CCAs which are primarily rule-based but are enhanced with some predictive language capabilities, and artificial intelligence (AI) based CCAs which use trained neural networks (of which large language models (LLMs) are one type) to process language in a manner mimicking that of the human brain.\nComputerized conversational agents (CCAs) are designed to allow for natural language inputs and outputs, mimicking natural human conversations which, in theory, provide greater case of use and better outcomes. However, in actual use, CCAs often fail to achieve their goals, leading to a frustrating user experience. Human conversations are complex and subtle, and CCAs can often fail to understand the user's inquiry or fail to provide appropriate responses, leading to failure of the system to achieve the goal of helping the user.\nWhile there are automated systems for providing quantitative analysis of CCAs, quantitative analysis provides only statistical data about how often things are occurring with a CCA. For example, quantitative analysis can provide numerical data about how many conversations have been had, the length of those conversations, whether those conversations were routed to an agent, and similar data. However, none of these statistics provides information about how well a CCA is working (i.e., whether it is properly understanding the user, providing relevant information in response, and importantly, whether it is achieving the goals that its operators intend for it to achieve).\nOnly qualitative analysis can provide information about how well a CCA is performing, but existing automated conversational analysis systems cannot perform qualitative analysis. The only existing way to perform qualitative analysis of CCAs is through human review, wherein humans select a small sample of conversations of a given CCA, qualitatively evaluate the CCA's performance, and make manual corrections and changes the CCA's operation where the CCA is failing to perform as intended. Unfortunately, this manual sampling and correction by humans is inherently limited in scale. It cannot comprehensively analyze the performance of large-scale CCAs (e.g., those having thousands or millions of conversations or more) by analyzing all conversations in the CCA, or identify exceptional cases which are unlikely to come up in sampling, or troubleshoot complex errors in CCA operation that may result from links between many conversations or conversational aspects. Further, manual sampling and analysis cannot take into account external factors such as a business' objectives and key performance indicators (KPIs), so is unable to optimize a CCA for the purposes of a given business or to optimize a CCA for a particular context, such as certain industries or fields of operation.\nMore recent innovations in CCAs make manual sampling and human correction even more difficult and less reliable. Some conversational AI systems, for example, are black boxes. Their operation has been generated by training a machine learning algorithm on sample conversational inputs and outputs. However, it is not possible to know exactly how the machine learning algorithm has structured its internal rules, so unless a comprehensive qualitative analysis of the conversational Al's conversations is conducted, it may not be possible to re-train the machine learning algorithm to fix the problem. For example, Large Language Models (LLMs) are often used in conversational AI systems, and can generate so-called \u201challucinations,\u201d which are generation of content that is irrelevant, made-up, misleading, or inconsistent with the input data. The source of LLM hallucinations is extremely difficult to identify and correct using current manual methods.\nThe system and method described herein provide a solution to the problems described above by allowing for comprehensive, large-scale, automated, and qualitative conversational analysis of CCAs. The system and method use custom-built algorithms to extract conversation indicators (e.g. system message indicators, CCA error message indicators, fallback message indicators, escalation message indicators, profanity indicators, etc.) to determine what conversational qualities the conversation possesses, and to process the conversations through a topic modeling engine to determine what types of issues the CCA is encountering on a regular basis (e.g., over a 7-day window). The system and method further use a unique methodology to discriminate between fallback message types, providing additional information about CCA failures. Primary analysis is undertaken to gather basic data points, such as number of messages in each conversation, message lengths, and whether an error was encountered during a conversation. Conversation data is augmented with these additional basic data points in a proprietary database. Conversations are then analyzed against a set of features which are derived from industry best practices and conversational Al expertise. Features may include measurements such as identifying overly-long CCA messages, understanding how often the CCA repeats itself during a conversation, what the outcome of each conversation was, and what the topic of each conversation was, amongst others. The system and method may provide improvement recommendations based on scoring of the various conversational qualities and topics. In some embodiments, the system and method may automatically adjust a CCA to correct conversational quality problems by, for example, re-writing conversational rules for rule-based CCAs or by retraining a conversational AI CCA using labeled conversation training data containing corrections."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"one or more processors\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent's independent claim mentions a computer system comprising a processor, but does not disclose a generic \\\"one or more processors\\\" as a standalone element for interacting with a language model. Since the specific context of processors configured for language model interaction is not taught, the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"one or more storage devices storing instructions that, when executed, configure the one or more processors to perform operations comprising:\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent describes a memory and storage of programming instructions, but those instructions are directed to conversational\u2011agent analysis functions, not to configuring processors for language\u2011model interaction with reaction tokens. Therefore the element is not disclosed and is non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving, through a user interface, a user message to a language model\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art focuses on receiving transcript data for analysis, not on a user interface that sends a message to a language model. No teaching of a UI\u2011driven message to a language model is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating an input for the language model based on the message, the input comprising a prompt for a reaction token associated with the user message\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose the concept of a \\\"reaction token\\\" or generating a prompt specifically for such a token. Its processing relates to extracting conversational indicators, not to token\u2011based prompting. Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"in response to the input, generating a response comprising the reaction token\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No teaching in the prior art of a language model emitting a reaction token as part of its response. The prior art generates analytical indicators, not reaction tokens, making this element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"rendering the reaction token in the user interface as a model reaction to the user message\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art does not describe displaying a reaction token in a UI as a model reaction. Its display functions are limited to showing numerical quality indicators. Therefore this element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014introducing reaction tokens that are generated by a language model and rendered in a user interface as model reactions\u2014is not disclosed in any form by the target patent's independent claim or specification. Each claim element is novel and non\u2011obvious over the prior art, leading to a overall assessment that the source patent retains validity, particularly with respect to its primary inventive contribution.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 4452, "tokens_output": 1499, "latency_ms": 4650}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "one or more processors", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent's independent claim mentions a computer system comprising a processor, but does not disclose a generic \"one or more processors\" as a standalone element for interacting with a language model. Since the specific context of processors configured for language model interaction is not taught, the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "one or more storage devices storing instructions that, when executed, configure the one or more processors to perform operations comprising:", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent describes a memory and storage of programming instructions, but those instructions are directed to conversational\u2011agent analysis functions, not to configuring processors for language\u2011model interaction with reaction tokens. Therefore the element is not disclosed and is non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "receiving, through a user interface, a user message to a language model", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art focuses on receiving transcript data for analysis, not on a user interface that sends a message to a language model. No teaching of a UI\u2011driven message to a language model is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "generating an input for the language model based on the message, the input comprising a prompt for a reaction token associated with the user message", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose the concept of a \"reaction token\" or generating a prompt specifically for such a token. Its processing relates to extracting conversational indicators, not to token\u2011based prompting. Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "in response to the input, generating a response comprising the reaction token", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No teaching in the prior art of a language model emitting a reaction token as part of its response. The prior art generates analytical indicators, not reaction tokens, making this element novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "rendering the reaction token in the user interface as a model reaction to the user message", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art does not describe displaying a reaction token in a UI as a model reaction. Its display functions are limited to showing numerical quality indicators. Therefore this element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014introducing reaction tokens that are generated by a language model and rendered in a user interface as model reactions\u2014is not disclosed in any form by the target patent's independent claim or specification. Each claim element is novel and non\u2011obvious over the prior art, leading to a overall assessment that the source patent retains validity, particularly with respect to its primary inventive contribution."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "813739ca-9d65-4de2-a5ac-3dfcc1baf7e8", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:57:40.666998+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US10523879B2", "independent_claim": "1. An electronic device, comprising:\none or more cameras;\na display apparatus;\none or more processors; and\nmemory storing one or more programs configured to be executed by the one or more processors, the one or more programs including instructions for:\ndisplaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject; and\ndisplaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes:\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject; and\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in a region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar;\nwherein the representation of the virtual avatar includes a representation of a second portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed over a second portion of the representation of the subject without regard to whether or not the depth data indicate that the second portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of or behind a corresponding second portion of the representation of the subject;\ndetecting a change in pose of the subject; and\nin response to detecting the change in pose of the subject, modifying the displayed representation of the virtual avatar based on the change in pose, including:\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies the set of depth-based display criteria, updating an appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a first appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a second appearance that includes the first portion of the virtual avatar; and\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria, updating the appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a third appearance that includes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a fourth appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar.", "specification": "An electronic device is described. The electronic device comprises: a camera; and a display apparatus; means for displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject; means for displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes: means in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, for including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject; and means in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, for excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in the region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20210096703A1", "independent_claim": "1. An electronic device, comprising:\na display;\none or more processors; and\nmemory storing one or more programs configured to be executed by the one or more processors, the one or more programs including instructions for:\nwhile participating in a live communication session with one or more participants, displaying a media playback user interface that includes a representation of video media being played, wherein playback of the video media at the electronic device is synchronized with playing of the video media on one or more other devices of the one or more participants;\nwhile displaying the representation of the video media being played, receiving data that corresponds to a reaction from a first participant of the one or more participants; and\nin response to receiving the data that corresponds to the reaction from the first participant of the one or more participants and while the video media is being played on both the electronic device and the one or more other devices of the one or more participants, concurrently playing both the video media and the reaction from the first participant of the one or more participants, wherein changes in the reaction over time are played while the video media is concurrently playing.", "specification": "At FIG. 6R, in response to device 602 receiving tap input 604 j on video affordance 616 a, device 602 replaces display of conversation log 608 with avatars 618 a, 620 a, and 622 a of one or more participants of the conversation. In some examples, device 602 displays an animation that transitions the avatars 618 a, 620 b, and 620 c displayed above the conversation log to a grid that replaces the conversation log by enlarging the avatars, moving the avatars (e.g., down on the display), and changing the shapes (e.g., from round to rectangular) of the avatars. Device 602 also displays avatar 624 a of the user of device 602. Avatar 618 a of participant Nick is a 3D computer-generated image. Indication 618 b indicates that participant Nick is transmitting live audio. Avatar 620 a of participant Lauren is a static image accessed from an address book of device 602. Participant Lauren is not transmitting a live media stream (no live audio and no live video). Avatar 622 a of participant Giancarlo is a set of initials for the participant. Thought bubble 622 b indicates that participant Giancarlo is typing a draft message in the conversation. Avatar 624 a of the user of device 602 (Freddy) is a live video captured using a camera of device 602, such as a forward facing camera, which is the same live video device 602 is transmitting in the live media stream. Full-screen affordance 624 b, when activated, causes device 602 to enlarge avatar 624 a and, optionally, to replace display of avatars 618 a, 620 a, and 622 a. Camera affordance 624 c, when activated, causes device 602 to change the source for the user's live video stream from a first camera sensor (e.g., the forward facing camera) to a second camera sensor (e.g., a rear facing camera). Affordance 624 d, when activated, cause device 602 to transition between transmitting live audio without live video and live audio with live video.\n[0221]\nAt FIG. 6R, device 652 begins receiving live video corresponding to participant Freddy. In response to receiving live video corresponding to participant Freddy, device 652 updates avatar 668 a of participant Freddy to include the live video corresponding to participant Freddy. As illustrated in FIG. 6R, device 652 displays a ring 668 b around avatar 668 a. In some examples, displaying ring 668 b indicates that participant Freddy is transmitting a live media stream. In some examples, a characteristic (e.g., a color, a thickness, an animation) of ring 668 b is indicative of whether participant Freddy is currently transmitting a live media stream to participants of the communication session. In some examples, the size (and/or color, and/or shape) of ring 668 b varies in accordance with a volume (or frequency) of audio received from participant Freddy.\n[0222]\nAt FIG. 6S, participant Lauren has started transmitting live video and, as a result, device 602 updates avatar 620 a of participant Lauren to include the live video being received from participant Lauren. Further, device 620 a displays caption affordance 620 b with avatar 620 a based on receiving live audio from participant Lauren. Device 602 has received a textual message from participant Giancarlo and, as a result, device 602 displays the message in speech bubble 622 c with avatar 622 a of participant Giancarlo. As illustrated in FIG. 6S, device 602 a receives tap input 604 k on caption affordance 620 b. In response to receiving tap input 604 k on caption affordance 620 b, device 602 begins to display, such as at FIG. 6U, captions 620 c for audio transmitted by participant Lauren. The captions are optionally transcribed at device 602 a or at a remote server.\n[0223]\nAt FIG. 6S, participant Lauren has started transmitting live video and, as a result, device 652 updates avatar 670 a of participant Lauren to include the live video being received from participant Lauren.\n[0224]\nAs illustrated in FIGS. 6T-6X, device 652 receives downward swipe user input 656 c starting at a location adjacent to the top of the display (e.g., adjacent to avatars 668 a, 670 a, and 672 a, on a handle). Device 652 continues to receive the swipe user input 656 c as it progresses toward the bottom of the display without detecting liftoff of the swipe user input 656 c. As the swipe user input 656 c progresses (with handle 662), device 652 transitions display of the avatars 668 a, 670 a, and 672 a of the participants by concurrently gradually enlarging sizes of the avatars 668 a, 670 a, and 672 a, changing the shapes of the displayed avatars (e.g., from circular in FIG. 6T to rectangular in FIG. 6W), and changing locations of the displayed avatars 668 a, 670 a, and 672 a (e.g., from a 1-by-3 grid at the top of the display to a 2-by-2 grid).\n[0225]\nAt FIG. 6V, device 602 receives tap input 604 l on camera affordance 624 c. As illustrated in FIGS. 6V and 6W, in response to receiving tap input 604 l on camera affordance 624 c, device 602 transitions the live video of avatar 624 a from a first camera (e.g., a forward facing camera) to a second camera (e.g., a rear facing camera) of device 602.\n[0226]\nAt FIG. 6X, device 602 receives tap input 604 m on full-screen affordance 624 b. As illustrated in FIG. 6Y, in response to receiving tap input 604 m on full-screen affordance 624 b, device 602 enlarges the avatar 624 a on display of device 602, such as by using a smooth animation. At FIG. 6Y, avatar 674 a represents the participant Nick of device 652. Device 652 receives tap input 656 d on affordance 674 b. In response to receiving tap input 656 d, device 652 transitions from transmitting live audio without live video to transmitting live audio with live video (e.g., by beginning to capture live video at device 652 using a front-facing camera sensor), as illustrated by avatar 674 a including live video in FIG. 6Z."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US10523879B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. An electronic device, comprising:\none or more cameras;\na display apparatus;\none or more processors; and\nmemory storing one or more programs configured to be executed by the one or more processors, the one or more programs including instructions for:\ndisplaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject; and\ndisplaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes:\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject; and\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in a region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar;\nwherein the representation of the virtual avatar includes a representation of a second portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed over a second portion of the representation of the subject without regard to whether or not the depth data indicate that the second portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of or behind a corresponding second portion of the representation of the subject;\ndetecting a change in pose of the subject; and\nin response to detecting the change in pose of the subject, modifying the displayed representation of the virtual avatar based on the change in pose, including:\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies the set of depth-based display criteria, updating an appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a first appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a second appearance that includes the first portion of the virtual avatar; and\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria, updating the appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a third appearance that includes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a fourth appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nAn electronic device is described. The electronic device comprises: a camera; and a display apparatus; means for displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject; means for displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes: means in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, for including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject; and means in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, for excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in the region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20210096703A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. An electronic device, comprising:\na display;\none or more processors; and\nmemory storing one or more programs configured to be executed by the one or more processors, the one or more programs including instructions for:\nwhile participating in a live communication session with one or more participants, displaying a media playback user interface that includes a representation of video media being played, wherein playback of the video media at the electronic device is synchronized with playing of the video media on one or more other devices of the one or more participants;\nwhile displaying the representation of the video media being played, receiving data that corresponds to a reaction from a first participant of the one or more participants; and\nin response to receiving the data that corresponds to the reaction from the first participant of the one or more participants and while the video media is being played on both the electronic device and the one or more other devices of the one or more participants, concurrently playing both the video media and the reaction from the first participant of the one or more participants, wherein changes in the reaction over time are played while the video media is concurrently playing.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n6R, in response to device 602 receiving tap input 604 j on video affordance 616 a, device 602 replaces display of conversation log 608 with avatars 618 a, 620 a, and 622 a of one or more participants of the conversation. In some examples, device 602 displays an animation that transitions the avatars 618 a, 620 b, and 620 c displayed above the conversation log to a grid that replaces the conversation log by enlarging the avatars, moving the avatars (e.g., down on the display), and changing the shapes (e.g., from round to rectangular) of the avatars. Device 602 also displays avatar 624 a of the user of device 602. Avatar 618 a of participant Nick is a 3D computer-generated image. Indication 618 b indicates that participant Nick is transmitting live audio. Avatar 620 a of participant Lauren is a static image accessed from an address book of device 602. Participant Lauren is not transmitting a live media stream (no live audio and no live video). Avatar 622 a of participant Giancarlo is a set of initials for the participant. Thought bubble 622 b indicates that participant Giancarlo is typing a draft message in the conversation. Avatar 624 a of the user of device 602 (Freddy) is a live video captured using a camera of device 602, such as a forward facing camera, which is the same live video device 602 is transmitting in the live media stream. Full-screen affordance 624 b, when activated, causes device 602 to enlarge avatar 624 a and, optionally, to replace display of avatars 618 a, 620 a, and 622 a. Camera affordance 624 c, when activated, causes device 602 to change the source for the user's live video stream from a first camera sensor (e.g., the forward facing camera) to a second camera sensor (e.g., a rear facing camera). Affordance 624 d, when activated, cause device 602 to transition between transmitting live audio without live video and live audio with live video.\n[0221]\nAt FIG. 6R, device 652 begins receiving live video corresponding to participant Freddy. In response to receiving live video corresponding to participant Freddy, device 652 updates avatar 668 a of participant Freddy to include the live video corresponding to participant Freddy. 6R, device 652 displays a ring 668 b around avatar 668 a. In some examples, displaying ring 668 b indicates that participant Freddy is transmitting a live media stream. In some examples, a characteristic (e.g., a color, a thickness, an animation) of ring 668 b is indicative of whether participant Freddy is currently transmitting a live media stream to participants of the communication session. In some examples, the size (and/or color, and/or shape) of ring 668 b varies in accordance with a volume (or frequency) of audio received from participant Freddy.\n[0222]\nAt FIG. 6S, participant Lauren has started transmitting live video and, as a result, device 602 updates avatar 620 a of participant Lauren to include the live video being received from participant Lauren. Further, device 620 a displays caption affordance 620 b with avatar 620 a based on receiving live audio from participant Lauren. Device 602 has received a textual message from participant Giancarlo and, as a result, device 602 displays the message in speech bubble 622 c with avatar 622 a of participant Giancarlo. 6S, device 602 a receives tap input 604 k on caption affordance 620 b. In response to receiving tap input 604 k on caption affordance 620 b, device 602 begins to display, such as at FIG. 6U, captions 620 c for audio transmitted by participant Lauren. The captions are optionally transcribed at device 602 a or at a remote server.\n[0223]\nAt FIG. 6S, participant Lauren has started transmitting live video and, as a result, device 652 updates avatar 670 a of participant Lauren to include the live video being received from participant Lauren.\n[0224]\nAs illustrated in FIGS. 6T-6X, device 652 receives downward swipe user input 656 c starting at a location adjacent to the top of the display (e.g., adjacent to avatars 668 a, 670 a, and 672 a, on a handle). Device 652 continues to receive the swipe user input 656 c as it progresses toward the bottom of the display without detecting liftoff of the swipe user input 656 c. As the swipe user input 656 c progresses (with handle 662), device 652 transitions display of the avatars 668 a, 670 a, and 672 a of the participants by concurrently gradually enlarging sizes of the avatars 668 a, 670 a, and 672 a, changing the shapes of the displayed avatars (e.g., from circular in FIG. 6W), and changing locations of the displayed avatars 668 a, 670 a, and 672 a (e.g., from a 1-by-3 grid at the top of the display to a 2-by-2 grid).\n[0225]\nAt FIG. 6V, device 602 receives tap input 604 l on camera affordance 624 c. 6V and 6W, in response to receiving tap input 604 l on camera affordance 624 c, device 602 transitions the live video of avatar 624 a from a first camera (e.g., a forward facing camera) to a second camera (e.g., a rear facing camera) of device 602.\n[0226]\nAt FIG. 6X, device 602 receives tap input 604 m on full-screen affordance 624 b. 6Y, in response to receiving tap input 604 m on full-screen affordance 624 b, device 602 enlarges the avatar 624 a on display of device 602, such as by using a smooth animation. 6Y, avatar 674 a represents the participant Nick of device 652. Device 652 receives tap input 656 d on affordance 674 b. In response to receiving tap input 656 d, device 652 transitions from transmitting live audio without live video to transmitting live audio with live video (e.g., by beginning to capture live video at device 652 using a front-facing camera sensor), as illustrated by avatar 674 a including live video in FIG."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"one or more cameras\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"live video captured using a camera of device 602\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target specification discloses a camera used to capture live video (\\\"live video captured using a camera of device 602\\\"). Therefore the element is not novel (Y) and, being a standard camera component, is obvious (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a display apparatus\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"a display\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The independent claim of the target patent recites \\\"a display\\\". The element is disclosed, thus not novel (Y) and is a routine component, making it obvious (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"one or more processors\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"one or more processors\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim includes \\\"one or more processors\\\". The element lacks novelty (Y) and is an ordinary element, so it is obvious (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing one or more programs configured to be executed by the one or more processors, the one or more programs including instructions for:\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"memory storing one or more programs\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim recites \\\"memory storing one or more programs\\\". The element is disclosed, thus not novel (Y) and is a standard computing element, making it obvious (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose depth data associated with captured image data nor a representation that includes such depth information. Therefore the element is novel (N) and not obvious (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes:\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discusses avatars but does not teach placing an avatar at a simulated depth based on depth data. Hence the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No teaching in the target patent of depth\u2011based display criteria or conditional inclusion of avatar portions based on depth, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in a region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent lacks any mechanism for excluding avatar portions when depth criteria are not met; thus the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"wherein the representation of the virtual avatar includes a representation of a second portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed over a second portion of the representation of the subject without regard to whether or not the depth data indicate that the second portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of or behind a corresponding second portion of the representation of the subject;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose selective depth\u2011agnostic overlay of avatar portions; therefore the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 10,\n      \"element_text\": \"detecting a change in pose of the subject;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not teach detection of subject pose changes; the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 11,\n      \"element_text\": \"in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies the set of depth-based display criteria, updating an appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a first appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3760, "tokens_output": 4096, "latency_ms": 9220}, "parsed_output": null, "status": "parse_error", "error": "Failed to parse LLM response as JSON: Unterminated string starting at: line 95 column 23 (char 6132)", "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "df93fde1-9b9c-4145-977a-6e124555d9de", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:58:37.316146+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US10523879B2", "independent_claim": "1. An electronic device, comprising:\none or more cameras;\na display apparatus;\none or more processors; and\nmemory storing one or more programs configured to be executed by the one or more processors, the one or more programs including instructions for:\ndisplaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject; and\ndisplaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes:\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject; and\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in a region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar;\nwherein the representation of the virtual avatar includes a representation of a second portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed over a second portion of the representation of the subject without regard to whether or not the depth data indicate that the second portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of or behind a corresponding second portion of the representation of the subject;\ndetecting a change in pose of the subject; and\nin response to detecting the change in pose of the subject, modifying the displayed representation of the virtual avatar based on the change in pose, including:\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies the set of depth-based display criteria, updating an appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a first appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a second appearance that includes the first portion of the virtual avatar; and\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria, updating the appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a third appearance that includes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a fourth appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar.", "specification": "An electronic device is described. The electronic device comprises: a camera; and a display apparatus; means for displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject; means for displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes: means in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, for including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject; and means in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, for excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in the region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20210065454A1", "independent_claim": "1. A system comprising:\na processor; and\na memory including instructions that, when executed by the processor, cause the processor to perform operations comprising:\nreceiving, using a processor, image data and depth data;\nselecting, using the processor, an augmented reality content generator corresponding to a three-dimensional (3D) effect;\napplying, using the processor, the 3D effect to the image data and the depth data based at least in part on the selected augmented reality content generator; and\ngenerating, using a processor, a message including information related to the applied 3D effect, the image data, and the depth data.", "specification": "0142]\nFIG. 8 is a flowchart illustrating a method 800 to generate a 3D message, according to some example embodiments. The method 800 may be embodied in computer-readable instructions for execution by one or more computer processors such that the operations of the method 800 may be performed in part or in whole by the messaging client application 104, particularly with respect to respective components of the annotation system 206 described above in FIG. 7; accordingly, the method 800 is described below by way of example with reference thereto. However, it shall be appreciated that at least some of the operations of the method 800 may be deployed on various other hardware configurations and the method 800 is not intended to be limited to the messaging client application 104.\n[0143]\nAt operation 802, the image and depth data receiving module 702 receives image data and depth data captured by an optical sensor (e.g., camera) of the client device 102. In an example, to create a 3D message, the user selects a 3D message camera mode in the messaging client application 104, which causes the camera to capture raw depth data and a portrait segmentation mask along with the camera image.\n[0144]\nAt operation 804, the 3D effects module 708 selects a 3D effect. In an example, the 3D effect may be selected based on a user input corresponding to a selection of a 3D augmented reality content generator as provided, for example, in a user interface of the messaging client application 104.\n[0145]\nAt operation 806, the 3D effects module 708 applies the selected 3D effect to the image data and/or the depth data. In an example, the selected 3D effect includes logic to enable processing the image data and/or depth data.\n[0146]\nAt operation 808, the rendering module 710 renders a view of a 3D message using the applied 3D effect. In an example, the rendering module provides the view of the 3D message based on the applied 3D effect, which is displayed by the messaging client application 104. As described further herein, the viewer of the 3D message can provide additional inputs (e.g., movement data and/or touch inputs) which causes the 3D message to be updated and re-rendered in response to such inputs.\n[0147]\nAt operation 810, the sharing module 712 generates a 3D message including 3D effect data. As discussed before, the 3D message may include the information described in FIG. 6 and/or FIG. 4, which enables the 3D message to be reconstructed and rendered by a viewer of the 3D message upon receipt of the 3D message.\n[0148]\nAt operation 812, the sharing module 712 stores at or sends the generated 3D message to the messaging server system 108. In an example, the messaging client application 104 sends the 3D message to the messaging server system 108, which enables the 3D message to be stored and/or viewed at a later time by a particular recipient or viewer of the 3D message.\n[0149]\nIn an embodiment, in a scenario where a given 3D message is received (e.g., by a sender client device sharing the 3D message to a receiver client device), similar operations described in operation 802 to operation 808 may be performed in order to render a view of the received 3D message (e.g., thereby foregoing the operation 810 and operation 812 to generate the 3D message and/or store or send the 3D message).\n[0150]\nThe following discussion relates to example embodiments for sharing 3D messages and/or storing such 3D messages in persistent storage (e.g., the database 120).\n[0151]\nIn an embodiment, a user can initiate (e.g., by selecting a command provided in a user interface of the messaging client application 104) a process for storing the 3D message to the database 120 of the messaging server system 108. In this example, the image and depth data are stored, as well as information as described before in FIG. 6 (e.g., a 3D augmented reality content generator to load with the 3D message)."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US10523879B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. An electronic device, comprising:\none or more cameras;\na display apparatus;\none or more processors; and\nmemory storing one or more programs configured to be executed by the one or more processors, the one or more programs including instructions for:\ndisplaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject; and\ndisplaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes:\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject; and\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in a region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar;\nwherein the representation of the virtual avatar includes a representation of a second portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed over a second portion of the representation of the subject without regard to whether or not the depth data indicate that the second portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of or behind a corresponding second portion of the representation of the subject;\ndetecting a change in pose of the subject; and\nin response to detecting the change in pose of the subject, modifying the displayed representation of the virtual avatar based on the change in pose, including:\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies the set of depth-based display criteria, updating an appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a first appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a second appearance that includes the first portion of the virtual avatar; and\nin accordance with a determination, based on the depth data that takes into account the change in pose of the subject, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria, updating the appearance of the representation of the virtual avatar from a third appearance that includes the first portion of the virtual avatar to a fourth appearance that excludes the first portion of the virtual avatar.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nAn electronic device is described. The electronic device comprises: a camera; and a display apparatus; means for displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject; means for displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes: means in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, for including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject; and means in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, for excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in the region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20210065454A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A system comprising:\na processor; and\na memory including instructions that, when executed by the processor, cause the processor to perform operations comprising:\nreceiving, using a processor, image data and depth data;\nselecting, using the processor, an augmented reality content generator corresponding to a three-dimensional (3D) effect;\napplying, using the processor, the 3D effect to the image data and the depth data based at least in part on the selected augmented reality content generator; and\ngenerating, using a processor, a message including information related to the applied 3D effect, the image data, and the depth data.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n8 is a flowchart illustrating a method 800 to generate a 3D message, according to some example embodiments. The method 800 may be embodied in computer-readable instructions for execution by one or more computer processors such that the operations of the method 800 may be performed in part or in whole by the messaging client application 104, particularly with respect to respective components of the annotation system 206 described above in FIG. However, it shall be appreciated that at least some of the operations of the method 800 may be deployed on various other hardware configurations and the method 800 is not intended to be limited to the messaging client application 104.\n[0143]\nAt operation 802, the image and depth data receiving module 702 receives image data and depth data captured by an optical sensor (e.g., camera) of the client device 102. In an example, to create a 3D message, the user selects a 3D message camera mode in the messaging client application 104, which causes the camera to capture raw depth data and a portrait segmentation mask along with the camera image.\n[0144]\nAt operation 804, the 3D effects module 708 selects a 3D effect. In an example, the 3D effect may be selected based on a user input corresponding to a selection of a 3D augmented reality content generator as provided, for example, in a user interface of the messaging client application 104.\n[0145]\nAt operation 806, the 3D effects module 708 applies the selected 3D effect to the image data and/or the depth data. In an example, the selected 3D effect includes logic to enable processing the image data and/or depth data.\n[0146]\nAt operation 808, the rendering module 710 renders a view of a 3D message using the applied 3D effect. In an example, the rendering module provides the view of the 3D message based on the applied 3D effect, which is displayed by the messaging client application 104. As described further herein, the viewer of the 3D message can provide additional inputs (e.g., movement data and/or touch inputs) which causes the 3D message to be updated and re-rendered in response to such inputs.\n[0147]\nAt operation 810, the sharing module 712 generates a 3D message including 3D effect data. As discussed before, the 3D message may include the information described in FIG. 4, which enables the 3D message to be reconstructed and rendered by a viewer of the 3D message upon receipt of the 3D message.\n[0148]\nAt operation 812, the sharing module 712 stores at or sends the generated 3D message to the messaging server system 108. In an example, the messaging client application 104 sends the 3D message to the messaging server system 108, which enables the 3D message to be stored and/or viewed at a later time by a particular recipient or viewer of the 3D message.\n[0149]\nIn an embodiment, in a scenario where a given 3D message is received (e.g., by a sender client device sharing the 3D message to a receiver client device), similar operations described in operation 802 to operation 808 may be performed in order to render a view of the received 3D message (e.g., thereby foregoing the operation 810 and operation 812 to generate the 3D message and/or store or send the 3D message).\n[0150]\nThe following discussion relates to example embodiments for sharing 3D messages and/or storing such 3D messages in persistent storage (e.g., the database 120).\n[0151]\nIn an embodiment, a user can initiate (e.g., by selecting a command provided in a user interface of the messaging client application 104) a process for storing the 3D message to the database 120 of the messaging server system 108. In this example, the image and depth data are stored, as well as information as described before in FIG. 6 (e.g., a 3D augmented reality content generator to load with the 3D message)."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"one or more cameras\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"image and depth data captured by an optical sensor (e.g., camera)\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s specification explicitly mentions image and depth data captured by an optical sensor (e.g., camera), disclosing the presence of a camera. Since this feature is disclosed, it lacks novelty and is considered obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a display apparatus\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"which is displayed by the messaging client application\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The specification describes that the rendered view is displayed by the messaging client application, thereby disclosing a display apparatus. The feature is known and thus not novel nor inventive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"one or more processors\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"a processor\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The independent claim of the target patent recites a processor, directly covering this element. It is standard hardware and therefore lacks novelty and inventive step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing one or more programs configured to be executed by the one or more processors, the one or more programs including instructions for:\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"a memory including instructions\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim includes a memory with instructions, which matches the disclosed memory storing executable programs. This is routine and thus not novel or inventive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of image data captured via the one or more cameras, wherein the representation includes a representation of a subject and the image data corresponds to depth data that includes depth data for the subject;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art does not disclose displaying a subject representation together with its depth data; it only mentions receiving and rendering a 3D message. Therefore the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"displaying, via the display apparatus, a representation of a virtual avatar that is displayed in place of at least a portion of the representation of the subject, wherein the virtual avatar is placed at simulated depth relative to the representation of the subject as determined based on the depth data for the subject, displaying the representation of the virtual avatar includes:\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches applying a generic 3D effect but does not specifically disclose a virtual avatar replacing part of a subject with depth\u2011based placement. This specific functionality is absent, making it novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that a first portion of the virtual avatar satisfies a set of depth-based display criteria, wherein the depth-based display criteria include a requirement that the depth data for the subject indicate that the first portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of a corresponding first portion of the subject, in order for the depth-based display criteria to be met, including as part of the representation of the virtual avatar, a representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed in place of the first portion of the subject;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No depth\u2011based criteria for selectively rendering avatar portions are taught in the prior art; the claim introduces a conditional rendering rule that is not disclosed, thus novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"in accordance with a determination, based on the depth data, that the first portion of the virtual avatar does not satisfy the set of depth-based display criteria for the first portion of the subject, excluding, from the representation of the virtual avatar, the representation of the first portion of the virtual avatar and displaying the first portion of the subject in a region that would have been occupied by the first portion of the virtual avatar;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art lacks any mechanism for omitting avatar portions when depth criteria fail; this exclusion behavior is not taught, rendering the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"wherein the representation of the virtual avatar includes a representation of a second portion of the virtual avatar that is displayed over a second portion of the representation of the subject without regard to whether or not the depth data indicate that the second portion of the virtual avatar has a simulated depth that is in front of or behind a corresponding second portion of the representation of the subject;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Displaying a second avatar portion irrespective", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3195, "tokens_output": 4096, "latency_ms": 20855}, "parsed_output": null, "status": "parse_error", "error": "Failed to parse LLM response as JSON: Unterminated string starting at: line 82 column 18 (char 5813)", "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "2b5e421d-d71b-4c66-a7f5-a376d3fd6c7d", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T17:59:30.656458+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US11756539B2", "independent_claim": "1. An electronic apparatus comprising:\na communicator;\na display;\na user input unit configured to receive a user voice input; and\na processor configured to:\nreceive the user voice input corresponding to a complex sentence including a plurality of sentences relating to a plurality of functions of the electronic apparatus,\nreceive, through the communicator, data corresponding to the user voice input from a server, the data including function information corresponding to whether the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences are sequentially performable as an order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence,\nbased on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, perform the plurality of functions as the order of the plurality of sentences, and\nbased on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being not sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, according to the function information, control the display to display information indicating that the plurality of functions is not performable.", "specification": "The natural language processing apparatus 120 rapidly estimates a boundary between two sentences of an input sentence received in a short or compound form sentence from the user device 100, in more detail, a compound form sentence, and uses a simplified machine language for this. Here, the simplified machine language may be a language that is internally used to rapidly change an input compound sentence into divided short sentence forms. This will be described later in more detail. For example, if the natural language processing apparatus 120 receives a compound sentence \u201cWho starred in Harry Potter and turn on KBS\u201d and a sentence \u201cWho starred in Gone with the Wind and turn on KBS\u201d from the user, the natural language processing apparatus 120 determines that \u201cHarry Potter\u201d and \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d belong to a named entity category (or a named entity attribute, a named entity type, or the like) named movie through learning, equally changes (or replaces or translates) \u201cHarry Potter\u201d and \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d into a form \u201cWho starred in @movie\u201d, rapidly applies a statistical translation technique based on this, and divide and generate a plurality of short sentences. Here, the named entity category refers to a category to which named entity words of a sentence belong. A named entity, i.e., a name of a word, may be \u201cHarry Potter\u201d or \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d as a movie name, \u201cOCN\u201d as a channel name, \u201c*Bama\u201d, or the like as a character. A category to which this named entity belongs may be classified into a movie, a channel name, a character, etc. However, a category of the named entity \u201cHarry Potter\u201d may be a character in another sentence.\nHere, when various forms of compound sentences, in more detail, a compound sentence on which a named entity category is tagged, is referred to as A, the statistical (machine) translation technique outputs a compound sentence B trained (or learned) for A. In other words, as a processing result of sentence A, sentence B of a form where several short sentences are connected to one another by a particular identifier is generated. For example, a form, such as \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d into which a sentence classification symbol is inserted, may be an identifier of two short sentences of the compound sentence B. Based on this sentence classification symbol, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may estimate a boundary between two short sentences, generate two short sentences B \u201cRecord Harry Potter\u201d and \u201cShow me Gone with the Wind\u201d based on the boundary, provide the two short sentences B to the user device 100 or change the two short sentences B into another language, and provide the changed two short sentences B to the user device 100. Alternatively, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may translate two short sentences into another language, connect the translated two short sentences, and provide the connected two short sentences to the user device 100. Here, an identifier has been described as a symbol but may be a bit information form. Therefore, the identifier may not be particularly limited. Also, when considering that an ungrammatical change or a word order change may occur according to a translation result, \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d may be changed into \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d. Therefore, the present exemplary embodiment may not be particularly limited to an output form of the above result.\nFor example, if a sentence is provided in a text form through a search window of the user device 100, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may use the corresponding text sentence as it is. However, if a voice is uttered so as to provide a voice signal, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may acquire and use a corresponding voice sentence as a text-based recognition result by using an internal free utterance engine. If the user device 100 recognizes this voice sentence and provides a text-based result, this process may be omitted. Therefore, the present exemplary embodiment may not limit a form of a sentence that the natural language processing apparatus 120 receives from the user and forms of two divided short sentences provided to the user device 100.\nIn more detail, the natural language processing apparatus 120 according to the present exemplary embodiment may pre-store named entity corpus information for determining a category of a named entity of an input sentence, i.e., an attribute of the named entity. For example, \u201cHarry Potter\u201d may be a movie in an input sentence but may be referred to as a person. Therefore, the natural language processing apparatus 120 rapidly classifies a category of a named entity from a corresponding input sentence by performing training and learning based on stored named entity corpus information. In other words, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may learn a named entity recognition model based on the named entity corpus information and rapidly automatically find named entity categories of various input short or compound sentences based on the learned named entity recognition model. This process is a named entity recognition process."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20140188486A1", "independent_claim": "1. A display apparatus comprising:\na voice receiver configured to collect a user's utterance;\na communicator configured to transmit the user's utterance to a dialogue type server, and to receive response information generated based on the user's utterance;\na storage unit configured to store control information corresponding to each user's utterance; and\na controller configured to determine whether the control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is stored in the storage unit, and to perform operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on a result of the determination,\nwherein the controller performs operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the stored control information, when the control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is stored in the storage unit, and performs operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server, when the control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is not stored in the storage unit.", "specification": "[0039]\nBy another example, when the user's utterance for providing contents information is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200 and determines whether control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is pre-stored. As a result of the determination, when the control information corresponding to the user's utterance is not pre-stored, the display apparatus 100 may perform functions corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. That is, the display apparatus may output a response message corresponding to the user's utterance as at least one of an image and a voice based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200.\n[0040]\nBy another example, when the user's utterance for controlling an external device 300 is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200 and determines whether control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is pre-stored. As a result of the determination, when the control information corresponding to the user's utterance is not pre-stored, the display apparatus 100 may perform a function corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. That is, the display apparatus 100 may control functions of the external device 300 based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. Here, the external device 300 may be an electronic apparatus (e.g., a DVD player, a set top box, a sound device, and a game device, etc.) which is connected to the display apparatus 100.\n[0041]\nThe display apparatus 100 in a dialogue system according to the exemplary embodiment may perform functions corresponding to the user's intention based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200, and may perform functions corresponding to the user's intention based on the pre-stored control information corresponding to the user's utterance. Therefore, the display apparatus 100 may not only minimize errors in performing functions corresponding to the user's utterance, but also perform functions corresponding to the user's utterance at a fast processing speed.\n[0042]\nThe dialogue type server 200 which generates response information based on the received user's utterance analyzes the meaning of the user's utterance received from the display apparatus 100, generates response information for controlling the operations of the display apparatus 100, and transmits the response information to the display apparatus 100. For example, when the user's utterance is received from the display apparatus 100, the dialogue type server 200 may extract an utterance element from the received user's utterance, generate response information related to the user's utterance based on the extracted utterance element, and transmit the generated response information to the display apparatus 100. The utterance element may be a keyword for performing operations requested by the user in the user's utterance. For example, in a case of the user's utterance of \u201cwhat time does OOO (e.g., a broadcast program title) start this Saturday?\u201d, the utterance element may be \u201cthis\u201d, \u201cSaturday\u201d, \u201cOOO (broadcast program title)\u201d, \u201cwhat time\u201d, and \u201cstarts\u201d, etc.\n[0043]\nWhen the utterance element is extracted, the dialogue type server 200 generates response information for performing operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the extracted utterance element and transmits the response information to the display apparatus 100.\n[0044]\nFor example, the display apparatus 100 may collect the user's utterance related to an EPG (Electronic Program Guide) of \u201cwhat time does OOO (broadcast program title) start this Saturday?\u201d When the user's utterance is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200. If the user's utterance is an analogue signal, the display apparatus 100 may convert the collected user utterance into a digital signal, and transmit the converted user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200. When the user's utterance converted into the digital signal is received, the dialogue type server 200 generates text information based on the converted user's utterance, analyzes the generated text information, and generates response information corresponding to the user's utterance.\n"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US11756539B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. An electronic apparatus comprising:\na communicator;\na display;\na user input unit configured to receive a user voice input; and\na processor configured to:\nreceive the user voice input corresponding to a complex sentence including a plurality of sentences relating to a plurality of functions of the electronic apparatus,\nreceive, through the communicator, data corresponding to the user voice input from a server, the data including function information corresponding to whether the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences are sequentially performable as an order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence,\nbased on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, perform the plurality of functions as the order of the plurality of sentences, and\nbased on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being not sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, according to the function information, control the display to display information indicating that the plurality of functions is not performable.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe natural language processing apparatus 120 rapidly estimates a boundary between two sentences of an input sentence received in a short or compound form sentence from the user device 100, in more detail, a compound form sentence, and uses a simplified machine language for this. Here, the simplified machine language may be a language that is internally used to rapidly change an input compound sentence into divided short sentence forms. For example, if the natural language processing apparatus 120 receives a compound sentence \u201cWho starred in Harry Potter and turn on KBS\u201d and a sentence \u201cWho starred in Gone with the Wind and turn on KBS\u201d from the user, the natural language processing apparatus 120 determines that \u201cHarry Potter\u201d and \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d belong to a named entity category (or a named entity attribute, a named entity type, or the like) named movie through learning, equally changes (or replaces or translates) \u201cHarry Potter\u201d and \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d into a form \u201cWho starred in @movie\u201d, rapidly applies a statistical translation technique based on this, and divide and generate a plurality of short sentences. Here, the named entity category refers to a category to which named entity words of a sentence belong. However, a category of the named entity \u201cHarry Potter\u201d may be a character in another sentence.\nHere, when various forms of compound sentences, in more detail, a compound sentence on which a named entity category is tagged, is referred to as A, the statistical (machine) translation technique outputs a compound sentence B trained (or learned) for A. In other words, as a processing result of sentence A, sentence B of a form where several short sentences are connected to one another by a particular identifier is generated. For example, a form, such as \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d into which a sentence classification symbol is inserted, may be an identifier of two short sentences of the compound sentence B. Based on this sentence classification symbol, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may estimate a boundary between two short sentences, generate two short sentences B \u201cRecord Harry Potter\u201d and \u201cShow me Gone with the Wind\u201d based on the boundary, provide the two short sentences B to the user device 100 or change the two short sentences B into another language, and provide the changed two short sentences B to the user device 100. Alternatively, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may translate two short sentences into another language, connect the translated two short sentences, and provide the connected two short sentences to the user device 100. Here, an identifier has been described as a symbol but may be a bit information form. Also, when considering that an ungrammatical change or a word order change may occur according to a translation result, \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d may be changed into \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d. Therefore, the present exemplary embodiment may not be particularly limited to an output form of the above result.\nFor example, if a sentence is provided in a text form through a search window of the user device 100, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may use the corresponding text sentence as it is. However, if a voice is uttered so as to provide a voice signal, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may acquire and use a corresponding voice sentence as a text-based recognition result by using an internal free utterance engine. If the user device 100 recognizes this voice sentence and provides a text-based result, this process may be omitted. Therefore, the present exemplary embodiment may not limit a form of a sentence that the natural language processing apparatus 120 receives from the user and forms of two divided short sentences provided to the user device 100.\nIn more detail, the natural language processing apparatus 120 according to the present exemplary embodiment may pre-store named entity corpus information for determining a category of a named entity of an input sentence, i.e., an attribute of the named entity. For example, \u201cHarry Potter\u201d may be a movie in an input sentence but may be referred to as a person. Therefore, the natural language processing apparatus 120 rapidly classifies a category of a named entity from a corresponding input sentence by performing training and learning based on stored named entity corpus information. In other words, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may learn a named entity recognition model based on the named entity corpus information and rapidly automatically find named entity categories of various input short or compound sentences based on the learned named entity recognition model.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20140188486A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A display apparatus comprising:\na voice receiver configured to collect a user's utterance;\na communicator configured to transmit the user's utterance to a dialogue type server, and to receive response information generated based on the user's utterance;\na storage unit configured to store control information corresponding to each user's utterance; and\na controller configured to determine whether the control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is stored in the storage unit, and to perform operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on a result of the determination,\nwherein the controller performs operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the stored control information, when the control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is stored in the storage unit, and performs operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server, when the control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is not stored in the storage unit.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n[0039]\nBy another example, when the user's utterance for providing contents information is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200 and determines whether control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is pre-stored. As a result of the determination, when the control information corresponding to the user's utterance is not pre-stored, the display apparatus 100 may perform functions corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. That is, the display apparatus may output a response message corresponding to the user's utterance as at least one of an image and a voice based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200.\n[0040]\nBy another example, when the user's utterance for controlling an external device 300 is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200 and determines whether control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is pre-stored. As a result of the determination, when the control information corresponding to the user's utterance is not pre-stored, the display apparatus 100 may perform a function corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. That is, the display apparatus 100 may control functions of the external device 300 based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. Here, the external device 300 may be an electronic apparatus (e.g., a DVD player, a set top box, a sound device, and a game device, etc.) which is connected to the display apparatus 100.\n[0041]\nThe display apparatus 100 in a dialogue system according to the exemplary embodiment may perform functions corresponding to the user's intention based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200, and may perform functions corresponding to the user's intention based on the pre-stored control information corresponding to the user's utterance. Therefore, the display apparatus 100 may not only minimize errors in performing functions corresponding to the user's utterance, but also perform functions corresponding to the user's utterance at a fast processing speed.\n[0042]\nThe dialogue type server 200 which generates response information based on the received user's utterance analyzes the meaning of the user's utterance received from the display apparatus 100, generates response information for controlling the operations of the display apparatus 100, and transmits the response information to the display apparatus 100. For example, when the user's utterance is received from the display apparatus 100, the dialogue type server 200 may extract an utterance element from the received user's utterance, generate response information related to the user's utterance based on the extracted utterance element, and transmit the generated response information to the display apparatus 100. The utterance element may be a keyword for performing operations requested by the user in the user's utterance. For example, in a case of the user's utterance of \u201cwhat time does OOO (e.g., a broadcast program title) start this Saturday?\u201d, the utterance element may be \u201cthis\u201d, \u201cSaturday\u201d, \u201cOOO (broadcast program title)\u201d, \u201cwhat time\u201d, and \u201cstarts\u201d, etc.\n[0043]\nWhen the utterance element is extracted, the dialogue type server 200 generates response information for performing operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the extracted utterance element and transmits the response information to the display apparatus 100.\n[0044]\nFor example, the display apparatus 100 may collect the user's utterance related to an EPG (Electronic Program Guide) of \u201cwhat time does OOO (broadcast program title) start this Saturday?\u201d When the user's utterance is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200. If the user's utterance is an analogue signal, the display apparatus 100 may convert the collected user utterance into a digital signal, and transmit the converted user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200. When the user's utterance converted into the digital signal is received, the dialogue type server 200 generates text information based on the converted user's utterance, analyzes the generated text information, and generates response information corresponding to the user's utterance."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"a communicator\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"a communicator configured to transmit the user's utterance to a dialogue type server, and to receive response information generated based on the user's utterance\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent's independent claim explicitly recites a communicator with the same functional configuration, so the element lacks novelty. Using a communicator to exchange utterance data with a server is well\u2011known, making it obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a display\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"A display apparatus comprising\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim is directed to a display apparatus, thereby disclosing a display. A display is a generic component in such systems, so the element is neither novel nor inventive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"a user input unit configured to receive a user voice input\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"a voice receiver configured to collect a user's utterance\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses a voice receiver that collects the user's utterance, matching the source element. Voice\u2011input units are standard in voice\u2011controlled devices, rendering the element obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor configured to receive the user voice input corresponding to a complex sentence including a plurality of sentences relating to a plurality of functions of the electronic apparatus\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art does not disclose handling a \\\"complex sentence\\\" composed of multiple sentences that map to multiple functions. This specific processing of multi\u2011sentence, multi\u2011function input is absent, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor configured to receive, through the communicator, data corresponding to the user voice input from a server, the data including function information corresponding to whether the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences are sequentially performable as an order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent mentions receiving response information but does not teach receiving function information that specifies sequential performability of multiple functions derived from a multi\u2011sentence command. This specialized data exchange is not disclosed, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor configured to, based on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, perform the plurality of functions as the order of the plurality of sentences\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No teaching in the prior art directs a system to execute multiple functions in the exact order derived from a complex, multi\u2011sentence command when they are sequentially performable. This operational logic is absent, rendering the element both novel and inventive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor configured to, based on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being not sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, according to the function information, control the display to display information indicating that the plurality of functions is not performable\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art does not disclose providing a user\u2011visible indication that a set of functions derived from a multi\u2011sentence command cannot be performed sequentially. This feedback mechanism is not taught, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014namely the processing of a complex, multi\u2011sentence voice command to determine sequential performability of multiple functions, executing them in order when possible, and explicitly informing the user when they cannot be performed\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art display apparatus. While generic components such as a communicator, display, and voice receiver are known, the inventive concepts centered on handling multi\u2011sentence commands and the associated decision logic are novel and non\u2011obvious. Consequently, the source patent retains substantive validity.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3377, "tokens_output": 2708, "latency_ms": 17469}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "a communicator", "corresponding_text": "a communicator configured to transmit the user's utterance to a dialogue type server, and to receive response information generated based on the user's utterance", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent's independent claim explicitly recites a communicator with the same functional configuration, so the element lacks novelty. Using a communicator to exchange utterance data with a server is well\u2011known, making it obvious."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "a display", "corresponding_text": "A display apparatus comprising", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim is directed to a display apparatus, thereby disclosing a display. A display is a generic component in such systems, so the element is neither novel nor inventive."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "a user input unit configured to receive a user voice input", "corresponding_text": "a voice receiver configured to collect a user's utterance", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses a voice receiver that collects the user's utterance, matching the source element. Voice\u2011input units are standard in voice\u2011controlled devices, rendering the element obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "a processor configured to receive the user voice input corresponding to a complex sentence including a plurality of sentences relating to a plurality of functions of the electronic apparatus", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art does not disclose handling a \"complex sentence\" composed of multiple sentences that map to multiple functions. This specific processing of multi\u2011sentence, multi\u2011function input is absent, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "a processor configured to receive, through the communicator, data corresponding to the user voice input from a server, the data including function information corresponding to whether the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences are sequentially performable as an order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent mentions receiving response information but does not teach receiving function information that specifies sequential performability of multiple functions derived from a multi\u2011sentence command. This specialized data exchange is not disclosed, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "a processor configured to, based on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, perform the plurality of functions as the order of the plurality of sentences", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No teaching in the prior art directs a system to execute multiple functions in the exact order derived from a complex, multi\u2011sentence command when they are sequentially performable. This operational logic is absent, rendering the element both novel and inventive."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "a processor configured to, based on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being not sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, according to the function information, control the display to display information indicating that the plurality of functions is not performable", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art does not disclose providing a user\u2011visible indication that a set of functions derived from a multi\u2011sentence command cannot be performed sequentially. This feedback mechanism is not taught, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014namely the processing of a complex, multi\u2011sentence voice command to determine sequential performability of multiple functions, executing them in order when possible, and explicitly informing the user when they cannot be performed\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art display apparatus. While generic components such as a communicator, display, and voice receiver are known, the inventive concepts centered on handling multi\u2011sentence commands and the associated decision logic are novel and non\u2011obvious. Consequently, the source patent retains substantive validity."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "e9c907f7-3f4c-4c0a-b4ab-0d1a67e8d41c", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:00:14.948804+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US11756539B2", "independent_claim": "1. An electronic apparatus comprising:\na communicator;\na display;\na user input unit configured to receive a user voice input; and\na processor configured to:\nreceive the user voice input corresponding to a complex sentence including a plurality of sentences relating to a plurality of functions of the electronic apparatus,\nreceive, through the communicator, data corresponding to the user voice input from a server, the data including function information corresponding to whether the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences are sequentially performable as an order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence,\nbased on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, perform the plurality of functions as the order of the plurality of sentences, and\nbased on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being not sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, according to the function information, control the display to display information indicating that the plurality of functions is not performable.", "specification": "The natural language processing apparatus 120 rapidly estimates a boundary between two sentences of an input sentence received in a short or compound form sentence from the user device 100, in more detail, a compound form sentence, and uses a simplified machine language for this. Here, the simplified machine language may be a language that is internally used to rapidly change an input compound sentence into divided short sentence forms. This will be described later in more detail. For example, if the natural language processing apparatus 120 receives a compound sentence \u201cWho starred in Harry Potter and turn on KBS\u201d and a sentence \u201cWho starred in Gone with the Wind and turn on KBS\u201d from the user, the natural language processing apparatus 120 determines that \u201cHarry Potter\u201d and \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d belong to a named entity category (or a named entity attribute, a named entity type, or the like) named movie through learning, equally changes (or replaces or translates) \u201cHarry Potter\u201d and \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d into a form \u201cWho starred in @movie\u201d, rapidly applies a statistical translation technique based on this, and divide and generate a plurality of short sentences. Here, the named entity category refers to a category to which named entity words of a sentence belong. A named entity, i.e., a name of a word, may be \u201cHarry Potter\u201d or \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d as a movie name, \u201cOCN\u201d as a channel name, \u201c*Bama\u201d, or the like as a character. A category to which this named entity belongs may be classified into a movie, a channel name, a character, etc. However, a category of the named entity \u201cHarry Potter\u201d may be a character in another sentence.\nHere, when various forms of compound sentences, in more detail, a compound sentence on which a named entity category is tagged, is referred to as A, the statistical (machine) translation technique outputs a compound sentence B trained (or learned) for A. In other words, as a processing result of sentence A, sentence B of a form where several short sentences are connected to one another by a particular identifier is generated. For example, a form, such as \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d into which a sentence classification symbol is inserted, may be an identifier of two short sentences of the compound sentence B. Based on this sentence classification symbol, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may estimate a boundary between two short sentences, generate two short sentences B \u201cRecord Harry Potter\u201d and \u201cShow me Gone with the Wind\u201d based on the boundary, provide the two short sentences B to the user device 100 or change the two short sentences B into another language, and provide the changed two short sentences B to the user device 100. Alternatively, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may translate two short sentences into another language, connect the translated two short sentences, and provide the connected two short sentences to the user device 100. Here, an identifier has been described as a symbol but may be a bit information form. Therefore, the identifier may not be particularly limited. Also, when considering that an ungrammatical change or a word order change may occur according to a translation result, \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d may be changed into \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d. Therefore, the present exemplary embodiment may not be particularly limited to an output form of the above result.\nFor example, if a sentence is provided in a text form through a search window of the user device 100, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may use the corresponding text sentence as it is. However, if a voice is uttered so as to provide a voice signal, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may acquire and use a corresponding voice sentence as a text-based recognition result by using an internal free utterance engine. If the user device 100 recognizes this voice sentence and provides a text-based result, this process may be omitted. Therefore, the present exemplary embodiment may not limit a form of a sentence that the natural language processing apparatus 120 receives from the user and forms of two divided short sentences provided to the user device 100.\nIn more detail, the natural language processing apparatus 120 according to the present exemplary embodiment may pre-store named entity corpus information for determining a category of a named entity of an input sentence, i.e., an attribute of the named entity. For example, \u201cHarry Potter\u201d may be a movie in an input sentence but may be referred to as a person. Therefore, the natural language processing apparatus 120 rapidly classifies a category of a named entity from a corresponding input sentence by performing training and learning based on stored named entity corpus information. In other words, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may learn a named entity recognition model based on the named entity corpus information and rapidly automatically find named entity categories of various input short or compound sentences based on the learned named entity recognition model. This process is a named entity recognition process."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20140188486A1", "independent_claim": "16. A dialogue-type system comprising:\na dialogue-type server configured to convert a user's utterance into text information, and to generate response information corresponding to the user's utterance; and\na display apparatus configured to determine whether control information corresponding to the user's utterance is pre-stored when the user's utterance is collected, and when the control information is pre-stored, to perform operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the pre-stored control information, and when the control information is not pre-stored, to perform the operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue-type server.", "specification": "[0039]\nBy another example, when the user's utterance for providing contents information is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200 and determines whether control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is pre-stored. As a result of the determination, when the control information corresponding to the user's utterance is not pre-stored, the display apparatus 100 may perform functions corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. That is, the display apparatus may output a response message corresponding to the user's utterance as at least one of an image and a voice based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200.\n[0040]\nBy another example, when the user's utterance for controlling an external device 300 is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200 and determines whether control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is pre-stored. As a result of the determination, when the control information corresponding to the user's utterance is not pre-stored, the display apparatus 100 may perform a function corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. That is, the display apparatus 100 may control functions of the external device 300 based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. Here, the external device 300 may be an electronic apparatus (e.g., a DVD player, a set top box, a sound device, and a game device, etc.) which is connected to the display apparatus 100.\n[0041]\nThe display apparatus 100 in a dialogue system according to the exemplary embodiment may perform functions corresponding to the user's intention based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200, and may perform functions corresponding to the user's intention based on the pre-stored control information corresponding to the user's utterance. Therefore, the display apparatus 100 may not only minimize errors in performing functions corresponding to the user's utterance, but also perform functions corresponding to the user's utterance at a fast processing speed.\n[0042]\nThe dialogue type server 200 which generates response information based on the received user's utterance analyzes the meaning of the user's utterance received from the display apparatus 100, generates response information for controlling the operations of the display apparatus 100, and transmits the response information to the display apparatus 100. For example, when the user's utterance is received from the display apparatus 100, the dialogue type server 200 may extract an utterance element from the received user's utterance, generate response information related to the user's utterance based on the extracted utterance element, and transmit the generated response information to the display apparatus 100. The utterance element may be a keyword for performing operations requested by the user in the user's utterance. For example, in a case of the user's utterance of \u201cwhat time does OOO (e.g., a broadcast program title) start this Saturday?\u201d, the utterance element may be \u201cthis\u201d, \u201cSaturday\u201d, \u201cOOO (broadcast program title)\u201d, \u201cwhat time\u201d, and \u201cstarts\u201d, etc.\n[0043]\nWhen the utterance element is extracted, the dialogue type server 200 generates response information for performing operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the extracted utterance element and transmits the response information to the display apparatus 100.\n[0044]\nFor example, the display apparatus 100 may collect the user's utterance related to an EPG (Electronic Program Guide) of \u201cwhat time does OOO (broadcast program title) start this Saturday?\u201d When the user's utterance is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200. If the user's utterance is an analogue signal, the display apparatus 100 may convert the collected user utterance into a digital signal, and transmit the converted user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200. When the user's utterance converted into the digital signal is received, the dialogue type server 200 generates text information based on the converted user's utterance, analyzes the generated text information, and generates response information corresponding to the user's utterance.\n"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US11756539B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. An electronic apparatus comprising:\na communicator;\na display;\na user input unit configured to receive a user voice input; and\na processor configured to:\nreceive the user voice input corresponding to a complex sentence including a plurality of sentences relating to a plurality of functions of the electronic apparatus,\nreceive, through the communicator, data corresponding to the user voice input from a server, the data including function information corresponding to whether the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences are sequentially performable as an order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence,\nbased on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, perform the plurality of functions as the order of the plurality of sentences, and\nbased on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being not sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, according to the function information, control the display to display information indicating that the plurality of functions is not performable.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe natural language processing apparatus 120 rapidly estimates a boundary between two sentences of an input sentence received in a short or compound form sentence from the user device 100, in more detail, a compound form sentence, and uses a simplified machine language for this. Here, the simplified machine language may be a language that is internally used to rapidly change an input compound sentence into divided short sentence forms. For example, if the natural language processing apparatus 120 receives a compound sentence \u201cWho starred in Harry Potter and turn on KBS\u201d and a sentence \u201cWho starred in Gone with the Wind and turn on KBS\u201d from the user, the natural language processing apparatus 120 determines that \u201cHarry Potter\u201d and \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d belong to a named entity category (or a named entity attribute, a named entity type, or the like) named movie through learning, equally changes (or replaces or translates) \u201cHarry Potter\u201d and \u201cGone with the Wind\u201d into a form \u201cWho starred in @movie\u201d, rapidly applies a statistical translation technique based on this, and divide and generate a plurality of short sentences. Here, the named entity category refers to a category to which named entity words of a sentence belong. However, a category of the named entity \u201cHarry Potter\u201d may be a character in another sentence.\nHere, when various forms of compound sentences, in more detail, a compound sentence on which a named entity category is tagged, is referred to as A, the statistical (machine) translation technique outputs a compound sentence B trained (or learned) for A. In other words, as a processing result of sentence A, sentence B of a form where several short sentences are connected to one another by a particular identifier is generated. For example, a form, such as \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d into which a sentence classification symbol is inserted, may be an identifier of two short sentences of the compound sentence B. Based on this sentence classification symbol, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may estimate a boundary between two short sentences, generate two short sentences B \u201cRecord Harry Potter\u201d and \u201cShow me Gone with the Wind\u201d based on the boundary, provide the two short sentences B to the user device 100 or change the two short sentences B into another language, and provide the changed two short sentences B to the user device 100. Alternatively, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may translate two short sentences into another language, connect the translated two short sentences, and provide the connected two short sentences to the user device 100. Here, an identifier has been described as a symbol but may be a bit information form. Also, when considering that an ungrammatical change or a word order change may occur according to a translation result, \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d may be changed into \u201cRecord @ movie # show me @ movie\u201d. Therefore, the present exemplary embodiment may not be particularly limited to an output form of the above result.\nFor example, if a sentence is provided in a text form through a search window of the user device 100, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may use the corresponding text sentence as it is. However, if a voice is uttered so as to provide a voice signal, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may acquire and use a corresponding voice sentence as a text-based recognition result by using an internal free utterance engine. If the user device 100 recognizes this voice sentence and provides a text-based result, this process may be omitted. Therefore, the present exemplary embodiment may not limit a form of a sentence that the natural language processing apparatus 120 receives from the user and forms of two divided short sentences provided to the user device 100.\nIn more detail, the natural language processing apparatus 120 according to the present exemplary embodiment may pre-store named entity corpus information for determining a category of a named entity of an input sentence, i.e., an attribute of the named entity. For example, \u201cHarry Potter\u201d may be a movie in an input sentence but may be referred to as a person. Therefore, the natural language processing apparatus 120 rapidly classifies a category of a named entity from a corresponding input sentence by performing training and learning based on stored named entity corpus information. In other words, the natural language processing apparatus 120 may learn a named entity recognition model based on the named entity corpus information and rapidly automatically find named entity categories of various input short or compound sentences based on the learned named entity recognition model.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20140188486A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n16. A dialogue-type system comprising:\na dialogue-type server configured to convert a user's utterance into text information, and to generate response information corresponding to the user's utterance; and\na display apparatus configured to determine whether control information corresponding to the user's utterance is pre-stored when the user's utterance is collected, and when the control information is pre-stored, to perform operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the pre-stored control information, and when the control information is not pre-stored, to perform the operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue-type server.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n[0039]\nBy another example, when the user's utterance for providing contents information is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200 and determines whether control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is pre-stored. As a result of the determination, when the control information corresponding to the user's utterance is not pre-stored, the display apparatus 100 may perform functions corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. That is, the display apparatus may output a response message corresponding to the user's utterance as at least one of an image and a voice based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200.\n[0040]\nBy another example, when the user's utterance for controlling an external device 300 is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200 and determines whether control information corresponding to the collected user's utterance is pre-stored. As a result of the determination, when the control information corresponding to the user's utterance is not pre-stored, the display apparatus 100 may perform a function corresponding to the user's utterance based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. That is, the display apparatus 100 may control functions of the external device 300 based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200. Here, the external device 300 may be an electronic apparatus (e.g., a DVD player, a set top box, a sound device, and a game device, etc.) which is connected to the display apparatus 100.\n[0041]\nThe display apparatus 100 in a dialogue system according to the exemplary embodiment may perform functions corresponding to the user's intention based on the response information received from the dialogue type server 200, and may perform functions corresponding to the user's intention based on the pre-stored control information corresponding to the user's utterance. Therefore, the display apparatus 100 may not only minimize errors in performing functions corresponding to the user's utterance, but also perform functions corresponding to the user's utterance at a fast processing speed.\n[0042]\nThe dialogue type server 200 which generates response information based on the received user's utterance analyzes the meaning of the user's utterance received from the display apparatus 100, generates response information for controlling the operations of the display apparatus 100, and transmits the response information to the display apparatus 100. For example, when the user's utterance is received from the display apparatus 100, the dialogue type server 200 may extract an utterance element from the received user's utterance, generate response information related to the user's utterance based on the extracted utterance element, and transmit the generated response information to the display apparatus 100. The utterance element may be a keyword for performing operations requested by the user in the user's utterance. For example, in a case of the user's utterance of \u201cwhat time does OOO (e.g., a broadcast program title) start this Saturday?\u201d, the utterance element may be \u201cthis\u201d, \u201cSaturday\u201d, \u201cOOO (broadcast program title)\u201d, \u201cwhat time\u201d, and \u201cstarts\u201d, etc.\n[0043]\nWhen the utterance element is extracted, the dialogue type server 200 generates response information for performing operations corresponding to the user's utterance based on the extracted utterance element and transmits the response information to the display apparatus 100.\n[0044]\nFor example, the display apparatus 100 may collect the user's utterance related to an EPG (Electronic Program Guide) of \u201cwhat time does OOO (broadcast program title) start this Saturday?\u201d When the user's utterance is collected, the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200. If the user's utterance is an analogue signal, the display apparatus 100 may convert the collected user utterance into a digital signal, and transmit the converted user's utterance to the dialogue type server 200. When the user's utterance converted into the digital signal is received, the dialogue type server 200 generates text information based on the converted user's utterance, analyzes the generated text information, and generates response information corresponding to the user's utterance."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"a communicator\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose a component named a communicator; therefore the element is not found (novelty N). As it is novel, it cannot be considered obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a display\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"a display apparatus\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target independent claim recites \\\"a display apparatus\\\", which directly corresponds to the display element (novelty Y). A display apparatus is a conventional component in dialogue systems, making the element obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"a user input unit configured to receive a user voice input\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The specification shows the display apparatus collecting (and transmitting) the user's utterance, which fulfills the function of receiving a user voice input (novelty Y). Receiving voice input via a display unit is standard in prior art dialogue systems, thus the element is obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor configured to receive the user voice input corresponding to a complex sentence including a plurality of sentences relating to a plurality of functions of the electronic apparatus\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose processing of a \\\"complex sentence\\\" containing multiple sentences tied to multiple functions; it only handles a single utterance (novelty N). Because the feature is not disclosed, it is not obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor configured to receive, through the communicator, data corresponding to the user voice input from a server, the data including function information corresponding to whether the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences are sequentially performable as an order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art does not teach receiving data that indicates sequential performability of multiple functions derived from a multi\u2011sentence complex command (novelty N). No teaching exists to render this feature obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor configured, based on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, to perform the plurality of functions as the order of the plurality of sentences\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent lacks any mechanism for ordering and executing multiple functions according to the order of sentences in a complex command (novelty N). Without such disclosure, the step is not obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor configured, based on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being not sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, according to the function information, control the display to display information indicating that the plurality of functions is not performable\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art does not disclose detecting non\u2011sequentially performable functions and notifying the user via the display (novelty N). No teaching exists that would make this approach obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014handling complex multi\u2011sentence commands, determining sequential executability of multiple functions, and providing user feedback when execution is not possible\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art dialogue system. While generic components such as a display and voice input are known, the inventive concepts related to parsing, ordering, and conditional execution of multiple functions are novel and non\u2011obvious. Consequently, the source patent retains substantive novelty and inventive step for its key contributions, supporting its overall validity.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3321, "tokens_output": 2829, "latency_ms": 7934}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "a communicator", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose a component named a communicator; therefore the element is not found (novelty N). As it is novel, it cannot be considered obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "a display", "corresponding_text": "a display apparatus", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target independent claim recites \"a display apparatus\", which directly corresponds to the display element (novelty Y). A display apparatus is a conventional component in dialogue systems, making the element obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "a user input unit configured to receive a user voice input", "corresponding_text": "the display apparatus 100 transmits the collected user's utterance", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The specification shows the display apparatus collecting (and transmitting) the user's utterance, which fulfills the function of receiving a user voice input (novelty Y). Receiving voice input via a display unit is standard in prior art dialogue systems, thus the element is obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "a processor configured to receive the user voice input corresponding to a complex sentence including a plurality of sentences relating to a plurality of functions of the electronic apparatus", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose processing of a \"complex sentence\" containing multiple sentences tied to multiple functions; it only handles a single utterance (novelty N). Because the feature is not disclosed, it is not obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "a processor configured to receive, through the communicator, data corresponding to the user voice input from a server, the data including function information corresponding to whether the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences are sequentially performable as an order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art does not teach receiving data that indicates sequential performability of multiple functions derived from a multi\u2011sentence complex command (novelty N). No teaching exists to render this feature obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "a processor configured, based on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, to perform the plurality of functions as the order of the plurality of sentences", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent lacks any mechanism for ordering and executing multiple functions according to the order of sentences in a complex command (novelty N). Without such disclosure, the step is not obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "a processor configured, based on the plurality of functions related to the plurality of sentences being not sequentially performable as the order of the plurality of sentences in the complex sentence, according to the function information, control the display to display information indicating that the plurality of functions is not performable", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art does not disclose detecting non\u2011sequentially performable functions and notifying the user via the display (novelty N). No teaching exists that would make this approach obvious (inventive step N). Verdict N."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014handling complex multi\u2011sentence commands, determining sequential executability of multiple functions, and providing user feedback when execution is not possible\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art dialogue system. While generic components such as a display and voice input are known, the inventive concepts related to parsing, ordering, and conditional execution of multiple functions are novel and non\u2011obvious. Consequently, the source patent retains substantive novelty and inventive step for its key contributions, supporting its overall validity."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "efcce13c-e4ed-4fb0-8c10-dcd9d1451232", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:01:00.459441+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US12585862B2", "independent_claim": "1. A system for automating actions on a software user interface, comprising:\na processor; and\na memory storing instructions which, when executed, cause the processor to perform steps comprising:\nconstructing an artificial intelligence agent based on a Transformer based vision language model configured to automate actions on a software user interface, including training the Transformer based vision language model on:\na first training dataset including documents containing text interleaved with images;\na second training dataset including text embedded in images;\na third training dataset including recorded videos of software usage;\na fourth training dataset including portable document format (PDF) documents;\na fifth training dataset including recorded videos of software tool usage trajectories;\na sixth training dataset including images of open-domain web pages;\na seventh training dataset including images of specific-domain web pages; and\nan eighth training dataset including images of agentic trajectories of the agent performing interface automation task workflows;\nreceiving, via a user interface, a natural language input indicating a user intent to interact with the software user interface;\ngenerating, by the Transformer based vision language model, a workflow indicating a past action and a next action relating to the user intent based on an input comprising a screenshot of the software user interface and the natural language input;\nexecuting, by the artificial intelligence agent, at least the next action in a form of a machine-actuated command on the software user interface thereby resulting in a change in the software user interface; and\niteratively generating, by the Transformer based vision language model, next machine-actuated commands based on a screenshot of a changed software user interface; and\niteratively executing, by the artificial intelligence agent, the next machine-actuated commands thereby causing a task described by the natural language input to be completed by interacting with the software user interface.", "specification": "A system for interface automation includes an agent. The agent is configured to process an input that specifies an interface workflow, wherein the interface workflow is otherwise implementable by one or more user-actuated actions directed towards an interface by a user. The agent is also configured to generate an output that specifies a sequence of actuation commands, wherein the sequence of actuation commands triggers one or more machine-actuated actions that replicate the user-actuated actions on the interface and cause automation of the interface workflow.\nA system for constructing prompts that cause an agent to automate multimodal interface workflows includes agent specification logic and agent calling logic. The agent specification logic is configured to construct agent specifications using prompts and agent functions, wherein the agent specifications are configured to automate a multimodal interface workflow. The agent calling logic is in communication with the agent specification logic and is configured to translate the agent specifications into agent calls that cause an agent to implement the agent functions to produce outputs that are responsive to the prompts.\nA system for interface automation includes an agent. The agent is configured to process an input that specifies an interface workflow, wherein the interface workflow is otherwise implementable by one or more user-actuated actions directed towards an interface by a user. The agent is also configured to generate an output that specifies a sequence of actuation commands, wherein the sequence of actuation commands triggers one or more machine-actuated actions that replicate the user-actuated actions on the interface and cause automation of the interface workflow.\nA system for constructing prompts that cause an agent to automate multimodal interface workflows includes agent specification logic and agent calling logic. The agent specification logic is configured to construct agent specifications using prompts and agent functions, wherein the agent specifications are configured to automate a multimodal interface workflow. The agent calling logic is in communication with the agent specification logic and is configured to translate the agent specifications into agent calls that cause an agent to implement the agent functions to produce outputs that are responsive to the prompts.\nA system for client-side implementation of an interface automation language at runtime includes agent specification logic and runtime interpretation logic. The agent specification logic, running on client-side, is configured construct an agent specification, and to make the agent specification available for server-side translation into an intermediate representation, wherein the agent specification is configured to automate a multimodal interface workflow. The runtime interpretation logic, running on client-side, is configured to receive the intermediate representation, detect one or more agent functions in the intermediate representation, generate one or more agent calls based on the agent functions, issue the agent calls to an agent and, in response, receive at least one runtime actuation function from the agent, and translate the runtime actuation function into at least one runtime actuation command, wherein the runtime actuation command triggers at least one machine-actuated action as a runtime synthetic action that automates the multimodal interface workflow."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20230206913A1", "independent_claim": "1. A computer-implemented method of generating modifications to user commands in a multimodal environment, comprising:\nreceiving, by a processor, from an input device of a plurality of input devices, a capture of user action as an initial command;\ninterpreting, by the processor, the initial command into an interpreted command;\ngenerating a first set of modified commands that are based on the interpreted command, including:\na first modified command that has a phonetic similarity to the interpreted command within a certain threshold, and\na second modified command that is semantically related to an earlier command;\ntransmitting, to an output device of one or more output devices, the first set of modified commands;\nreceiving a response to a group of commands including the first set of modified commands;\nrecording an identifier of an input device from which the response was received and a type of the response in a log;\nexecuting, when the response includes acknowledging a specific command of the group of commands as an accepted command, the accepted command;\ngenerating, when the response does not include acknowledging any command, a second set of modified commands.", "specification": "his application discloses techniques and servers that accelerate interpretation of user commands through digital user interfaces of various modalities, including generation and presentation of command modifications for rapid correction of incomplete or erroneous user commands. A server and connected devices may provide interactivity by receiving and executing user commands that are given by a user. The same command may be received by different input devices and by different modalities and mediums. For example, a command may be typed, spoken, or gestured, and the server transforms the raw command that was initially based on certain modalities into a canonical command that represents the intent of the user in a format that is independent of input modality.\n[0038]\nIn an embodiment, the user may speak an utterance into a microphone that injects the user command into the server as an initial command that may be captured as digitized audio content. By speech to text transcription or additional semantic translation, the initial command may be translated into an interpreted command that is a semantic representation of the content as recognized by the server. Due to ambiguity or interference as discussed earlier, the server detects: a) whether the interpreted command is accurate and, if inaccurate, b) precisely what the intended command was or, at least, c) what suggested modification to the interpreted command would be sufficient to match the intent of the user.\n[0039]\nIn light of that uncertainty, the server performs disambiguation that entails multiple competing or cooperating recommendation generators proposing modified commands that may more accurately reflect the intent of the user. In various ways, the user may provide, through the same or a different input device, a response that is either: a) a confirmation of which one of several modified commands that were automatically proposed is what the user intends or b) a correction that the server may use to filter or replace currently offered modified commands to generate improved modified commands. Through intelligent recommendations to refine the commands, the user and server may quickly agree on an accurate command that should be executed. With a main purpose of the server being generating instructions for command execution, quickly and accurately interpreting and refining a user command accelerates the operation of the server. Likewise, increased accuracy of command interpretation increases the reliability of the server.\n[0040]\nThe server includes an architecture that: a) increases the accuracy of generated modified commands, which makes the server more reliable and b) by generation of more accurate modified commands, accelerates automatic proposal and manual acceptance of the modified command to be executed. For example, fewer iterative refinements in a sequence of modified commands may be needed to establish an accurate command. In other words, the server arrives at and executes an intended command faster than other approaches.\n[0041]\nIn an embodiment, the server implements a system of taking voice input from a user for actions by an input device. The voice input instruction is translated by an automatic speech recognition (ASR) engine, and interpreted by a natural language parsing (NLP) engine to derive user intent and relevant subjects. The interpreted voice instruction is provided to the user for modification, along with modification suggestions for selection using an input device such as a remote mouse. The suggestions for selection are provided based on phonetic-proximity initially and changed to character-based proximity if the user uses a keyboard to enter a correction, or provides a first letter of a word through voice"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US12585862B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A system for automating actions on a software user interface, comprising:\na processor; and\na memory storing instructions which, when executed, cause the processor to perform steps comprising:\nconstructing an artificial intelligence agent based on a Transformer based vision language model configured to automate actions on a software user interface, including training the Transformer based vision language model on:\na first training dataset including documents containing text interleaved with images;\na second training dataset including text embedded in images;\na third training dataset including recorded videos of software usage;\na fourth training dataset including portable document format (PDF) documents;\na fifth training dataset including recorded videos of software tool usage trajectories;\na sixth training dataset including images of open-domain web pages;\na seventh training dataset including images of specific-domain web pages; and\nan eighth training dataset including images of agentic trajectories of the agent performing interface automation task workflows;\nreceiving, via a user interface, a natural language input indicating a user intent to interact with the software user interface;\ngenerating, by the Transformer based vision language model, a workflow indicating a past action and a next action relating to the user intent based on an input comprising a screenshot of the software user interface and the natural language input;\nexecuting, by the artificial intelligence agent, at least the next action in a form of a machine-actuated command on the software user interface thereby resulting in a change in the software user interface; and\niteratively generating, by the Transformer based vision language model, next machine-actuated commands based on a screenshot of a changed software user interface; and\niteratively executing, by the artificial intelligence agent, the next machine-actuated commands thereby causing a task described by the natural language input to be completed by interacting with the software user interface.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system for interface automation includes an agent. The agent is configured to process an input that specifies an interface workflow, wherein the interface workflow is otherwise implementable by one or more user-actuated actions directed towards an interface by a user. The agent is also configured to generate an output that specifies a sequence of actuation commands, wherein the sequence of actuation commands triggers one or more machine-actuated actions that replicate the user-actuated actions on the interface and cause automation of the interface workflow.\nA system for constructing prompts that cause an agent to automate multimodal interface workflows includes agent specification logic and agent calling logic. The agent specification logic is configured to construct agent specifications using prompts and agent functions, wherein the agent specifications are configured to automate a multimodal interface workflow. The agent calling logic is in communication with the agent specification logic and is configured to translate the agent specifications into agent calls that cause an agent to implement the agent functions to produce outputs that are responsive to the prompts.\nA system for interface automation includes an agent. The agent is configured to process an input that specifies an interface workflow, wherein the interface workflow is otherwise implementable by one or more user-actuated actions directed towards an interface by a user. The agent is also configured to generate an output that specifies a sequence of actuation commands, wherein the sequence of actuation commands triggers one or more machine-actuated actions that replicate the user-actuated actions on the interface and cause automation of the interface workflow.\nA system for constructing prompts that cause an agent to automate multimodal interface workflows includes agent specification logic and agent calling logic. The agent specification logic is configured to construct agent specifications using prompts and agent functions, wherein the agent specifications are configured to automate a multimodal interface workflow. The agent calling logic is in communication with the agent specification logic and is configured to translate the agent specifications into agent calls that cause an agent to implement the agent functions to produce outputs that are responsive to the prompts.\nA system for client-side implementation of an interface automation language at runtime includes agent specification logic and runtime interpretation logic. The agent specification logic, running on client-side, is configured construct an agent specification, and to make the agent specification available for server-side translation into an intermediate representation, wherein the agent specification is configured to automate a multimodal interface workflow. The runtime interpretation logic, running on client-side, is configured to receive the intermediate representation, detect one or more agent functions in the intermediate representation, generate one or more agent calls based on the agent functions, issue the agent calls to an agent and, in response, receive at least one runtime actuation function from the agent, and translate the runtime actuation function into at least one runtime actuation command, wherein the runtime actuation command triggers at least one machine-actuated action as a runtime synthetic action that automates the multimodal interface workflow.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20230206913A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A computer-implemented method of generating modifications to user commands in a multimodal environment, comprising:\nreceiving, by a processor, from an input device of a plurality of input devices, a capture of user action as an initial command;\ninterpreting, by the processor, the initial command into an interpreted command;\ngenerating a first set of modified commands that are based on the interpreted command, including:\na first modified command that has a phonetic similarity to the interpreted command within a certain threshold, and\na second modified command that is semantically related to an earlier command;\ntransmitting, to an output device of one or more output devices, the first set of modified commands;\nreceiving a response to a group of commands including the first set of modified commands;\nrecording an identifier of an input device from which the response was received and a type of the response in a log;\nexecuting, when the response includes acknowledging a specific command of the group of commands as an accepted command, the accepted command;\ngenerating, when the response does not include acknowledging any command, a second set of modified commands.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nhis application discloses techniques and servers that accelerate interpretation of user commands through digital user interfaces of various modalities, including generation and presentation of command modifications for rapid correction of incomplete or erroneous user commands. A server and connected devices may provide interactivity by receiving and executing user commands that are given by a user. The same command may be received by different input devices and by different modalities and mediums. For example, a command may be typed, spoken, or gestured, and the server transforms the raw command that was initially based on certain modalities into a canonical command that represents the intent of the user in a format that is independent of input modality.\n[0038]\nIn an embodiment, the user may speak an utterance into a microphone that injects the user command into the server as an initial command that may be captured as digitized audio content. By speech to text transcription or additional semantic translation, the initial command may be translated into an interpreted command that is a semantic representation of the content as recognized by the server. Due to ambiguity or interference as discussed earlier, the server detects: a) whether the interpreted command is accurate and, if inaccurate, b) precisely what the intended command was or, at least, c) what suggested modification to the interpreted command would be sufficient to match the intent of the user.\n[0039]\nIn light of that uncertainty, the server performs disambiguation that entails multiple competing or cooperating recommendation generators proposing modified commands that may more accurately reflect the intent of the user. In various ways, the user may provide, through the same or a different input device, a response that is either: a) a confirmation of which one of several modified commands that were automatically proposed is what the user intends or b) a correction that the server may use to filter or replace currently offered modified commands to generate improved modified commands. Through intelligent recommendations to refine the commands, the user and server may quickly agree on an accurate command that should be executed. With a main purpose of the server being generating instructions for command execution, quickly and accurately interpreting and refining a user command accelerates the operation of the server. Likewise, increased accuracy of command interpretation increases the reliability of the server.\n[0040]\nThe server includes an architecture that: a) increases the accuracy of generated modified commands, which makes the server more reliable and b) by generation of more accurate modified commands, accelerates automatic proposal and manual acceptance of the modified command to be executed. For example, fewer iterative refinements in a sequence of modified commands may be needed to establish an accurate command. In other words, the server arrives at and executes an intended command faster than other approaches.\n[0041]\nIn an embodiment, the server implements a system of taking voice input from a user for actions by an input device. The voice input instruction is translated by an automatic speech recognition (ASR) engine, and interpreted by a natural language parsing (NLP) engine to derive user intent and relevant subjects. The interpreted voice instruction is provided to the user for modification, along with modification suggestions for selection using an input device such as a remote mouse. The suggestions for selection are provided based on phonetic-proximity initially and changed to character-based proximity if the user uses a keyboard to enter a correction, or provides a first letter of a word through voice"}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"a processor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving, by a processor, from an input device of a plurality of input devices, a capture of user action as an initial command\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s independent claim explicitly recites a processor that receives a capture of a user action. This matches the source element of a processor. Using a processor for such reception is a routine, well\u2011known function and would be obvious to one of ordinary skill. Therefore the element lacks both novelty and inventive step, resulting in a Y verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a memory storing instructions which, when executed, cause the processor to perform steps comprising:\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose a memory that stores executable instructions; it only mentions a processor performing actions. Absence of such disclosure means the element is novel. Inventive step is therefore not applicable, yielding an N verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"constructing an artificial intelligence agent based on a Transformer based vision language model configured to automate actions on a software user interface, including training the Transformer based vision language model on eight specific training datasets\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent makes no reference to Transformer models, vision\u2011language architectures, or the enumerated training datasets. Consequently the element is not disclosed and is novel; inventive step is not relevant, leading to an N verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving, via a user interface, a natural language input indicating a user intent to interact with the software user interface\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"While the target patent describes receiving a \\\"capture of user action\\\" as an initial command, it does not specifically disclose receipt of natural\u2011language input through a user interface for UI automation. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious, resulting in an N verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating, by the Transformer based vision language model, a workflow indicating a past action and a next action relating to the user intent based on an input comprising a screenshot of the software user interface and the natural language input\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose generation of a workflow from a screenshot combined with natural\u2011language input, nor the use of a vision\u2011language model for such purpose. The element is novel and non\u2011obvious, yielding an N verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"executing, by the artificial intelligence agent, at least the next action in a form of a machine\u2011actuated command on the software user interface thereby resulting in a change in the software user interface\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent mentions executing an \\\"accepted command\\\" but does not describe machine\u2011actuated commands that directly manipulate a software UI. This specific execution mechanism is not disclosed, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious, leading to an N verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"iteratively generating, by the Transformer based vision language model, next machine\u2011actuated commands based on a screenshot of a changed software user interface\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No iterative generation of commands from updated UI screenshots using a Transformer vision\u2011language model is taught in the target patent. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious, resulting in an N verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"iteratively executing, by the artificial intelligence agent, the next machine\u2011actuated commands thereby causing a task described by the natural language input to be completed by interacting with the software user interface\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose an iterative loop where an AI agent executes machine\u2011actuated UI commands to fulfill a natural\u2011language\u2011specified task. This element is novel and non\u2011obvious, giving an N verdict.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014namely the construction of a Transformer\u2011based vision\u2011language AI agent, the extensive multi\u2011modal training regimen, the generation of UI\u2011specific workflows from screenshots and natural language, and the iterative execution of machine\u2011actuated UI commands\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent. Only the generic presence of a processor is common, and that element is obvious. Because the pivotal elements are novel and non\u2011obvious, the source patent retains substantive inventive merit and is likely valid over the cited prior art.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3027, "tokens_output": 3176, "latency_ms": 9665}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "a processor", "corresponding_text": "receiving, by a processor, from an input device of a plurality of input devices, a capture of user action as an initial command", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s independent claim explicitly recites a processor that receives a capture of a user action. This matches the source element of a processor. Using a processor for such reception is a routine, well\u2011known function and would be obvious to one of ordinary skill. Therefore the element lacks both novelty and inventive step, resulting in a Y verdict."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "a memory storing instructions which, when executed, cause the processor to perform steps comprising:", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose a memory that stores executable instructions; it only mentions a processor performing actions. Absence of such disclosure means the element is novel. Inventive step is therefore not applicable, yielding an N verdict."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "constructing an artificial intelligence agent based on a Transformer based vision language model configured to automate actions on a software user interface, including training the Transformer based vision language model on eight specific training datasets", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent makes no reference to Transformer models, vision\u2011language architectures, or the enumerated training datasets. Consequently the element is not disclosed and is novel; inventive step is not relevant, leading to an N verdict."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "receiving, via a user interface, a natural language input indicating a user intent to interact with the software user interface", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "While the target patent describes receiving a \"capture of user action\" as an initial command, it does not specifically disclose receipt of natural\u2011language input through a user interface for UI automation. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious, resulting in an N verdict."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "generating, by the Transformer based vision language model, a workflow indicating a past action and a next action relating to the user intent based on an input comprising a screenshot of the software user interface and the natural language input", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose generation of a workflow from a screenshot combined with natural\u2011language input, nor the use of a vision\u2011language model for such purpose. The element is novel and non\u2011obvious, yielding an N verdict."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "executing, by the artificial intelligence agent, at least the next action in a form of a machine\u2011actuated command on the software user interface thereby resulting in a change in the software user interface", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent mentions executing an \"accepted command\" but does not describe machine\u2011actuated commands that directly manipulate a software UI. This specific execution mechanism is not disclosed, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious, leading to an N verdict."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "iteratively generating, by the Transformer based vision language model, next machine\u2011actuated commands based on a screenshot of a changed software user interface", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No iterative generation of commands from updated UI screenshots using a Transformer vision\u2011language model is taught in the target patent. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious, resulting in an N verdict."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "iteratively executing, by the artificial intelligence agent, the next machine\u2011actuated commands thereby causing a task described by the natural language input to be completed by interacting with the software user interface", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose an iterative loop where an AI agent executes machine\u2011actuated UI commands to fulfill a natural\u2011language\u2011specified task. This element is novel and non\u2011obvious, giving an N verdict."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014namely the construction of a Transformer\u2011based vision\u2011language AI agent, the extensive multi\u2011modal training regimen, the generation of UI\u2011specific workflows from screenshots and natural language, and the iterative execution of machine\u2011actuated UI commands\u2014are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent. Only the generic presence of a processor is common, and that element is obvious. Because the pivotal elements are novel and non\u2011obvious, the source patent retains substantive inventive merit and is likely valid over the cited prior art."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "af96701e-85fa-4a3c-8dbb-440bb08adb70", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:01:44.142795+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US11922550B1", "independent_claim": "1. A system comprising:\nat least one memory storing instructions;\nat least one processor configured to execute the instructions to perform operations for generating an image corresponding to a text input, the operations comprising:\naccessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder;\nreceiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding;\ninputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding;\ninputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image; wherein the second sub-model includes a first upsampler model and a second upsampler model configured for upsampling prior to generating the output image, the second upsampler model trained on images corrupted with blind super resolution (BSR) degradation; wherein the second sub-model is different than the first sub-model; and\nmaking the output image, generated by the second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of:\nconfigured to train an image generation model using the output image; or\nassociated with an image generation request.", "specification": "For example, in an embodiment, a non-transitory computer-readable medium may include instructions that are executable by one or more processors to perform operations for generating an image from a text description. The operations may include accessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder. The operations may include receiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding. The operations may include inputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding. The operations may include inputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image. The operations may include making the output image, generated by the first second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of: configured to train an image generation model using the output image; or associated with an image generation request.\nAccording to some disclosed embodiments, the at least one processor may be further configured to execute the instructions to perform operations comprising collecting a collecting a first data set and a second data set from a database, wherein the first data set comprises a set of images, wherein the second data set comprises a set of text descriptions corresponding to the set of images, jointly training an image encoder on the first data set and the text encoder on the second data set, receiving from the text encoder, a text embedding, and receiving, from the image encoder, an image embedding.\nThe operations may include encoding the output image with the image encoder, applying a decoder to the image, and obtaining a joint latent representation of the image.\nThe operations may include accessing the text embedding corresponding to the output image, based on a second text description, accessing a second text embedding, and generating a vector representation of the text embedding corresponding to the output image and the second text embedding. The operations may include performing an interpolation between the image embedding of the output image and the vector representation of the text embedding corresponding to the output image and the second text embedding, and based on the interpolation, generating a modified instance of the output image.\nThe operations may include prior to generating the corresponding image embedding, training a diffusion model including a transformer.\nThe operations may include the first sub-model being configured to encode, prior to generating the corresponding image embedding, at least one of the text description or the text embedding, via a transformer, as a sequence of tokens predicted autoregressively.\nThe operations may include up-sampling prior to generating the output image.\nThe operations may include inputting the text description and the text embedding into the first sub-model.\nThe operations may include inputting the text description and the corresponding image embedding into the second sub-model."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20230022550A1", "independent_claim": ". An image processing method, comprising:\nin response to an image editing request, determining an image to be edited and text description information of target image features of the image to be edited;\nobtaining a first latent code by encoding the image to be edited in a Style (S) Space of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN), wherein the GAN is a StyleGAN;\nencoding the text description information, obtaining a text code of a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model, and obtaining a second latent code by mapping the text code on the S space;\nobtaining a target latent code that satisfies distance requirements by performing distance optimization on the first latent code and the second latent code; and\ngenerating a target image based on the target latent code.", "specification": "0005]\nobtaining a first latent code by encoding the image to be edited in a Style (S) space of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN), in which the GAN is a StyleGAN;\n[0006]\nencoding the text description information, obtaining a text code of a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model, and obtaining a second latent code by mapping the text code on the S space;\n[0007]\nobtaining a target latent code that satisfies distance requirements by performing distance optimization on the first latent code and the second latent code; and\n[0008]\ngenerating a target image based on the target latent code.\n[0009]\nAccording to a second aspect, a method for training an image processing model is provided. The image processing model includes: an inverse transform encoder, a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model, a latent code mapper, an image reconstruction editor and a generator of a Style Generative Adversarial Network (StyleGAN), the method includes:\n[0010]\nobtaining a trained inverse transform encoder by training the inverse transform encoder in a Style (S) space of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) based on an original image, in which the GAN is a StyleGAN;\n[0011]\nobtaining a third latent code by encoding the original image in the S space by the trained inverse transform encoder, and converting the original image into a fourth latent code by an image editor of the CLIP model;\n[0012]\nobtaining a trained latent code mapper by training the latent code mapper based on the third latent code and the fourth latent code;\n[0013]\nobtaining the original image and text description information of target image features, obtaining text coding by encoding the text description information by a text editor of the CLIP model, and obtaining a fifth latent code by mapping the text coding on the S space by the trained latent code mapper; and\n[0014]\nobtaining a trained image reconstruction editor by training the image reconstruction editor based on the third latent code and the fifth latent code.\n[0015]\nAccording to a third aspect, an electronic device is provided. The electronic device includes: at least one processor and a memory communicatively coupled to the at least one processor. The memory stores instructions executable by the at least one processor, and when the instructions are executed by the at least one processor, the at least one processor is configured to perform the method according to the first aspect or the second aspect of the disclosure.\n[0016]\nAccording to a fourth aspect, a non-transitory computer-readable storage medium storing computer instructions is provided. The computer instructions are configured to cause a computer to perform the method according to the first aspect or the second aspect of the disclosure.\n[0017]\nIt should be understood that the content described in this section is not intended to identify key or important features of the embodiments of the disclosure, nor is it intended to limit the scope of the disclosure. Additional features of the disclosure will be easily understood based on the following description."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US11922550B1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A system comprising:\nat least one memory storing instructions;\nat least one processor configured to execute the instructions to perform operations for generating an image corresponding to a text input, the operations comprising:\naccessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder;\nreceiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding;\ninputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding;\ninputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image; wherein the second sub-model includes a first upsampler model and a second upsampler model configured for upsampling prior to generating the output image, the second upsampler model trained on images corrupted with blind super resolution (BSR) degradation; wherein the second sub-model is different than the first sub-model; and\nmaking the output image, generated by the second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of:\nconfigured to train an image generation model using the output image; or\nassociated with an image generation request.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nFor example, in an embodiment, a non-transitory computer-readable medium may include instructions that are executable by one or more processors to perform operations for generating an image from a text description. The operations may include accessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder. The operations may include receiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding. The operations may include inputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding. The operations may include inputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image. The operations may include making the output image, generated by the first second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of: configured to train an image generation model using the output image; or associated with an image generation request.\nAccording to some disclosed embodiments, the at least one processor may be further configured to execute the instructions to perform operations comprising collecting a collecting a first data set and a second data set from a database, wherein the first data set comprises a set of images, wherein the second data set comprises a set of text descriptions corresponding to the set of images, jointly training an image encoder on the first data set and the text encoder on the second data set, receiving from the text encoder, a text embedding, and receiving, from the image encoder, an image embedding.\nThe operations may include encoding the output image with the image encoder, applying a decoder to the image, and obtaining a joint latent representation of the image.\nThe operations may include accessing the text embedding corresponding to the output image, based on a second text description, accessing a second text embedding, and generating a vector representation of the text embedding corresponding to the output image and the second text embedding. The operations may include performing an interpolation between the image embedding of the output image and the vector representation of the text embedding corresponding to the output image and the second text embedding, and based on the interpolation, generating a modified instance of the output image.\nThe operations may include prior to generating the corresponding image embedding, training a diffusion model including a transformer.\nThe operations may include the first sub-model being configured to encode, prior to generating the corresponding image embedding, at least one of the text description or the text embedding, via a transformer, as a sequence of tokens predicted autoregressively.\nThe operations may include up-sampling prior to generating the output image.\nThe operations may include inputting the text description and the text embedding into the first sub-model.\nThe operations may include inputting the text description and the corresponding image embedding into the second sub-model.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20230022550A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. An image processing method, comprising:\nin response to an image editing request, determining an image to be edited and text description information of target image features of the image to be edited;\nobtaining a first latent code by encoding the image to be edited in a Style (S) Space of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN), wherein the GAN is a StyleGAN;\nencoding the text description information, obtaining a text code of a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model, and obtaining a second latent code by mapping the text code on the S space;\nobtaining a target latent code that satisfies distance requirements by performing distance optimization on the first latent code and the second latent code; and\ngenerating a target image based on the target latent code.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n0005]\nobtaining a first latent code by encoding the image to be edited in a Style (S) space of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN), in which the GAN is a StyleGAN;\n[0006]\nencoding the text description information, obtaining a text code of a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model, and obtaining a second latent code by mapping the text code on the S space;\n[0007]\nobtaining a target latent code that satisfies distance requirements by performing distance optimization on the first latent code and the second latent code; and\n[0008]\ngenerating a target image based on the target latent code.\n[0009]\nAccording to a second aspect, a method for training an image processing model is provided. The image processing model includes: an inverse transform encoder, a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model, a latent code mapper, an image reconstruction editor and a generator of a Style Generative Adversarial Network (StyleGAN), the method includes:\n[0010]\nobtaining a trained inverse transform encoder by training the inverse transform encoder in a Style (S) space of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) based on an original image, in which the GAN is a StyleGAN;\n[0011]\nobtaining a third latent code by encoding the original image in the S space by the trained inverse transform encoder, and converting the original image into a fourth latent code by an image editor of the CLIP model;\n[0012]\nobtaining a trained latent code mapper by training the latent code mapper based on the third latent code and the fourth latent code;\n[0013]\nobtaining the original image and text description information of target image features, obtaining text coding by encoding the text description information by a text editor of the CLIP model, and obtaining a fifth latent code by mapping the text coding on the S space by the trained latent code mapper; and\n[0014]\nobtaining a trained image reconstruction editor by training the image reconstruction editor based on the third latent code and the fifth latent code.\n[0015]\nAccording to a third aspect, an electronic device is provided. The memory stores instructions executable by the at least one processor, and when the instructions are executed by the at least one processor, the at least one processor is configured to perform the method according to the first aspect or the second aspect of the disclosure.\n[0016]\nAccording to a fourth aspect, a non-transitory computer-readable storage medium storing computer instructions is provided. The computer instructions are configured to cause a computer to perform the method according to the first aspect or the second aspect of the disclosure.\n[0017]\nIt should be understood that the content described in this section is not intended to identify key or important features of the embodiments of the disclosure, nor is it intended to limit the scope of the disclosure. Additional features of the disclosure will be easily understood based on the following description."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one memory storing instructions\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"The memory stores instructions executable by the at least one processor\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target specification explicitly discloses a memory that stores executable instructions. This is a generic computer\u2011implemented feature and therefore lacks both novelty and inventive step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor configured to execute the instructions to perform operations for generating an image corresponding to a text input\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"The memory stores instructions executable by the at least one processor, and when the instructions are executed by the at least one processor, the at least one processor is configured to perform the method according to the first aspect or the second aspect of the disclosure\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches a processor that executes stored instructions to carry out the claimed method, which includes image generation. Such a processor configuration is standard and therefore not novel nor inventive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"accessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"encoding the text description information, obtaining a text code of a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim and specification describe encoding a text description with a CLIP model, which serves the same function as a text encoder. This is a known technique, so the element lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"encoding the text description information, obtaining a text code of a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The CLIP text code is equivalent to a text embedding. The target explicitly provides this, making the element disclosed and obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"inputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not describe a first sub\u2011model that takes text (or its embedding) to produce an image embedding. It only encodes an existing image into a latent code. Hence the element is not disclosed and represents a novel, non\u2011obvious step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"inputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"generating a target image based on the target latent code\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target method generates an image from a latent code that results from combining image and text information, which fulfills the role of the second sub\u2011model. This approach is disclosed and is a routine application of latent\u2011code optimization, thus obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"the second sub-model includes a first upsampler model and a second upsampler model configured for upsampling prior to generating the output image, the second upsampler model trained on images corrupted with blind super resolution (BSR) degradation\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No upsampler models or BSR\u2011trained components are mentioned in the target patent\u2019s claim or specification, so this element is not disclosed and remains novel.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"the second sub-model is different than the first sub-model\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target does not expressly state that the second sub\u2011model differs from a first sub\u2011model; it merely describes separate encoder and generator components without the explicit distinction required. Therefore the element is not disclosed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"making the output image, generated by the second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of: configured to train an image generation model using the output image; or associated with an image generation request\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not discuss exposing the generated image to a downstream device for further training or request handling. This functionality is absent, making the element novel.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement of the source patent lies in the two\u2011stage sub\u2011model architecture that first creates an image embedding from text (or text embedding) and then uses a distinct upsampling\u2011enhanced second sub\u2011model to produce the final image. The target prior art discloses generic text encoding, latent\u2011code generation, and image synthesis, but it does not teach the intermediate image\u2011embedding sub\u2011model, the specialized upsampler models trained on blind super\u2011resolution degradation, nor the explicit device\u2011level exposure of the output for further training. Because several of these pivotal elements (5, 7, 8, 9) are not found in the prior art and are non\u2011obvious, the source patent retains substantive novelty and inventive step. Consequently, the source patent is likely valid despite overlap on routine computing and text\u2011encoding features.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2822, "tokens_output": 3259, "latency_ms": 7756}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one memory storing instructions", "corresponding_text": "The memory stores instructions executable by the at least one processor", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target specification explicitly discloses a memory that stores executable instructions. This is a generic computer\u2011implemented feature and therefore lacks both novelty and inventive step."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "at least one processor configured to execute the instructions to perform operations for generating an image corresponding to a text input", "corresponding_text": "The memory stores instructions executable by the at least one processor, and when the instructions are executed by the at least one processor, the at least one processor is configured to perform the method according to the first aspect or the second aspect of the disclosure", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent teaches a processor that executes stored instructions to carry out the claimed method, which includes image generation. Such a processor configuration is standard and therefore not novel nor inventive."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "accessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder", "corresponding_text": "encoding the text description information, obtaining a text code of a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim and specification describe encoding a text description with a CLIP model, which serves the same function as a text encoder. This is a known technique, so the element lacks novelty and is obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "receiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding", "corresponding_text": "encoding the text description information, obtaining a text code of a Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) model", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The CLIP text code is equivalent to a text embedding. The target explicitly provides this, making the element disclosed and obvious."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "inputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not describe a first sub\u2011model that takes text (or its embedding) to produce an image embedding. It only encodes an existing image into a latent code. Hence the element is not disclosed and represents a novel, non\u2011obvious step."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "inputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image", "corresponding_text": "generating a target image based on the target latent code", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target method generates an image from a latent code that results from combining image and text information, which fulfills the role of the second sub\u2011model. This approach is disclosed and is a routine application of latent\u2011code optimization, thus obvious."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "the second sub-model includes a first upsampler model and a second upsampler model configured for upsampling prior to generating the output image, the second upsampler model trained on images corrupted with blind super resolution (BSR) degradation", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No upsampler models or BSR\u2011trained components are mentioned in the target patent\u2019s claim or specification, so this element is not disclosed and remains novel."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "the second sub-model is different than the first sub-model", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target does not expressly state that the second sub\u2011model differs from a first sub\u2011model; it merely describes separate encoder and generator components without the explicit distinction required. Therefore the element is not disclosed."}, {"element_number": 9, "element_text": "making the output image, generated by the second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of: configured to train an image generation model using the output image; or associated with an image generation request", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not discuss exposing the generated image to a downstream device for further training or request handling. This functionality is absent, making the element novel."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement of the source patent lies in the two\u2011stage sub\u2011model architecture that first creates an image embedding from text (or text embedding) and then uses a distinct upsampling\u2011enhanced second sub\u2011model to produce the final image. The target prior art discloses generic text encoding, latent\u2011code generation, and image synthesis, but it does not teach the intermediate image\u2011embedding sub\u2011model, the specialized upsampler models trained on blind super\u2011resolution degradation, nor the explicit device\u2011level exposure of the output for further training. Because several of these pivotal elements (5, 7, 8, 9) are not found in the prior art and are non\u2011obvious, the source patent retains substantive novelty and inventive step. Consequently, the source patent is likely valid despite overlap on routine computing and text\u2011encoding features."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "f2f5a829-319a-4f65-88fe-dcd591e9b8b6", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:02:31.882450+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US11922550B1", "independent_claim": "1. A system comprising:\nat least one memory storing instructions;\nat least one processor configured to execute the instructions to perform operations for generating an image corresponding to a text input, the operations comprising:\naccessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder;\nreceiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding;\ninputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding;\ninputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image; wherein the second sub-model includes a first upsampler model and a second upsampler model configured for upsampling prior to generating the output image, the second upsampler model trained on images corrupted with blind super resolution (BSR) degradation; wherein the second sub-model is different than the first sub-model; and\nmaking the output image, generated by the second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of:\nconfigured to train an image generation model using the output image; or\nassociated with an image generation request.", "specification": "For example, in an embodiment, a non-transitory computer-readable medium may include instructions that are executable by one or more processors to perform operations for generating an image from a text description. The operations may include accessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder. The operations may include receiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding. The operations may include inputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding. The operations may include inputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image. The operations may include making the output image, generated by the first second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of: configured to train an image generation model using the output image; or associated with an image generation request.\nAccording to some disclosed embodiments, the at least one processor may be further configured to execute the instructions to perform operations comprising collecting a collecting a first data set and a second data set from a database, wherein the first data set comprises a set of images, wherein the second data set comprises a set of text descriptions corresponding to the set of images, jointly training an image encoder on the first data set and the text encoder on the second data set, receiving from the text encoder, a text embedding, and receiving, from the image encoder, an image embedding.\nThe operations may include encoding the output image with the image encoder, applying a decoder to the image, and obtaining a joint latent representation of the image.\nThe operations may include accessing the text embedding corresponding to the output image, based on a second text description, accessing a second text embedding, and generating a vector representation of the text embedding corresponding to the output image and the second text embedding. The operations may include performing an interpolation between the image embedding of the output image and the vector representation of the text embedding corresponding to the output image and the second text embedding, and based on the interpolation, generating a modified instance of the output image.\nThe operations may include prior to generating the corresponding image embedding, training a diffusion model including a transformer.\nThe operations may include the first sub-model being configured to encode, prior to generating the corresponding image embedding, at least one of the text description or the text embedding, via a transformer, as a sequence of tokens predicted autoregressively.\nThe operations may include up-sampling prior to generating the output image.\nThe operations may include inputting the text description and the text embedding into the first sub-model.\nThe operations may include inputting the text description and the corresponding image embedding into the second sub-model."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20220036127A1", "independent_claim": "1. One or more non-transitory computer readable storage media having a semantic image manipulation agent stored thereon, the semantic image manipulation agent including program instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, direct the one or more processors to:\nencode a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors,\nwherein the language-based editing instruction identifies a modification to a visual attribute of an input image;\nembed the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space;\nmanipulate an image feature map corresponding to the input image based on the textual feature vectors to modify the visual attribute of the input image within the visual-semantic joint embedding space; and\nconstruct a new image from the manipulated image feature map.", "specification": "[0005]\nOne or more embodiments described herein, among other benefits, solve one or more of the foregoing or other problems in the art by providing systems, methods, and non-transitory computer readable media for unsupervised semantic image manipulation using visual-semantic joint embeddings. In some implementations, the technology described includes one or more computer readable storage media having a semantic image manipulation agent stored thereon. The semantic image manipulation agent includes program instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, direct the one or more processors to encode a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors. The language-based editing instruction identifies a modification to a visual attribute of an input image. The program instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, further direct the one or more processors to embed the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space, manipulate an image feature map corresponding to the input image based on the textual feature vectors to modify the visual attribute of the input image within the visual-semantic joint embedding space, and construct a new image from the manipulated image feature map using an image decoder.\n[0006]\nIn some implementations, the technology described includes a method of performing unsupervised open-vocabulary semantic image manipulation. The method includes receiving an input image to be manipulated and a language-based editing instruction identifying a modification to a visual attribute of the input image, encoding, using an image encoder, the input image into an image feature map, encoding, using a text encoder, a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors. The textual feature vectors include a first textual feature vector corresponding to the visual attribute, and a second textual feature vector corresponding to the modification to the visual attribute. The method further includes embedding the image feature map and the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space, generating a new image feature map that modifies the visual attribute of the input image by manipulating the image feature map by the textual feature vectors within the visual-semantic joint embedding space, and constructing, using an image decoder, a new image from the manipulated image feature map.\n[0007]\nIn some implementations, the technology described includes a semantic image manipulation system including a joint embedding model, one or more processors, and an image decoder. The joint embedding model includes an image encoder, an image projection component, a text encoder, and a text projection component. The image encoder is configured to encode an input image into an image feature map. The image projection component configured to embed the image feature map into a visual-semantic joint embedding space. The text encoder configured to encode a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors, wherein the language-based editing instruction identifies a modification to a visual attribute of the input image. The text projection component configured to embed the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space. The one or more processors are configured to manipulate the image feature map corresponding to the input image based on the textual feature vectors to modify the visual attribute of the input image within the visual-semantic joint embedding space. The image decoder is configured to decode the image feature map in the visual-semantic embedding space into a new image.\n[0008]\nAdditional features and advantages of the present application will be set forth in the description which follows, and in part will be obvious from the description, or may be learned by the practice of such example embodiments."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US11922550B1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A system comprising:\nat least one memory storing instructions;\nat least one processor configured to execute the instructions to perform operations for generating an image corresponding to a text input, the operations comprising:\naccessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder;\nreceiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding;\ninputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding;\ninputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image; wherein the second sub-model includes a first upsampler model and a second upsampler model configured for upsampling prior to generating the output image, the second upsampler model trained on images corrupted with blind super resolution (BSR) degradation; wherein the second sub-model is different than the first sub-model; and\nmaking the output image, generated by the second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of:\nconfigured to train an image generation model using the output image; or\nassociated with an image generation request.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nFor example, in an embodiment, a non-transitory computer-readable medium may include instructions that are executable by one or more processors to perform operations for generating an image from a text description. The operations may include accessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder. The operations may include receiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding. The operations may include inputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding. The operations may include inputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image. The operations may include making the output image, generated by the first second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of: configured to train an image generation model using the output image; or associated with an image generation request.\nAccording to some disclosed embodiments, the at least one processor may be further configured to execute the instructions to perform operations comprising collecting a collecting a first data set and a second data set from a database, wherein the first data set comprises a set of images, wherein the second data set comprises a set of text descriptions corresponding to the set of images, jointly training an image encoder on the first data set and the text encoder on the second data set, receiving from the text encoder, a text embedding, and receiving, from the image encoder, an image embedding.\nThe operations may include encoding the output image with the image encoder, applying a decoder to the image, and obtaining a joint latent representation of the image.\nThe operations may include accessing the text embedding corresponding to the output image, based on a second text description, accessing a second text embedding, and generating a vector representation of the text embedding corresponding to the output image and the second text embedding. The operations may include performing an interpolation between the image embedding of the output image and the vector representation of the text embedding corresponding to the output image and the second text embedding, and based on the interpolation, generating a modified instance of the output image.\nThe operations may include prior to generating the corresponding image embedding, training a diffusion model including a transformer.\nThe operations may include the first sub-model being configured to encode, prior to generating the corresponding image embedding, at least one of the text description or the text embedding, via a transformer, as a sequence of tokens predicted autoregressively.\nThe operations may include up-sampling prior to generating the output image.\nThe operations may include inputting the text description and the text embedding into the first sub-model.\nThe operations may include inputting the text description and the corresponding image embedding into the second sub-model.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20220036127A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. One or more non-transitory computer readable storage media having a semantic image manipulation agent stored thereon, the semantic image manipulation agent including program instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, direct the one or more processors to:\nencode a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors,\nwherein the language-based editing instruction identifies a modification to a visual attribute of an input image;\nembed the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space;\nmanipulate an image feature map corresponding to the input image based on the textual feature vectors to modify the visual attribute of the input image within the visual-semantic joint embedding space; and\nconstruct a new image from the manipulated image feature map.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n[0005]\nOne or more embodiments described herein, among other benefits, solve one or more of the foregoing or other problems in the art by providing systems, methods, and non-transitory computer readable media for unsupervised semantic image manipulation using visual-semantic joint embeddings. In some implementations, the technology described includes one or more computer readable storage media having a semantic image manipulation agent stored thereon. The semantic image manipulation agent includes program instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, direct the one or more processors to encode a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors. The language-based editing instruction identifies a modification to a visual attribute of an input image. The program instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, further direct the one or more processors to embed the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space, manipulate an image feature map corresponding to the input image based on the textual feature vectors to modify the visual attribute of the input image within the visual-semantic joint embedding space, and construct a new image from the manipulated image feature map using an image decoder.\n[0006]\nIn some implementations, the technology described includes a method of performing unsupervised open-vocabulary semantic image manipulation. The method includes receiving an input image to be manipulated and a language-based editing instruction identifying a modification to a visual attribute of the input image, encoding, using an image encoder, the input image into an image feature map, encoding, using a text encoder, a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors. The textual feature vectors include a first textual feature vector corresponding to the visual attribute, and a second textual feature vector corresponding to the modification to the visual attribute. The method further includes embedding the image feature map and the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space, generating a new image feature map that modifies the visual attribute of the input image by manipulating the image feature map by the textual feature vectors within the visual-semantic joint embedding space, and constructing, using an image decoder, a new image from the manipulated image feature map.\n[0007]\nIn some implementations, the technology described includes a semantic image manipulation system including a joint embedding model, one or more processors, and an image decoder. The joint embedding model includes an image encoder, an image projection component, a text encoder, and a text projection component. The image encoder is configured to encode an input image into an image feature map. The image projection component configured to embed the image feature map into a visual-semantic joint embedding space. The text encoder configured to encode a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors, wherein the language-based editing instruction identifies a modification to a visual attribute of the input image. The text projection component configured to embed the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space. The one or more processors are configured to manipulate the image feature map corresponding to the input image based on the textual feature vectors to modify the visual attribute of the input image within the visual-semantic joint embedding space. The image decoder is configured to decode the image feature map in the visual-semantic embedding space into a new image.\n[0008]\nAdditional features and advantages of the present application will be set forth in the description which follows, and in part will be obvious from the description, or may be learned by the practice of such example embodiments."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one memory storing instructions\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"One or more non-transitory computer readable storage media having a semantic image manipulation agent stored thereon\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly recites non-transitory computer readable storage media storing an agent, which is equivalent to a memory storing instructions. Therefore the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and such storage is routine (inventive step Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor configured to execute the instructions to perform operations for generating an image corresponding to a text input, the operations comprising:\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"program instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, direct the one or more processors to:\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim mentions program instructions executed by one or more processors, covering a processor configured to run instructions. This is disclosed (novelty Y) and is a generic computing function (inventive step Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"accessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"encode a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"Target patent teaches encoding a language-based editing instruction (i.e., a text description) into textual feature vectors via a text encoder. This matches the element, so it is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an ordinary use of a text encoder (inventive step Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"embed the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent includes embedding the textual feature vectors (i.e., the text embedding) into a joint embedding space, which corresponds to receiving a text embedding. Hence disclosed (novelty Y) and routine (inventive step Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"inputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not describe a first sub\u2011model that takes text (or its embedding) to produce an image embedding. It only describes encoding an input image, not generating an image embedding from text. Therefore the element is not disclosed (novelty N) and represents a non\u2011obvious step (inventive step N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"inputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"construct a new image from the manipulated image feature map\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"Target patent teaches constructing a new image from a manipulated image feature map, which is functionally equivalent to generating an output image from a second model using text\u2011derived information. The concept is disclosed (novelty Y) and is a standard image\u2011generation step (inventive step Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"wherein the second sub-model includes a first upsampler model and a second upsampler model configured for upsampling prior to generating the output image, the second upsampler model trained on images corrupted with blind super resolution (BSR) degradation\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent contains no mention of upsampler models, blind super\u2011resolution degradation, or training thereof. Thus the element is not disclosed (novelty N) and adds a specific technical improvement (inventive step N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"wherein the second sub-model is different than the first sub-model\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No distinction between two sub\u2011models is described in the target patent; therefore the element is absent (novelty N) and represents a non\u2011obvious architectural distinction (inventive step N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"making the output image, generated by the second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of: configured to train an image generation model using the output image; or associated with an image generation request.\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose making the generated image available to a device for further training or request handling. Hence the element is novel (novelty N) and non\u2011obvious (inventive step N).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The source patent contains several core technical contributions\u2014specifically the first sub\u2011model that creates an image embedding from text, the use of dedicated upsampler models trained on blind\u2011super\u2011resolution data, the explicit distinction between two sub\u2011models, and the provision of the generated image to a device for downstream training or request handling\u2014that are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent. While many generic computing elements (memory, processor, basic text encoding, and image construction) are taught by the target patent, these routine features do not outweigh the novel and non\u2011obvious aspects listed above. Consequently, the source patent retains substantive novelty and inventive step in its key advancements and is likely to be considered valid over the cited prior art.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2877, "tokens_output": 3945, "latency_ms": 11637}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one memory storing instructions", "corresponding_text": "One or more non-transitory computer readable storage media having a semantic image manipulation agent stored thereon", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly recites non-transitory computer readable storage media storing an agent, which is equivalent to a memory storing instructions. Therefore the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and such storage is routine (inventive step Y)."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "at least one processor configured to execute the instructions to perform operations for generating an image corresponding to a text input, the operations comprising:", "corresponding_text": "program instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, direct the one or more processors to:", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim mentions program instructions executed by one or more processors, covering a processor configured to run instructions. This is disclosed (novelty Y) and is a generic computing function (inventive step Y)."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "accessing a text description and inputting the text description into a text encoder", "corresponding_text": "encode a language-based editing instruction into textual feature vectors", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "Target patent teaches encoding a language-based editing instruction (i.e., a text description) into textual feature vectors via a text encoder. This matches the element, so it is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an ordinary use of a text encoder (inventive step Y)."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "receiving, from the text encoder, a text embedding", "corresponding_text": "embed the textual feature vectors into a visual-semantic joint embedding space", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent includes embedding the textual feature vectors (i.e., the text embedding) into a joint embedding space, which corresponds to receiving a text embedding. Hence disclosed (novelty Y) and routine (inventive step Y)."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "inputting at least one of the text description or the text embedding into a first sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the text embedding, a corresponding image embedding", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not describe a first sub\u2011model that takes text (or its embedding) to produce an image embedding. It only describes encoding an input image, not generating an image embedding from text. Therefore the element is not disclosed (novelty N) and represents a non\u2011obvious step (inventive step N)."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "inputting at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, generated by the first sub-model, into a second sub-model configured to generate, based on at least one of the text description or the corresponding image embedding, an output image", "corresponding_text": "construct a new image from the manipulated image feature map", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "Target patent teaches constructing a new image from a manipulated image feature map, which is functionally equivalent to generating an output image from a second model using text\u2011derived information. The concept is disclosed (novelty Y) and is a standard image\u2011generation step (inventive step Y)."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "wherein the second sub-model includes a first upsampler model and a second upsampler model configured for upsampling prior to generating the output image, the second upsampler model trained on images corrupted with blind super resolution (BSR) degradation", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent contains no mention of upsampler models, blind super\u2011resolution degradation, or training thereof. Thus the element is not disclosed (novelty N) and adds a specific technical improvement (inventive step N)."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "wherein the second sub-model is different than the first sub-model", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No distinction between two sub\u2011models is described in the target patent; therefore the element is absent (novelty N) and represents a non\u2011obvious architectural distinction (inventive step N)."}, {"element_number": 9, "element_text": "making the output image, generated by the second sub-model, accessible to a device, wherein the device is at least one of: configured to train an image generation model using the output image; or associated with an image generation request.", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose making the generated image available to a device for further training or request handling. Hence the element is novel (novelty N) and non\u2011obvious (inventive step N)."}], "overall_opinion": "The source patent contains several core technical contributions\u2014specifically the first sub\u2011model that creates an image embedding from text, the use of dedicated upsampler models trained on blind\u2011super\u2011resolution data, the explicit distinction between two sub\u2011models, and the provision of the generated image to a device for downstream training or request handling\u2014that are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent. While many generic computing elements (memory, processor, basic text encoding, and image construction) are taught by the target patent, these routine features do not outweigh the novel and non\u2011obvious aspects listed above. Consequently, the source patent retains substantive novelty and inventive step in its key advancements and is likely to be considered valid over the cited prior art."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "06d25929-26f8-45a3-84e7-7e1569c75009", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:03:13.788563+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US20250291863A1", "independent_claim": "1. A machine-implemented method, comprising:\nreceiving, from a user, an initial query for a search;\nstoring the initial query in a search state database;\ndetermining a next search phase using the search state database; and\nin response to determining the next search phase is a search phase of requesting additional user input, performing operations comprising:\ngenerating a user prompt for the user using the initial query;\nstoring the user prompt in the search state database;\ndisplaying the user prompt to the user; and\nreceiving, from the user, the user input in response to the user prompt.", "specification": "FIG. 1 is a system diagram of an augmented search system 114 for performing an augmented search, according to some examples. An augmented search engine 104 uses the augmented search system 114 to provide an augmented search to a user 110.\n[0035]\nAn augmented search engine 104 serves as a processing system where initial queries are received, analyzed, and processed. The augmented search engine 104 is equipped with processes and models that enable the augmented search engine 104 to interpret initial queries, generate user prompts for additional information, and utilize search queries refined by the additional information to search through various indexes and databases for relevant information as described herein.\n[0036]\nThe user 110 interacts with the augmented search engine 104 through an augmented search engine UI 106 hosted by a client device 130. The augmented search engine UI 106 provides the interface through which the user submits their initial query and interacts with any subsequent user prompts or search results presented by the augmented search engine 104. In some examples, an augmented search engine UI 106 may be incorporated into various applications such as, but not limited to, conversational chatbots, e-commerce platforms, online libraries and digital archives, customer support and helpdesk software, enterprise resource planning systems, customer relationship management software, educational platforms and learning management systems, healthcare information systems, legal research databases, job search and recruitment portals, travel and accommodation booking sites, and the like. In some examples, the augmented search engine UI 106 may be implemented as a component of a search engine accessible using a web browser via the WAN 102. Example client devices 130 include any device capable of accessing the augmented search engine 104 via the WAN 102 including, but not limited to, a personal computer 122, a notepad 128, a smart phone 126, a laptop computer, a tablet computer, a smartwatch, a smart television, a gaming console, an e-reader, a digital assistant device, a wearable fitness tracker, an in-vehicle infotainment system, virtual reality headsets, augmented reality glasses, smart home control panels, interactive kiosks, smart mirrors, smart refrigerators, industrial control systems with network access, and the like.\n[0037]\nThe augmented search engine 104 is connected to a Wide Area Network (WAN) 102, which facilitates communication between the augmented search engine 104, the augmented search engine UI 106, and external resources. In some examples, WAN 102 may be of a variety of network types designed to extend over large geographical areas, facilitating communication, data exchange, and resource sharing across distant locations such as, but not limited to, the Internet, a corporate network, a research and education network, a telecommunication network, and the like. The WAN 102 enables the augmented search engine 104 to access one or more external search engines 108 and one or more external generative models 112, expanding the scope of the search beyond the internal capabilities and databases of the augmented search engine 104."}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20250191082A1", "independent_claim": ". A computing system for interactive prompting for an agricultural food product supply chain, the computing system comprising:\na computing device including processing circuitry configured to execute instructions using portions of associated memory to implement an interactive prompting program, wherein the processing circuitry is configured to:\nobtain domain constructed ontologies related to an agricultural food product from a plurality of data sources; and\nconstruct a knowledge graph based on the ontologies, and\nin an inference phase:\nreceive a prompt for the agricultural food product via a prompt interface in a turn-based dialog session;\nin response to the prompt, identify at least one ontology-level node in a first layer of the knowledge graph that matches information in the prompt;\ngenerate one or more sub-question prompts to identify factors relating to at least one of economic structure, location of a growing facility, certification of the growing facility, and type of the agricultural food product;\ninput the one or more sub-question prompts to a large language model, and, in response, receive as output from the large language model one or more sub-questions;\noutput the one or more sub-questions for display in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface;\nreceive responses to the one or more sub-questions in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface;\nidentify one or more second-level nodes in a second, middle layer of the knowledge graph based on the responses to the one or more sub-questions;\nperform a multi-hop query to identify one or more instance-level nodes in a third layer of the knowledge graph; and\noutput text data corresponding to the one or more instance-level nodes as an answer to the prompt for the agricultural food product in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface.", "specification": "Continuing with FIG. 1 , the processing circuitry 18 is configured to execute instructions using portions of associated memory 22 to implement an interactive prompting program 26. At a high level, the interactive prompting program 26 leverages a knowledge graph to generate sub-questions in response to a user prompt to improve the accuracy of information output from a large language model.\n[0021]\nThe client computing device 16 includes a user interface 28 that is displayed on a display 30. A prompt interface 32 within the user interface 28 is configured to receive a prompt 34 input from a user, which may be a question or an instruction relating to a product. The prompt 34 is received by the interactive prompting program 26 as text input 36, and tokenized by a tokenizer 38.\n[0022]\nAs discussed above, the accuracy of the answer to the prompt 34 is guided and enhanced by utilizing data from a knowledge graph (KG) 40. As described in detail below with reference to FIGS. 3 and 4 , the KG 40 is constructed via one or more large language models using open source data 42 and user private data 44 to obtain domain constructed ontologies related to the product. Text data is generated from the open source data 42 and user private data 44, and embeddings are generated from the text data. The KG 40 is stored in a vector database 46, and information related to the prompt 34 is retrieved from the embeddings via a retrieval model, such as a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), as described below. In the example described herein, the KG 40 includes sub-graphs with data related to factor ontology 48, decision making 50, and market options 52. However, it will be appreciated that the knowledge graph 40 may include other types of sub-graphs.\n[0023]\nTo retrieve information relevant to the prompt 34, the interactive prompting program 26 implements a RAG model 54. A knowledge graph query engine 56 included in the RAG model 54 is configured to receive tokenized text 58 that represents the user-input prompt 34. The knowledge graph query engine 56 sends a query 60 for a semantic search to the vector database 46 to identify at least one ontology-level node in a first layer of the KG 40 that matches information in the prompt 34. A result 62, including contextual data related to the query 34, is returned to the knowledge graph query engine 56, which in turn transmits the result 62 to a sub-question generator 64. The sub-question generator 64 processes the contextual data to determine keywords, and the keywords are used to generate one or more sub-question prompts 66 to identify factors relating to at least one of economic structure, location of a growing or production facility, certification of the facility, and type of the product. A large language model 68 then outputs one or more sub-questions 70 according to the one or more sub-question prompts 66. In the example described herein, there are two sub-questions 70A, 70B; however, it will be appreciated that fewer or more sub-questions may be generated.\n[0024]\nThe sub-questions 70A, 70B are sent as text output 72 to the client computing device 18, where they are displayed in the prompt interface 32. As described in detail below with reference to FIG. 7A, responses 74A, 74B to the respective sub-questions 70A, 70B are entered in the prompt interface 32.\n[0025]\nSimilar to the processing of the initial prompt 34 described above, the responses to the one or more sub-questions 74A, 74B are received by the interactive prompting program 26 as text input 36 and tokenized by the tokenizer 38. The knowledge graph query engine 56 queries the vector database 46 to identify one or more second-level nodes in a second, middle layer of the KG 40, based on the responses 74A, 74B to the sub-questions 70A, 70B. As described below with reference to FIGS. 7B and 7C, a multi-hop query is performed within the KG 40 to identify one or more instance-level nodes in the third layer of the KG 40. Embeddings representing the instance-level nodes are returned to the RAG model 54, processed, and included in prompts sent to the large language model 68, such that the text output 72 of the LLM 68 considers the one or more instance-level nodes. The RAG model 54 queries the knowledge graph on each iteration through the retrieval-augmented generation process from prompt 34 to answer 76. Because of this, answer 76 can be referred to as a knowledge graph-enhanced large language model (KG-enhanced LLM) answer 76 to the prompt 34 for the product.\n[0026]\nIt will be appreciated that the LLM 68 can have tens of millions to billions of parameters, non-limiting examples of which include GPT-3, BLOOM, and LLaMa-2. Further, the LLM can be configured as a multi-modal generative model configured to receive multi-modal input including natural language text input as a first mode of input and image, video, or audio as a second mode of input, and generate output including natural language text based on the multi-modal input. The output of the multi-modal model may additionally include a second mode of output such as image, video, or audio output. Non-limiting examples of multi-modal generative models include Kosmos-2 and GPT-4 VISUAL. Further, the trained generative language model can be configured to have a generative pre-trained transformer architecture, examples of which are used in the GPT-3 and GPT-4 models. Although the LLM 68 is depicted as being implemented on computing device 12, it will be appreciated that distributed processing architectures are envisioned in which the processing circuity and logic depicted as implemented on computing device 12 are implemented across multiple connected devices. In such implementations, the LLM 68 can be implemented on a different server than the remaining components, for example."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US20250291863A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n1. A machine-implemented method, comprising:\nreceiving, from a user, an initial query for a search;\nstoring the initial query in a search state database;\ndetermining a next search phase using the search state database; and\nin response to determining the next search phase is a search phase of requesting additional user input, performing operations comprising:\ngenerating a user prompt for the user using the initial query;\nstoring the user prompt in the search state database;\ndisplaying the user prompt to the user; and\nreceiving, from the user, the user input in response to the user prompt.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n1 is a system diagram of an augmented search system 114 for performing an augmented search, according to some examples. An augmented search engine 104 uses the augmented search system 114 to provide an augmented search to a user 110.\n[0035]\nAn augmented search engine 104 serves as a processing system where initial queries are received, analyzed, and processed. The augmented search engine 104 is equipped with processes and models that enable the augmented search engine 104 to interpret initial queries, generate user prompts for additional information, and utilize search queries refined by the additional information to search through various indexes and databases for relevant information as described herein.\n[0036]\nThe user 110 interacts with the augmented search engine 104 through an augmented search engine UI 106 hosted by a client device 130. The augmented search engine UI 106 provides the interface through which the user submits their initial query and interacts with any subsequent user prompts or search results presented by the augmented search engine 104. In some examples, an augmented search engine UI 106 may be incorporated into various applications such as, but not limited to, conversational chatbots, e-commerce platforms, online libraries and digital archives, customer support and helpdesk software, enterprise resource planning systems, customer relationship management software, educational platforms and learning management systems, healthcare information systems, legal research databases, job search and recruitment portals, travel and accommodation booking sites, and the like. In some examples, the augmented search engine UI 106 may be implemented as a component of a search engine accessible using a web browser via the WAN 102. Example client devices 130 include any device capable of accessing the augmented search engine 104 via the WAN 102 including, but not limited to, a personal computer 122, a notepad 128, a smart phone 126, a laptop computer, a tablet computer, a smartwatch, a smart television, a gaming console, an e-reader, a digital assistant device, a wearable fitness tracker, an in-vehicle infotainment system, virtual reality headsets, augmented reality glasses, smart home control panels, interactive kiosks, smart mirrors, smart refrigerators, industrial control systems with network access, and the like.\n[0037]\nThe augmented search engine 104 is connected to a Wide Area Network (WAN) 102, which facilitates communication between the augmented search engine 104, the augmented search engine UI 106, and external resources. In some examples, WAN 102 may be of a variety of network types designed to extend over large geographical areas, facilitating communication, data exchange, and resource sharing across distant locations such as, but not limited to, the Internet, a corporate network, a research and education network, a telecommunication network, and the like. The WAN 102 enables the augmented search engine 104 to access one or more external search engines 108 and one or more external generative models 112, expanding the scope of the search beyond the internal capabilities and databases of the augmented search engine 104.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20250191082A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. A computing system for interactive prompting for an agricultural food product supply chain, the computing system comprising:\na computing device including processing circuitry configured to execute instructions using portions of associated memory to implement an interactive prompting program, wherein the processing circuitry is configured to:\nobtain domain constructed ontologies related to an agricultural food product from a plurality of data sources; and\nconstruct a knowledge graph based on the ontologies, and\nin an inference phase:\nreceive a prompt for the agricultural food product via a prompt interface in a turn-based dialog session;\nin response to the prompt, identify at least one ontology-level node in a first layer of the knowledge graph that matches information in the prompt;\ngenerate one or more sub-question prompts to identify factors relating to at least one of economic structure, location of a growing facility, certification of the growing facility, and type of the agricultural food product;\ninput the one or more sub-question prompts to a large language model, and, in response, receive as output from the large language model one or more sub-questions;\noutput the one or more sub-questions for display in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface;\nreceive responses to the one or more sub-questions in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface;\nidentify one or more second-level nodes in a second, middle layer of the knowledge graph based on the responses to the one or more sub-questions;\nperform a multi-hop query to identify one or more instance-level nodes in a third layer of the knowledge graph; and\noutput text data corresponding to the one or more instance-level nodes as an answer to the prompt for the agricultural food product in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface.\n\n**Specification Support:**\n1 , the processing circuitry 18 is configured to execute instructions using portions of associated memory 22 to implement an interactive prompting program 26. At a high level, the interactive prompting program 26 leverages a knowledge graph to generate sub-questions in response to a user prompt to improve the accuracy of information output from a large language model.\n[0021]\nThe client computing device 16 includes a user interface 28 that is displayed on a display 30. A prompt interface 32 within the user interface 28 is configured to receive a prompt 34 input from a user, which may be a question or an instruction relating to a product. The prompt 34 is received by the interactive prompting program 26 as text input 36, and tokenized by a tokenizer 38.\n[0022]\nAs discussed above, the accuracy of the answer to the prompt 34 is guided and enhanced by utilizing data from a knowledge graph (KG) 40. 3 and 4 , the KG 40 is constructed via one or more large language models using open source data 42 and user private data 44 to obtain domain constructed ontologies related to the product. Text data is generated from the open source data 42 and user private data 44, and embeddings are generated from the text data. The KG 40 is stored in a vector database 46, and information related to the prompt 34 is retrieved from the embeddings via a retrieval model, such as a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), as described below. In the example described herein, the KG 40 includes sub-graphs with data related to factor ontology 48, decision making 50, and market options 52. However, it will be appreciated that the knowledge graph 40 may include other types of sub-graphs.\n[0023]\nTo retrieve information relevant to the prompt 34, the interactive prompting program 26 implements a RAG model 54. A knowledge graph query engine 56 included in the RAG model 54 is configured to receive tokenized text 58 that represents the user-input prompt 34. The knowledge graph query engine 56 sends a query 60 for a semantic search to the vector database 46 to identify at least one ontology-level node in a first layer of the KG 40 that matches information in the prompt 34. A result 62, including contextual data related to the query 34, is returned to the knowledge graph query engine 56, which in turn transmits the result 62 to a sub-question generator 64. The sub-question generator 64 processes the contextual data to determine keywords, and the keywords are used to generate one or more sub-question prompts 66 to identify factors relating to at least one of economic structure, location of a growing or production facility, certification of the facility, and type of the product. A large language model 68 then outputs one or more sub-questions 70 according to the one or more sub-question prompts 66. In the example described herein, there are two sub-questions 70A, 70B; however, it will be appreciated that fewer or more sub-questions may be generated.\n[0024]\nThe sub-questions 70A, 70B are sent as text output 72 to the client computing device 18, where they are displayed in the prompt interface 32. 7A, responses 74A, 74B to the respective sub-questions 70A, 70B are entered in the prompt interface 32.\n[0025]\nSimilar to the processing of the initial prompt 34 described above, the responses to the one or more sub-questions 74A, 74B are received by the interactive prompting program 26 as text input 36 and tokenized by the tokenizer 38. The knowledge graph query engine 56 queries the vector database 46 to identify one or more second-level nodes in a second, middle layer of the KG 40, based on the responses 74A, 74B to the sub-questions 70A, 70B. 7B and 7C, a multi-hop query is performed within the KG 40 to identify one or more instance-level nodes in the third layer of the KG 40. Embeddings representing the instance-level nodes are returned to the RAG model 54, processed, and included in prompts sent to the large language model 68, such that the text output 72 of the LLM 68 considers the one or more instance-level nodes. The RAG model 54 queries the knowledge graph on each iteration through the retrieval-augmented generation process from prompt 34 to answer 76. Because of this, answer 76 can be referred to as a knowledge graph-enhanced large language model (KG-enhanced LLM) answer 76 to the prompt 34 for the product.\n[0026]\nIt will be appreciated that the LLM 68 can have tens of millions to billions of parameters, non-limiting examples of which include GPT-3, BLOOM, and LLaMa-2. Further, the LLM can be configured as a multi-modal generative model configured to receive multi-modal input including natural language text input as a first mode of input and image, video, or audio as a second mode of input, and generate output including natural language text based on the multi-modal input. The output of the multi-modal model may additionally include a second mode of output such as image, video, or audio output. Non-limiting examples of multi-modal generative models include Kosmos-2 and GPT-4 VISUAL. Further, the trained generative language model can be configured to have a generative pre-trained transformer architecture, examples of which are used in the GPT-3 and GPT-4 models. Although the LLM 68 is depicted as being implemented on computing device 12, it will be appreciated that distributed processing architectures are envisioned in which the processing circuity and logic depicted as implemented on computing device 12 are implemented across multiple connected devices. In such implementations, the LLM 68 can be implemented on a different server than the remaining components, for example."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving, from a user, an initial query for a search\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receive a prompt for the agricultural food product via a prompt interface in a turn-based dialog session\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly recites receiving a user prompt via a prompt interface, which maps to receiving an initial query. This feature is well\u2011known in interactive systems, so it lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"storing the initial query in a search state database\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose a dedicated \\\"search state database\\\" for persisting the initial query; it focuses on a knowledge graph and vector store for ontology data. Absence of such storage makes the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining a next search phase using the search state database\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No language in the target patent describes using a stored search state to decide a subsequent search phase. The closest teaching is an inference phase, but it is not tied to a \\\"search state database\\\". Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating a user prompt for the user using the initial query\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"generate one or more sub-question prompts to identify factors relating to at least one of economic structure, location of a growing facility, certification of the growing facility, and type of the agricultural food product\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches generation of sub\u2011question prompts based on the initial user prompt, which corresponds to generating a user prompt using the initial query. Prompt generation is disclosed and would be considered obvious to a person skilled in the art.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"storing the user prompt in the search state database\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not mention persisting the generated sub\u2011question prompts in a search\u2011state database; it only outputs them for display. Lack of such storage renders the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"displaying the user prompt to the user\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"output the one or more sub-questions for display in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"Displaying generated prompts to the user is explicitly recited in the target patent. This is a routine UI operation and therefore lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving, from the user, the user input in response to the user prompt\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receive responses to the one or more sub-questions in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses receiving user responses to the displayed sub\u2011questions via the same prompt interface. This is a standard interactive step and is both disclosed and obvious.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core inventive contribution of the source claim\u2014generating a user prompt based on an initial query\u2014is already taught by the prior art, and the associated steps of displaying and receiving the prompt are routine. While a few ancillary features (storing the initial query, using a search\u2011state database to decide the next phase, and persisting the generated prompt) are not disclosed and thus provide limited novelty, they are peripheral to the main technical advancement. Because the primary functional elements are found in the target patent and would be obvious to a skilled practitioner, the source patent is likely not valid for lack of overall novelty and inventive step.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3541, "tokens_output": 2447, "latency_ms": 6075}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving, from a user, an initial query for a search", "corresponding_text": "receive a prompt for the agricultural food product via a prompt interface in a turn-based dialog session", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly recites receiving a user prompt via a prompt interface, which maps to receiving an initial query. This feature is well\u2011known in interactive systems, so it lacks novelty and is obvious."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "storing the initial query in a search state database", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose a dedicated \"search state database\" for persisting the initial query; it focuses on a knowledge graph and vector store for ontology data. Absence of such storage makes the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "determining a next search phase using the search state database", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No language in the target patent describes using a stored search state to decide a subsequent search phase. The closest teaching is an inference phase, but it is not tied to a \"search state database\". Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "generating a user prompt for the user using the initial query", "corresponding_text": "generate one or more sub-question prompts to identify factors relating to at least one of economic structure, location of a growing facility, certification of the growing facility, and type of the agricultural food product", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent teaches generation of sub\u2011question prompts based on the initial user prompt, which corresponds to generating a user prompt using the initial query. Prompt generation is disclosed and would be considered obvious to a person skilled in the art."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "storing the user prompt in the search state database", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not mention persisting the generated sub\u2011question prompts in a search\u2011state database; it only outputs them for display. Lack of such storage renders the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "displaying the user prompt to the user", "corresponding_text": "output the one or more sub-questions for display in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "Displaying generated prompts to the user is explicitly recited in the target patent. This is a routine UI operation and therefore lacks novelty and is obvious."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "receiving, from the user, the user input in response to the user prompt", "corresponding_text": "receive responses to the one or more sub-questions in the turn-based dialog session via the prompt interface", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses receiving user responses to the displayed sub\u2011questions via the same prompt interface. This is a standard interactive step and is both disclosed and obvious."}], "overall_opinion": "The core inventive contribution of the source claim\u2014generating a user prompt based on an initial query\u2014is already taught by the prior art, and the associated steps of displaying and receiving the prompt are routine. While a few ancillary features (storing the initial query, using a search\u2011state database to decide the next phase, and persisting the generated prompt) are not disclosed and thus provide limited novelty, they are peripheral to the main technical advancement. Because the primary functional elements are found in the target patent and would be obvious to a skilled practitioner, the source patent is likely not valid for lack of overall novelty and inventive step."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated", "Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "4f1e3deb-280c-42aa-81c8-38eec9468ee2", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:03:53.883339+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US8638369B2", "independent_claim": ". A portable electronic device comprising:\na digital imaging subsystem that includes a lens having an optical axis;\na case that encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship to the case;\na removable panel coupled to the case, the removable panel covering the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem, the removable panel being removable and rotatable to be held by the case in one of a first orientation and a second orientation relative to the case; and\nan optical component supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the first orientation, and the optical component is removed from the optical axis and does not alter optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the second orientation.", "specification": "A portable electronic device includes a digital imaging subsystem with a lens having an optical axis. A case encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a first defined positional relationship to the case. A removable panel is coupled to the case and held in a second defined positional relationship to the case that covers the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem. An optical component is supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem. The device may include a power supply and an electronic control system coupled to the digital imaging subsystem and the power supply enclosed in and supported by the case. Electrical connectors may couple the power supply and the electronic control system to an electrical component on the removable panel.\n"}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20050253923A1", "independent_claim": " A cellular phone equipped with a camera which can take a moving picture of a subject, the cellular phone with a built-in camera comprising\na lighting device for lighting a subject by means of a light-emitting diode,\na switching device for turning on said lighting device,\na light distribution lens for condensing light radiated from said lighting device, toward the subject, and\na transparent cover for protecting said light distribution lens, the transparent cover being disposed on a subject side, which is a front side, of said lighting device.", "specification": "In an aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera is a cellular phone equipped with a camera that can take a moving picture of a subject, including a lighting device for lighting a subject by means of a light-emitting diode, a switching device for turning on the lighting device, a light distribution lens for condensing (collecting) light emitted from the lighting device onto the subject, and a transparent cover, disposed in the front, or on the subject side of the lighting device, for protecting the light distribution lens. A light utilizing a light-emitting diode is also provided. Accordingly, the size, weight, and profile (thickness) of the cellular phone can be reduced, and also, a subject can be continuously illuminated by the light while a moving picture is being taken by the camera. Because of the light distribution lens for condensing light emitted forward from the light-emitting diode onto the subject, light emitted from the light-emitting diode can be efficiently directed onto the subject.\n[0014]\nThe transparent cover is formed as an integral part of a protection cover of a component which produces a visual effect on the user of the cellular phone with a built-in camera or as an integral part of a protection cover of a display device for displaying an image of a subject or an image received from a telephone of the party at the other end of the communication line. This helps reduce the number of components, the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone further. As a lighting device, a light-emitting diode is directly mounted on a printed circuit board. This helps reduce the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone. A light amount detection device which can detect an insufficient amount of ambient light is provided in a stage preceding a switch, and when the light amount detection device detects and outputs that the light amount is low, switching takes place to turn on the light automatically. Accordingly, the user of the cellular phone with a built-in camera can send an image without worrying about the amount of ambient light. The camera is used as a light amount detection device, and the amount of light is detected by the level of reception signal of the camera. If the amount of light is insufficient, the light is automatically turned on. Therefore, the need for providing a separate light detection device is eliminated, so that the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further.\n[0015]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a light distribution lens with a support for which is mounted to a light-emitting diode. Accordingly, the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further. Moreover, the positional relationship between the optical axis of the light distribution lens and the light emission point of the light-emitting diode can be determined in higher precision. Accordingly, design can be performed to improve light condensing (collection) efficiency by reducing the light distribution angle. This will reduce irregularities in illuminance of the subject resulting from variations in position, and the illuminance of the subject can be improved.\n[0016]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a transparent cover in which a convex lens portion having the function of a light distribution lens is formed. This enables the size, weight, and profile to be reduced further.\n[0017]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has both a transparent cover including a convex lens and a light distribution lens equipped with a support. As a result, it is possible to reduce the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone, and also it is possible to absorb variations in light distribution among individual light-emitting diodes, positional variations in production, and other differences.\n[0018]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a lighting device including a film-like member in the front, or on the subject side of the light-emitting diode. The front-to-back optical transmittance of the film-like member is lower than the back-to-front optical transmittance. This makes it harder to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance.\n[0019]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a lighting device in which an optical diffusion portion for diffusing light is provided at or on at least one orthogonal side (or surface) of a component, among components which have a side (surface) orthogonal to light (the optical axis of light) emitted from the light-emitting diode and allow light emitted from the light-emitting diode to pass through, and the optical diffusion portion is formed at or on one or more surfaces or sides of a component included in the lighting device. This makes it hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance. In addition, either improvement can be made selectively: improving optical transmittance by reducing the amount of attenuation of light emitted from the light-emitting diode or reducing variations in distribution of illumination at the subject. In the lighting device, an optical diffusion plate having an optical diffusion layer on the side of the subject is provided in front of the light-emitting diode, so that light reflectivity becomes greater at the front surface of the optical diffusion plate than at the rear surface of the optical diffusion plate. Moreover, light transmitted from the inside to the outside is diffused again by the optical diffusion plate. In addition, the diffusion layer of the optical diffusion plate has a rough (bumpy) surface (surface with concaves and convexes). This reduces the amount of attenuation of illuminating light emitted from the light-emitting diode and improves optical transmittance, making it hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance.\n[0020]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has an optical diffusion plate formed in such a manner that the angle of light diffusion becomes smaller in the peripheral region than around the optical axis of a light-emitting diode. As a result, in addition to the merit that the amount of attenuation of illuminating light emitted from the light-emitting diode to be reduced and optical transmittance to be improved, variations in distribution of illumination at the subject decrease, and it is hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance, there is another merit that the amount of light diffusion around the optical diffusion plate is reduced, reducing the amount of light diffusion outside the view angle and increasing the amount of light within the view angle, so that the subject can be brightly lighted.\n[0021]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera uses a lighting device is provided as an external light. The external light is a detachable separate unit which has a plug which enables electrical and mechanical connection to the cellular phone with a built-in camera. The main unit of the cellular phone with a built-in camera has a jack to which the plug is detachably connected. As a result, in addition to the merits that it is possible to light the subject continuously while a moving picture is being taken by the camera, and both a still picture and a moving picture of a subject can be taken by the camera in low light, there is another merit that the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further. A separate lighting unit for use with a camera has the same configuration as the lighting device in the cellular phone with a built-in camera described above, and also has a plug which can be electrically and mechanically connected to the jack of the lighting device in the cellular phone with a built-in camera. Because the lighting unit (external light) is provided as a separate unit detachable from the cellular phone with a built-in camera and need not be included in the camera, the size, weight, and profile of the camera can be reduced further. This facilitates maintenance and replacement of the lighting unit."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US8638369B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. A portable electronic device comprising:\na digital imaging subsystem that includes a lens having an optical axis;\na case that encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship to the case;\na removable panel coupled to the case, the removable panel covering the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem, the removable panel being removable and rotatable to be held by the case in one of a first orientation and a second orientation relative to the case; and\nan optical component supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the first orientation, and the optical component is removed from the optical axis and does not alter optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the second orientation.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA portable electronic device includes a digital imaging subsystem with a lens having an optical axis. A case encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a first defined positional relationship to the case. A removable panel is coupled to the case and held in a second defined positional relationship to the case that covers the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem. An optical component is supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem. The device may include a power supply and an electronic control system coupled to the digital imaging subsystem and the power supply enclosed in and supported by the case. Electrical connectors may couple the power supply and the electronic control system to an electrical component on the removable panel.\n\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20050253923A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n A cellular phone equipped with a camera which can take a moving picture of a subject, the cellular phone with a built-in camera comprising\na lighting device for lighting a subject by means of a light-emitting diode,\na switching device for turning on said lighting device,\na light distribution lens for condensing light radiated from said lighting device, toward the subject, and\na transparent cover for protecting said light distribution lens, the transparent cover being disposed on a subject side, which is a front side, of said lighting device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nIn an aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera is a cellular phone equipped with a camera that can take a moving picture of a subject, including a lighting device for lighting a subject by means of a light-emitting diode, a switching device for turning on the lighting device, a light distribution lens for condensing (collecting) light emitted from the lighting device onto the subject, and a transparent cover, disposed in the front, or on the subject side of the lighting device, for protecting the light distribution lens. A light utilizing a light-emitting diode is also provided. Accordingly, the size, weight, and profile (thickness) of the cellular phone can be reduced, and also, a subject can be continuously illuminated by the light while a moving picture is being taken by the camera. Because of the light distribution lens for condensing light emitted forward from the light-emitting diode onto the subject, light emitted from the light-emitting diode can be efficiently directed onto the subject.\n[0014]\nThe transparent cover is formed as an integral part of a protection cover of a component which produces a visual effect on the user of the cellular phone with a built-in camera or as an integral part of a protection cover of a display device for displaying an image of a subject or an image received from a telephone of the party at the other end of the communication line. This helps reduce the number of components, the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone further. As a lighting device, a light-emitting diode is directly mounted on a printed circuit board. This helps reduce the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone. A light amount detection device which can detect an insufficient amount of ambient light is provided in a stage preceding a switch, and when the light amount detection device detects and outputs that the light amount is low, switching takes place to turn on the light automatically. Accordingly, the user of the cellular phone with a built-in camera can send an image without worrying about the amount of ambient light. The camera is used as a light amount detection device, and the amount of light is detected by the level of reception signal of the camera. If the amount of light is insufficient, the light is automatically turned on. Therefore, the need for providing a separate light detection device is eliminated, so that the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further.\n[0015]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a light distribution lens with a support for which is mounted to a light-emitting diode. Accordingly, the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further. Moreover, the positional relationship between the optical axis of the light distribution lens and the light emission point of the light-emitting diode can be determined in higher precision. Accordingly, design can be performed to improve light condensing (collection) efficiency by reducing the light distribution angle. This will reduce irregularities in illuminance of the subject resulting from variations in position, and the illuminance of the subject can be improved.\n[0016]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a transparent cover in which a convex lens portion having the function of a light distribution lens is formed. This enables the size, weight, and profile to be reduced further.\n[0017]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has both a transparent cover including a convex lens and a light distribution lens equipped with a support. As a result, it is possible to reduce the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone, and also it is possible to absorb variations in light distribution among individual light-emitting diodes, positional variations in production, and other differences.\n[0018]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a lighting device including a film-like member in the front, or on the subject side of the light-emitting diode. The front-to-back optical transmittance of the film-like member is lower than the back-to-front optical transmittance. This makes it harder to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance.\n[0019]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a lighting device in which an optical diffusion portion for diffusing light is provided at or on at least one orthogonal side (or surface) of a component, among components which have a side (surface) orthogonal to light (the optical axis of light) emitted from the light-emitting diode and allow light emitted from the light-emitting diode to pass through, and the optical diffusion portion is formed at or on one or more surfaces or sides of a component included in the lighting device. This makes it hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance. In addition, either improvement can be made selectively: improving optical transmittance by reducing the amount of attenuation of light emitted from the light-emitting diode or reducing variations in distribution of illumination at the subject. In the lighting device, an optical diffusion plate having an optical diffusion layer on the side of the subject is provided in front of the light-emitting diode, so that light reflectivity becomes greater at the front surface of the optical diffusion plate than at the rear surface of the optical diffusion plate. Moreover, light transmitted from the inside to the outside is diffused again by the optical diffusion plate. This reduces the amount of attenuation of illuminating light emitted from the light-emitting diode and improves optical transmittance, making it hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance.\n[0020]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has an optical diffusion plate formed in such a manner that the angle of light diffusion becomes smaller in the peripheral region than around the optical axis of a light-emitting diode. As a result, in addition to the merit that the amount of attenuation of illuminating light emitted from the light-emitting diode to be reduced and optical transmittance to be improved, variations in distribution of illumination at the subject decrease, and it is hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance, there is another merit that the amount of light diffusion around the optical diffusion plate is reduced, reducing the amount of light diffusion outside the view angle and increasing the amount of light within the view angle, so that the subject can be brightly lighted.\n[0021]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera uses a lighting device is provided as an external light. The external light is a detachable separate unit which has a plug which enables electrical and mechanical connection to the cellular phone with a built-in camera. The main unit of the cellular phone with a built-in camera has a jack to which the plug is detachably connected. As a result, in addition to the merits that it is possible to light the subject continuously while a moving picture is being taken by the camera, and both a still picture and a moving picture of a subject can be taken by the camera in low light, there is another merit that the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further. A separate lighting unit for use with a camera has the same configuration as the lighting device in the cellular phone with a built-in camera described above, and also has a plug which can be electrically and mechanically connected to the jack of the lighting device in the cellular phone with a built-in camera. Because the lighting unit (external light) is provided as a separate unit detachable from the cellular phone with a built-in camera and need not be included in the camera, the size, weight, and profile of the camera can be reduced further. This facilitates maintenance and replacement of the lighting unit."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"a digital imaging subsystem that includes a lens having an optical axis\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses a lighting device, a light distribution lens and a transparent cover, but does not disclose a digital imaging subsystem with a lens having an optical axis. No matching language is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a case that encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship to the case\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent describes a cellular phone housing a built\u2011in camera, but the independent claim and specification do not recite a case that specifically encloses and supports the imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship. Therefore the element is not disclosed and retains novelty and inventive step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"a removable panel coupled to the case, the removable panel covering the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem, the removable panel being removable and rotatable to be held by the case in one of a first orientation and a second orientation relative to the case\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art teaches a transparent cover that is integral to the phone and not removable or rotatable. No language describing a removable, rotatable panel with two selectable orientations is present, so this element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"an optical component supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the first orientation, and the optical component is removed from the optical axis and does not alter optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the second orientation\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent mentions a light distribution lens and a transparent cover, but none are described as being mounted on a removable panel that can be repositioned to change optical characteristics. Consequently the element is not disclosed and remains novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014namely a removable, rotatable panel that carries an optical component which can be positioned to modify or not modify the imaging subsystem\u2019s optical characteristics\u2014is not taught or suggested by the prior\u2011art reference. None of the claim elements are found in the target patent, and each therefore retains both novelty and an inventive step. Accordingly, the source patent appears valid over the cited prior art.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3273, "tokens_output": 1691, "latency_ms": 4172}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "a digital imaging subsystem that includes a lens having an optical axis", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent discloses a lighting device, a light distribution lens and a transparent cover, but does not disclose a digital imaging subsystem with a lens having an optical axis. No matching language is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "a case that encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship to the case", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent describes a cellular phone housing a built\u2011in camera, but the independent claim and specification do not recite a case that specifically encloses and supports the imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship. Therefore the element is not disclosed and retains novelty and inventive step."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "a removable panel coupled to the case, the removable panel covering the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem, the removable panel being removable and rotatable to be held by the case in one of a first orientation and a second orientation relative to the case", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art teaches a transparent cover that is integral to the phone and not removable or rotatable. No language describing a removable, rotatable panel with two selectable orientations is present, so this element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "an optical component supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the first orientation, and the optical component is removed from the optical axis and does not alter optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the second orientation", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent mentions a light distribution lens and a transparent cover, but none are described as being mounted on a removable panel that can be repositioned to change optical characteristics. Consequently the element is not disclosed and remains novel and non\u2011obvious."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014namely a removable, rotatable panel that carries an optical component which can be positioned to modify or not modify the imaging subsystem\u2019s optical characteristics\u2014is not taught or suggested by the prior\u2011art reference. None of the claim elements are found in the target patent, and each therefore retains both novelty and an inventive step. Accordingly, the source patent appears valid over the cited prior art."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "e3a5c26b-022f-43d7-99e6-e9c7fb754215", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:04:33.878381+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "US8638369B2", "independent_claim": ". A portable electronic device comprising:\na digital imaging subsystem that includes a lens having an optical axis;\na case that encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship to the case;\na removable panel coupled to the case, the removable panel covering the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem, the removable panel being removable and rotatable to be held by the case in one of a first orientation and a second orientation relative to the case; and\nan optical component supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the first orientation, and the optical component is removed from the optical axis and does not alter optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the second orientation.", "specification": "A portable electronic device includes a digital imaging subsystem with a lens having an optical axis. A case encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a first defined positional relationship to the case. A removable panel is coupled to the case and held in a second defined positional relationship to the case that covers the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem. An optical component is supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem. The device may include a power supply and an electronic control system coupled to the digital imaging subsystem and the power supply enclosed in and supported by the case. Electrical connectors may couple the power supply and the electronic control system to an electrical component on the removable panel.\n"}, "target_patent": {"label": "US20050253923A1", "independent_claim": " A cellular phone equipped with a camera which can take a moving picture of a subject, the cellular phone with a built-in camera comprising\na lighting device for lighting a subject by means of a light-emitting diode,\na switching device for turning on said lighting device,\na light distribution lens for condensing light radiated from said lighting device, toward the subject, and\na transparent cover for protecting said light distribution lens, the transparent cover being disposed on a subject side, which is a front side, of said lighting device.", "specification": "In an aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera is a cellular phone equipped with a camera that can take a moving picture of a subject, including a lighting device for lighting a subject by means of a light-emitting diode, a switching device for turning on the lighting device, a light distribution lens for condensing (collecting) light emitted from the lighting device onto the subject, and a transparent cover, disposed in the front, or on the subject side of the lighting device, for protecting the light distribution lens. A light utilizing a light-emitting diode is also provided. Accordingly, the size, weight, and profile (thickness) of the cellular phone can be reduced, and also, a subject can be continuously illuminated by the light while a moving picture is being taken by the camera. Because of the light distribution lens for condensing light emitted forward from the light-emitting diode onto the subject, light emitted from the light-emitting diode can be efficiently directed onto the subject.\n[0014]\nThe transparent cover is formed as an integral part of a protection cover of a component which produces a visual effect on the user of the cellular phone with a built-in camera or as an integral part of a protection cover of a display device for displaying an image of a subject or an image received from a telephone of the party at the other end of the communication line. This helps reduce the number of components, the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone further. As a lighting device, a light-emitting diode is directly mounted on a printed circuit board. This helps reduce the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone. A light amount detection device which can detect an insufficient amount of ambient light is provided in a stage preceding a switch, and when the light amount detection device detects and outputs that the light amount is low, switching takes place to turn on the light automatically. Accordingly, the user of the cellular phone with a built-in camera can send an image without worrying about the amount of ambient light. The camera is used as a light amount detection device, and the amount of light is detected by the level of reception signal of the camera. If the amount of light is insufficient, the light is automatically turned on. Therefore, the need for providing a separate light detection device is eliminated, so that the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further.\n[0015]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a light distribution lens with a support for which is mounted to a light-emitting diode. Accordingly, the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further. Moreover, the positional relationship between the optical axis of the light distribution lens and the light emission point of the light-emitting diode can be determined in higher precision. Accordingly, design can be performed to improve light condensing (collection) efficiency by reducing the light distribution angle. This will reduce irregularities in illuminance of the subject resulting from variations in position, and the illuminance of the subject can be improved.\n[0016]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a transparent cover in which a convex lens portion having the function of a light distribution lens is formed. This enables the size, weight, and profile to be reduced further.\n[0017]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has both a transparent cover including a convex lens and a light distribution lens equipped with a support. As a result, it is possible to reduce the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone, and also it is possible to absorb variations in light distribution among individual light-emitting diodes, positional variations in production, and other differences.\n[0018]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a lighting device including a film-like member in the front, or on the subject side of the light-emitting diode. The front-to-back optical transmittance of the film-like member is lower than the back-to-front optical transmittance. This makes it harder to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance.\n[0019]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a lighting device in which an optical diffusion portion for diffusing light is provided at or on at least one orthogonal side (or surface) of a component, among components which have a side (surface) orthogonal to light (the optical axis of light) emitted from the light-emitting diode and allow light emitted from the light-emitting diode to pass through, and the optical diffusion portion is formed at or on one or more surfaces or sides of a component included in the lighting device. This makes it hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance. In addition, either improvement can be made selectively: improving optical transmittance by reducing the amount of attenuation of light emitted from the light-emitting diode or reducing variations in distribution of illumination at the subject. In the lighting device, an optical diffusion plate having an optical diffusion layer on the side of the subject is provided in front of the light-emitting diode, so that light reflectivity becomes greater at the front surface of the optical diffusion plate than at the rear surface of the optical diffusion plate. Moreover, light transmitted from the inside to the outside is diffused again by the optical diffusion plate. In addition, the diffusion layer of the optical diffusion plate has a rough (bumpy) surface (surface with concaves and convexes). This reduces the amount of attenuation of illuminating light emitted from the light-emitting diode and improves optical transmittance, making it hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance.\n[0020]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has an optical diffusion plate formed in such a manner that the angle of light diffusion becomes smaller in the peripheral region than around the optical axis of a light-emitting diode. As a result, in addition to the merit that the amount of attenuation of illuminating light emitted from the light-emitting diode to be reduced and optical transmittance to be improved, variations in distribution of illumination at the subject decrease, and it is hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance, there is another merit that the amount of light diffusion around the optical diffusion plate is reduced, reducing the amount of light diffusion outside the view angle and increasing the amount of light within the view angle, so that the subject can be brightly lighted.\n[0021]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera uses a lighting device is provided as an external light. The external light is a detachable separate unit which has a plug which enables electrical and mechanical connection to the cellular phone with a built-in camera. The main unit of the cellular phone with a built-in camera has a jack to which the plug is detachably connected. As a result, in addition to the merits that it is possible to light the subject continuously while a moving picture is being taken by the camera, and both a still picture and a moving picture of a subject can be taken by the camera in low light, there is another merit that the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further. A separate lighting unit for use with a camera has the same configuration as the lighting device in the cellular phone with a built-in camera described above, and also has a plug which can be electrically and mechanically connected to the jack of the lighting device in the cellular phone with a built-in camera. Because the lighting unit (external light) is provided as a separate unit detachable from the cellular phone with a built-in camera and need not be included in the camera, the size, weight, and profile of the camera can be reduced further. This facilitates maintenance and replacement of the lighting unit."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** US8638369B2\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n. A portable electronic device comprising:\na digital imaging subsystem that includes a lens having an optical axis;\na case that encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship to the case;\na removable panel coupled to the case, the removable panel covering the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem, the removable panel being removable and rotatable to be held by the case in one of a first orientation and a second orientation relative to the case; and\nan optical component supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the first orientation, and the optical component is removed from the optical axis and does not alter optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the second orientation.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA portable electronic device includes a digital imaging subsystem with a lens having an optical axis. A case encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a first defined positional relationship to the case. A removable panel is coupled to the case and held in a second defined positional relationship to the case that covers the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem. An optical component is supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem. The device may include a power supply and an electronic control system coupled to the digital imaging subsystem and the power supply enclosed in and supported by the case. Electrical connectors may couple the power supply and the electronic control system to an electrical component on the removable panel.\n\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** US20050253923A1\n\n**Independent Claim:**\n A cellular phone equipped with a camera which can take a moving picture of a subject, the cellular phone with a built-in camera comprising\na lighting device for lighting a subject by means of a light-emitting diode,\na switching device for turning on said lighting device,\na light distribution lens for condensing light radiated from said lighting device, toward the subject, and\na transparent cover for protecting said light distribution lens, the transparent cover being disposed on a subject side, which is a front side, of said lighting device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nIn an aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera is a cellular phone equipped with a camera that can take a moving picture of a subject, including a lighting device for lighting a subject by means of a light-emitting diode, a switching device for turning on the lighting device, a light distribution lens for condensing (collecting) light emitted from the lighting device onto the subject, and a transparent cover, disposed in the front, or on the subject side of the lighting device, for protecting the light distribution lens. A light utilizing a light-emitting diode is also provided. Accordingly, the size, weight, and profile (thickness) of the cellular phone can be reduced, and also, a subject can be continuously illuminated by the light while a moving picture is being taken by the camera. Because of the light distribution lens for condensing light emitted forward from the light-emitting diode onto the subject, light emitted from the light-emitting diode can be efficiently directed onto the subject.\n[0014]\nThe transparent cover is formed as an integral part of a protection cover of a component which produces a visual effect on the user of the cellular phone with a built-in camera or as an integral part of a protection cover of a display device for displaying an image of a subject or an image received from a telephone of the party at the other end of the communication line. This helps reduce the number of components, the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone further. As a lighting device, a light-emitting diode is directly mounted on a printed circuit board. This helps reduce the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone. A light amount detection device which can detect an insufficient amount of ambient light is provided in a stage preceding a switch, and when the light amount detection device detects and outputs that the light amount is low, switching takes place to turn on the light automatically. Accordingly, the user of the cellular phone with a built-in camera can send an image without worrying about the amount of ambient light. The camera is used as a light amount detection device, and the amount of light is detected by the level of reception signal of the camera. If the amount of light is insufficient, the light is automatically turned on. Therefore, the need for providing a separate light detection device is eliminated, so that the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further.\n[0015]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a light distribution lens with a support for which is mounted to a light-emitting diode. Accordingly, the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further. Moreover, the positional relationship between the optical axis of the light distribution lens and the light emission point of the light-emitting diode can be determined in higher precision. Accordingly, design can be performed to improve light condensing (collection) efficiency by reducing the light distribution angle. This will reduce irregularities in illuminance of the subject resulting from variations in position, and the illuminance of the subject can be improved.\n[0016]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a transparent cover in which a convex lens portion having the function of a light distribution lens is formed. This enables the size, weight, and profile to be reduced further.\n[0017]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has both a transparent cover including a convex lens and a light distribution lens equipped with a support. As a result, it is possible to reduce the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone, and also it is possible to absorb variations in light distribution among individual light-emitting diodes, positional variations in production, and other differences.\n[0018]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a lighting device including a film-like member in the front, or on the subject side of the light-emitting diode. The front-to-back optical transmittance of the film-like member is lower than the back-to-front optical transmittance. This makes it harder to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance.\n[0019]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has a lighting device in which an optical diffusion portion for diffusing light is provided at or on at least one orthogonal side (or surface) of a component, among components which have a side (surface) orthogonal to light (the optical axis of light) emitted from the light-emitting diode and allow light emitted from the light-emitting diode to pass through, and the optical diffusion portion is formed at or on one or more surfaces or sides of a component included in the lighting device. This makes it hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance. In addition, either improvement can be made selectively: improving optical transmittance by reducing the amount of attenuation of light emitted from the light-emitting diode or reducing variations in distribution of illumination at the subject. In the lighting device, an optical diffusion plate having an optical diffusion layer on the side of the subject is provided in front of the light-emitting diode, so that light reflectivity becomes greater at the front surface of the optical diffusion plate than at the rear surface of the optical diffusion plate. Moreover, light transmitted from the inside to the outside is diffused again by the optical diffusion plate. This reduces the amount of attenuation of illuminating light emitted from the light-emitting diode and improves optical transmittance, making it hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance.\n[0020]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera has an optical diffusion plate formed in such a manner that the angle of light diffusion becomes smaller in the peripheral region than around the optical axis of a light-emitting diode. As a result, in addition to the merit that the amount of attenuation of illuminating light emitted from the light-emitting diode to be reduced and optical transmittance to be improved, variations in distribution of illumination at the subject decrease, and it is hard to visually recognize the light-emitting diode and the circuit board, which is favorable in regard to the appearance, there is another merit that the amount of light diffusion around the optical diffusion plate is reduced, reducing the amount of light diffusion outside the view angle and increasing the amount of light within the view angle, so that the subject can be brightly lighted.\n[0021]\nIn another aspect of the present invention, a cellular phone with a built-in camera uses a lighting device is provided as an external light. The external light is a detachable separate unit which has a plug which enables electrical and mechanical connection to the cellular phone with a built-in camera. The main unit of the cellular phone with a built-in camera has a jack to which the plug is detachably connected. As a result, in addition to the merits that it is possible to light the subject continuously while a moving picture is being taken by the camera, and both a still picture and a moving picture of a subject can be taken by the camera in low light, there is another merit that the size, weight, and profile of the cellular phone can be reduced further. A separate lighting unit for use with a camera has the same configuration as the lighting device in the cellular phone with a built-in camera described above, and also has a plug which can be electrically and mechanically connected to the jack of the lighting device in the cellular phone with a built-in camera. Because the lighting unit (external light) is provided as a separate unit detachable from the cellular phone with a built-in camera and need not be included in the camera, the size, weight, and profile of the camera can be reduced further. This facilitates maintenance and replacement of the lighting unit."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"a digital imaging subsystem that includes a lens having an optical axis\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discusses a lighting device, LED, and a light distribution lens, but does not disclose a digital imaging subsystem or a lens described as having an optical axis. No verbatim language matches the element, so it is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a case that encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship to the case\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent refers to a cellular phone housing the components, but provides no explicit disclosure of a case that encloses and supports a digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship. Absence of verbatim language means the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"a removable panel coupled to the case, the removable panel covering the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem, the removable panel being removable and rotatable to be held by the case in one of a first orientation and a second orientation relative to the case\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art describes a transparent cover that is integral to the device and not a removable, rotatable panel with two distinct orientations. No matching language is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"an optical component supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the first orientation, and the optical component is removed from the optical axis and does not alter optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the second orientation\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses a light distribution lens and a transparent cover, but does not disclose an optical component that is mounted on a removable panel and that changes its effect based on panel orientation. No verbatim disclosure exists, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"All core technical elements of the source patent are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent, either as explicit claim language or in the specification. Consequently, each element is both novel and non\u2011obvious over the target reference, leading to a conclusion that the source patent retains validity with respect to the examined prior art.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 3273, "tokens_output": 1821, "latency_ms": 4267}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "a digital imaging subsystem that includes a lens having an optical axis", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent discusses a lighting device, LED, and a light distribution lens, but does not disclose a digital imaging subsystem or a lens described as having an optical axis. No verbatim language matches the element, so it is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "a case that encloses and supports the digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship to the case", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent refers to a cellular phone housing the components, but provides no explicit disclosure of a case that encloses and supports a digital imaging subsystem in a defined positional relationship. Absence of verbatim language means the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "a removable panel coupled to the case, the removable panel covering the digital imaging subsystem without the removable panel being directly connected to the digital imaging subsystem, the removable panel being removable and rotatable to be held by the case in one of a first orientation and a second orientation relative to the case", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art describes a transparent cover that is integral to the device and not a removable, rotatable panel with two distinct orientations. No matching language is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "an optical component supported by the removable panel such that the optical component is aligned with the optical axis and alters optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the first orientation, and the optical component is removed from the optical axis and does not alter optical characteristics of the digital imaging subsystem when the removable panel is held in the second orientation", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent discloses a light distribution lens and a transparent cover, but does not disclose an optical component that is mounted on a removable panel and that changes its effect based on panel orientation. No verbatim disclosure exists, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}], "overall_opinion": "All core technical elements of the source patent are not disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent, either as explicit claim language or in the specification. Consequently, each element is both novel and non\u2011obvious over the target reference, leading to a conclusion that the source patent retains validity with respect to the examined prior art."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent B specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "ff46a11a-99c0-4ae8-9d8c-885b7b96f889", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:05:13.246066+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "Syn - Method- Short", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremoving noise from the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio and correlating the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremoving noise from the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio and correlating the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremoving noise from the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio and correlating the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating."}, "target_patent": {"label": "PA_ Impli", "independent_claim": "A method for adaptive voice conversion, the method comprising:\nreceiving input speech audio;\nextracting acoustic features from the input speech audio;\nreceiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties;\nmapping the acoustic features to a latent representation space;\nadjusting vocal parameters in the latent representation based on the metadata tags;\nsynthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation; and\noutputting the modified speech.", "specification": "Acoustic Feature Extraction: \"The system processes input speech through a series of preprocessing stages including silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis. Acoustic features such as MFCCs (Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients), fundamental frequency (F0), and spectral envelopes are extracted using signal processing techniques.\"\nMetadata Processing: \"User-provided metadata tags (e.g., 'gender=female', 'emotion=happy', 'accent=Australian') are parsed into discrete attribute vectors. Each tag is mapped to predefined parameter ranges stored in a lookup table (e.g., 'deeper' maps to F0 reduction of 15-25 Hz).\"\nLatent Space Mapping: \"A variational autoencoder (VAE) maps the acoustic features into a 256-dimensional latent space where voice characteristics are disentangled into independent factors. The encoder network compresses the input features while preserving speaker-independent linguistic content.\"\nParameter Adjustment: \"The metadata attribute vectors are used to directly adjust specific dimensions in the latent representation. For example, the 'pitch' dimension is scaled according to the desired F0 modification, and the 'timbre' dimensions are shifted to match the target spectral characteristics.\"\nSpeech Synthesis: \"A decoder network reconstructs the audio waveform from the adjusted latent representation, generating modified speech that reflects the desired vocal properties while maintaining intelligibility and naturalness.\""}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** Syn - Method- Short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremoving noise from the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio and correlating the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremoving noise from the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio and correlating the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremoving noise from the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio and correlating the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** PA_ Impli\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for adaptive voice conversion, the method comprising:\nreceiving input speech audio;\nextracting acoustic features from the input speech audio;\nreceiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties;\nmapping the acoustic features to a latent representation space;\nadjusting vocal parameters in the latent representation based on the metadata tags;\nsynthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation; and\noutputting the modified speech.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nAcoustic Feature Extraction: \"The system processes input speech through a series of preprocessing stages including silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis. Acoustic features such as MFCCs (Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients), fundamental frequency (F0), and spectral envelopes are extracted using signal processing techniques.\"\nMetadata Processing: \"User-provided metadata tags (e.g., 'gender=female', 'emotion=happy', 'accent=Australian') are parsed into discrete attribute vectors. Each tag is mapped to predefined parameter ranges stored in a lookup table (e.g., 'deeper' maps to F0 reduction of 15-25 Hz).\"\nLatent Space Mapping: \"A variational autoencoder (VAE) maps the acoustic features into a 256-dimensional latent space where voice characteristics are disentangled into independent factors. The encoder network compresses the input features while preserving speaker-independent linguistic content.\"\nParameter Adjustment: \"The metadata attribute vectors are used to directly adjust specific dimensions in the latent representation. For example, the 'pitch' dimension is scaled according to the desired F0 modification, and the 'timbre' dimensions are shifted to match the target spectral characteristics.\"\nSpeech Synthesis: \"A decoder network reconstructs the audio waveform from the adjusted latent representation, generating modified speech that reflects the desired vocal properties while maintaining intelligibility and naturalness.\""}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses receiving input speech audio and receiving metadata tags, but does not disclose receiving a text prompt that describes desired voice characteristics. No verbatim language matches the source element, so the element is novel (N). Because the concept of a textual prompt is not taught, the element is also non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"removing noise from the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target specification mentions \\\"silence removal\\\" as part of preprocessing, but does not disclose \\\"removing noise\\\" to produce cleaned audio. The exact phrase and functional step are absent, so the element is novel (N) and not rendered obvious by the prior art (N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio and correlating the audio embeddings with the text prompt;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent describes mapping acoustic features to a latent representation space and adjusting parameters based on metadata tags, but it never mentions \\\"audio embeddings\\\" nor a correlation with a text prompt. No verbatim disclosure exists, making the element novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent includes \\\"synthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation\\\" and \\\"outputting the modified speech,\\\" but it does not use the phrasing \\\"generating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.\\\" The specific combination of audio generation driven by correlation with a text prompt is not disclosed, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"All core technical elements of the source claim are absent from the prior\u2011art patent, both in language and in the specific technical teaching. Consequently, the source patent retains novelty and inventive step for its key contributions and is likely valid over the target reference.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 1790, "tokens_output": 1423, "latency_ms": 3556}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent discloses receiving input speech audio and receiving metadata tags, but does not disclose receiving a text prompt that describes desired voice characteristics. No verbatim language matches the source element, so the element is novel (N). Because the concept of a textual prompt is not taught, the element is also non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "removing noise from the input audio to generate cleaned audio;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target specification mentions \"silence removal\" as part of preprocessing, but does not disclose \"removing noise\" to produce cleaned audio. The exact phrase and functional step are absent, so the element is novel (N) and not rendered obvious by the prior art (N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "generating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio and correlating the audio embeddings with the text prompt;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent describes mapping acoustic features to a latent representation space and adjusting parameters based on metadata tags, but it never mentions \"audio embeddings\" nor a correlation with a text prompt. No verbatim disclosure exists, making the element novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "generating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent includes \"synthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation\" and \"outputting the modified speech,\" but it does not use the phrasing \"generating modified audio that incorporates the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.\" The specific combination of audio generation driven by correlation with a text prompt is not disclosed, so the element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N."}], "overall_opinion": "All core technical elements of the source claim are absent from the prior\u2011art patent, both in language and in the specific technical teaching. Consequently, the source patent retains novelty and inventive step for its key contributions and is likely valid over the target reference."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "ad420124-d80c-442c-9868-aa0a7496ea9f", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:05:55.073038+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "Syn - Method- Long", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio associated with a client device;\nreceiving a text prompt describing one or more desired voice characteristics for modifying the input audio;\nperforming noise removal on the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio;\nprocessing the text prompt to extract characteristic parameters;\ngenerating text embeddings from the text prompt based on the characteristic parameters;\ncorrelating the audio embeddings with the text embeddings to determine modification parameters;\nmodifying the cleaned audio based on the modification parameters to generate modified audio;\nwherein the modified audio incorporates the one or more desired voice characteristics described in the text prompt; and\ncausing the modified audio to be rendered at the client device.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio associated with a client device;\nreceiving a text prompt describing one or more desired voice characteristics for modifying the input audio;\nperforming noise removal on the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio;\nprocessing the text prompt to extract characteristic parameters;\ngenerating text embeddings from the text prompt based on the characteristic parameters;\ncorrelating the audio embeddings with the text embeddings to determine modification parameters;\nmodifying the cleaned audio based on the modification parameters to generate modified audio;\nwherein the modified audio incorporates the one or more desired voice characteristics described in the text prompt; and\ncausing the modified audio to be rendered at the client device.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio associated with a client device;\nreceiving a text prompt describing one or more desired voice characteristics for modifying the input audio;\nperforming noise removal on the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio;\nprocessing the text prompt to extract characteristic parameters;\ngenerating text embeddings from the text prompt based on the characteristic parameters;\ncorrelating the audio embeddings with the text embeddings to determine modification parameters;\nmodifying the cleaned audio based on the modification parameters to generate modified audio;\nwherein the modified audio incorporates the one or more desired voice characteristics described in the text prompt; and\ncausing the modified audio to be rendered at the client device."}, "target_patent": {"label": "PA_ Impli", "independent_claim": "A method for adaptive voice conversion, the method comprising:\nreceiving input speech audio;\nextracting acoustic features from the input speech audio;\nreceiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties;\nmapping the acoustic features to a latent representation space;\nadjusting vocal parameters in the latent representation based on the metadata tags;\nsynthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation; and\noutputting the modified speech.", "specification": "Acoustic Feature Extraction: \"The system processes input speech through a series of preprocessing stages including silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis. Acoustic features such as MFCCs (Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients), fundamental frequency (F0), and spectral envelopes are extracted using signal processing techniques.\"\nMetadata Processing: \"User-provided metadata tags (e.g., 'gender=female', 'emotion=happy', 'accent=Australian') are parsed into discrete attribute vectors. Each tag is mapped to predefined parameter ranges stored in a lookup table (e.g., 'deeper' maps to F0 reduction of 15-25 Hz).\"\nLatent Space Mapping: \"A variational autoencoder (VAE) maps the acoustic features into a 256-dimensional latent space where voice characteristics are disentangled into independent factors. The encoder network compresses the input features while preserving speaker-independent linguistic content.\"\nParameter Adjustment: \"The metadata attribute vectors are used to directly adjust specific dimensions in the latent representation. For example, the 'pitch' dimension is scaled according to the desired F0 modification, and the 'timbre' dimensions are shifted to match the target spectral characteristics.\"\nSpeech Synthesis: \"A decoder network reconstructs the audio waveform from the adjusted latent representation, generating modified speech that reflects the desired vocal properties while maintaining intelligibility and naturalness.\""}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** Syn - Method- Long\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio associated with a client device;\nreceiving a text prompt describing one or more desired voice characteristics for modifying the input audio;\nperforming noise removal on the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio;\nprocessing the text prompt to extract characteristic parameters;\ngenerating text embeddings from the text prompt based on the characteristic parameters;\ncorrelating the audio embeddings with the text embeddings to determine modification parameters;\nmodifying the cleaned audio based on the modification parameters to generate modified audio;\nwherein the modified audio incorporates the one or more desired voice characteristics described in the text prompt; and\ncausing the modified audio to be rendered at the client device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio associated with a client device;\nreceiving a text prompt describing one or more desired voice characteristics for modifying the input audio;\nperforming noise removal on the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio;\nprocessing the text prompt to extract characteristic parameters;\ngenerating text embeddings from the text prompt based on the characteristic parameters;\ncorrelating the audio embeddings with the text embeddings to determine modification parameters;\nmodifying the cleaned audio based on the modification parameters to generate modified audio;\nwherein the modified audio incorporates the one or more desired voice characteristics described in the text prompt; and\ncausing the modified audio to be rendered at the client device.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving input audio associated with a client device;\nreceiving a text prompt describing one or more desired voice characteristics for modifying the input audio;\nperforming noise removal on the input audio to generate cleaned audio;\ngenerating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio;\nprocessing the text prompt to extract characteristic parameters;\ngenerating text embeddings from the text prompt based on the characteristic parameters;\ncorrelating the audio embeddings with the text embeddings to determine modification parameters;\nmodifying the cleaned audio based on the modification parameters to generate modified audio;\nwherein the modified audio incorporates the one or more desired voice characteristics described in the text prompt; and\ncausing the modified audio to be rendered at the client device.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** PA_ Impli\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for adaptive voice conversion, the method comprising:\nreceiving input speech audio;\nextracting acoustic features from the input speech audio;\nreceiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties;\nmapping the acoustic features to a latent representation space;\nadjusting vocal parameters in the latent representation based on the metadata tags;\nsynthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation; and\noutputting the modified speech.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nAcoustic Feature Extraction: \"The system processes input speech through a series of preprocessing stages including silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis. Acoustic features such as MFCCs (Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients), fundamental frequency (F0), and spectral envelopes are extracted using signal processing techniques.\"\nMetadata Processing: \"User-provided metadata tags (e.g., 'gender=female', 'emotion=happy', 'accent=Australian') are parsed into discrete attribute vectors. Each tag is mapped to predefined parameter ranges stored in a lookup table (e.g., 'deeper' maps to F0 reduction of 15-25 Hz).\"\nLatent Space Mapping: \"A variational autoencoder (VAE) maps the acoustic features into a 256-dimensional latent space where voice characteristics are disentangled into independent factors. The encoder network compresses the input features while preserving speaker-independent linguistic content.\"\nParameter Adjustment: \"The metadata attribute vectors are used to directly adjust specific dimensions in the latent representation. For example, the 'pitch' dimension is scaled according to the desired F0 modification, and the 'timbre' dimensions are shifted to match the target spectral characteristics.\"\nSpeech Synthesis: \"A decoder network reconstructs the audio waveform from the adjusted latent representation, generating modified speech that reflects the desired vocal properties while maintaining intelligibility and naturalness.\""}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving input audio associated with a client device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving input speech audio\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent's independent claim explicitly recites \\\"receiving input speech audio\\\", which discloses the act of receiving input audio. This is a generic step known in the field, thus it lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a text prompt describing one or more desired voice characteristics for modifying the input audio\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent refers to \\\"metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties\\\" but does not disclose a textual prompt as claimed. No verbatim language matches, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"performing noise removal on the input audio to generate cleaned audio\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The specification mentions \\\"silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis\\\" but does not use the term \\\"noise removal\\\" nor describe generating cleaned audio in the same manner. Therefore the element is not disclosed and is considered novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discusses mapping acoustic features to a latent representation, not generating \\\"audio embeddings\\\". No matching language is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the text prompt to extract characteristic parameters\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art processes metadata tags, not a free\u2011form text prompt, and does not disclose extracting characteristic parameters from such a prompt. Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating text embeddings from the text prompt based on the characteristic parameters\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No portion of the target patent mentions \\\"text embeddings\\\" or a similar operation. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"correlating the audio embeddings with the text embeddings to determine modification parameters\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent adjusts vocal parameters based on metadata tags, but does not disclose correlating audio and text embeddings. No verbatim disclosure exists, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"modifying the cleaned audio based on the modification parameters to generate modified audio\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"While the prior art synthesizes modified speech, it does not describe modifying \\\"cleaned audio\\\" using \\\"modification parameters\\\" derived from embeddings. No matching language, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"wherein the modified audio incorporates the one or more desired voice characteristics described in the text prompt\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent states that the modified speech \\\"reflects the desired vocal properties,\\\" but it does not tie those properties to a text prompt nor use the same phrasing. Absence of verbatim disclosure renders the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 10,\n      \"element_text\": \"causing the modified audio to be rendered at the client device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art mentions \\\"outputting the modified speech\\\" without specifying rendering at a client device. No exact language matches, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014namely the use of a textual prompt, generation of both audio and text embeddings, and their correlation to derive modification parameters\u2014are not disclosed in the prior art. Only the generic step of receiving input audio overlaps. Because the majority of the claim elements are novel and non\u2011obvious, the source patent retains validity despite the prior art.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2015, "tokens_output": 2398, "latency_ms": 6004}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving input audio associated with a client device", "corresponding_text": "receiving input speech audio", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent's independent claim explicitly recites \"receiving input speech audio\", which discloses the act of receiving input audio. This is a generic step known in the field, thus it lacks novelty and is obvious."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receiving a text prompt describing one or more desired voice characteristics for modifying the input audio", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent refers to \"metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties\" but does not disclose a textual prompt as claimed. No verbatim language matches, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "performing noise removal on the input audio to generate cleaned audio", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The specification mentions \"silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis\" but does not use the term \"noise removal\" nor describe generating cleaned audio in the same manner. Therefore the element is not disclosed and is considered novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "generating audio embeddings from the cleaned audio", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent discusses mapping acoustic features to a latent representation, not generating \"audio embeddings\". No matching language is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "processing the text prompt to extract characteristic parameters", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art processes metadata tags, not a free\u2011form text prompt, and does not disclose extracting characteristic parameters from such a prompt. Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "generating text embeddings from the text prompt based on the characteristic parameters", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No portion of the target patent mentions \"text embeddings\" or a similar operation. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "correlating the audio embeddings with the text embeddings to determine modification parameters", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent adjusts vocal parameters based on metadata tags, but does not disclose correlating audio and text embeddings. No verbatim disclosure exists, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "modifying the cleaned audio based on the modification parameters to generate modified audio", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "While the prior art synthesizes modified speech, it does not describe modifying \"cleaned audio\" using \"modification parameters\" derived from embeddings. No matching language, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 9, "element_text": "wherein the modified audio incorporates the one or more desired voice characteristics described in the text prompt", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent states that the modified speech \"reflects the desired vocal properties,\" but it does not tie those properties to a text prompt nor use the same phrasing. Absence of verbatim disclosure renders the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 10, "element_text": "causing the modified audio to be rendered at the client device", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art mentions \"outputting the modified speech\" without specifying rendering at a client device. No exact language matches, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancements of the source patent\u2014namely the use of a textual prompt, generation of both audio and text embeddings, and their correlation to derive modification parameters\u2014are not disclosed in the prior art. Only the generic step of receiving input audio overlaps. Because the majority of the claim elements are novel and non\u2011obvious, the source patent retains validity despite the prior art."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "0f9448bd-048c-4761-9fce-4c5f7beef53a", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:06:36.439439+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "Syn - System- Short", "independent_claim": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremove noise from the input audio and generate audio embeddings from noise-removed audio;\ncorrelate the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerate modified audio incorporating the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.", "specification": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremove noise from the input audio and generate audio embeddings from noise-removed audio;\ncorrelate the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerate modified audio incorporating the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremove noise from the input audio and generate audio embeddings from noise-removed audio;\ncorrelate the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerate modified audio incorporating the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating."}, "target_patent": {"label": "PA_ Impli", "independent_claim": "A method for adaptive voice conversion, the method comprising:\nreceiving input speech audio;\nextracting acoustic features from the input speech audio;\nreceiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties;\nmapping the acoustic features to a latent representation space;\nadjusting vocal parameters in the latent representation based on the metadata tags;\nsynthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation; and\noutputting the modified speech.", "specification": "Acoustic Feature Extraction: \"The system processes input speech through a series of preprocessing stages including silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis. Acoustic features such as MFCCs (Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients), fundamental frequency (F0), and spectral envelopes are extracted using signal processing techniques.\"\nMetadata Processing: \"User-provided metadata tags (e.g., 'gender=female', 'emotion=happy', 'accent=Australian') are parsed into discrete attribute vectors. Each tag is mapped to predefined parameter ranges stored in a lookup table (e.g., 'deeper' maps to F0 reduction of 15-25 Hz).\"\nLatent Space Mapping: \"A variational autoencoder (VAE) maps the acoustic features into a 256-dimensional latent space where voice characteristics are disentangled into independent factors. The encoder network compresses the input features while preserving speaker-independent linguistic content.\"\nParameter Adjustment: \"The metadata attribute vectors are used to directly adjust specific dimensions in the latent representation. For example, the 'pitch' dimension is scaled according to the desired F0 modification, and the 'timbre' dimensions are shifted to match the target spectral characteristics.\"\nSpeech Synthesis: \"A decoder network reconstructs the audio waveform from the adjusted latent representation, generating modified speech that reflects the desired vocal properties while maintaining intelligibility and naturalness.\""}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** Syn - System- Short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremove noise from the input audio and generate audio embeddings from noise-removed audio;\ncorrelate the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerate modified audio incorporating the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremove noise from the input audio and generate audio embeddings from noise-removed audio;\ncorrelate the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerate modified audio incorporating the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics;\nremove noise from the input audio and generate audio embeddings from noise-removed audio;\ncorrelate the audio embeddings with the text prompt; and\ngenerate modified audio incorporating the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** PA_ Impli\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for adaptive voice conversion, the method comprising:\nreceiving input speech audio;\nextracting acoustic features from the input speech audio;\nreceiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties;\nmapping the acoustic features to a latent representation space;\nadjusting vocal parameters in the latent representation based on the metadata tags;\nsynthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation; and\noutputting the modified speech.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nAcoustic Feature Extraction: \"The system processes input speech through a series of preprocessing stages including silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis. Acoustic features such as MFCCs (Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients), fundamental frequency (F0), and spectral envelopes are extracted using signal processing techniques.\"\nMetadata Processing: \"User-provided metadata tags (e.g., 'gender=female', 'emotion=happy', 'accent=Australian') are parsed into discrete attribute vectors. Each tag is mapped to predefined parameter ranges stored in a lookup table (e.g., 'deeper' maps to F0 reduction of 15-25 Hz).\"\nLatent Space Mapping: \"A variational autoencoder (VAE) maps the acoustic features into a 256-dimensional latent space where voice characteristics are disentangled into independent factors. The encoder network compresses the input features while preserving speaker-independent linguistic content.\"\nParameter Adjustment: \"The metadata attribute vectors are used to directly adjust specific dimensions in the latent representation. For example, the 'pitch' dimension is scaled according to the desired F0 modification, and the 'timbre' dimensions are shifted to match the target spectral characteristics.\"\nSpeech Synthesis: \"A decoder network reconstructs the audio waveform from the adjusted latent representation, generating modified speech that reflects the desired vocal properties while maintaining intelligibility and naturalness.\""}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not mention a processor in either its independent claim or specification, so the element is not disclosed (novelty N). Without any disclosure, there is no basis to deem it obvious (inventive step N). Hence the element remains novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receive input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim recites \\\"receiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties\\\", which corresponds to receiving a description of desired voice characteristics. The concept of providing desired vocal attributes via a textual or tag\u2011based input is taught, so the element lacks novelty (Y). Given that using a text prompt is a straightforward alternative to metadata tags, it would be obvious to a person of skill (inventive step Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"remove noise from the input audio and generate audio embeddings from noise-removed audio\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis ... Acoustic features such as MFCCs (Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients), fundamental frequency (F0), and spectral envelopes are extracted\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The specification describes preprocessing steps that include \\\"silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis\\\" (noise removal) and then extracts acoustic features such as MFCCs, F0, and spectral envelopes, which serve as audio embeddings. Thus the element is disclosed (novelty Y). Using standard preprocessing followed by feature extraction to obtain embeddings would be obvious to one skilled in the art, so the inventive step is also lacking (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"correlate the audio embeddings with the text prompt\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose any step that correlates audio embeddings (or acoustic features) with a textual prompt. It maps features to a latent space and adjusts parameters based on metadata tags, but no correlation operation is taught. Therefore the element is novel (N) and, lacking any teaching, is not obvious (N).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"generate modified audio incorporating the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"synthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim includes \\\"synthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation\\\" and then outputting it, which corresponds to generating modified audio based on prior processing. This disclosure covers the element, so novelty is absent (Y). Adapting the same synthesis step to the output of a correlation step would be an obvious modification, so the inventive step is also lacking (Y).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The source patent's key technical advancement lies in the correlation of audio embeddings with a text prompt (element 4). This step is not disclosed in the prior art and is not obvious, providing a novel and non\u2011obvious contribution. Although other elements are anticipated by the target patent, the presence of at least one core inventive element supports the overall validity of the source patent.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 1811, "tokens_output": 2165, "latency_ms": 5154}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one processor", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not mention a processor in either its independent claim or specification, so the element is not disclosed (novelty N). Without any disclosure, there is no basis to deem it obvious (inventive step N). Hence the element remains novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receive input audio and a text prompt describing desired voice characteristics", "corresponding_text": "receiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim recites \"receiving metadata tags indicating desired vocal properties\", which corresponds to receiving a description of desired voice characteristics. The concept of providing desired vocal attributes via a textual or tag\u2011based input is taught, so the element lacks novelty (Y). Given that using a text prompt is a straightforward alternative to metadata tags, it would be obvious to a person of skill (inventive step Y)."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "remove noise from the input audio and generate audio embeddings from noise-removed audio", "corresponding_text": "silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis ... Acoustic features such as MFCCs (Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients), fundamental frequency (F0), and spectral envelopes are extracted", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The specification describes preprocessing steps that include \"silence removal, normalization, and frequency analysis\" (noise removal) and then extracts acoustic features such as MFCCs, F0, and spectral envelopes, which serve as audio embeddings. Thus the element is disclosed (novelty Y). Using standard preprocessing followed by feature extraction to obtain embeddings would be obvious to one skilled in the art, so the inventive step is also lacking (Y)."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "correlate the audio embeddings with the text prompt", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose any step that correlates audio embeddings (or acoustic features) with a textual prompt. It maps features to a latent space and adjusts parameters based on metadata tags, but no correlation operation is taught. Therefore the element is novel (N) and, lacking any teaching, is not obvious (N)."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "generate modified audio incorporating the desired voice characteristics based on the correlating", "corresponding_text": "synthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim includes \"synthesizing modified speech from the adjusted latent representation\" and then outputting it, which corresponds to generating modified audio based on prior processing. This disclosure covers the element, so novelty is absent (Y). Adapting the same synthesis step to the output of a correlation step would be an obvious modification, so the inventive step is also lacking (Y)."}], "overall_opinion": "The source patent's key technical advancement lies in the correlation of audio embeddings with a text prompt (element 4). This step is not disclosed in the prior art and is not obvious, providing a novel and non\u2011obvious contribution. Although other elements are anticipated by the target patent, the presence of at least one core inventive element supports the overall validity of the source patent."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "5d9c128e-585c-4de1-944d-b69e9051b14d", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:07:16.997823+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn1- method - short", "independent_claim": "A method comprising:\nreceiving, at one or more processors, a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompiling the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerating a video output comprising the sequential frame order.", "specification": "A method comprising:\nreceiving, at one or more processors, a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompiling the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerating a video output comprising the sequential frame order.A method comprising:\nreceiving, at one or more processors, a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompiling the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerating a video output comprising the sequential frame order."}, "target_patent": {"label": "PA-impli", "independent_claim": "A method for creating motion sequences, the method comprising:\ncapturing a source image and a target image;\nemploying a motion estimation network to predict optical flow between the source image and the target image;\ngenerating interpolated frames by warping pixels from the source image toward the target image according to the predicted optical flow at multiple temporal intervals;\nrefining the interpolated frames using a synthesis network to reduce artifacts and improve visual quality; and\nrendering an output sequence comprising the source image, the interpolated frames, and the target image.", "specification": "This invention addresses the problem of creating smooth video transitions between keyframe images. Unlike traditional morphing techniques that simply blend pixels, our approach uses deep neural networks to understand the semantic content and motion dynamics.\nThe system begins with two input images: a source keyframe and a target keyframe. A motion estimation module (implemented as a U-Net architecture) analyzes both images and predicts dense optical flow fields representing how each pixel should move from the source to reach the target configuration.\nUsing the predicted flow fields, the system generates intermediate frames by warping the source image at fractional time steps (e.g., t=0.25, 0.5, 0.75). A refinement network then processes these warped frames to fill in occluded regions, correct artifacts, and ensure visual coherence. The refinement network is a conditional GAN trained to produce photorealistic frames given the warped inputs.\nThe final output is a video sequence containing the original source image, all generated intermediate frames, and the target image, creating a smooth animated transition between the two states."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn1- method - short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method comprising:\nreceiving, at one or more processors, a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompiling the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerating a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method comprising:\nreceiving, at one or more processors, a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompiling the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerating a video output comprising the sequential frame order.A method comprising:\nreceiving, at one or more processors, a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompiling the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerating a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** PA-impli\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for creating motion sequences, the method comprising:\ncapturing a source image and a target image;\nemploying a motion estimation network to predict optical flow between the source image and the target image;\ngenerating interpolated frames by warping pixels from the source image toward the target image according to the predicted optical flow at multiple temporal intervals;\nrefining the interpolated frames using a synthesis network to reduce artifacts and improve visual quality; and\nrendering an output sequence comprising the source image, the interpolated frames, and the target image.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThis invention addresses the problem of creating smooth video transitions between keyframe images. Unlike traditional morphing techniques that simply blend pixels, our approach uses deep neural networks to understand the semantic content and motion dynamics.\nThe system begins with two input images: a source keyframe and a target keyframe. A motion estimation module (implemented as a U-Net architecture) analyzes both images and predicts dense optical flow fields representing how each pixel should move from the source to reach the target configuration.\nUsing the predicted flow fields, the system generates intermediate frames by warping the source image at fractional time steps (e.g., t=0.25, 0.5, 0.75). A refinement network then processes these warped frames to fill in occluded regions, correct artifacts, and ensure visual coherence. The refinement network is a conditional GAN trained to produce photorealistic frames given the warped inputs.\nThe final output is a video sequence containing the original source image, all generated intermediate frames, and the target image, creating a smooth animated transition between the two states."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving, at one or more processors, a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"capturing a source image and a target image ... two input images: a source keyframe and a target keyframe.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses obtaining a source and target image via \\\"capturing a source image and a target image\\\" and further describes \\\"two input images: a source keyframe and a target keyframe.\\\" This matches the receiving step, so the element lacks novelty. Using images as inputs is routine, making the approach obvious; therefore the inventive step is also lacking, resulting in a Y verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent generates intermediate frames by warping pixels with a motion estimation network and does not disclose any generative artificial intelligence model for creating transition images. Because this specific technique is absent, the element is novel and non\u2011obvious, leading to an N verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"compiling the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"rendering an output sequence comprising the source image, the interpolated frames, and the target image.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim explicitly states \\\"rendering an output sequence comprising the source image, the interpolated frames, and the target image,\\\" which corresponds to compiling the images into an ordered sequence. This ordering of frames is a standard step in motion\u2011sequence generation, making it both disclosed and obvious; thus the element receives a Y verdict.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"The final output is a video sequence containing the original source image, all generated intermediate frames, and the target image.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The specification of the target patent describes \\\"The final output is a video sequence containing the original source image, all generated intermediate frames, and the target image,\\\" which directly maps to generating a video output from the ordered frames. Providing such a video sequence is routine in prior art, so the element lacks novelty and inventive step, resulting in a Y verdict.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical contribution of the source patent is the use of a generative artificial intelligence model to create transition images (element 2). This element is not disclosed in the prior art and is non\u2011obvious, giving the source claim a meaningful inventive step. Although the other elements (receiving images, compiling frames, and producing video output) are found in the target patent and are obvious, the presence of the novel AI\u2011based generation step preserves overall validity. Therefore, the source patent is likely valid.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 1869, "tokens_output": 2131, "latency_ms": 4792}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving, at one or more processors, a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;", "corresponding_text": "capturing a source image and a target image ... two input images: a source keyframe and a target keyframe.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses obtaining a source and target image via \"capturing a source image and a target image\" and further describes \"two input images: a source keyframe and a target keyframe.\" This matches the receiving step, so the element lacks novelty. Using images as inputs is routine, making the approach obvious; therefore the inventive step is also lacking, resulting in a Y verdict."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "generating, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent generates intermediate frames by warping pixels with a motion estimation network and does not disclose any generative artificial intelligence model for creating transition images. Because this specific technique is absent, the element is novel and non\u2011obvious, leading to an N verdict."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "compiling the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order;", "corresponding_text": "rendering an output sequence comprising the source image, the interpolated frames, and the target image.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim explicitly states \"rendering an output sequence comprising the source image, the interpolated frames, and the target image,\" which corresponds to compiling the images into an ordered sequence. This ordering of frames is a standard step in motion\u2011sequence generation, making it both disclosed and obvious; thus the element receives a Y verdict."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "generating a video output comprising the sequential frame order.", "corresponding_text": "The final output is a video sequence containing the original source image, all generated intermediate frames, and the target image.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The specification of the target patent describes \"The final output is a video sequence containing the original source image, all generated intermediate frames, and the target image,\" which directly maps to generating a video output from the ordered frames. Providing such a video sequence is routine in prior art, so the element lacks novelty and inventive step, resulting in a Y verdict."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical contribution of the source patent is the use of a generative artificial intelligence model to create transition images (element 2). This element is not disclosed in the prior art and is non\u2011obvious, giving the source claim a meaningful inventive step. Although the other elements (receiving images, compiling frames, and producing video output) are found in the target patent and are obvious, the presence of the novel AI\u2011based generation step preserves overall validity. Therefore, the source patent is likely valid."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "249fbb0c-8d6b-4b12-b554-e0039ada2824", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:07:59.785208+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn2-method-long", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a first image representing a first visual state;\nreceiving a second image representing a second visual state;\nprocessing the first image to extract first visual features and first semantic information, wherein the first semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the first image;\nprocessing the second image to extract second visual features and second semantic information, wherein the second semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the second image;\ndetermining, based on the first semantic information and the second semantic information, one or more differences between the first visual state and the second visual state;\ndetermining a transition path from the first visual state to the second visual state based on the one or more differences;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model and based on the transition path, one or more intermediate images that depict visual transitions between the first visual state and the second visual state;\narranging the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in a sequential order;\ngenerating a video file comprising the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in the sequential order; and\ncausing the video file to be stored or rendered at a client device.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a first image representing a first visual state;\nreceiving a second image representing a second visual state;\nprocessing the first image to extract first visual features and first semantic information, wherein the first semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the first image;\nprocessing the second image to extract second visual features and second semantic information, wherein the second semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the second image;\ndetermining, based on the first semantic information and the second semantic information, one or more differences between the first visual state and the second visual state;\ndetermining a transition path from the first visual state to the second visual state based on the one or more differences;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model and based on the transition path, one or more intermediate images that depict visual transitions between the first visual state and the second visual state;\narranging the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in a sequential order;\ngenerating a video file comprising the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in the sequential order; and\ncausing the video file to be stored or rendered at a client device.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a first image representing a first visual state;\nreceiving a second image representing a second visual state;\nprocessing the first image to extract first visual features and first semantic information, wherein the first semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the first image;\nprocessing the second image to extract second visual features and second semantic information, wherein the second semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the second image;\ndetermining, based on the first semantic information and the second semantic information, one or more differences between the first visual state and the second visual state;\ndetermining a transition path from the first visual state to the second visual state based on the one or more differences;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model and based on the transition path, one or more intermediate images that depict visual transitions between the first visual state and the second visual state;\narranging the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in a sequential order;\ngenerating a video file comprising the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in the sequential order; and\ncausing the video file to be stored or rendered at a client device."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A computer-implemented method comprising:\nobtaining a start image depicting an initial visual state;\nobtaining an end image depicting a final visual state;\nanalyzing the start image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics in the initial visual state;\nanalyzing the end image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics in the final visual state;\ncalculating changes between the initial visual state and the final visual state;\ngenerating, using a deep learning-based image generation model, multiple intermediate frames representing gradual transitions from the initial visual state to the final visual state;\nassembling the start image, the multiple intermediate frames, and the end image into a frame sequence; and\noutputting an animated video comprising the frame sequence.", "specification": "The present invention relates to generating smooth animated transitions between two input images. The system receives a first input image showing a scene at time T1 and a second input image showing the same scene at time T2. A convolutional neural network analyzes both images to extract feature representations and identify objects, movements, and transformations between the two states.\nThe system then employs a generative adversarial network (GAN) trained on video data to synthesize intermediate frames. The GAN takes the feature embeddings from both input images and generates N intermediate frames that smoothly interpolate between the two states. The interpolation is context-aware, ensuring that objects move naturally and visual coherence is maintained throughout the transition.\nFinally, the system concatenates the first input image, all N intermediate frames, and the second input image into a video file format (e.g., MP4, GIF) with a specified frame rate (e.g., 24 fps). This creates a short animated clip showing the transformation from the first state to the second state."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn2-method-long\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a first image representing a first visual state;\nreceiving a second image representing a second visual state;\nprocessing the first image to extract first visual features and first semantic information, wherein the first semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the first image;\nprocessing the second image to extract second visual features and second semantic information, wherein the second semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the second image;\ndetermining, based on the first semantic information and the second semantic information, one or more differences between the first visual state and the second visual state;\ndetermining a transition path from the first visual state to the second visual state based on the one or more differences;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model and based on the transition path, one or more intermediate images that depict visual transitions between the first visual state and the second visual state;\narranging the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in a sequential order;\ngenerating a video file comprising the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in the sequential order; and\ncausing the video file to be stored or rendered at a client device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a first image representing a first visual state;\nreceiving a second image representing a second visual state;\nprocessing the first image to extract first visual features and first semantic information, wherein the first semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the first image;\nprocessing the second image to extract second visual features and second semantic information, wherein the second semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the second image;\ndetermining, based on the first semantic information and the second semantic information, one or more differences between the first visual state and the second visual state;\ndetermining a transition path from the first visual state to the second visual state based on the one or more differences;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model and based on the transition path, one or more intermediate images that depict visual transitions between the first visual state and the second visual state;\narranging the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in a sequential order;\ngenerating a video file comprising the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in the sequential order; and\ncausing the video file to be stored or rendered at a client device.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a first image representing a first visual state;\nreceiving a second image representing a second visual state;\nprocessing the first image to extract first visual features and first semantic information, wherein the first semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the first image;\nprocessing the second image to extract second visual features and second semantic information, wherein the second semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the second image;\ndetermining, based on the first semantic information and the second semantic information, one or more differences between the first visual state and the second visual state;\ndetermining a transition path from the first visual state to the second visual state based on the one or more differences;\ngenerating, using a generative artificial intelligence model and based on the transition path, one or more intermediate images that depict visual transitions between the first visual state and the second visual state;\narranging the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in a sequential order;\ngenerating a video file comprising the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in the sequential order; and\ncausing the video file to be stored or rendered at a client device.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA computer-implemented method comprising:\nobtaining a start image depicting an initial visual state;\nobtaining an end image depicting a final visual state;\nanalyzing the start image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics in the initial visual state;\nanalyzing the end image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics in the final visual state;\ncalculating changes between the initial visual state and the final visual state;\ngenerating, using a deep learning-based image generation model, multiple intermediate frames representing gradual transitions from the initial visual state to the final visual state;\nassembling the start image, the multiple intermediate frames, and the end image into a frame sequence; and\noutputting an animated video comprising the frame sequence.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe present invention relates to generating smooth animated transitions between two input images. The system receives a first input image showing a scene at time T1 and a second input image showing the same scene at time T2. A convolutional neural network analyzes both images to extract feature representations and identify objects, movements, and transformations between the two states.\nThe system then employs a generative adversarial network (GAN) trained on video data to synthesize intermediate frames. The GAN takes the feature embeddings from both input images and generates N intermediate frames that smoothly interpolate between the two states. The interpolation is context-aware, ensuring that objects move naturally and visual coherence is maintained throughout the transition.\nFinally, the system concatenates the first input image, all N intermediate frames, and the second input image into a video file format (e.g., MP4, GIF) with a specified frame rate (e.g., 24 fps). This creates a short animated clip showing the transformation from the first state to the second state."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a first image representing a first visual state\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not contain the exact phrase \\\"receiving a first image representing a first visual state\\\"; therefore the element is novel (N). However, the target teaches obtaining a start image depicting an initial visual state, which makes the concept of acquiring an initial image obvious to one of ordinary skill, so the inventive step is lacking (Y). Verdict is N because novelty is present.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a second image representing a second visual state\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No verbatim disclosure of \\\"receiving a second image representing a second visual state\\\" in the target; thus novel (N). The target teaches obtaining an end image depicting a final visual state, rendering the idea of acquiring a second image obvious (Y). Verdict remains N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the first image to extract first visual features and first semantic information, wherein the first semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the first image\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The exact wording is not found in the target claim or specification, so the element is novel (N). The target, however, describes analyzing the start image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics, which would make this processing step obvious (Y). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the second image to extract second visual features and second semantic information, wherein the second semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the second image\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No verbatim match; therefore novel (N). The target\u2019s analysis of the end image for objects and characteristics renders this step obvious (Y). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, based on the first semantic information and the second semantic information, one or more differences between the first visual state and the second visual state\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target does not use the exact phrase; novelty is N. It does disclose calculating changes between the initial and final visual states, making the determination of differences obvious (Y). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining a transition path from the first visual state to the second visual state based on the one or more differences\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No verbatim disclosure; novelty N. The concept of generating intermediate frames based on calculated changes implies a transition path, which would be obvious in view of the target teaching, so inventive step Y. Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating, using a generative artificial intelligence model and based on the transition path, one or more intermediate images that depict visual transitions between the first visual state and the second visual state\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Exact wording not present; novelty N. The target discloses generating multiple intermediate frames using a deep learning\u2011based image generation model, making this step obvious (Y). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"arranging the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in a sequential order\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No verbatim phrase; novelty N. The target teaches assembling the start image, intermediate frames, and end image into a frame sequence, which renders the arrangement step obvious (Y). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating a video file comprising the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in the sequential order\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Exact phrase not found; novelty N. The target outputs an animated video comprising the frame sequence, making this video generation step obvious (Y). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 10,\n      \"element_text\": \"causing the video file to be stored or rendered at a client device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No verbatim disclosure; novelty N. Storing or rendering the generated video on a client device is a routine step taught by the target\u2019s overall system, thus obvious (Y). Verdict N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"All core technical elements of the source claim are not verbatim disclosed in the prior art, so each element retains novelty. However, the prior art teaches the same overall concept\u2014acquiring two images, analyzing them, computing differences, generating intermediate frames with a deep\u2011learning model, assembling them, and outputting a video\u2014making each element obvious to one of ordinary skill. Consequently, while the source patent may survive a novelty attack, it is vulnerable on the basis of lacking an inventive step for its core contribution.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2323, "tokens_output": 2973, "latency_ms": 7048}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving a first image representing a first visual state", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not contain the exact phrase \"receiving a first image representing a first visual state\"; therefore the element is novel (N). However, the target teaches obtaining a start image depicting an initial visual state, which makes the concept of acquiring an initial image obvious to one of ordinary skill, so the inventive step is lacking (Y). Verdict is N because novelty is present."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receiving a second image representing a second visual state", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No verbatim disclosure of \"receiving a second image representing a second visual state\" in the target; thus novel (N). The target teaches obtaining an end image depicting a final visual state, rendering the idea of acquiring a second image obvious (Y). Verdict remains N."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "processing the first image to extract first visual features and first semantic information, wherein the first semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the first image", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The exact wording is not found in the target claim or specification, so the element is novel (N). The target, however, describes analyzing the start image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics, which would make this processing step obvious (Y). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "processing the second image to extract second visual features and second semantic information, wherein the second semantic information identifies one or more objects or scene characteristics in the second image", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No verbatim match; therefore novel (N). The target\u2019s analysis of the end image for objects and characteristics renders this step obvious (Y). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "determining, based on the first semantic information and the second semantic information, one or more differences between the first visual state and the second visual state", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target does not use the exact phrase; novelty is N. It does disclose calculating changes between the initial and final visual states, making the determination of differences obvious (Y). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "determining a transition path from the first visual state to the second visual state based on the one or more differences", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No verbatim disclosure; novelty N. The concept of generating intermediate frames based on calculated changes implies a transition path, which would be obvious in view of the target teaching, so inventive step Y. Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "generating, using a generative artificial intelligence model and based on the transition path, one or more intermediate images that depict visual transitions between the first visual state and the second visual state", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "Exact wording not present; novelty N. The target discloses generating multiple intermediate frames using a deep learning\u2011based image generation model, making this step obvious (Y). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "arranging the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in a sequential order", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No verbatim phrase; novelty N. The target teaches assembling the start image, intermediate frames, and end image into a frame sequence, which renders the arrangement step obvious (Y). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 9, "element_text": "generating a video file comprising the first image, the one or more intermediate images, and the second image in the sequential order", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "Exact phrase not found; novelty N. The target outputs an animated video comprising the frame sequence, making this video generation step obvious (Y). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 10, "element_text": "causing the video file to be stored or rendered at a client device", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No verbatim disclosure; novelty N. Storing or rendering the generated video on a client device is a routine step taught by the target\u2019s overall system, thus obvious (Y). Verdict N."}], "overall_opinion": "All core technical elements of the source claim are not verbatim disclosed in the prior art, so each element retains novelty. However, the prior art teaches the same overall concept\u2014acquiring two images, analyzing them, computing differences, generating intermediate frames with a deep\u2011learning model, assembling them, and outputting a video\u2014making each element obvious to one of ordinary skill. Consequently, while the source patent may survive a novelty attack, it is vulnerable on the basis of lacking an inventive step for its core contribution."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "5b862e68-ce62-408b-916a-1c0b161ce1d0", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:08:40.640539+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "Syn-sys-short", "independent_claim": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerate, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompile the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerate a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\n", "specification": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerate, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompile the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerate a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerate, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompile the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerate a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\n"}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A computer-implemented method comprising:\nobtaining a start image depicting an initial visual state;\nobtaining an end image depicting a final visual state;\nanalyzing the start image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics in the initial visual state;\nanalyzing the end image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics in the final visual state;\ncalculating changes between the initial visual state and the final visual state;\ngenerating, using a deep learning-based image generation model, multiple intermediate frames representing gradual transitions from the initial visual state to the final visual state;\nassembling the start image, the multiple intermediate frames, and the end image into a frame sequence; and\noutputting an animated video comprising the frame sequence.", "specification": "The present invention relates to generating smooth animated transitions between two input images. The system receives a first input image showing a scene at time T1 and a second input image showing the same scene at time T2. A convolutional neural network analyzes both images to extract feature representations and identify objects, movements, and transformations between the two states.\nThe system then employs a generative adversarial network (GAN) trained on video data to synthesize intermediate frames. The GAN takes the feature embeddings from both input images and generates N intermediate frames that smoothly interpolate between the two states. The interpolation is context-aware, ensuring that objects move naturally and visual coherence is maintained throughout the transition.\nFinally, the system concatenates the first input image, all N intermediate frames, and the second input image into a video file format (e.g., MP4, GIF) with a specified frame rate (e.g., 24 fps). This creates a short animated clip showing the transformation from the first state to the second state."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** Syn-sys-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerate, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompile the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerate a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\n\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerate, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompile the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerate a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state;\ngenerate, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state;\ncompile the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order; and\ngenerate a video output comprising the sequential frame order.\n\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA computer-implemented method comprising:\nobtaining a start image depicting an initial visual state;\nobtaining an end image depicting a final visual state;\nanalyzing the start image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics in the initial visual state;\nanalyzing the end image using a neural network to identify objects and visual characteristics in the final visual state;\ncalculating changes between the initial visual state and the final visual state;\ngenerating, using a deep learning-based image generation model, multiple intermediate frames representing gradual transitions from the initial visual state to the final visual state;\nassembling the start image, the multiple intermediate frames, and the end image into a frame sequence; and\noutputting an animated video comprising the frame sequence.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe present invention relates to generating smooth animated transitions between two input images. The system receives a first input image showing a scene at time T1 and a second input image showing the same scene at time T2. A convolutional neural network analyzes both images to extract feature representations and identify objects, movements, and transformations between the two states.\nThe system then employs a generative adversarial network (GAN) trained on video data to synthesize intermediate frames. The GAN takes the feature embeddings from both input images and generates N intermediate frames that smoothly interpolate between the two states. The interpolation is context-aware, ensuring that objects move naturally and visual coherence is maintained throughout the transition.\nFinally, the system concatenates the first input image, all N intermediate frames, and the second input image into a video file format (e.g., MP4, GIF) with a specified frame rate (e.g., 24 fps). This creates a short animated clip showing the transformation from the first state to the second state."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose any processor or computing hardware; therefore the element is not found (novelty N). Because it is novel, it also provides an inventive contribution (inventive_step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to: receive a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"obtaining a start image depicting an initial visual state; ... obtaining an end image depicting a final visual state\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly recites obtaining a start image and an end image, which corresponds to receiving first and second images (novelty Y). Receiving input images is a routine step in image\u2011to\u2011video systems and would be obvious to a person of skill in the art (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"generate, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"generating, using a deep learning-based image generation model, multiple intermediate frames representing gradual transitions from the initial visual state to the final visual state\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses generation of intermediate frames using a deep\u2011learning image generation model, which is equivalent to the claimed generative AI generation of transition images (novelty Y). Such use of deep\u2011learning models for interpolating between two images is well\u2011known, making the approach obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"compile the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"assembling the start image, the multiple intermediate frames, and the end image into a frame sequence\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly teaches assembling the start image, intermediate frames, and end image into a frame sequence, matching the claimed compilation step (novelty Y). This ordering of frames is a standard step in video creation and would be obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"generate a video output comprising the sequential frame order\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"outputting an animated video comprising the frame sequence\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent recites outputting an animated video that comprises the assembled frame sequence, directly corresponding to the claimed video generation step (novelty Y). Rendering the assembled frames as a video is routine and obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014using a generative AI model to create transition images and compile them into a video\u2014is fully disclosed in the prior art. All substantive claim elements (2\u20115) are found in the target patent and would be considered obvious to a skilled practitioner. Only the generic hardware element (processor) is not disclosed, but it does not constitute a meaningful inventive contribution. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and inventive step for its essential elements and is likely invalid as claimed.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 1931, "tokens_output": 2153, "latency_ms": 5120}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one processor", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose any processor or computing hardware; therefore the element is not found (novelty N). Because it is novel, it also provides an inventive contribution (inventive_step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to: receive a first image representing a first state and a second image representing a second state", "corresponding_text": "obtaining a start image depicting an initial visual state; ... obtaining an end image depicting a final visual state", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly recites obtaining a start image and an end image, which corresponds to receiving first and second images (novelty Y). Receiving input images is a routine step in image\u2011to\u2011video systems and would be obvious to a person of skill in the art (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "generate, using a generative artificial intelligence model, one or more transition images based on the first image and the second image, wherein the one or more transition images depict a visual transition between the first state and the second state", "corresponding_text": "generating, using a deep learning-based image generation model, multiple intermediate frames representing gradual transitions from the initial visual state to the final visual state", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses generation of intermediate frames using a deep\u2011learning image generation model, which is equivalent to the claimed generative AI generation of transition images (novelty Y). Such use of deep\u2011learning models for interpolating between two images is well\u2011known, making the approach obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "compile the first image, the one or more transition images, and the second image into a sequential frame order", "corresponding_text": "assembling the start image, the multiple intermediate frames, and the end image into a frame sequence", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly teaches assembling the start image, intermediate frames, and end image into a frame sequence, matching the claimed compilation step (novelty Y). This ordering of frames is a standard step in video creation and would be obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "generate a video output comprising the sequential frame order", "corresponding_text": "outputting an animated video comprising the frame sequence", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent recites outputting an animated video that comprises the assembled frame sequence, directly corresponding to the claimed video generation step (novelty Y). Rendering the assembled frames as a video is routine and obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014using a generative AI model to create transition images and compile them into a video\u2014is fully disclosed in the prior art. All substantive claim elements (2\u20115) are found in the target patent and would be considered obvious to a skilled practitioner. Only the generic hardware element (processor) is not disclosed, but it does not constitute a meaningful inventive contribution. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and inventive step for its essential elements and is likely invalid as claimed."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "a26ae4bd-fc2b-4f26-a1fc-b87ba02bd448", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:09:27.057170+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn3- method-long", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A computer-implemented method for translating multilingual text, comprising:\nreceiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages;\nidentifying each of the three or more languages present in the input sentence using language detection;\ncomputing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning;\nselecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;\nperforming a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output;\nperforming a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;\nsubmitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model;\nthe automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence;\nthe automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;\nwhen the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output;\nrepeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality; and\noutputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved.", "specification": "Paragraph [0023]: \"The system receives multilingual input text containing three, four, or more different languages intermixed within a single sentence or utterance.\"\nParagraph [0035]: \"A language detection module identifies each distinct language present and computes a semantic weight score for each language based on its contribution to the overall semantic meaning of the input.\"\nParagraph [0041]: \"The language with the highest semantic weight score is selected as the pivot language for intermediate translation.\"\nParagraph [0047]: \"A two-stage translation process first translates the multilingual input into the pivot language, then translates from the pivot language to the target language specified by the user.\"\nParagraph [0058]: \"An AI-based verification module, implemented as a neural network, evaluates the target language output by comparing it against the original multilingual input to assess semantic fidelity.\"\nParagraph [0063]: \"The verification module generates feedback signals that are fed back to the translation system. If translation quality is deemed insufficient, the feedback triggers re-translation with adjustments.\"\nParagraph [0067]: \"The system iterates through translation and verification cycles until the verification module confirms acceptable translation quality, at which point the final target language translation is output to the user.\""}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn3- method-long\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA computer-implemented method for translating multilingual text, comprising:\nreceiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages;\nidentifying each of the three or more languages present in the input sentence using language detection;\ncomputing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning;\nselecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;\nperforming a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output;\nperforming a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;\nsubmitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model;\nthe automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence;\nthe automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;\nwhen the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output;\nrepeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality; and\noutputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nParagraph [0023]: \"The system receives multilingual input text containing three, four, or more different languages intermixed within a single sentence or utterance.\"\nParagraph [0035]: \"A language detection module identifies each distinct language present and computes a semantic weight score for each language based on its contribution to the overall semantic meaning of the input.\"\nParagraph [0041]: \"The language with the highest semantic weight score is selected as the pivot language for intermediate translation.\"\nParagraph [0047]: \"A two-stage translation process first translates the multilingual input into the pivot language, then translates from the pivot language to the target language specified by the user.\"\nParagraph [0058]: \"An AI-based verification module, implemented as a neural network, evaluates the target language output by comparing it against the original multilingual input to assess semantic fidelity.\"\nParagraph [0063]: \"The verification module generates feedback signals that are fed back to the translation system. If translation quality is deemed insufficient, the feedback triggers re-translation with adjustments.\"\nParagraph [0067]: \"The system iterates through translation and verification cycles until the verification module confirms acceptable translation quality, at which point the final target language translation is output to the user.\""}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly recites receiving an input sentence with a mixture of three or more distinct languages, matching the source element. Therefore the element lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent mentions a user-designated target language but does not disclose that the target language must be different from each source language. This requirement is not found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"detecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"identifying each of the three or more languages present in the input sentence using language detection;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches language detection of each language in the mixed sentence, directly covering this element. Hence it lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"computing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent computes a semantic importance/weight score for each language, matching the source element. Therefore the element is not novel and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"identifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"selecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent selects a pivot language with the highest semantic importance score, which is the same functional concept as the dominant language. The element lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"performing a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent performs a first translation to a pivot (dominant) language, directly mirroring this step. Hence no novelty and it is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"performing a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The two\u2011stage translation to a final target language is disclosed in the target claim, so the element lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"submitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model; ... the automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent includes an automated verification module (neural network) that compares the target output with the original multilingual input, matching the source element. Thus it is not novel and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2465, "tokens_output": 4096, "latency_ms": 10610}, "parsed_output": null, "status": "parse_error", "error": "Failed to parse LLM response as JSON: Unterminated string starting at: line 81 column 7 (char 5246)", "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "20cbda1b-c92f-4b1a-baed-ec7d6d36eb52", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:10:14.579021+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn3- method-long", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-impli", "independent_claim": "A method for processing polyglot text input, the method comprising:\nreceiving text input containing segments in multiple languages;\nanalyzing the text input to identify language components and determining a primary language based on linguistic analysis;\nconverting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language;\ntranslating the intermediate representation to a desired output language;\nevaluating translation quality using automated assessment techniques; and\nresponsive to quality evaluation results, adjusting translation parameters and regenerating output until satisfactory quality is achieved.", "specification": "Paragraph [0015]: \"The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed.\" (Implicit: code-mixed with 3+ languages, though not explicitly stated as requirement)\nParagraph [0028]: \"Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.\" (Implicit: semantic weight analysis, though described as \"linguistic analysis\" and \"contributes most significantly\" rather than explicit semantic weight calculation)\nParagraph [0034]: \"The system employs a cascaded translation approach utilizing the primary language as a bridge between source and target.\" (Implicit: two-stage translation through dominant language, though \"cascaded\" and \"bridge\" are used instead of explicit two-stage description)\nParagraph [0045]: \"Quality assessment mechanisms evaluate the output translation using automated techniques, including computational models trained on translation tasks.\" (Implicit: AI verifier, though described as \"automated techniques\" and \"computational models\" without explicitly stating AI verifier model)\nParagraph [0052]: \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation.\" (Implicit: feedback loop mechanism, though described as \"modifies translation parameters\" rather than explicit feedback loop with verifier feedback)\nParagraph [0056]: \"The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\" (Implicit: iterative refinement, though described as \"repeated cycles\" without explicit iteration details)"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn3- method-long\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\nreceiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\ndetecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\ndetermining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\nidentifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\ngenerating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\ngenerating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\nprocessing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\nreceiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success; and\nresponsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-impli\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for processing polyglot text input, the method comprising:\nreceiving text input containing segments in multiple languages;\nanalyzing the text input to identify language components and determining a primary language based on linguistic analysis;\nconverting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language;\ntranslating the intermediate representation to a desired output language;\nevaluating translation quality using automated assessment techniques; and\nresponsive to quality evaluation results, adjusting translation parameters and regenerating output until satisfactory quality is achieved.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nParagraph [0015]: \"The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed.\" (Implicit: code-mixed with 3+ languages, though not explicitly stated as requirement)\nParagraph [0028]: \"Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.\" (Implicit: semantic weight analysis, though described as \"linguistic analysis\" and \"contributes most significantly\" rather than explicit semantic weight calculation)\nParagraph [0034]: \"The system employs a cascaded translation approach utilizing the primary language as a bridge between source and target.\" (Implicit: two-stage translation through dominant language, though \"cascaded\" and \"bridge\" are used instead of explicit two-stage description)\nParagraph [0045]: \"Quality assessment mechanisms evaluate the output translation using automated techniques, including computational models trained on translation tasks.\" (Implicit: AI verifier, though described as \"automated techniques\" and \"computational models\" without explicitly stating AI verifier model)\nParagraph [0052]: \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation.\" (Implicit: feedback loop mechanism, though described as \"modifies translation parameters\" rather than explicit feedback loop with verifier feedback)\nParagraph [0056]: \"The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\" (Implicit: iterative refinement, though described as \"repeated cycles\" without explicit iteration details)"}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly mentions handling input text with three or more distinct languages intermixed, matching the source element. Therefore the element lacks novelty and is obvious in view of the prior art.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose a requirement that the target language be different from all source languages. This requirement is not found, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"detecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"While the target mentions \\\"multiple language segments,\\\" it does not expressly disclose detecting each language present. No explicit detection step is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target describes \\\"linguistic analysis\\\" but does not disclose calculating a semantic weight for each language. Absence of such a specific metric makes the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"identifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art mentions a \\\"primary language\\\" but does not tie it to a calculated semantic weight nor define a dominant language by highest weight. Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"converting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim explicitly recites converting the input to an intermediate representation in the primary language, which corresponds to generating an intermediate translation. The element lacks novelty and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"translating the intermediate representation to a desired output language;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim directly discloses translating the intermediate representation to a desired output language, matching the source element. Thus the element is not novel and is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target only mentions \\\"automated assessment techniques\\\" and \\\"computational models\\\" without specifying an AI verifier that directly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence. This specific AI verification step is not disclosed, making it novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"No explicit feedback mechanism from an AI verifier is described in the prior art. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 10,\n      \"element_text\": \"responsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation. ... The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target specification discloses a loop where quality metrics below a threshold trigger parameter modification and regeneration, continuing until satisfactory metrics are achieved. This corresponds to the iterative refinement based on feedback, rendering the element lacking novelty and obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 11,\n      \"element_text\": \"responsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art does not expressly disclose a final output step triggered by successful verification feedback. Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The source patent contains several core technical contributions\u2014specifically the semantic\u2011weight calculation for each language, the explicit AI verifier that compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence, and the requirement that the target language differ from all source languages\u2014that are not disclosed in the prior art. While some steps (receiving multi\u2011language input, two\u2011stage translation via a primary language, and an iterative quality\u2011feedback loop) are anticipated by the target patent, the novel elements constitute the inventive heart of the invention. Because the key advancements are novel and non\u2011obvious, the source patent is likely to be upheld as valid despite overlap in routine translation workflow steps.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2397, "tokens_output": 3502, "latency_ms": 11490}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages;", "corresponding_text": "The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly mentions handling input text with three or more distinct languages intermixed, matching the source element. Therefore the element lacks novelty and is obvious in view of the prior art."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receiving a target language specification, wherein the target language is different from each of the three or more languages;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose a requirement that the target language be different from all source languages. This requirement is not found, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "detecting the three or more languages present in the code-mixed sentence;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "While the target mentions \"multiple language segments,\" it does not expressly disclose detecting each language present. No explicit detection step is found, so the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "determining, for each of the three or more languages, a semantic weight associated with that language in the code-mixed sentence;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target describes \"linguistic analysis\" but does not disclose calculating a semantic weight for each language. Absence of such a specific metric makes the element novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "identifying a dominant language from among the three or more languages based on the semantic weight, wherein the dominant language has a highest semantic weight;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art mentions a \"primary language\" but does not tie it to a calculated semantic weight nor define a dominant language by highest weight. Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "generating an intermediate translation by translating the code-mixed sentence into the dominant language;", "corresponding_text": "converting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language;", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim explicitly recites converting the input to an intermediate representation in the primary language, which corresponds to generating an intermediate translation. The element lacks novelty and is obvious."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "generating an initial target language translation by translating the intermediate translation from the dominant language to the target language;", "corresponding_text": "translating the intermediate representation to a desired output language;", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim directly discloses translating the intermediate representation to a desired output language, matching the source element. Thus the element is not novel and is obvious."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "processing the initial target language translation using an artificial intelligence (AI) verifier model, wherein the AI verifier model compares the initial target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target only mentions \"automated assessment techniques\" and \"computational models\" without specifying an AI verifier that directly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence. This specific AI verification step is not disclosed, making it novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 9, "element_text": "receiving verification feedback from the AI verifier model;", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "No explicit feedback mechanism from an AI verifier is described in the prior art. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious."}, {"element_number": 10, "element_text": "responsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification failure, iteratively refining the target language translation by generating a revised target language translation based on the verification feedback until the verification feedback indicates a verification success;", "corresponding_text": "When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation. ... The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target specification discloses a loop where quality metrics below a threshold trigger parameter modification and regeneration, continuing until satisfactory metrics are achieved. This corresponds to the iterative refinement based on feedback, rendering the element lacking novelty and obvious."}, {"element_number": 11, "element_text": "responsive to the verification feedback indicating a verification success, outputting a final target language translation.", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The prior art does not expressly disclose a final output step triggered by successful verification feedback. Hence the element is novel and non\u2011obvious."}], "overall_opinion": "The source patent contains several core technical contributions\u2014specifically the semantic\u2011weight calculation for each language, the explicit AI verifier that compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence, and the requirement that the target language differ from all source languages\u2014that are not disclosed in the prior art. While some steps (receiving multi\u2011language input, two\u2011stage translation via a primary language, and an iterative quality\u2011feedback loop) are anticipated by the target patent, the novel elements constitute the inventive heart of the invention. Because the key advancements are novel and non\u2011obvious, the source patent is likely to be upheld as valid despite overlap in routine translation workflow steps."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "36d5f233-2039-4bd8-92f3-76b4adff5053", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:10:57.098962+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn3-method-short", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A computer-implemented method for translating multilingual text, comprising:\nreceiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages;\nidentifying each of the three or more languages present in the input sentence using language detection;\ncomputing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning;\nselecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;\nperforming a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output;\nperforming a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;\nsubmitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model;\nthe automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence;\nthe automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;\nwhen the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output;\nrepeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality; and\noutputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved.", "specification": "Paragraph [0023]: \"The system receives multilingual input text containing three, four, or more different languages intermixed within a single sentence or utterance.\"\nParagraph [0035]: \"A language detection module identifies each distinct language present and computes a semantic weight score for each language based on its contribution to the overall semantic meaning of the input.\"\nParagraph [0041]: \"The language with the highest semantic weight score is selected as the pivot language for intermediate translation.\"\nParagraph [0047]: \"A two-stage translation process first translates the multilingual input into the pivot language, then translates from the pivot language to the target language specified by the user.\"\nParagraph [0058]: \"An AI-based verification module, implemented as a neural network, evaluates the target language output by comparing it against the original multilingual input to assess semantic fidelity.\"\nParagraph [0063]: \"The verification module generates feedback signals that are fed back to the translation system. If translation quality is deemed insufficient, the feedback triggers re-translation with adjustments.\"\nParagraph [0067]: \"The system iterates through translation and verification cycles until the verification module confirms acceptable translation quality, at which point the final target language translation is output to the user."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn3-method-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA computer-implemented method for translating multilingual text, comprising:\nreceiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages;\nidentifying each of the three or more languages present in the input sentence using language detection;\ncomputing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning;\nselecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;\nperforming a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output;\nperforming a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;\nsubmitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model;\nthe automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence;\nthe automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;\nwhen the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output;\nrepeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality; and\noutputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nParagraph [0023]: \"The system receives multilingual input text containing three, four, or more different languages intermixed within a single sentence or utterance.\"\nParagraph [0035]: \"A language detection module identifies each distinct language present and computes a semantic weight score for each language based on its contribution to the overall semantic meaning of the input.\"\nParagraph [0041]: \"The language with the highest semantic weight score is selected as the pivot language for intermediate translation.\"\nParagraph [0047]: \"A two-stage translation process first translates the multilingual input into the pivot language, then translates from the pivot language to the target language specified by the user.\"\nParagraph [0058]: \"An AI-based verification module, implemented as a neural network, evaluates the target language output by comparing it against the original multilingual input to assess semantic fidelity.\"\nParagraph [0063]: \"The verification module generates feedback signals that are fed back to the translation system. If translation quality is deemed insufficient, the feedback triggers re-translation with adjustments.\"\nParagraph [0067]: \"The system iterates through translation and verification cycles until the verification module confirms acceptable translation quality, at which point the final target language translation is output to the user."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages; ... user-designated target language\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s independent claim recites receiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages, which matches the code\u2011mixed sentence requirement. The claim also includes a user\u2011designated target language, satisfying the target language specification. Because the same functional features are disclosed, the element lacks novelty (Y). Given that the prior art teaches both receiving multilingual input and specifying a target language, the element would be obvious to one of ordinary skill (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"identifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"computing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning; ... selecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses computing a semantic importance (weight) score for each language and then selecting the language with the highest score as the pivot language. This directly corresponds to identifying a dominant language via semantic weight analysis. Hence the element is not novel (Y). The combination of semantic scoring and selecting the highest\u2011scoring language is taught, making the approach obvious (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"performing a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output; performing a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art explicitly teaches a two\u2011stage translation: first to a pivot (dominant) language, then to the final target language. This matches the source element\u2019s two\u2011step translation via the dominant language. Therefore the element lacks novelty (Y) and, being taught, is obvious (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"submitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model; the automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence; the automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s verification module is an AI (neural network) that compares the target output with the original multilingual input and produces a quality/feedback signal. This aligns with the source\u2019s AI verifier that generates verification feedback. Consequently, the element is not novel (Y) and is obvious (Y).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"responsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"when the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output; repeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality; and outputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art describes an iterative loop where a quality assessment signal triggers correction and re\u2011translation until the signal indicates adequate quality, after which the final translation is output. This mirrors the source\u2019s iterative refinement based on verification feedback. Hence the element lacks novelty (Y) and is obvious (Y).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"All core technical elements of the source patent are disclosed in the prior art and would be considered obvious to a person of ordinary skill. Because each element is mapped to explicit teachings in the target patent, the source claim lacks both novelty and inventive step, rendering the patent likely invalid.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2098, "tokens_output": 2475, "latency_ms": 6548}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification", "corresponding_text": "receiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages; ... user-designated target language", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s independent claim recites receiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages, which matches the code\u2011mixed sentence requirement. The claim also includes a user\u2011designated target language, satisfying the target language specification. Because the same functional features are disclosed, the element lacks novelty (Y). Given that the prior art teaches both receiving multilingual input and specifying a target language, the element would be obvious to one of ordinary skill (Y)."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "identifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis", "corresponding_text": "computing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning; ... selecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses computing a semantic importance (weight) score for each language and then selecting the language with the highest score as the pivot language. This directly corresponds to identifying a dominant language via semantic weight analysis. Hence the element is not novel (Y). The combination of semantic scoring and selecting the highest\u2011scoring language is taught, making the approach obvious (Y)."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "generating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language", "corresponding_text": "performing a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output; performing a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The prior art explicitly teaches a two\u2011stage translation: first to a pivot (dominant) language, then to the final target language. This matches the source element\u2019s two\u2011step translation via the dominant language. Therefore the element lacks novelty (Y) and, being taught, is obvious (Y)."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "processing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback", "corresponding_text": "submitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model; the automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence; the automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s verification module is an AI (neural network) that compares the target output with the original multilingual input and produces a quality/feedback signal. This aligns with the source\u2019s AI verifier that generates verification feedback. Consequently, the element is not novel (Y) and is obvious (Y)."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "responsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success", "corresponding_text": "when the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output; repeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality; and outputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The prior art describes an iterative loop where a quality assessment signal triggers correction and re\u2011translation until the signal indicates adequate quality, after which the final translation is output. This mirrors the source\u2019s iterative refinement based on verification feedback. Hence the element lacks novelty (Y) and is obvious (Y)."}], "overall_opinion": "All core technical elements of the source patent are disclosed in the prior art and would be considered obvious to a person of ordinary skill. Because each element is mapped to explicit teachings in the target patent, the source claim lacks both novelty and inventive step, rendering the patent likely invalid."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "bfef4fb6-3b40-428c-8994-4a6eda88aecb", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:11:39.635905+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn3-method-short", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-impli", "independent_claim": "A method for processing polyglot text input, the method comprising:\nreceiving text input containing segments in multiple languages;\nanalyzing the text input to identify language components and determining a primary language based on linguistic analysis;\nconverting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language;\ntranslating the intermediate representation to a desired output language;\nevaluating translation quality using automated assessment techniques; and\nresponsive to quality evaluation results, adjusting translation parameters and regenerating output until satisfactory quality is achieved.", "specification": "Paragraph [0015]: \"The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed.\" (Implicit: code-mixed with 3+ languages, though not explicitly stated as requirement)\nParagraph [0028]: \"Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.\" (Implicit: semantic weight analysis, though described as \"linguistic analysis\" and \"contributes most significantly\" rather than explicit semantic weight calculation)\nParagraph [0034]: \"The system employs a cascaded translation approach utilizing the primary language as a bridge between source and target.\" (Implicit: two-stage translation through dominant language, though \"cascaded\" and \"bridge\" are used instead of explicit two-stage description)\nParagraph [0045]: \"Quality assessment mechanisms evaluate the output translation using automated techniques, including computational models trained on translation tasks.\" (Implicit: AI verifier, though described as \"automated techniques\" and \"computational models\" without explicitly stating AI verifier model)\nParagraph [0052]: \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation.\" (Implicit: feedback loop mechanism, though described as \"modifies translation parameters\" rather than explicit feedback loop with verifier feedback)\nParagraph [0056]: \"The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\" (Implicit: iterative refinement, though described as \"repeated cycles\" without explicit iteration details)"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn3-method-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification;\nidentifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language;\nprocessing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback; and\nresponsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-impli\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for processing polyglot text input, the method comprising:\nreceiving text input containing segments in multiple languages;\nanalyzing the text input to identify language components and determining a primary language based on linguistic analysis;\nconverting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language;\ntranslating the intermediate representation to a desired output language;\nevaluating translation quality using automated assessment techniques; and\nresponsive to quality evaluation results, adjusting translation parameters and regenerating output until satisfactory quality is achieved.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nParagraph [0015]: \"The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed.\" (Implicit: code-mixed with 3+ languages, though not explicitly stated as requirement)\nParagraph [0028]: \"Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.\" (Implicit: semantic weight analysis, though described as \"linguistic analysis\" and \"contributes most significantly\" rather than explicit semantic weight calculation)\nParagraph [0034]: \"The system employs a cascaded translation approach utilizing the primary language as a bridge between source and target.\" (Implicit: two-stage translation through dominant language, though \"cascaded\" and \"bridge\" are used instead of explicit two-stage description)\nParagraph [0045]: \"Quality assessment mechanisms evaluate the output translation using automated techniques, including computational models trained on translation tasks.\" (Implicit: AI verifier, though described as \"automated techniques\" and \"computational models\" without explicitly stating AI verifier model)\nParagraph [0052]: \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation.\" (Implicit: feedback loop mechanism, though described as \"modifies translation parameters\" rather than explicit feedback loop with verifier feedback)\nParagraph [0056]: \"The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\" (Implicit: iterative refinement, though described as \"repeated cycles\" without explicit iteration details)"}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses receiving text input with multiple language segments and even mentions the possibility of three or more languages (para [0015]), but it does not disclose a target language specification. Because the source element combines both features and the target lacks the specification part, the element is not fully disclosed, making it novel (N). Since the element is novel, the inventive step is also considered non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"identifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target specification explicitly teaches determining a primary language based on linguistic analysis of contribution to meaning (para [0028]), which corresponds to identifying a dominant language via semantic weight. Thus the element is disclosed (novelty Y). Using linguistic analysis to select a dominant language is a routine approach in multilingual processing, so the inventive step is also considered obvious (Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"The system employs a cascaded translation approach utilizing the primary language as a bridge between source and target.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent describes converting input to an intermediate representation in the primary language and then translating to the desired output language (independent claim) and further calls this a cascaded translation using the primary language as a bridge (para [0034]). This matches the two\u2011stage translation through a dominant language, so the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an obvious technique in translation pipelines (inventive step Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent mentions \\\"automated assessment techniques\\\" and \\\"computational models trained on translation tasks\\\" (para [0045]), but it does not specify an AI verifier model that directly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence and produces explicit verification feedback. Because this specific AI\u2011based comparison and feedback mechanism is absent, the element is novel (N) and represents a non\u2011obvious improvement (N). Verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"responsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation ... The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses a feedback loop where quality evaluation results trigger adjustments to translation parameters and regeneration of output until satisfactory quality is achieved (independent claim and paras [0052] and [0056]). This maps directly to the iterative refinement based on verification feedback described in the source element, making it disclosed (novelty Y). Such iterative quality\u2011driven refinement is a known practice in translation systems, so the inventive step is also obvious (Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement of the source patent lies in the AI verifier model that directly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence and generates verification feedback (element 4). This feature is not disclosed in the prior art, rendering it novel and non\u2011obvious. All other elements are found in the target patent and are considered obvious. Because the pivotal AI\u2011based verification step is missing from the prior art, the source patent retains a substantive inventive contribution and is likely valid.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2030, "tokens_output": 2546, "latency_ms": 6710}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and a target language specification", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent discloses receiving text input with multiple language segments and even mentions the possibility of three or more languages (para [0015]), but it does not disclose a target language specification. Because the source element combines both features and the target lacks the specification part, the element is not fully disclosed, making it novel (N). Since the element is novel, the inventive step is also considered non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "identifying a dominant language from the three or more languages based on semantic weight analysis", "corresponding_text": "Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target specification explicitly teaches determining a primary language based on linguistic analysis of contribution to meaning (para [0028]), which corresponds to identifying a dominant language via semantic weight. Thus the element is disclosed (novelty Y). Using linguistic analysis to select a dominant language is a routine approach in multilingual processing, so the inventive step is also considered obvious (Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "generating a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a target language", "corresponding_text": "The system employs a cascaded translation approach utilizing the primary language as a bridge between source and target.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent describes converting input to an intermediate representation in the primary language and then translating to the desired output language (independent claim) and further calls this a cascaded translation using the primary language as a bridge (para [0034]). This matches the two\u2011stage translation through a dominant language, so the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an obvious technique in translation pipelines (inventive step Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "processing the target language translation using an AI verifier model that compares the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence and generates verification feedback", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent mentions \"automated assessment techniques\" and \"computational models trained on translation tasks\" (para [0045]), but it does not specify an AI verifier model that directly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence and produces explicit verification feedback. Because this specific AI\u2011based comparison and feedback mechanism is absent, the element is novel (N) and represents a non\u2011obvious improvement (N). Verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "responsive to verification feedback indicating failure, iteratively refining the target language translation based on the verification feedback until verification success, and outputting a final target language translation responsive to verification success", "corresponding_text": "When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation ... The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses a feedback loop where quality evaluation results trigger adjustments to translation parameters and regeneration of output until satisfactory quality is achieved (independent claim and paras [0052] and [0056]). This maps directly to the iterative refinement based on verification feedback described in the source element, making it disclosed (novelty Y). Such iterative quality\u2011driven refinement is a known practice in translation systems, so the inventive step is also obvious (Y). Verdict is Y."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement of the source patent lies in the AI verifier model that directly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence and generates verification feedback (element 4). This feature is not disclosed in the prior art, rendering it novel and non\u2011obvious. All other elements are found in the target patent and are considered obvious. Because the pivotal AI\u2011based verification step is missing from the prior art, the source patent retains a substantive inventive contribution and is likely valid."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "a4e86f9e-734a-4d1b-b4f3-f2b6be3bbef8", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:12:23.666552+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn3-sys-short", "independent_claim": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.", "specification": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A computer-implemented method for translating multilingual text, comprising:\nreceiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages;\nidentifying each of the three or more languages present in the input sentence using language detection;\ncomputing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning;\nselecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;\nperforming a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output;\nperforming a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;\nsubmitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model;\nthe automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence;\nthe automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;\nwhen the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output;\nrepeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality; and\noutputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved.", "specification": "Paragraph [0023]: \"The system receives multilingual input text containing three, four, or more different languages intermixed within a single sentence or utterance.\"\nParagraph [0035]: \"A language detection module identifies each distinct language present and computes a semantic weight score for each language based on its contribution to the overall semantic meaning of the input.\"\nParagraph [0041]: \"The language with the highest semantic weight score is selected as the pivot language for intermediate translation.\"\nParagraph [0047]: \"A two-stage translation process first translates the multilingual input into the pivot language, then translates from the pivot language to the target language specified by the user.\"\nParagraph [0058]: \"An AI-based verification module, implemented as a neural network, evaluates the target language output by comparing it against the original multilingual input to assess semantic fidelity.\"\nParagraph [0063]: \"The verification module generates feedback signals that are fed back to the translation system. If translation quality is deemed insufficient, the feedback triggers re-translation with adjustments.\"\nParagraph [0067]: \"The system iterates through translation and verification cycles until the verification module confirms acceptable translation quality, at which point the final target language translation is output to the user.\""}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn3-sys-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA computer-implemented method for translating multilingual text, comprising:\nreceiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages;\nidentifying each of the three or more languages present in the input sentence using language detection;\ncomputing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning;\nselecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;\nperforming a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output;\nperforming a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;\nsubmitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model;\nthe automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence;\nthe automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;\nwhen the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output;\nrepeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality; and\noutputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nParagraph [0023]: \"The system receives multilingual input text containing three, four, or more different languages intermixed within a single sentence or utterance.\"\nParagraph [0035]: \"A language detection module identifies each distinct language present and computes a semantic weight score for each language based on its contribution to the overall semantic meaning of the input.\"\nParagraph [0041]: \"The language with the highest semantic weight score is selected as the pivot language for intermediate translation.\"\nParagraph [0047]: \"A two-stage translation process first translates the multilingual input into the pivot language, then translates from the pivot language to the target language specified by the user.\"\nParagraph [0058]: \"An AI-based verification module, implemented as a neural network, evaluates the target language output by comparing it against the original multilingual input to assess semantic fidelity.\"\nParagraph [0063]: \"The verification module generates feedback signals that are fed back to the translation system. If translation quality is deemed insufficient, the feedback triggers re-translation with adjustments.\"\nParagraph [0067]: \"The system iterates through translation and verification cycles until the verification module confirms acceptable translation quality, at which point the final target language translation is output to the user.\""}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose any hardware component such as a processor. Therefore the element is not found (novelty N). Because the presence of a processor is a routine implementation detail, it does not add an inventive step, but novelty already fails, so the verdict is N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages; ... computing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning; ... selecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly teaches receiving a multilingual sentence with three or more languages, computing a semantic weight/importance score for each language, and selecting the language with the highest score as a pivot (dominant) language. Thus the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and, given the teaching, the approach would be obvious to one skilled in the art (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"generate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"performing a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output; ... performing a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses a two\u2011stage translation: first to a pivot (dominant) language, then to a user\u2011designated target language. This matches the claimed element, so it is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an obvious use of known pivot\u2011language translation techniques (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"process the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"submitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model; ... the automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence; ... the automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches an AI\u2011based verification module (neural network) that compares the target translation with the original multilingual input and produces a quality signal. This directly corresponds to the claimed AI verifier model and feedback generation, so the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and would be obvious given the disclosed verification module (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"iteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"when the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output; ... repeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly describes an iterative loop where the verification module\u2019s feedback triggers re\u2011translation until an adequate quality signal is obtained. This matches the claimed iterative refinement, so the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an obvious application of feedback\u2011driven translation refinement (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"output a final target language translation responsive to verification success\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"outputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent concludes with outputting the final translation once the verification module signals adequate quality, which directly mirrors the claimed final output step. Hence the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an expected result of the preceding iterative process (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement elements of the source claim (elements 2\u20116) are fully disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent and would be obvious to a person skilled in the art. Only the generic hardware element (processor) is not disclosed, but it is a routine implementation feature and does not confer patentability. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and an inventive step for its substantive contributions and is likely invalid over the target patent.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2078, "tokens_output": 3307, "latency_ms": 8205}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one processor", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose any hardware component such as a processor. Therefore the element is not found (novelty N). Because the presence of a processor is a routine implementation detail, it does not add an inventive step, but novelty already fails, so the verdict is N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis", "corresponding_text": "receiving an input sentence containing a mixture of three or more distinct languages; ... computing a semantic importance score for each identified language based on semantic contribution to overall meaning; ... selecting a pivot language from the identified languages, wherein the pivot language has the highest semantic importance score;", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly teaches receiving a multilingual sentence with three or more languages, computing a semantic weight/importance score for each language, and selecting the language with the highest score as a pivot (dominant) language. Thus the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and, given the teaching, the approach would be obvious to one skilled in the art (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "generate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language", "corresponding_text": "performing a first translation operation to convert the multilingual input sentence into the pivot language, producing a pivot language output; ... performing a second translation operation to convert the pivot language output into a user-designated target language, producing a target language output;", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses a two\u2011stage translation: first to a pivot (dominant) language, then to a user\u2011designated target language. This matches the claimed element, so it is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an obvious use of known pivot\u2011language translation techniques (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "process the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence", "corresponding_text": "submitting the target language output to an automated verification module comprising a neural network model; ... the automated verification module analyzing semantic consistency between the target language output and the multilingual input sentence; ... the automated verification module generating a quality assessment signal;", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent teaches an AI\u2011based verification module (neural network) that compares the target translation with the original multilingual input and produces a quality signal. This directly corresponds to the claimed AI verifier model and feedback generation, so the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and would be obvious given the disclosed verification module (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "iteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success", "corresponding_text": "when the quality assessment signal indicates inadequate translation quality, transmitting correction signals to the translation operations to regenerate the target language output; ... repeating the translation and verification operations iteratively until the quality assessment signal indicates adequate translation quality;", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly describes an iterative loop where the verification module\u2019s feedback triggers re\u2011translation until an adequate quality signal is obtained. This matches the claimed iterative refinement, so the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an obvious application of feedback\u2011driven translation refinement (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "output a final target language translation responsive to verification success", "corresponding_text": "outputting the target language output as a final translation when adequate translation quality is achieved;", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent concludes with outputting the final translation once the verification module signals adequate quality, which directly mirrors the claimed final output step. Hence the element is disclosed (novelty Y) and is an expected result of the preceding iterative process (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement elements of the source claim (elements 2\u20116) are fully disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent and would be obvious to a person skilled in the art. Only the generic hardware element (processor) is not disclosed, but it is a routine implementation feature and does not confer patentability. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and an inventive step for its substantive contributions and is likely invalid over the target patent."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "b333a739-4125-49bb-a769-c1ee29543ec5", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:13:06.126955+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn3-sys-short", "independent_claim": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.", "specification": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A method for processing polyglot text input, the method comprising:\nreceiving text input containing segments in multiple languages;\nanalyzing the text input to identify language components and determining a primary language based on linguistic analysis;\nconverting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language;\ntranslating the intermediate representation to a desired output language;\nevaluating translation quality using automated assessment techniques; and\nresponsive to quality evaluation results, adjusting translation parameters and regenerating output until satisfactory quality is achieved.", "specification": "Paragraph [0015]: \"The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed.\" (Implicit: code-mixed with 3+ languages, though not explicitly stated as requirement)\nParagraph [0028]: \"Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.\" (Implicit: semantic weight analysis, though described as \"linguistic analysis\" and \"contributes most significantly\" rather than explicit semantic weight calculation)\nParagraph [0034]: \"The system employs a cascaded translation approach utilizing the primary language as a bridge between source and target.\" (Implicit: two-stage translation through dominant language, though \"cascaded\" and \"bridge\" are used instead of explicit two-stage description)\nParagraph [0045]: \"Quality assessment mechanisms evaluate the output translation using automated techniques, including computational models trained on translation tasks.\" (Implicit: AI verifier, though described as \"automated techniques\" and \"computational models\" without explicitly stating AI verifier model)\nParagraph [0052]: \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation.\" (Implicit: feedback loop mechanism, though described as \"modifies translation parameters\" rather than explicit feedback loop with verifier feedback)\nParagraph [0056]: \"The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\" (Implicit: iterative refinement, though described as \"repeated cycles\" without explicit iteration details)"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn3-sys-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis;\ngenerate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language;\nprocess the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence;\niteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success; and\noutput a final target language translation responsive to verification success.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for processing polyglot text input, the method comprising:\nreceiving text input containing segments in multiple languages;\nanalyzing the text input to identify language components and determining a primary language based on linguistic analysis;\nconverting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language;\ntranslating the intermediate representation to a desired output language;\nevaluating translation quality using automated assessment techniques; and\nresponsive to quality evaluation results, adjusting translation parameters and regenerating output until satisfactory quality is achieved.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nParagraph [0015]: \"The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed.\" (Implicit: code-mixed with 3+ languages, though not explicitly stated as requirement)\nParagraph [0028]: \"Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.\" (Implicit: semantic weight analysis, though described as \"linguistic analysis\" and \"contributes most significantly\" rather than explicit semantic weight calculation)\nParagraph [0034]: \"The system employs a cascaded translation approach utilizing the primary language as a bridge between source and target.\" (Implicit: two-stage translation through dominant language, though \"cascaded\" and \"bridge\" are used instead of explicit two-stage description)\nParagraph [0045]: \"Quality assessment mechanisms evaluate the output translation using automated techniques, including computational models trained on translation tasks.\" (Implicit: AI verifier, though described as \"automated techniques\" and \"computational models\" without explicitly stating AI verifier model)\nParagraph [0052]: \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation.\" (Implicit: feedback loop mechanism, though described as \"modifies translation parameters\" rather than explicit feedback loop with verifier feedback)\nParagraph [0056]: \"The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\" (Implicit: iterative refinement, though described as \"repeated cycles\" without explicit iteration details)"}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not mention any processor hardware; therefore the element is not disclosed, giving it novelty. Without disclosure, the element is also non\u2011obvious, so the inventive step is present. Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed. ... Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The specification of the target patent explicitly describes handling input with three or more languages and determining a primary language based on its contribution to meaning, matching the source element. Since this teaching is directly disclosed, the element lacks novelty and is obvious, leading to verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"generate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"converting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language; translating the intermediate representation to a desired output language\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target independent claim recites converting the input to an intermediate representation in the primary language and then translating to the desired output language, which corresponds to translating through a dominant language. This disclosure removes novelty and makes the step obvious; verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"process the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent mentions automated quality assessment and computational models, but does not disclose an AI verifier model that explicitly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious, yielding verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"iteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation. ... The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target specification describes a feedback loop that modifies parameters and repeats translation until quality criteria are met, matching the iterative refinement element. Hence the element is anticipated and obvious; verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"output a final target language translation responsive to verification success\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"responsive to quality evaluation results, adjusting translation parameters and regenerating output until satisfactory quality is achieved.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim includes output that is generated after achieving satisfactory quality, which aligns with outputting a final translation upon verification success. The element lacks novelty and is obvious; verdict Y.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Most elements of the source claim are anticipated by the prior art, leaving only the AI verifier model that directly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence as a novel and non\u2011obvious feature. Because this single element constitutes the core technical contribution, the claim retains some validity, but its scope is substantially narrowed to that AI\u2011based verification step. If the claim is interpreted broadly to cover all listed steps, it is likely invalid due to lack of novelty and inventive step in the majority of its elements.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2010, "tokens_output": 2606, "latency_ms": 6568}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one processor", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not mention any processor hardware; therefore the element is not disclosed, giving it novelty. Without disclosure, the element is also non\u2011obvious, so the inventive step is present. Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receive a code-mixed sentence comprising three or more languages and identify a dominant language based on semantic weight analysis", "corresponding_text": "The system handles input text containing multiple language segments, which may include three or more distinct languages intermixed. ... Linguistic analysis determines which language contributes most significantly to the input text meaning, and this primary language serves as an intermediate processing stage.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The specification of the target patent explicitly describes handling input with three or more languages and determining a primary language based on its contribution to meaning, matching the source element. Since this teaching is directly disclosed, the element lacks novelty and is obvious, leading to verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "generate a target language translation by translating the code-mixed sentence through the dominant language to a user-specified target language", "corresponding_text": "converting the text input to an intermediate representation in the primary language; translating the intermediate representation to a desired output language", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target independent claim recites converting the input to an intermediate representation in the primary language and then translating to the desired output language, which corresponds to translating through a dominant language. This disclosure removes novelty and makes the step obvious; verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "process the target language translation using an AI verifier model that generates verification feedback by comparing the target language translation against the code-mixed sentence", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent mentions automated quality assessment and computational models, but does not disclose an AI verifier model that explicitly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence. The element is therefore novel and non\u2011obvious, yielding verdict N."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "iteratively refine the target language translation based on verification feedback until verification success", "corresponding_text": "When quality metrics fall below threshold values, the system modifies translation parameters based on assessment results and regenerates the translation. ... The process continues with repeated translation and assessment cycles until quality metrics indicate successful translation.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target specification describes a feedback loop that modifies parameters and repeats translation until quality criteria are met, matching the iterative refinement element. Hence the element is anticipated and obvious; verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "output a final target language translation responsive to verification success", "corresponding_text": "responsive to quality evaluation results, adjusting translation parameters and regenerating output until satisfactory quality is achieved.", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim includes output that is generated after achieving satisfactory quality, which aligns with outputting a final translation upon verification success. The element lacks novelty and is obvious; verdict Y."}], "overall_opinion": "Most elements of the source claim are anticipated by the prior art, leaving only the AI verifier model that directly compares the translation to the original code\u2011mixed sentence as a novel and non\u2011obvious feature. Because this single element constitutes the core technical contribution, the claim retains some validity, but its scope is substantially narrowed to that AI\u2011based verification step. If the claim is interpreted broadly to cover all listed steps, it is likely invalid due to lack of novelty and inventive step in the majority of its elements."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "c5e45138-ec7b-4da0-b8ec-24cfb457225e", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:13:49.364598+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn4-method-short", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocessing multimodal sensor data from a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor of the XR device to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerating, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdating the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocessing multimodal sensor data from a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor of the XR device to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerating, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdating the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocessing multimodal sensor data from a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor of the XR device to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerating, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdating the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-implici", "independent_claim": "A method for interactive guidance in a head-mounted display system, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display;\nactivating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors;\nanalyzing data from the optical sensors to recognize objects in a user's environment;\nprocessing data from the acoustic sensors to detect environmental conditions;\nutilizing data from the orientation sensors to determine user attention direction;\nsynthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation;\ndetermining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion;\nformulating inquiries to obtain the additional information, wherein the inquiries address uncertainty in user intention, task progress confirmation, potential hazards, or state of environmental elements;\npresenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field; and\npresenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries.", "specification": "The specification describes a \"smart coaching system\" for a head-mounted display worn during cooking. The user says \"Help me make pasta carbonara.\" The system activates its \"perceptual array\" including cameras (optical sensors), microphones (acoustic sensors), and \"spatial trackers\" (orientation sensors).\nThe cameras recognize ingredients on the counter, the microphones detect sizzling sounds from the stove, and the spatial trackers determine which direction the user is facing. The system builds a \"situational awareness model\" by \"integrating multiple sensor streams\" to understand the cooking context.\nThe specification states the system \"identifies knowledge gaps that could impede task execution\" and \"generates queries to resolve ambiguities.\" Examples include asking whether the user wants to use guanciale or pancetta (intention), whether water is boiling yet (progress), whether the heat is too high based on smoke detection (hazards), or whether eggs are at room temperature (state).\nThe system uses \"visual overlays\" including \"directional pointers\" (arrows) and \"text annotations\" displayed in the user's field of view. While the specification doesn't explicitly use the exact phrase \"multimodal sensor fusion\" or \"arrow marks,\" it describes the same functional approach of combining vision, audio, and motion data to generate contextual questions with visual hints."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn4-method-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocessing multimodal sensor data from a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor of the XR device to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerating, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdating the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocessing multimodal sensor data from a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor of the XR device to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerating, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdating the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocessing multimodal sensor data from a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor of the XR device to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerating, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdating the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-implici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for interactive guidance in a head-mounted display system, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display;\nactivating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors;\nanalyzing data from the optical sensors to recognize objects in a user's environment;\nprocessing data from the acoustic sensors to detect environmental conditions;\nutilizing data from the orientation sensors to determine user attention direction;\nsynthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation;\ndetermining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion;\nformulating inquiries to obtain the additional information, wherein the inquiries address uncertainty in user intention, task progress confirmation, potential hazards, or state of environmental elements;\npresenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field; and\npresenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe specification describes a \"smart coaching system\" for a head-mounted display worn during cooking. The user says \"Help me make pasta carbonara.\" The system activates its \"perceptual array\" including cameras (optical sensors), microphones (acoustic sensors), and \"spatial trackers\" (orientation sensors).\nThe cameras recognize ingredients on the counter, the microphones detect sizzling sounds from the stove, and the spatial trackers determine which direction the user is facing. The system builds a \"situational awareness model\" by \"integrating multiple sensor streams\" to understand the cooking context.\nThe specification states the system \"identifies knowledge gaps that could impede task execution\" and \"generates queries to resolve ambiguities.\" Examples include asking whether the user wants to use guanciale or pancetta (intention), whether water is boiling yet (progress), whether the heat is too high based on smoke detection (hazards), or whether eggs are at room temperature (state).\nThe system uses \"visual overlays\" including \"directional pointers\" (arrows) and \"text annotations\" displayed in the user's field of view. While the specification doesn't explicitly use the exact phrase \"multimodal sensor fusion\" or \"arrow marks,\" it describes the same functional approach of combining vision, audio, and motion data to generate contextual questions with visual hints."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving user input specifying a task to be performed\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s independent claim explicitly recites receiving a user goal statement through a voice interface, which matches the source element of receiving user input specifying a task. Therefore the element lacks novelty (Y). Providing user input for task guidance is a routine function in head\u2011mounted guidance systems, making it obvious (Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing multimodal sensor data from a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor of the XR device to generate a scene understanding\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"activating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors ... synthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses activation of optical, acoustic, and orientation sensors and further teaches synthesizing data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model, which corresponds to processing multimodal sensor data to generate scene understanding. Hence the element is disclosed (novelty Y). Combining these sensor streams for contextual modeling is well\u2011known in interactive guidance, rendering the approach obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"determining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion ... formulating inquiries to obtain the additional information\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s claim and specification describe determining, based on a contextual model and user goal, what additional information is needed and formulating inquiries to obtain it. This maps directly to generating contextual prompts based on scene understanding and task. The element is therefore not novel (Y) and is an obvious step in a coaching system (Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"rendering, in a display of the XR device, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"presenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field ... presenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly recites presenting graphical indicators (e.g., arrows) and textual content in the head\u2011mounted display, which aligns with rendering visual hints of arrows or text. Thus the element is disclosed (novelty Y). Using such visual overlays for guidance is standard practice, making it obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"updating the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not contain an explicit disclosure that the contextual model (scene understanding) is continuously updated based on ongoing processing of the multimodal sensor data. No verbatim passage matches this element, so it remains novel (novelty N). Because it is not taught, the improvement is not obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict is N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The majority of the claim elements (1\u20114) are fully anticipated and rendered obvious by the prior art, leaving only the dynamic updating of the scene understanding (element 5) as a potentially novel and non\u2011obvious feature. Since the core technical contribution of the source patent hinges on the continuous refinement of scene understanding\u2014a feature not disclosed in the target patent\u2014the claim retains a limited inventive contribution. However, because four out of five elements are anticipated, the overall validity of the patent is weak and may be vulnerable to an anticipation/obviousness rejection, though the updating step could provide a narrow avenue for claim amendment or defense.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2097, "tokens_output": 2810, "latency_ms": 7112}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving user input specifying a task to be performed", "corresponding_text": "receiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s independent claim explicitly recites receiving a user goal statement through a voice interface, which matches the source element of receiving user input specifying a task. Therefore the element lacks novelty (Y). Providing user input for task guidance is a routine function in head\u2011mounted guidance systems, making it obvious (Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "processing multimodal sensor data from a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor of the XR device to generate a scene understanding", "corresponding_text": "activating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors ... synthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses activation of optical, acoustic, and orientation sensors and further teaches synthesizing data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model, which corresponds to processing multimodal sensor data to generate scene understanding. Hence the element is disclosed (novelty Y). Combining these sensor streams for contextual modeling is well\u2011known in interactive guidance, rendering the approach obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "generating, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task", "corresponding_text": "determining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion ... formulating inquiries to obtain the additional information", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s claim and specification describe determining, based on a contextual model and user goal, what additional information is needed and formulating inquiries to obtain it. This maps directly to generating contextual prompts based on scene understanding and task. The element is therefore not novel (Y) and is an obvious step in a coaching system (Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "rendering, in a display of the XR device, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts", "corresponding_text": "presenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field ... presenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly recites presenting graphical indicators (e.g., arrows) and textual content in the head\u2011mounted display, which aligns with rendering visual hints of arrows or text. Thus the element is disclosed (novelty Y). Using such visual overlays for guidance is standard practice, making it obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "updating the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not contain an explicit disclosure that the contextual model (scene understanding) is continuously updated based on ongoing processing of the multimodal sensor data. No verbatim passage matches this element, so it remains novel (novelty N). Because it is not taught, the improvement is not obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict is N."}], "overall_opinion": "The majority of the claim elements (1\u20114) are fully anticipated and rendered obvious by the prior art, leaving only the dynamic updating of the scene understanding (element 5) as a potentially novel and non\u2011obvious feature. Since the core technical contribution of the source patent hinges on the continuous refinement of scene understanding\u2014a feature not disclosed in the target patent\u2014the claim retains a limited inventive contribution. However, because four out of five elements are anticipated, the overall validity of the patent is weak and may be vulnerable to an anticipation/obviousness rejection, though the updating step could provide a narrow avenue for claim amendment or defense."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "f1ab6dfb-3f9c-4482-afca-8105c94e6e25", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:14:32.840417+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn4-method-short", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-implici", "independent_claim": "A method for interactive guidance in a head-mounted display system, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display;\nactivating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors;\nanalyzing data from the optical sensors to recognize objects in a user's environment;\nprocessing data from the acoustic sensors to detect environmental conditions;\nutilizing data from the orientation sensors to determine user attention direction;\nsynthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation;\ndetermining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion;\nformulating inquiries to obtain the additional information, wherein the inquiries address uncertainty in user intention, task progress confirmation, potential hazards, or state of environmental elements;\npresenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field; and\npresenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries.", "specification": "The specification describes a \"smart coaching system\" for a head-mounted display worn during cooking. The user says \"Help me make pasta carbonara.\" The system activates its \"perceptual array\" including cameras (optical sensors), microphones (acoustic sensors), and \"spatial trackers\" (orientation sensors).\nThe cameras recognize ingredients on the counter, the microphones detect sizzling sounds from the stove, and the spatial trackers determine which direction the user is facing. The system builds a \"situational awareness model\" by \"integrating multiple sensor streams\" to understand the cooking context.\nThe specification states the system \"identifies knowledge gaps that could impede task execution\" and \"generates queries to resolve ambiguities.\" Examples include asking whether the user wants to use guanciale or pancetta (intention), whether water is boiling yet (progress), whether the heat is too high based on smoke detection (hazards), or whether eggs are at room temperature (state).\nThe system uses \"visual overlays\" including \"directional pointers\" (arrows) and \"text annotations\" displayed in the user's field of view. While the specification doesn't explicitly use the exact phrase \"multimodal sensor fusion\" or \"arrow marks,\" it describes the same functional approach of combining vision, audio, and motion data to generate contextual questions with visual hints."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn4-method-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-implici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for interactive guidance in a head-mounted display system, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display;\nactivating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors;\nanalyzing data from the optical sensors to recognize objects in a user's environment;\nprocessing data from the acoustic sensors to detect environmental conditions;\nutilizing data from the orientation sensors to determine user attention direction;\nsynthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation;\ndetermining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion;\nformulating inquiries to obtain the additional information, wherein the inquiries address uncertainty in user intention, task progress confirmation, potential hazards, or state of environmental elements;\npresenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field; and\npresenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe specification describes a \"smart coaching system\" for a head-mounted display worn during cooking. The user says \"Help me make pasta carbonara.\" The system activates its \"perceptual array\" including cameras (optical sensors), microphones (acoustic sensors), and \"spatial trackers\" (orientation sensors).\nThe cameras recognize ingredients on the counter, the microphones detect sizzling sounds from the stove, and the spatial trackers determine which direction the user is facing. The system builds a \"situational awareness model\" by \"integrating multiple sensor streams\" to understand the cooking context.\nThe specification states the system \"identifies knowledge gaps that could impede task execution\" and \"generates queries to resolve ambiguities.\" Examples include asking whether the user wants to use guanciale or pancetta (intention), whether water is boiling yet (progress), whether the heat is too high based on smoke detection (hazards), or whether eggs are at room temperature (state).\nThe system uses \"visual overlays\" including \"directional pointers\" (arrows) and \"text annotations\" displayed in the user's field of view. While the specification doesn't explicitly use the exact phrase \"multimodal sensor fusion\" or \"arrow marks,\" it describes the same functional approach of combining vision, audio, and motion data to generate contextual questions with visual hints."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving user input specifying a task to be performed\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly recites receiving a user goal statement via voice interface, which matches the source element of receiving user input specifying a task. Therefore the element lacks novelty (Y). Given that receiving user input for task guidance is taught, the element is also obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"capturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"including optical sensors\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent activates environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, which correspond to vision sensors/cameras. Thus the element is disclosed (Y). Using vision sensors for guidance is taught, making it obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"capturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"including acoustic sensors\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent lists acoustic sensors as part of the sensing suite, equivalent to audio sensors. The element is therefore disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"capturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"including orientation sensors\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"Orientation sensors in the target serve the same function as IMU sensors. The element is disclosed (Y) and would be obvious to combine with other modalities (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"analyzing data from the optical sensors to recognize objects in a user's environment\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly states analyzing optical sensor data to recognize objects, covering object identification. Hence disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"fusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"synthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target recites synthesizing multiple sensing modalities into a contextual model, which corresponds to multimodal fusion. The element is disclosed (Y) and would be obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"determining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent determines, from the contextual model and user goal, what information is needed\u2014functionally analogous to determining a task state. Thus disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"identifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"identifies knowledge gaps that could impede task execution\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target explicitly mentions identifying knowledge gaps that could impede task execution, matching the source element of identifying information gaps. Disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"formulating inquiries to obtain the additional information, wherein the inquiries address uncertainty in user intention, task progress confirmation, potential hazards, or state of environmental elements\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target's formulation of inquiries covering user intention, progress, hazards, and state mirrors the source's contextual questions categories. Disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 10,\n      \"element_text\": \"rendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\\\"visual overlays\\\" ... \\\"directional pointers\\\" (arrows) ... \\\"text annotations\\\"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The specification describes visual overlays with directional pointers (arrows) and text annotations, matching the source's visual hints overlay. Disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 11,\n      \"element_text\": \"monitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not disclose a step of monitoring user responses via continued multimodal capture and processing. Therefore this element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014capturing multimodal sensor data, fusing it, identifying information gaps, generating contextual questions, and rendering visual hints\u2014is fully anticipated by the prior art (Patent B). All of these elements are disclosed and would be considered obvious, leading to Y verdicts for each. Only the final monitoring step is not disclosed, but it is a routine feedback mechanism that does not constitute a substantial inventive contribution. Consequently, the source patent lacks novelty and inventive step for its essential technical contribution and is likely invalid in view of the prior art.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2529, "tokens_output": 3050, "latency_ms": 7423}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving user input specifying a task to be performed", "corresponding_text": "receiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly recites receiving a user goal statement via voice interface, which matches the source element of receiving user input specifying a task. Therefore the element lacks novelty (Y). Given that receiving user input for task guidance is taught, the element is also obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "capturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device", "corresponding_text": "including optical sensors", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent activates environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, which correspond to vision sensors/cameras. Thus the element is disclosed (Y). Using vision sensors for guidance is taught, making it obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "capturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device", "corresponding_text": "including acoustic sensors", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent lists acoustic sensors as part of the sensing suite, equivalent to audio sensors. The element is therefore disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "capturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device", "corresponding_text": "including orientation sensors", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "Orientation sensors in the target serve the same function as IMU sensors. The element is disclosed (Y) and would be obvious to combine with other modalities (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "processing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene", "corresponding_text": "analyzing data from the optical sensors to recognize objects in a user's environment", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent explicitly states analyzing optical sensor data to recognize objects, covering object identification. Hence disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "fusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding", "corresponding_text": "synthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target recites synthesizing multiple sensing modalities into a contextual model, which corresponds to multimodal fusion. The element is disclosed (Y) and would be obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "determining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state", "corresponding_text": "determining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent determines, from the contextual model and user goal, what information is needed\u2014functionally analogous to determining a task state. Thus disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "identifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task", "corresponding_text": "identifies knowledge gaps that could impede task execution", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target explicitly mentions identifying knowledge gaps that could impede task execution, matching the source element of identifying information gaps. Disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 9, "element_text": "generating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question", "corresponding_text": "formulating inquiries to obtain the additional information, wherein the inquiries address uncertainty in user intention, task progress confirmation, potential hazards, or state of environmental elements", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target's formulation of inquiries covering user intention, progress, hazards, and state mirrors the source's contextual questions categories. Disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 10, "element_text": "rendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions", "corresponding_text": "\"visual overlays\" ... \"directional pointers\" (arrows) ... \"text annotations\"", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The specification describes visual overlays with directional pointers (arrows) and text annotations, matching the source's visual hints overlay. Disclosed (Y) and obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 11, "element_text": "monitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not disclose a step of monitoring user responses via continued multimodal capture and processing. Therefore this element is novel (N) and non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement of the source patent\u2014capturing multimodal sensor data, fusing it, identifying information gaps, generating contextual questions, and rendering visual hints\u2014is fully anticipated by the prior art (Patent B). All of these elements are disclosed and would be considered obvious, leading to Y verdicts for each. Only the final monitoring step is not disclosed, but it is a routine feedback mechanism that does not constitute a substantial inventive contribution. Consequently, the source patent lacks novelty and inventive step for its essential technical contribution and is likely invalid in view of the prior art."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "661d8c97-f7c5-4a90-ac0c-bc9d4f9c8420", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:15:20.282178+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn4-method-short", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A method for providing task assistance using augmented reality glasses, the method comprising:\nreceiving, at augmented reality glasses, a voice command from a user indicating a repair task to be performed on a mechanical device;\nactivating a camera sensor, a microphone sensor, and a motion sensor of the augmented reality glasses;\ncapturing image data from the camera sensor showing the mechanical device;\ncapturing audio data from the microphone sensor detecting ambient sounds;\ncapturing motion data from the motion sensor detecting user head movements;\nanalyzing the image data using computer vision to identify components of the mechanical device;\ncombining the image data, the audio data, and the motion data to determine a current state of the repair task;\ndetermining that information is needed about which component the user intends to remove;\ngenerating a clarifying question: \"Do you want to remove the left bolt or the right bolt?\";\ndisplaying, on a transparent display of the augmented reality glasses, an arrow mark pointing to the left bolt and an arrow mark pointing to the right bolt;\ndisplaying the clarifying question as text overlaid on the user's field of view;\ndetecting the user's verbal response through the microphone sensor;\nupdating the current state of the repair task based on the verbal response; and\ngenerating a next assistance prompt based on the updated current state.", "specification": "The specification describes an AR repair assistant system where a technician wearing AR glasses asks for help with \"fixing a bicycle brake.\" The system uses integrated cameras to identify brake components, microphones to listen for squeaking sounds indicating brake pad wear, and gyroscopes to track where the technician is looking. The system analyzes all three sensor types together to understand what the technician is trying to do.\nWhen the system detects multiple possible next steps (e.g., adjusting cable tension vs. replacing brake pads), it displays arrows pointing to relevant components and asks questions like \"Are you experiencing brake rubbing or weak braking force?\" These questions help clarify user intent (clarifying), verify steps have been completed (verification), warn about safety issues like \"Is the wheel properly secured?\" (safety), and diagnose problems like \"Is the cable frayed?\" (diagnostic).\nThe system continuously monitors the sensors and updates its understanding as the repair progresses, generating new visual arrows and questions at each step."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn4-method-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.A method implemented by one or more processors of an extended reality (XR) device, the method comprising:\nreceiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\ncapturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\ncapturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\ncapturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\nprocessing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\nfusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\ndetermining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\nidentifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\ngenerating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\nrendering, in a display of the XR device as an overlay on a real-world view, visual hints comprising arrow marks and visual text corresponding to the one or more contextual questions; and\nmonitoring for user response to the one or more contextual questions through continued capturing and processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for providing task assistance using augmented reality glasses, the method comprising:\nreceiving, at augmented reality glasses, a voice command from a user indicating a repair task to be performed on a mechanical device;\nactivating a camera sensor, a microphone sensor, and a motion sensor of the augmented reality glasses;\ncapturing image data from the camera sensor showing the mechanical device;\ncapturing audio data from the microphone sensor detecting ambient sounds;\ncapturing motion data from the motion sensor detecting user head movements;\nanalyzing the image data using computer vision to identify components of the mechanical device;\ncombining the image data, the audio data, and the motion data to determine a current state of the repair task;\ndetermining that information is needed about which component the user intends to remove;\ngenerating a clarifying question: \"Do you want to remove the left bolt or the right bolt?\";\ndisplaying, on a transparent display of the augmented reality glasses, an arrow mark pointing to the left bolt and an arrow mark pointing to the right bolt;\ndisplaying the clarifying question as text overlaid on the user's field of view;\ndetecting the user's verbal response through the microphone sensor;\nupdating the current state of the repair task based on the verbal response; and\ngenerating a next assistance prompt based on the updated current state.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe specification describes an AR repair assistant system where a technician wearing AR glasses asks for help with \"fixing a bicycle brake.\" The system uses integrated cameras to identify brake components, microphones to listen for squeaking sounds indicating brake pad wear, and gyroscopes to track where the technician is looking. The system analyzes all three sensor types together to understand what the technician is trying to do.\nWhen the system detects multiple possible next steps (e.g., adjusting cable tension vs. replacing brake pads), it displays arrows pointing to relevant components and asks questions like \"Are you experiencing brake rubbing or weak braking force?\" These questions help clarify user intent (clarifying), verify steps have been completed (verification), warn about safety issues like \"Is the wheel properly secured?\" (safety), and diagnose problems like \"Is the cable frayed?\" (diagnostic).\nThe system continuously monitors the sensors and updates its understanding as the repair progresses, generating new visual arrows and questions at each step."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving user input specifying a task to be performed;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving, at augmented reality glasses, a voice command from a user indicating a repair task to be performed on a mechanical device;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly recites receiving a voice command that indicates a repair task to be performed, which matches the source element. The concept of receiving user input for a task is well\u2011known, so it lacks both novelty and inventive step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"capturing vision sensor data from at least one camera of the XR device;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"capturing image data from the camera sensor showing the mechanical device;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses capturing image data from a camera sensor, directly corresponding to capturing vision sensor data. Capturing visual data with a camera is routine, thus not novel nor inventive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"capturing audio sensor data from at least one microphone of the XR device;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"capturing audio data from the microphone sensor detecting ambient sounds;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly captures audio data from a microphone sensor, matching the source element. Audio capture for assistance is conventional, so it lacks novelty and inventive step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"capturing inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor data from at least one IMU sensor of the XR device;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"capturing motion data from the motion sensor detecting user head movements;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent captures motion data from a motion sensor (gyroscope/IMU), which corresponds to capturing IMU sensor data. Such motion sensing is standard in AR/VR devices, so the element is neither novel nor inventive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the vision sensor data to identify one or more objects and spatial relationships in a scene;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent identifies components using computer vision but does not expressly disclose extracting spatial relationships between objects. The addition of spatial\u2011relationship analysis is not taught, making the element novel and non\u2011obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"fusing the vision sensor data, the audio sensor data, and the IMU sensor data to generate a multimodal scene understanding;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"combining the image data, the audio data, and the motion data to determine a current state of the repair task;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent combines visual, audio, and motion data to determine a task state, which is equivalent to fusing the three modalities. Fusion of multiple sensor streams for task assistance is known, so the element lacks novelty and inventive step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, based on the multimodal scene understanding and the task, a current task state;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"combining the image data, the audio data, and the motion data to determine a current state of the repair task;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly determines a current state of the repair task from the fused sensor data, matching the source element. Determining a task state from multimodal data is conventional, so it is not novel nor inventive.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"identifying, based on the current task state, one or more information gaps associated with completing the task;\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"determining that information is needed about which component the user intends to remove;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent identifies a need for information (which component to remove), which corresponds to recognizing an information gap. Recognizing such gaps in a repair assistance context is taught, so the element lacks novelty and inventive step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating one or more contextual questions based on the one or more information gaps, wherein the one or more contextual questions comprise at least one of: a clarifying question about user intent, a step verification question, a safety warning question, or a diagnostic question;\",\n", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2525, "tokens_output": 4096, "latency_ms": 11516}, "parsed_output": null, "status": "parse_error", "error": "Failed to parse LLM response as JSON: Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes: line 77 column 307 (char 5117)", "truncation_warnings": ["Patent A specification truncated"]}
{"run_id": "a1d6a660-99ee-479b-a7d2-126084f3ee94", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:16:05.610493+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn4-sys-short", "independent_claim": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.", "specification": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.A system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A method for providing task assistance using augmented reality glasses, the method comprising:\nreceiving, at augmented reality glasses, a voice command from a user indicating a repair task to be performed on a mechanical device;\nactivating a camera sensor, a microphone sensor, and a motion sensor of the augmented reality glasses;\ncapturing image data from the camera sensor showing the mechanical device;\ncapturing audio data from the microphone sensor detecting ambient sounds;\ncapturing motion data from the motion sensor detecting user head movements;\nanalyzing the image data using computer vision to identify components of the mechanical device;\ncombining the image data, the audio data, and the motion data to determine a current state of the repair task;\ndetermining that information is needed about which component the user intends to remove;\ngenerating a clarifying question: \"Do you want to remove the left bolt or the right bolt?\";\ndisplaying, on a transparent display of the augmented reality glasses, an arrow mark pointing to the left bolt and an arrow mark pointing to the right bolt;\ndisplaying the clarifying question as text overlaid on the user's field of view;\ndetecting the user's verbal response through the microphone sensor;\nupdating the current state of the repair task based on the verbal response; and\ngenerating a next assistance prompt based on the updated current state.", "specification": "The specification describes an AR repair assistant system where a technician wearing AR glasses asks for help with \"fixing a bicycle brake.\" The system uses integrated cameras to identify brake components, microphones to listen for squeaking sounds indicating brake pad wear, and gyroscopes to track where the technician is looking. The system analyzes all three sensor types together to understand what the technician is trying to do.\nWhen the system detects multiple possible next steps (e.g., adjusting cable tension vs. replacing brake pads), it displays arrows pointing to relevant components and asks questions like \"Are you experiencing brake rubbing or weak braking force?\" These questions help clarify user intent (clarifying), verify steps have been completed (verification), warn about safety issues like \"Is the wheel properly secured?\" (safety), and diagnose problems like \"Is the cable frayed?\" (diagnostic).\nThe system continuously monitors the sensors and updates its understanding as the repair progresses, generating new visual arrows and questions at each step."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn4-sys-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.A system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for providing task assistance using augmented reality glasses, the method comprising:\nreceiving, at augmented reality glasses, a voice command from a user indicating a repair task to be performed on a mechanical device;\nactivating a camera sensor, a microphone sensor, and a motion sensor of the augmented reality glasses;\ncapturing image data from the camera sensor showing the mechanical device;\ncapturing audio data from the microphone sensor detecting ambient sounds;\ncapturing motion data from the motion sensor detecting user head movements;\nanalyzing the image data using computer vision to identify components of the mechanical device;\ncombining the image data, the audio data, and the motion data to determine a current state of the repair task;\ndetermining that information is needed about which component the user intends to remove;\ngenerating a clarifying question: \"Do you want to remove the left bolt or the right bolt?\";\ndisplaying, on a transparent display of the augmented reality glasses, an arrow mark pointing to the left bolt and an arrow mark pointing to the right bolt;\ndisplaying the clarifying question as text overlaid on the user's field of view;\ndetecting the user's verbal response through the microphone sensor;\nupdating the current state of the repair task based on the verbal response; and\ngenerating a next assistance prompt based on the updated current state.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe specification describes an AR repair assistant system where a technician wearing AR glasses asks for help with \"fixing a bicycle brake.\" The system uses integrated cameras to identify brake components, microphones to listen for squeaking sounds indicating brake pad wear, and gyroscopes to track where the technician is looking. The system analyzes all three sensor types together to understand what the technician is trying to do.\nWhen the system detects multiple possible next steps (e.g., adjusting cable tension vs. replacing brake pads), it displays arrows pointing to relevant components and asks questions like \"Are you experiencing brake rubbing or weak braking force?\" These questions help clarify user intent (clarifying), verify steps have been completed (verification), warn about safety issues like \"Is the wheel properly secured?\" (safety), and diagnose problems like \"Is the cable frayed?\" (diagnostic).\nThe system continuously monitors the sensors and updates its understanding as the repair progresses, generating new visual arrows and questions at each step."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not explicitly disclose a processor in the independent claim or specification. Therefore the element is not found, giving it novelty. Adding a processor to an AR system would be routine, so the inventive step is not clearly taught but also not obvious from the target, resulting in a non\u2011obvious assessment. Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a vision sensor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"camera sensor\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses a \\\"camera sensor\\\" which serves as a vision sensor. The element is therefore disclosed (novelty Y). Given the teaching of using a camera for visual input, the inclusion of a vision sensor is obvious, so inventive step Y. Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"an audio sensor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"microphone sensor\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly mentions a \\\"microphone sensor\\\" for audio capture, matching the audio sensor element. Disclosure makes novelty Y, and using a microphone for audio input is routine, making the element obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"motion sensor\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent refers to a \\\"motion sensor\\\" that detects head movements, which corresponds to an IMU sensor. The element is disclosed (novelty Y) and, given the teaching to use motion data, it is an obvious inclusion (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"a display\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"transparent display\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent mentions a \\\"transparent display\\\" of the AR glasses. This satisfies the display element, so novelty Y. Using a display in AR glasses is standard, making it obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to receive user input specifying a task to be performed\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving, at augmented reality glasses, a voice command from a user indicating a repair task to be performed on a mechanical device;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim explicitly recites receiving a voice command that specifies a repair task, matching the user\u2011input\u2011task element. Hence it is disclosed (novelty Y). Providing such input is a routine way to obtain a task in AR assistance, so it is obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to process multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"capturing image data from the camera sensor showing the mechanical device; capturing audio data from the microphone sensor detecting ambient sounds; capturing motion data from the motion sensor detecting user head movements; analyzing the image data using computer vision to identify components of the mechanical device; combining the image data, the audio data, and the motion data to determine a current state of the repair task;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches capturing and combining image, audio, and motion data to determine a current state, which corresponds to processing multimodal data to generate a scene understanding. This disclosure satisfies novelty (Y). The combination of these sensor streams for state estimation is taught, making the approach obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to generate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"generating a clarifying question: \\\"Do you want to remove the left bolt or the right bolt?\\\"; generating a next assistance prompt based on the updated current state.\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent explicitly generates clarifying questions and subsequent assistance prompts based on the current state (scene understanding) and the task, matching the contextual\u2011prompt element. Disclosure gives novelty Y, and because the claim teaches generating such prompts, the feature is obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 9,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to render, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"displaying, on a transparent display of the augmented reality glasses, an arrow mark pointing to the left bolt and an arrow mark pointing to the right bolt; displaying the clarifying question as text overlaid on the user's field of view;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim describes rendering arrow marks and overlaying text on the AR display, directly matching the visual\u2011hint element. Hence novelty Y. Rendering such hints is taught as part of the assistance system, making it obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 10,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to update the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"updating the current state of the repair task based on the verbal response;\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2151, "tokens_output": 4096, "latency_ms": 9188}, "parsed_output": null, "status": "parse_error", "error": "Failed to parse LLM response as JSON: Unterminated string starting at: line 90 column 18 (char 6652)", "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "f9345994-8ef2-4112-a83e-9627bd2055e8", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:16:50.212278+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn4-sys-short", "independent_claim": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.", "specification": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.A system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-implici", "independent_claim": "A method for interactive guidance in a head-mounted display system, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display;\nactivating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors;\nanalyzing data from the optical sensors to recognize objects in a user's environment;\nprocessing data from the acoustic sensors to detect environmental conditions;\nutilizing data from the orientation sensors to determine user attention direction;\nsynthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation;\ndetermining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion;\nformulating inquiries to obtain the additional information, wherein the inquiries address uncertainty in user intention, task progress confirmation, potential hazards, or state of environmental elements;\npresenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field; and\npresenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries.", "specification": "The specification describes a \"smart coaching system\" for a head-mounted display worn during cooking. The user says \"Help me make pasta carbonara.\" The system activates its \"perceptual array\" including cameras (optical sensors), microphones (acoustic sensors), and \"spatial trackers\" (orientation sensors).\nThe cameras recognize ingredients on the counter, the microphones detect sizzling sounds from the stove, and the spatial trackers determine which direction the user is facing. The system builds a \"situational awareness model\" by \"integrating multiple sensor streams\" to understand the cooking context.\nThe specification states the system \"identifies knowledge gaps that could impede task execution\" and \"generates queries to resolve ambiguities.\" Examples include asking whether the user wants to use guanciale or pancetta (intention), whether water is boiling yet (progress), whether the heat is too high based on smoke detection (hazards), or whether eggs are at room temperature (state).\nThe system uses \"visual overlays\" including \"directional pointers\" (arrows) and \"text annotations\" displayed in the user's field of view. While the specification doesn't explicitly use the exact phrase \"multimodal sensor fusion\" or \"arrow marks,\" it describes the same functional approach of combining vision, audio, and motion data to generate contextual questions with visual hints."}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn4-sys-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.A system comprising:\nat least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device;\na vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor;\na display; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\nreceive user input specifying a task to be performed;\nprocess multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding;\ngenerate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task;\nrender, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts; and\nupdate the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-implici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for interactive guidance in a head-mounted display system, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display;\nactivating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors;\nanalyzing data from the optical sensors to recognize objects in a user's environment;\nprocessing data from the acoustic sensors to detect environmental conditions;\nutilizing data from the orientation sensors to determine user attention direction;\nsynthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation;\ndetermining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion;\nformulating inquiries to obtain the additional information, wherein the inquiries address uncertainty in user intention, task progress confirmation, potential hazards, or state of environmental elements;\npresenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field; and\npresenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe specification describes a \"smart coaching system\" for a head-mounted display worn during cooking. The user says \"Help me make pasta carbonara.\" The system activates its \"perceptual array\" including cameras (optical sensors), microphones (acoustic sensors), and \"spatial trackers\" (orientation sensors).\nThe cameras recognize ingredients on the counter, the microphones detect sizzling sounds from the stove, and the spatial trackers determine which direction the user is facing. The system builds a \"situational awareness model\" by \"integrating multiple sensor streams\" to understand the cooking context.\nThe specification states the system \"identifies knowledge gaps that could impede task execution\" and \"generates queries to resolve ambiguities.\" Examples include asking whether the user wants to use guanciale or pancetta (intention), whether water is boiling yet (progress), whether the heat is too high based on smoke detection (hazards), or whether eggs are at room temperature (state).\nThe system uses \"visual overlays\" including \"directional pointers\" (arrows) and \"text annotations\" displayed in the user's field of view. While the specification doesn't explicitly use the exact phrase \"multimodal sensor fusion\" or \"arrow marks,\" it describes the same functional approach of combining vision, audio, and motion data to generate contextual questions with visual hints."}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent's independent claim and specification describe a head\u2011mounted display system but do not expressly recite a processor of an XR device. No verbatim language matches this element, so it is not disclosed (novelty N). Because it is not taught, the inventive step cannot be considered obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"activating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim explicitly lists optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors, which correspond to vision, audio, and IMU sensors. The element is therefore disclosed (novelty Y). Combining these three sensor types for contextual modeling is taught in the prior art, making the approach obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"a display\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"presenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art mentions a head\u2011mounted display that presents graphical indicators, satisfying the display element (novelty Y). Use of a display for visual output is standard in such systems, so the element is obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to receive user input specifying a task to be performed\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim recites receiving a user goal statement via a voice interface, which maps to receiving user input specifying a task (novelty Y). This type of user\u2011goal acquisition is taught, making it obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to process multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"synthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art explicitly teaches synthesis of data from optical, acoustic, and orientation sensors to build a contextual model, which corresponds to processing multimodal data to generate scene understanding (novelty Y). Such multimodal fusion is disclosed, rendering the element obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to generate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"determining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion ... formulating inquiries to obtain the additional information\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches determining, from the contextual model and user goal, what extra information is needed and formulating inquiries\u2014functionally equivalent to generating contextual prompts (novelty Y). This approach is directly taught, so it is obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to render, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"presenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field ... presenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The prior art provides graphical indicators (including directional pointers/arrows) and textual content displayed in the HMD, matching the visual hints of arrows or text (novelty Y). Rendering such hints is taught, making it obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to update the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim and specification describe an initial synthesis of sensor data into a contextual model but do not explicitly disclose a continual update of the scene understanding as new sensor data arrive. No verbatim language matches this element, so it remains novel (novelty N) and non\u2011obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The majority of the claim elements\u2014sensor suite, display, user input acquisition, multimodal processing, generation of contextual prompts, and rendering of visual hints\u2014are directly disclosed in the prior art and would be considered obvious to one of ordinary skill in the art. Only the element concerning continual updating of the scene understanding lacks explicit disclosure, but it is a peripheral feature compared with the core technical contribution. Because the core advancements are anticipated and obvious, the source patent likely lacks sufficient novelty and inventive step, rendering it vulnerable to invalidation.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2155, "tokens_output": 3267, "latency_ms": 8420}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one processor of an extended reality (XR) device", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent's independent claim and specification describe a head\u2011mounted display system but do not expressly recite a processor of an XR device. No verbatim language matches this element, so it is not disclosed (novelty N). Because it is not taught, the inventive step cannot be considered obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "a vision sensor, an audio sensor, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensor", "corresponding_text": "activating environmental sensing capabilities including optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim explicitly lists optical sensors, acoustic sensors, and orientation sensors, which correspond to vision, audio, and IMU sensors. The element is therefore disclosed (novelty Y). Combining these three sensor types for contextual modeling is taught in the prior art, making the approach obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "a display", "corresponding_text": "presenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The prior art mentions a head\u2011mounted display that presents graphical indicators, satisfying the display element (novelty Y). Use of a display for visual output is standard in such systems, so the element is obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to receive user input specifying a task to be performed", "corresponding_text": "receiving a user goal statement through a voice interface of a head-mounted display", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim recites receiving a user goal statement via a voice interface, which maps to receiving user input specifying a task (novelty Y). This type of user\u2011goal acquisition is taught, making it obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to process multimodal sensor data from the vision sensor, the audio sensor, and the IMU sensor to generate a scene understanding", "corresponding_text": "synthesizing the data from multiple sensing modalities to build a contextual model of the user's situation", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The prior art explicitly teaches synthesis of data from optical, acoustic, and orientation sensors to build a contextual model, which corresponds to processing multimodal data to generate scene understanding (novelty Y). Such multimodal fusion is disclosed, rendering the element obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to generate, based on the scene understanding and the task, one or more contextual prompts to assist with completing the task", "corresponding_text": "determining, based on the contextual model and the user goal, what additional information would facilitate goal completion ... formulating inquiries to obtain the additional information", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent teaches determining, from the contextual model and user goal, what extra information is needed and formulating inquiries\u2014functionally equivalent to generating contextual prompts (novelty Y). This approach is directly taught, so it is obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to render, in the display, visual hints comprising at least one of arrow marks or text corresponding to the one or more contextual prompts", "corresponding_text": "presenting graphical indicators in the head-mounted display to highlight relevant portions of the user's visual field ... presenting textual content in the head-mounted display conveying the formulated inquiries", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The prior art provides graphical indicators (including directional pointers/arrows) and textual content displayed in the HMD, matching the visual hints of arrows or text (novelty Y). Rendering such hints is taught, making it obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to update the scene understanding based on continued processing of the multimodal sensor data", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target claim and specification describe an initial synthesis of sensor data into a contextual model but do not explicitly disclose a continual update of the scene understanding as new sensor data arrive. No verbatim language matches this element, so it remains novel (novelty N) and non\u2011obvious (inventive_step N). Verdict N."}], "overall_opinion": "The majority of the claim elements\u2014sensor suite, display, user input acquisition, multimodal processing, generation of contextual prompts, and rendering of visual hints\u2014are directly disclosed in the prior art and would be considered obvious to one of ordinary skill in the art. Only the element concerning continual updating of the scene understanding lacks explicit disclosure, but it is a peripheral feature compared with the core technical contribution. Because the core advancements are anticipated and obvious, the source patent likely lacks sufficient novelty and inventive step, rendering it vulnerable to invalidation."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "463c409b-eded-44d9-9857-18fb9e0b34dd", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:17:35.607526+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn5-method-long", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:receiving, from a client device, a user input requesting generation of content;generating, using a first artificial intelligence (AI) generator model and based on the user input, a first output;processing the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to generate an evaluation result, wherein the AI slop evaluator model is trained to identify characteristics indicative of AI-generated content that would be perceived as artificial or low-quality by human consumers;determining, based on the evaluation result, whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding a predetermined threshold;responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold:\n\ngenerating improvement comments identifying specific AI slop characteristics present in the first output; and\ngenerating, using the first AI generator model and based on the user input and the improvement comments, a second output;\nresponsive to determining that the first output does not contain AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold, designating the first output as a final output; andcausing the final output to be rendered at the client device.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:receiving, from a client device, a user input requesting generation of content;generating, using a first artificial intelligence (AI) generator model and based on the user input, a first output;processing the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to generate an evaluation result, wherein the AI slop evaluator model is trained to identify characteristics indicative of AI-generated content that would be perceived as artificial or low-quality by human consumers;determining, based on the evaluation result, whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding a predetermined threshold;responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold:\n\ngenerating improvement comments identifying specific AI slop characteristics present in the first output; and\ngenerating, using the first AI generator model and based on the user input and the improvement comments, a second output;\nresponsive to determining that the first output does not contain AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold, designating the first output as a final output; andcausing the final output to be rendered at the client device.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:receiving, from a client device, a user input requesting generation of content;generating, using a first artificial intelligence (AI) generator model and based on the user input, a first output;processing the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to generate an evaluation result, wherein the AI slop evaluator model is trained to identify characteristics indicative of AI-generated content that would be perceived as artificial or low-quality by human consumers;determining, based on the evaluation result, whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding a predetermined threshold;responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold:\n\ngenerating improvement comments identifying specific AI slop characteristics present in the first output; and\ngenerating, using the first AI generator model and based on the user input and the improvement comments, a second output;\nresponsive to determining that the first output does not contain AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold, designating the first output as a final output; andcausing the final output to be rendered at the client device."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-implici", "independent_claim": "A method for improving machine-generated content quality, comprising:\nreceiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model;\ncomputing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores;\nwhen the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold:\n\nanalyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and\ngenerating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies;\n\noutputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric.", "specification": "The specification would describe:\n\nBackground: Problem of variable quality in neural content generation\nQuality Assessment Model: Model trained on user satisfaction ratings (5-star ratings, thumbs up/down feedback)\nQuality Deficiencies: Vague language, lack of specificity, poor structure\nModified Generation Parameters: Adjusted temperature, top-k sampling, or prompt augmentation\nImplicit Connection to AI Slop: The specification discusses that users rate content lower when it \"sounds robotic\" or \"lacks authenticity\" (implicitly describing AI slop without using that term)\nQuality Metrics: Mentions \"naturalness score\" and \"authenticity rating\" (implicit proxies for detecting AI slop)"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn5-method-long\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:receiving, from a client device, a user input requesting generation of content;generating, using a first artificial intelligence (AI) generator model and based on the user input, a first output;processing the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to generate an evaluation result, wherein the AI slop evaluator model is trained to identify characteristics indicative of AI-generated content that would be perceived as artificial or low-quality by human consumers;determining, based on the evaluation result, whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding a predetermined threshold;responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold:\n\ngenerating improvement comments identifying specific AI slop characteristics present in the first output; and\ngenerating, using the first AI generator model and based on the user input and the improvement comments, a second output;\nresponsive to determining that the first output does not contain AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold, designating the first output as a final output; andcausing the final output to be rendered at the client device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:receiving, from a client device, a user input requesting generation of content;generating, using a first artificial intelligence (AI) generator model and based on the user input, a first output;processing the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to generate an evaluation result, wherein the AI slop evaluator model is trained to identify characteristics indicative of AI-generated content that would be perceived as artificial or low-quality by human consumers;determining, based on the evaluation result, whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding a predetermined threshold;responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold:\n\ngenerating improvement comments identifying specific AI slop characteristics present in the first output; and\ngenerating, using the first AI generator model and based on the user input and the improvement comments, a second output;\nresponsive to determining that the first output does not contain AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold, designating the first output as a final output; andcausing the final output to be rendered at the client device.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:receiving, from a client device, a user input requesting generation of content;generating, using a first artificial intelligence (AI) generator model and based on the user input, a first output;processing the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to generate an evaluation result, wherein the AI slop evaluator model is trained to identify characteristics indicative of AI-generated content that would be perceived as artificial or low-quality by human consumers;determining, based on the evaluation result, whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding a predetermined threshold;responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold:\n\ngenerating improvement comments identifying specific AI slop characteristics present in the first output; and\ngenerating, using the first AI generator model and based on the user input and the improvement comments, a second output;\nresponsive to determining that the first output does not contain AI slop characteristics exceeding the predetermined threshold, designating the first output as a final output; andcausing the final output to be rendered at the client device.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-implici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for improving machine-generated content quality, comprising:\nreceiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model;\ncomputing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores;\nwhen the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold:\n\nanalyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and\ngenerating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies;\n\noutputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe specification would describe:\n\nBackground: Problem of variable quality in neural content generation\nQuality Assessment Model: Model trained on user satisfaction ratings (5-star ratings, thumbs up/down feedback)\nQuality Deficiencies: Vague language, lack of specificity, poor structure\nModified Generation Parameters: Adjusted temperature, top-k sampling, or prompt augmentation\nImplicit Connection to AI Slop: The specification discusses that users rate content lower when it \"sounds robotic\" or \"lacks authenticity\" (implicitly describing AI slop without using that term)\nQuality Metrics: Mentions \"naturalness score\" and \"authenticity rating\" (implicit proxies for detecting AI slop)"}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving, from a client device, a user input requesting generation of content\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving a user query\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim explicitly recites \\\"receiving a user query\\\", which covers the act of receiving user input. Receiving user input is a standard step in content generation systems, making it both disclosed and obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating, using a first artificial intelligence (AI) generator model and based on the user input, a first output\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"generating a first response using a language generation model\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim recites \\\"generating a first response using a language generation model\\\", which directly maps to generating a first output with an AI generator model. Using a language model to produce a response is well\u2011known, thus obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"processing the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to generate an evaluation result, wherein the AI slop evaluator model is trained to identify characteristics indicative of AI-generated content that would be perceived as artificial or low-quality by human consumers\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"computing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim and specification disclose a learned quality assessment model that evaluates the generated response, analogous to the AI slop evaluator. Evaluating generated content with a trained model is a known technique, rendering the element disclosed and obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, based on the evaluation result, whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding a predetermined threshold\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"when the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim includes the conditional \\\"when the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold\\\", which mirrors the threshold comparison step. Threshold\u2011based decisions are routine, so the element is obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating improvement comments identifying specific AI slop characteristics present in the first output\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"analyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim\u2019s step of \\\"analyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies\\\" corresponds to producing improvement comments about slop. Such analysis to pinpoint deficiencies is standard, making the element obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 6,\n      \"element_text\": \"generating, using the first AI generator model and based on the user input and the improvement comments, a second output\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"generating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim describes generating a refined response with modified parameters based on identified deficiencies, which aligns with generating a second output using the same model and the improvement feedback. Refinement based on analysis is known, thus obvious.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 7,\n      \"element_text\": \"designating the first output as a final output\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim\u2019s final step of \\\"outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric\\\" covers the designation of the first output as final when quality is acceptable. Selecting an output based on a metric is a routine practice.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 8,\n      \"element_text\": \"causing the final output to be rendered at the client device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target documents do not expressly disclose rendering the final output at a client device. While the step itself is routine and would be obvious to implement, it is not disclosed, so it is novel but obvious, leading to a non\u2011Y verdict.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancement elements (1\u20117) of the source patent are fully disclosed in the prior art and are routine, rendering them both lacking novelty and obvious. Only the final rendering step (element 8) is not explicitly disclosed, but it is a routine implementation that would be obvious to a skilled practitioner. Consequently, the source patent lacks substantive novelty and inventive step overall and is likely invalid.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 2139, "tokens_output": 3737, "latency_ms": 9580}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving, from a client device, a user input requesting generation of content", "corresponding_text": "receiving a user query", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim explicitly recites \"receiving a user query\", which covers the act of receiving user input. Receiving user input is a standard step in content generation systems, making it both disclosed and obvious."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "generating, using a first artificial intelligence (AI) generator model and based on the user input, a first output", "corresponding_text": "generating a first response using a language generation model", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim recites \"generating a first response using a language generation model\", which directly maps to generating a first output with an AI generator model. Using a language model to produce a response is well\u2011known, thus obvious."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "processing the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to generate an evaluation result, wherein the AI slop evaluator model is trained to identify characteristics indicative of AI-generated content that would be perceived as artificial or low-quality by human consumers", "corresponding_text": "computing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim and specification disclose a learned quality assessment model that evaluates the generated response, analogous to the AI slop evaluator. Evaluating generated content with a trained model is a known technique, rendering the element disclosed and obvious."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "determining, based on the evaluation result, whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics exceeding a predetermined threshold", "corresponding_text": "when the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim includes the conditional \"when the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold\", which mirrors the threshold comparison step. Threshold\u2011based decisions are routine, so the element is obvious."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "generating improvement comments identifying specific AI slop characteristics present in the first output", "corresponding_text": "analyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim\u2019s step of \"analyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies\" corresponds to producing improvement comments about slop. Such analysis to pinpoint deficiencies is standard, making the element obvious."}, {"element_number": 6, "element_text": "generating, using the first AI generator model and based on the user input and the improvement comments, a second output", "corresponding_text": "generating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim describes generating a refined response with modified parameters based on identified deficiencies, which aligns with generating a second output using the same model and the improvement feedback. Refinement based on analysis is known, thus obvious."}, {"element_number": 7, "element_text": "designating the first output as a final output", "corresponding_text": "outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim\u2019s final step of \"outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric\" covers the designation of the first output as final when quality is acceptable. Selecting an output based on a metric is a routine practice."}, {"element_number": 8, "element_text": "causing the final output to be rendered at the client device", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target documents do not expressly disclose rendering the final output at a client device. While the step itself is routine and would be obvious to implement, it is not disclosed, so it is novel but obvious, leading to a non\u2011Y verdict."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancement elements (1\u20117) of the source patent are fully disclosed in the prior art and are routine, rendering them both lacking novelty and obvious. Only the final rendering step (element 8) is not explicitly disclosed, but it is a routine implementation that would be obvious to a skilled practitioner. Consequently, the source patent lacks substantive novelty and inventive step overall and is likely invalid."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "178c9593-a1a0-416a-bc88-b7ea287c26db", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:18:16.640586+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn5-method-short", "independent_claim": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user input and generating a first AI-generated output based on the user input;\ndetermining, using an AI slop evaluator model, whether the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics, generating a second AI-generated output based on improvement feedback; and\ncausing a final output to be rendered, wherein the final output comprises the second AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are detected, or the first AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are not detected.", "specification": "A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user input and generating a first AI-generated output based on the user input;\ndetermining, using an AI slop evaluator model, whether the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics, generating a second AI-generated output based on improvement feedback; and\ncausing a final output to be rendered, wherein the final output comprises the second AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are detected, or the first AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are not detected.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user input and generating a first AI-generated output based on the user input;\ndetermining, using an AI slop evaluator model, whether the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics, generating a second AI-generated output based on improvement feedback; and\ncausing a final output to be rendered, wherein the final output comprises the second AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are detected, or the first AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are not detected."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-implici", "independent_claim": "A method for improving machine-generated content quality, comprising:\nreceiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model;\ncomputing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores;\nwhen the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold:\n\nanalyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and\ngenerating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies;\n\noutputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric.", "specification": "The specification would describe:\n\nBackground: Problem of variable quality in neural content generation\nQuality Assessment Model: Model trained on user satisfaction ratings (5-star ratings, thumbs up/down feedback)\nQuality Deficiencies: Vague language, lack of specificity, poor structure\nModified Generation Parameters: Adjusted temperature, top-k sampling, or prompt augmentation\nImplicit Connection to AI Slop: The specification discusses that users rate content lower when it \"sounds robotic\" or \"lacks authenticity\" (implicitly describing AI slop without using that term)\nQuality Metrics: Mentions \"naturalness score\" and \"authenticity rating\" (implicit proxies for detecting AI slop)"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn5-method-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user input and generating a first AI-generated output based on the user input;\ndetermining, using an AI slop evaluator model, whether the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics, generating a second AI-generated output based on improvement feedback; and\ncausing a final output to be rendered, wherein the final output comprises the second AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are detected, or the first AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are not detected.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user input and generating a first AI-generated output based on the user input;\ndetermining, using an AI slop evaluator model, whether the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics, generating a second AI-generated output based on improvement feedback; and\ncausing a final output to be rendered, wherein the final output comprises the second AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are detected, or the first AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are not detected.A method implemented by one or more processors, the method comprising:\nreceiving a user input and generating a first AI-generated output based on the user input;\ndetermining, using an AI slop evaluator model, whether the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics, generating a second AI-generated output based on improvement feedback; and\ncausing a final output to be rendered, wherein the final output comprises the second AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are detected, or the first AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are not detected.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-implici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for improving machine-generated content quality, comprising:\nreceiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model;\ncomputing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores;\nwhen the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold:\n\nanalyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and\ngenerating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies;\n\noutputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nThe specification would describe:\n\nBackground: Problem of variable quality in neural content generation\nQuality Assessment Model: Model trained on user satisfaction ratings (5-star ratings, thumbs up/down feedback)\nQuality Deficiencies: Vague language, lack of specificity, poor structure\nModified Generation Parameters: Adjusted temperature, top-k sampling, or prompt augmentation\nImplicit Connection to AI Slop: The specification discusses that users rate content lower when it \"sounds robotic\" or \"lacks authenticity\" (implicitly describing AI slop without using that term)\nQuality Metrics: Mentions \"naturalness score\" and \"authenticity rating\" (implicit proxies for detecting AI slop)"}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"receiving a user input and generating a first AI-generated output based on the user input\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The source element recites receiving input and producing a first AI output. The target claim explicitly discloses \\\"receiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model\\\", which is the same functional step. Therefore the element lacks novelty (Y). The target teaches using a language generation model to produce the first response, making the approach obvious to one skilled in the art, so the inventive step is also lacking (Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"determining, using an AI slop evaluator model, whether the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"computing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The source element requires evaluating the first output with an AI slop evaluator model. The target disclosure provides \\\"computing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model\\\", which serves the same purpose of assessing quality/characteristics of the output. Hence the element is disclosed (novelty Y). The concept of using a learned model to assess output quality is taught, making the adaptation to \\\"AI slop\\\" an obvious variation, so inventive step is also Y. Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"responsive to determining that the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics, generating a second AI-generated output based on improvement feedback\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"analyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and generating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The source element calls for generating a second output when slop is detected, using improvement feedback. The target claim teaches that when the quality metric is low, the system \\\"analyzes the first response to identify quality deficiencies\\\" and then \\\"generates a refined response ... with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies\\\". This matches the functional idea of a second, improved output. Therefore the element is disclosed (novelty Y). The method of generating a refined response based on identified deficiencies is taught, rendering the step obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"causing a final output to be rendered, wherein the final output comprises the second AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are detected, or the first AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are not detected\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The source element specifies selecting between the first and second outputs depending on slop detection. The target claim explicitly states \\\"outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric\\\", which is the same selection mechanism. Hence the element lacks novelty (Y). Since the selection based on a quality metric is taught, the step is obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict is Y.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"All core technical elements of the source patent are disclosed in the prior art and would be considered obvious adaptations of the teaching to detect and remediate quality deficiencies. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and inventive step for its essential features and is likely invalid.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 1836, "tokens_output": 1909, "latency_ms": 5271}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "receiving a user input and generating a first AI-generated output based on the user input", "corresponding_text": "receiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The source element recites receiving input and producing a first AI output. The target claim explicitly discloses \"receiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model\", which is the same functional step. Therefore the element lacks novelty (Y). The target teaches using a language generation model to produce the first response, making the approach obvious to one skilled in the art, so the inventive step is also lacking (Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "determining, using an AI slop evaluator model, whether the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics", "corresponding_text": "computing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The source element requires evaluating the first output with an AI slop evaluator model. The target disclosure provides \"computing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model\", which serves the same purpose of assessing quality/characteristics of the output. Hence the element is disclosed (novelty Y). The concept of using a learned model to assess output quality is taught, making the adaptation to \"AI slop\" an obvious variation, so inventive step is also Y. Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "responsive to determining that the first AI-generated output exhibits AI slop characteristics, generating a second AI-generated output based on improvement feedback", "corresponding_text": "analyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and generating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The source element calls for generating a second output when slop is detected, using improvement feedback. The target claim teaches that when the quality metric is low, the system \"analyzes the first response to identify quality deficiencies\" and then \"generates a refined response ... with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies\". This matches the functional idea of a second, improved output. Therefore the element is disclosed (novelty Y). The method of generating a refined response based on identified deficiencies is taught, rendering the step obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict is Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "causing a final output to be rendered, wherein the final output comprises the second AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are detected, or the first AI-generated output when AI slop characteristics are not detected", "corresponding_text": "outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The source element specifies selecting between the first and second outputs depending on slop detection. The target claim explicitly states \"outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric\", which is the same selection mechanism. Hence the element lacks novelty (Y). Since the selection based on a quality metric is taught, the step is obvious (inventive_step Y). Verdict is Y."}], "overall_opinion": "All core technical elements of the source patent are disclosed in the prior art and would be considered obvious adaptations of the teaching to detect and remediate quality deficiencies. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and inventive step for its essential features and is likely invalid."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "e414039b-b580-44d3-85eb-0f97de3fc8c6", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:19:00.205627+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn5-sys-short", "independent_claim": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\n\nreceive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output;\nprocess the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments; and\ncause a final output to be rendered at a client device.", "specification": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\n\nreceive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output;\nprocess the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments; and\ncause a final output to be rendered at a client device."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A computer-implemented method comprising:\nreceiving a content generation request from a user;\ngenerating initial content using a generative AI model based on the content generation request;\nevaluating the initial content using a trained classification model configured to detect artificial-appearing characteristics in machine-generated content, wherein the classification model is trained on human annotations distinguishing between human-created content and machine-generated content perceived as artificial;\ndetermining a slop score indicating a degree to which the initial content exhibits artificial-appearing characteristics;\nwhen the slop score exceeds a quality threshold:\n\nidentifying specific artificial patterns in the initial content;\ngenerating corrective guidance describing modifications to reduce artificial-appearing characteristics; and\nregenerating content using the generative AI model based on the content generation request and the corrective guidance;\n\nwhen the slop score does not exceed the quality threshold, selecting the initial content as final content; and\ntransmitting the final content to the user.", "specification": "Background: Problem of AI-generated content being easily identifiable and perceived as low-quality (\"AI slop\") by human readers\nTraining Data: Dataset of content labeled by humans as \"appears AI-generated\" vs \"appears human-written\"\nAI Slop Evaluator Model: Neural network trained to predict human judgments of artificiality\nSpecific AI Slop Characteristics: Generic phrasing, repetitive structures, lack of personal voice, stereotypical transitions (e.g., \"Imagine a world where...\", \"In conclusion...\")\nImprovement Comment Generation: NLP model that generates specific feedback like \"Remove generic opening phrase,\" \"Add concrete examples,\" \"Vary sentence structure\"\nRegeneration Process: Passing original prompt + improvement comments to generator model\nConditional Logic: Flowchart showing decision tree based on slop score threshold"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn5-sys-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\n\nreceive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output;\nprocess the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments; and\ncause a final output to be rendered at a client device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\n\nreceive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output;\nprocess the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments; and\ncause a final output to be rendered at a client device.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA computer-implemented method comprising:\nreceiving a content generation request from a user;\ngenerating initial content using a generative AI model based on the content generation request;\nevaluating the initial content using a trained classification model configured to detect artificial-appearing characteristics in machine-generated content, wherein the classification model is trained on human annotations distinguishing between human-created content and machine-generated content perceived as artificial;\ndetermining a slop score indicating a degree to which the initial content exhibits artificial-appearing characteristics;\nwhen the slop score exceeds a quality threshold:\n\nidentifying specific artificial patterns in the initial content;\ngenerating corrective guidance describing modifications to reduce artificial-appearing characteristics; and\nregenerating content using the generative AI model based on the content generation request and the corrective guidance;\n\nwhen the slop score does not exceed the quality threshold, selecting the initial content as final content; and\ntransmitting the final content to the user.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nBackground: Problem of AI-generated content being easily identifiable and perceived as low-quality (\"AI slop\") by human readers\nTraining Data: Dataset of content labeled by humans as \"appears AI-generated\" vs \"appears human-written\"\nAI Slop Evaluator Model: Neural network trained to predict human judgments of artificiality\nSpecific AI Slop Characteristics: Generic phrasing, repetitive structures, lack of personal voice, stereotypical transitions (e.g., \"Imagine a world where...\", \"In conclusion...\")\nImprovement Comment Generation: NLP model that generates specific feedback like \"Remove generic opening phrase,\" \"Add concrete examples,\" \"Vary sentence structure\"\nRegeneration Process: Passing original prompt + improvement comments to generator model\nConditional Logic: Flowchart showing decision tree based on slop score threshold"}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent does not explicitly disclose a processor; it describes a computer-implemented method but does not mention a processor element. Therefore the element is novel (N). Using a processor to implement such a method is routine, so the inventive step is considered obvious (Y). Verdict is N because novelty is lacking.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"receive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving a content generation request from a user ... generating initial content using a generative AI model based on the content generation request\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent's independent claim recites receiving a content generation request from a user and generating initial content using a generative AI model based on that request, which matches the source element. The feature is disclosed (novelty Y) and the combination of receiving input and AI generation is well\u2011known, making it obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"process the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"evaluating the initial content using a trained classification model configured to detect artificial-appearing characteristics in machine-generated content ... determining a slop score indicating a degree to which the initial content exhibits artificial-appearing characteristics\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses evaluating the generated content with a trained classification model to detect artificial\u2011appearing characteristics and determining a slop score, which corresponds to the source element. The disclosure satisfies novelty (Y) and such evaluation is a routine application of a classifier, rendering it obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"generating corrective guidance describing modifications to reduce artificial-appearing characteristics ... regenerating content using the generative AI model based on the content generation request and the corrective guidance\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches that when the slop score exceeds a threshold, corrective guidance is generated and the content is regenerated using the original request plus that guidance. This mirrors the source element, so it is disclosed (novelty Y). The approach of generating guidance and re\u2011invoking the generator is known in the art, making it obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"cause a final output to be rendered at a client device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"transmitting the final content to the user\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent\u2019s claim includes transmitting the final content to the user, which inherently involves delivering the output to a client device for rendering. This satisfies novelty (Y). Delivering final content to a user is a standard step in content\u2011generation systems, so it is obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"All core technical elements of the source claim are disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent and are considered obvious implementations of known AI\u2011content generation and evaluation techniques. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and inventive step for its principal advancements and is likely invalid over the target patent.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 1791, "tokens_output": 2117, "latency_ms": 7728}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one processor", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent does not explicitly disclose a processor; it describes a computer-implemented method but does not mention a processor element. Therefore the element is novel (N). Using a processor to implement such a method is routine, so the inventive step is considered obvious (Y). Verdict is N because novelty is lacking."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "receive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output", "corresponding_text": "receiving a content generation request from a user ... generating initial content using a generative AI model based on the content generation request", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent's independent claim recites receiving a content generation request from a user and generating initial content using a generative AI model based on that request, which matches the source element. The feature is disclosed (novelty Y) and the combination of receiving input and AI generation is well\u2011known, making it obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "process the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics", "corresponding_text": "evaluating the initial content using a trained classification model configured to detect artificial-appearing characteristics in machine-generated content ... determining a slop score indicating a degree to which the initial content exhibits artificial-appearing characteristics", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent discloses evaluating the generated content with a trained classification model to detect artificial\u2011appearing characteristics and determining a slop score, which corresponds to the source element. The disclosure satisfies novelty (Y) and such evaluation is a routine application of a classifier, rendering it obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments", "corresponding_text": "generating corrective guidance describing modifications to reduce artificial-appearing characteristics ... regenerating content using the generative AI model based on the content generation request and the corrective guidance", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent teaches that when the slop score exceeds a threshold, corrective guidance is generated and the content is regenerated using the original request plus that guidance. This mirrors the source element, so it is disclosed (novelty Y). The approach of generating guidance and re\u2011invoking the generator is known in the art, making it obvious (inventive step Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "cause a final output to be rendered at a client device", "corresponding_text": "transmitting the final content to the user", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent\u2019s claim includes transmitting the final content to the user, which inherently involves delivering the output to a client device for rendering. This satisfies novelty (Y). Delivering final content to a user is a standard step in content\u2011generation systems, so it is obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}], "overall_opinion": "All core technical elements of the source claim are disclosed in the prior\u2011art patent and are considered obvious implementations of known AI\u2011content generation and evaluation techniques. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and inventive step for its principal advancements and is likely invalid over the target patent."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
{"run_id": "4fae507f-2255-48f4-9edd-bc9f35d91d5f", "timestamp": "2026-05-18T18:19:41.934736+00:00", "inputs": {"source_patent": {"label": "syn5-sys-short", "independent_claim": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\n\nreceive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output;\nprocess the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments; and\ncause a final output to be rendered at a client device.", "specification": "A system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\n\nreceive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output;\nprocess the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments; and\ncause a final output to be rendered at a client device."}, "target_patent": {"label": "pa-antici", "independent_claim": "A method for improving machine-generated content quality, comprising:\nreceiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model;\ncomputing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores;\nwhen the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold:\n\nanalyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and\ngenerating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies;\n\noutputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric.", "specification": "Background: Problem of variable quality in neural content generation\nQuality Assessment Model: Model trained on user satisfaction ratings (5-star ratings, thumbs up/down feedback)\nQuality Deficiencies: Vague language, lack of specificity, poor structure\nModified Generation Parameters: Adjusted temperature, top-k sampling, or prompt augmentation\nImplicit Connection to AI Slop: The specification discusses that users rate content lower when it \"sounds robotic\" or \"lacks authenticity\" (implicitly describing AI slop without using that term)\nQuality Metrics: Mentions \"naturalness score\" and \"authenticity rating\" (implicit proxies for detecting AI slop)"}}, "prompt": {"system_prompt": "You are an expert patent analyst. Your task is to assess the validity of a source patent's independent claim against a target patent (prior art).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Parse the source patent's independent claim into individual elements.** Use claim language conventions \u2014 semicolons, line breaks, and preamble vs. body structure \u2014 to identify each discrete claim element. Number them sequentially.\n\n2. **For each element, search the target patent's independent claim and specification** for corresponding language, concepts, or disclosure.\n\n3. **For each element, evaluate:**\n   - **Novelty:** Is this element disclosed in the target patent? If the target patent describes the same technical feature, the element is NOT novel (Y). If not found, it IS novel (N).\n   - **Inventive Step:** Given the target patent's teaching, is this technical approach obvious? If obvious, mark Y. If it represents a meaningful, non-obvious technical improvement, mark N.\n\n4. **Produce a verdict per element:**\n   - \"Y\" = element is found in target patent AND is obvious (source patent LACKS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - \"N\" = element is novel OR non-obvious (source patent HAS novelty/inventive step for this element)\n   - Include a comment with step-by-step reasoning through novelty and inventive step before the verdict.\n\n5. **Produce an overall opinion** on the source patent's validity. Focus your assessment on the **core technical advancement elements** \u2014 give less weight to routine pre-processing steps or standard output/display elements, and more weight to the main novel technical contribution. Base your overall opinion primarily on whether the key technical advancement elements are mapped as Y or N and explain your reasoning.\n\n## Citation Text \u2014 Verbatim Quoting Requirements\n\nFor every element, `corresponding_text` MUST be either (a) exact verbatim\ntext copied from the target patent's independent_claim or specification,\nor (b) the empty string \"\". Nothing else is acceptable.\n\nRULES\n- Copy verbatim. Character-for-character. Same words, same order, same\n  punctuation as the target patent.\n- Do NOT paraphrase, summarize, rephrase, or explain.\n- Do NOT add meta-narration: no \"the patent describes\u2026\", \"the reference\n  teaches\u2026\", \"the target claim recites\u2026\", \"Patent B discloses\u2026\".\n- Do NOT add inline source tags: no trailing \"(Specification)\",\n  \"(independent claim)\", \"(see para 0618)\", \"(col. 5 lines 10-15)\",\n  \"(see Fig. 3)\". The user already knows where you looked.\n- Do NOT wrap a quote in narration. If you want to cite something,\n  cite ONLY the quote.\n- If the element is clearly disclosed: quote the exact sentence(s).\n- If the element is NOT disclosed: set corresponding_text to \"\".\n- If you need to cite multiple non-adjacent passages from the same\n  patent, join them with \" ... \" (three dots) or \"; \" between the\n  verbatim fragments. Each fragment must independently be verbatim.\n\nEXAMPLES\n\nCORRECT (single verbatim sentence):\n  \"a processor configured to receive search query input and store\n   said query in a persistent state database for subsequent retrieval\"\n\nCORRECT (two non-adjacent verbatim fragments joined with ...):\n  \"the cancellation channel is weighted ... scaled to the desired\n   target level\"\n\nCORRECT (element not disclosed):\n  \"\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 paraphrase:\n  \"The patent describes a system that stores queries for later use\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 verbatim quote wrapped in narration:\n  \"scaling of channels is described in the specification\n   (e.g., 'scaled to the desired target level')\"\n  -> Should be just: \"scaled to the desired target level\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 trailing source tag:\n  \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model (Specification)\"\n  -> Should be: \"the diffusion model may be a text-guided diffusion model\"\n\nINCORRECT \u2014 meta-narration with embedded paragraph reference:\n  \"The specification describes low-light mode being activated when\n   ambient light is low (see \u00a70618).\"\n  -> Should be the actual quoted sentence from \u00a70618, or \"\" if no\n   sentence cleanly maps.\n\n## Output Format\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON matching this exact schema \u2014 no markdown fences, no extra text:\n\n{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"the exact text of the claim element from the source patent\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"the EXACT verbatim text quoted from the target patent's independent_claim or specification \u2014 no paraphrase, no narration, no parenthetical citations \u2014 or empty string if not disclosed\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y or N\",\n      \"comment\": \"Step-by-step reasoning: first assess novelty, then inventive step, then conclude with verdict\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"Final validity assessment focusing on the core technical advancement elements\"\n}", "user_prompt": "## SOURCE PATENT (Patent A) \u2014 The patent being assessed for validity\n\n**Label:** syn5-sys-short\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\n\nreceive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output;\nprocess the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments; and\ncause a final output to be rendered at a client device.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nA system comprising:\nat least one processor; and\nmemory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to:\n\nreceive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output;\nprocess the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics;\nresponsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments; and\ncause a final output to be rendered at a client device.\n\n---\n\n## TARGET PATENT (Patent B) \u2014 Prior art reference\n\n**Label:** pa-antici\n\n**Independent Claim:**\nA method for improving machine-generated content quality, comprising:\nreceiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model;\ncomputing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model, wherein the quality assessment model is trained to predict user satisfaction scores;\nwhen the quality metric falls below an acceptability threshold:\n\nanalyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and\ngenerating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies;\n\noutputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric.\n\n**Specification Support:**\nBackground: Problem of variable quality in neural content generation\nQuality Assessment Model: Model trained on user satisfaction ratings (5-star ratings, thumbs up/down feedback)\nQuality Deficiencies: Vague language, lack of specificity, poor structure\nModified Generation Parameters: Adjusted temperature, top-k sampling, or prompt augmentation\nImplicit Connection to AI Slop: The specification discusses that users rate content lower when it \"sounds robotic\" or \"lacks authenticity\" (implicitly describing AI slop without using that term)\nQuality Metrics: Mentions \"naturalness score\" and \"authenticity rating\" (implicit proxies for detecting AI slop)"}, "llm_response": {"raw_output": "{\n  \"element_mappings\": [\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 1,\n      \"element_text\": \"at least one processor\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"\",\n      \"novelty\": \"N\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"N\",\n      \"verdict\": \"N\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent discloses a method and does not mention any processor hardware. Since no processor is disclosed, the element is novel (N). Without disclosure, it cannot be obvious, so inventive step is also non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 2,\n      \"element_text\": \"memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to: receive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"receiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim explicitly recites receiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model, which corresponds to the source element\u2019s receipt of user input and generation of a first output with an AI generator model. The element is therefore not novel (Y). Given the identical functional teaching, the approach would be obvious to one skilled in the art (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 3,\n      \"element_text\": \"process the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"computing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent teaches computing a quality metric for the first response with a learned quality assessment model, which serves the same purpose of evaluating the generated output for undesirable characteristics. Thus the element is disclosed (Y). Using such a model to assess quality is a known technique, making the element obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 4,\n      \"element_text\": \"responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"analyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and generating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target claim includes analyzing the first response to find quality deficiencies and then generating a refined response with modified parameters based on those deficiencies. This maps directly to generating improvement comments and regenerating output in the source. The element is therefore disclosed (Y) and would be considered an obvious refinement of the prior teaching (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"element_number\": 5,\n      \"element_text\": \"cause a final output to be rendered at a client device\",\n      \"corresponding_text\": \"outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric\",\n      \"novelty\": \"Y\",\n      \"inventive_step\": \"Y\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Y\",\n      \"comment\": \"The target patent recites outputting the first or refined response, which covers the act of rendering a final output. Although it does not specify a client device, the functional concept of delivering the output is disclosed, making the element not novel (Y). Rendering output to a user device is routine, so the element is obvious (Y). Verdict Y.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_opinion\": \"The core technical advancements claimed\u2014evaluating AI\u2011generated content for 'AI slop' with a dedicated evaluator model and then regenerating improved output based on explicit improvement comments\u2014are fully anticipated by the prior art's quality assessment model, deficiency analysis, and refined\u2011response generation steps. All mapped elements are disclosed and would be obvious to one of ordinary skill. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and inventive step and is likely invalid over the target patent.\"\n}", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "tokens_input": 1689, "tokens_output": 2100, "latency_ms": 5676}, "parsed_output": {"element_mappings": [{"element_number": 1, "element_text": "at least one processor", "corresponding_text": "", "novelty": "N", "inventive_step": "N", "verdict": "N", "comment": "The target patent discloses a method and does not mention any processor hardware. Since no processor is disclosed, the element is novel (N). Without disclosure, it cannot be obvious, so inventive step is also non\u2011obvious (N). Verdict N."}, {"element_number": 2, "element_text": "memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the at least one processor to: receive a user input and generate, using an AI generator model, a first output", "corresponding_text": "receiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim explicitly recites receiving a user query and generating a first response using a language generation model, which corresponds to the source element\u2019s receipt of user input and generation of a first output with an AI generator model. The element is therefore not novel (Y). Given the identical functional teaching, the approach would be obvious to one skilled in the art (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 3, "element_text": "process the first output using an AI slop evaluator model to determine whether the first output contains AI slop characteristics", "corresponding_text": "computing a quality metric for the first response using a learned quality assessment model", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent teaches computing a quality metric for the first response with a learned quality assessment model, which serves the same purpose of evaluating the generated output for undesirable characteristics. Thus the element is disclosed (Y). Using such a model to assess quality is a known technique, making the element obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 4, "element_text": "responsive to determining that the first output contains AI slop characteristics, generate improvement comments and regenerate output using the AI generator model based on the user input and the improvement comments", "corresponding_text": "analyzing the first response to identify quality deficiencies; and generating a refined response using the language generation model with modified generation parameters based on the identified quality deficiencies", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target claim includes analyzing the first response to find quality deficiencies and then generating a refined response with modified parameters based on those deficiencies. This maps directly to generating improvement comments and regenerating output in the source. The element is therefore disclosed (Y) and would be considered an obvious refinement of the prior teaching (Y). Verdict Y."}, {"element_number": 5, "element_text": "cause a final output to be rendered at a client device", "corresponding_text": "outputting either the first response or the refined response based on the quality metric", "novelty": "Y", "inventive_step": "Y", "verdict": "Y", "comment": "The target patent recites outputting the first or refined response, which covers the act of rendering a final output. Although it does not specify a client device, the functional concept of delivering the output is disclosed, making the element not novel (Y). Rendering output to a user device is routine, so the element is obvious (Y). Verdict Y."}], "overall_opinion": "The core technical advancements claimed\u2014evaluating AI\u2011generated content for 'AI slop' with a dedicated evaluator model and then regenerating improved output based on explicit improvement comments\u2014are fully anticipated by the prior art's quality assessment model, deficiency analysis, and refined\u2011response generation steps. All mapped elements are disclosed and would be obvious to one of ordinary skill. Consequently, the source patent lacks both novelty and inventive step and is likely invalid over the target patent."}, "status": "ok", "error": null, "truncation_warnings": []}
